window.LMS_DATA = {"meta":{"generated_from":"AI Roles Skills Taxonomy, Oct 20 2026, Nugen IT Services","built_at":"2026-10-20 16:59 UTC","build_id":"2026-10-20_1659_UTC","topic_count":584,"ok_count":584},"tiers":{"1":{"title":"Tier 1 — Shared core (AI Engineer base)","blurb":"Everything in this tier is assumed by Tiers 2 and 3. It is the AI Engineer baseline."},"2":{"title":"Tier 2 — AI Architect (adds)","blurb":"The Architect owns the decisions and boundaries; Tier 1 is implemented by engineers under them."},"3":{"title":"Tier 3 — Forward Deployed Engineer (adds)","blurb":"The FDE takes Tier 1 into a specific customer's systems, data, security posture and people."}},"role_paths":{"all":{"title":"Everything","desc":"All three tiers, no trimming.","rules":{}},"llm-app":{"title":"LLM-app AI Engineer","desc":"Tier 1 minus ML-fundamentals depth; distributed training and feature stores dropped.","rules":{"tier2":"skip","tier3":"skip","ml-fundamentals":"background","caveat":"skip"}},"ml-heavy":{"title":"ML-heavy AI Engineer","desc":"Tier 1 in full.","rules":{"tier2":"skip","tier3":"skip"}},"architect":{"title":"AI Architect","desc":"Tier 1 breadth + Tier 2.","rules":{"tier3":"skip"}},"fde":{"title":"Forward Deployed Engineer","desc":"Tier 1 + Tier 3, with Tier 2 Reliability and Security as background reading.","rules":{"tier2":"skip","reliability":"background","security-governance-compliance":"background"}}},"stages":[{"id":"foundations","title":"Programming foundations","blurb":"The Python, backend and service skills every later module builds on."},{"id":"ml-core","title":"ML and model foundations","blurb":"How models learn, how transformers work inside, and how they are adapted."},{"id":"llm-apps","title":"Building LLM applications","blurb":"Prompting, retrieval, multimodal inputs and agents that use tools reliably."},{"id":"production","title":"Quality and production","blurb":"Evaluating, serving, observing and securing AI systems in production."},{"id":"architecture","title":"Architecture","blurb":"The AI Architect's decisions: system shape, reliability, data, cost, platform and governance."},{"id":"delivery","title":"Customer delivery","blurb":"The Forward Deployed Engineer's work: scoping, integrating, deploying and driving adoption."}],"modules":[{"id":"ml-fundamentals","tier":1,"order":0,"title":"ML fundamentals","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the machine learning that sits underneath every model an AI engineer touches, in\nthree connected parts. The first part covers problem framing. It looks at where the training signal\ncomes from (supervised, unsupervised or self-supervised), at the three main problem shapes\n(classification, regression and clustering), and at the loss function, which is the differentiable\nstand-in for the thing you actually want. The second part covers how a loss becomes a trained model.\nBackpropagation produces the gradient. Gradient descent and the optimizers turn it into updates.\nLearning-rate schedules, warmup, gradient clipping, batch size, gradient accumulation and mixed\nprecision decide whether those updates are stable, fast and affordable. Normalization, residual\nconnections and dropout are the architectural pieces that make deep networks trainable and keep them\nfrom memorizing. The third part covers evaluation: train-test leakage, class imbalance, calibration,\nprecision, recall and F1, ROC-AUC and the confusion matrix that all of those are derived from. After\nthe module you can read a fine-tuning config and say what every hyperparameter does and how they\ndepend on each other. You can diagnose a loss curve that spikes, plateaus or never moves. You can set\nup an evaluation that cannot flatter the model, and you can choose a decision threshold from what each\nkind of error costs. It matters most for AI Engineers, who train, fine-tune and evaluate models\ndirectly, and it gives Architects and FDEs the vocabulary to question a training budget or a headline\nmetric.","why":{"engineer":"You will fine-tune models with AdamW, warmup, cosine decay, clipping, bf16 and gradient accumulation, and debug NaNs, loss spikes and runs that never learn. You will also build routers, guardrails and judges whose thresholds, calibration and confusion matrices decide whether they are safe to ship.","architect":"Training memory, step count and GPU hours follow from batch size, sequence length, precision and the roughly 16 bytes per parameter of optimizer state. Evaluation design (group and time splits, prevalence-aware metrics, calibrated scores feeding cascades) decides whether offline numbers will hold in production, so both belong in the system design.","fde":"Customers arrive with a 0.99 AUC model that fails in production, a fraud or abuse detector tuned for accuracy on 1% positives, or a request to fine-tune on two thousand tickets. You need to find the leak, reset the threshold to their costs and size the fine-tune on their own data, quickly."},"mental_model":"Hold one loop and one gap. The loop is training. A batch goes through the network forward, a loss turns\nthe output into one number, backpropagation sends that number's sensitivity back to every parameter,\nand an optimizer takes a step whose size is set by the learning rate schedule. Every training topic\ncontrols one part of that loop. Loss functions decide what the gradient says. Batch size and\naccumulation decide how noisy the gradient is. Warmup, clipping and mixed precision stop a single bad\nstep from wrecking the run. Normalization and residual connections keep the signal and the gradient at\na usable scale through depth. Dropout and weight decay make memorizing harder. The gap sits between\noffline evaluation and production. A model outputs scores, a threshold turns scores into decisions, and\nthe confusion matrix counts what those decisions got right and wrong. Every evaluation topic asks\nwhether that count will still hold on future data. Leakage makes the gap look smaller than it is.\nImbalance and accuracy hide which errors are being made. ROC-AUC measures ranking, calibration measures\nwhether the probabilities can be trusted, and precision and recall measure the errors at the threshold\nyou ship. When something goes wrong, first decide whether the problem is in the loop (the model did\nnot learn) or in the gap (the model learned the wrong thing, or the measurement was wrong).","order":["supervised-vs-unsupervised-vs-self-supervised-learning","classification","regression","clustering","loss-functions","gradient-descent","backpropagation","optimizers","learning-rate-schedulers","warmup","gradient-clipping","batch-size","gradient-accumulation","mixed-precision","normalization","residual-connections","dropout-regularization","train-test-leakage","confusion-matrix","precision-recall-f1","roc-auc","class-imbalance","calibration"],"order_reasons":{"supervised-vs-unsupervised-vs-self-supervised-learning":"starts with where the training signal comes from, which frames every later choice about labels, pre-training and fine-tuning.","classification":"the most common problem shape, and it introduces logits, softmax and the separation of scoring from deciding.","regression":"the quantity-shaped counterpart, showing that picking a loss means deciding what noise you expect and what you are estimating.","clustering":"the unsupervised shape for data with no labels yet, done on embeddings, before any loss is trained.","loss-functions":"turns each problem shape into a differentiable target and explains why cross-entropy and contrastive losses dominate.","gradient-descent":"the update rule that minimizes the loss, and where the learning rate and the noise of mini-batches first appear.","backpropagation":"how the gradient is computed in one backward pass, and why training memory grows with activations.","optimizers":"SGD, Adam and AdamW turn raw gradients into per-parameter updates and add optimizer-state memory.","learning-rate-schedulers":"makes the learning rate change over time, so it has to be planned in optimizer steps.","warmup":"the start of every schedule, needed because Adam's statistics and the early gradients cannot be trusted.","gradient-clipping":"limits how big a single update can be, building on backpropagation's exploding products and Adam's moment estimates.","batch-size":"couples gradient noise, hardware use, learning rate and step count into one decision.","gradient-accumulation":"reaches the effective batch the recipe asks for when memory only holds a small micro-batch.","mixed-precision":"trades number format for speed and activation memory, which matters for clipping and accumulation order.","normalization":"keeps activations at a stable scale through depth, and where it sits (Pre-LN or Post-LN) explains the need for warmup.","residual-connections":"the identity path that keeps gradients alive in deep stacks, best read alongside normalization placement.","dropout-regularization":"closes the training part by trading training fit for generalization, framed by the gap between training and validation loss.","train-test-leakage":"opens evaluation with the rule that a held-out set has to simulate the future, or every metric after it lies.","confusion-matrix":"the raw table of counts that every classification metric after it is derived from.","precision-recall-f1":"the detection metrics read from the table, and how the threshold trades one against the other.","roc-auc":"scores the ranking across every threshold, which only makes sense once metrics at a single threshold are clear.","class-imbalance":"uses the table, precision and recall, and AUC to show how defaults fail on rare positives and how to fix the threshold first.","calibration":"ends the module by turning scores into trustworthy probabilities for cost-based thresholds, abstention and cascades, repairing what imbalance fixes distort."},"capstone":{"brief":"A support team wants an \"escalate to a senior agent\" classifier for incoming tickets. About 4% of\ntickets need escalating. A missed escalation costs roughly twenty times as much as an unnecessary one,\nand tickets the model is unsure about should go to a human review queue. Use a public support or\ncomplaint dataset, or a synthetic one with realistic customer IDs and timestamps. Start by embedding and\nclustering the tickets to understand what they contain, and use the clusters to draw a stratified sample\nfor labelling or to check the existing labels. Train a TF-IDF logistic regression baseline. Then\nfine-tune a small pre-trained encoder with a training loop you write yourself. Use AdamW, warmup\nfollowed by cosine decay, global-norm gradient clipping, bf16 autocast and gradient accumulation to\nreach a target effective batch, and log loss, learning rate, gradient norm before clipping, and the\nfraction of steps that were clipped. Run one ablation (no warmup, no clipping, or a learning rate ten\ntimes higher) and explain how the curves change. Split the data by customer and by time, and measure\nhow much a naive random split inflates the scores. Evaluate with a confusion matrix, precision–recall\nand ROC curves, and a reliability diagram. Compare class weighting with plain threshold tuning. Apply\ntemperature scaling, then derive the escalation threshold and the review band from the cost ratio, not\nfrom 0.5.","deliverables":["A reproducible training script or notebook containing the baseline and the fine-tuned encoder. Its config names peak learning rate, warmup steps, schedule, clip norm, precision, micro-batch, accumulation steps and effective batch. Logged curves show loss, learning rate, gradient norm before clipping and the fraction of clipped steps, and there is a one-paragraph explanation of the ablation run.","A leakage audit giving metrics under a random split, a customer-group split and a time split, with exact and near-duplicate checks across splits and a list of every fitted preprocessing step and where it is fitted.","An evaluation report with the confusion matrix at the shipped threshold, the precision–recall curve with average precision, ROC-AUC with a bootstrap confidence interval, and per-cluster recall, for both the baseline and the fine-tuned model, with and without class weighting.","A calibration and decision note: a reliability diagram, ECE and Brier score before and after temperature scaling on a split the calibrator never saw, the threshold derived from the 20:1 cost ratio, the review band, and the expected escalations, misses and review-queue volume per thousand tickets at production prevalence.","A short cluster summary that names the main ticket groups found by clustering, gives their sizes, and states which labelling or evaluation-sampling decision each one changed."],"rubric":["The effective batch equals micro-batch × accumulation steps × devices and is stated explicitly. Warmup and total steps are counted in optimizer steps. Printed learning rates at step 0, end of warmup, midpoint and end match the plan, and the ablation's change in its curves is explained by the right mechanism (early Adam variance, exploding gradient norm, noise floor).","The reported headline metric comes from the group or time split, not the random split, the gap between the two is quantified, and no scaler, vectorizer, calibrator or threshold was fitted on data that the final evaluation scores.","Accuracy is not used as the headline number. The confusion matrix, precision and recall at the shipped threshold, and average precision are reported together with prevalence and support, and precision measured on any enriched or rebalanced set is converted to production prevalence.","The threshold is derived from the cost ratio using calibrated probabilities (close to C_FP / (C_FP + C_FN), about 0.05). Calibration error is lower after temperature scaling while ROC-AUC stays the same, and the note shows the expected cost at the chosen threshold compared with the cost at 0.5.","The class-weighting comparison reports its effect on average precision and on calibration separately, and the recommendation states whether weighting is worth keeping once threshold tuning and recalibration are in place."]}},"topics":[{"id":"supervised-vs-unsupervised-vs-self-supervised-learning","key":"ml-fundamentals/supervised-vs-unsupervised-vs-self-supervised-learning","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Supervised vs unsupervised vs self-supervised learning","taxonomy_name":"supervised vs unsupervised vs self-supervised learning","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["ml-fundamentals/classification","ml-fundamentals/regression","ml-fundamentals/clustering","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","training-adaptation/pre-training","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/data-labeling","rag-retrieval/embedding-models"],"tldr":"The three paradigms differ only in where the training signal comes from: a human label (supervised), a\nproperty of the data (unsupervised), or a label manufactured from the input (self-supervised). LLMs exist\nbecause self-supervision turned the internet into free labels.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"classification","key":"ml-fundamentals/classification","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Classification","taxonomy_name":"classification","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["ml-fundamentals/regression","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/confusion-matrix","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","ml-fundamentals/roc-auc","ml-fundamentals/calibration","ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","model-internals/softmax"],"tldr":"Classification predicts which of a fixed set of categories an input belongs to: score each class, turn\nscores into probabilities, then apply a separate decision rule that encodes the cost of each mistake.\nEvery LLM token is a classification over the vocabulary.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"regression","key":"ml-fundamentals/regression","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Regression","taxonomy_name":"regression","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["ml-fundamentals/classification","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","training-adaptation/overfitting","python-for-ai/scikit-learn-basics","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization"],"tldr":"Regression predicts a continuous quantity (latency, cost, a quality score). Choosing squared versus\nabsolute error chooses mean versus median and how much outliers steer the fit. It powers reward models,\ncost predictors and judge scores, and fails through extrapolation, leverage and leakage.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"clustering","key":"ml-fundamentals/clustering","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Clustering","taxonomy_name":"clustering","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["ml-fundamentals/supervised-vs-unsupervised-vs-self-supervised-learning","ml-fundamentals/classification","model-internals/embeddings","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","training-adaptation/data-curation","customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling"],"tldr":"Clustering groups items by similarity without labels, answering \"what kinds of things are in this data?\"\nIn AI engineering it runs on embeddings to find intents, deduplicate and sample evals. The distance\nmetric and the question matter more than the algorithm.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"loss-functions","key":"ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Loss functions (cross-entropy, contrastive)","taxonomy_name":"loss functions (cross-entropy, contrastive)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["ml-fundamentals/classification","ml-fundamentals/regression","ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","model-internals/softmax","model-internals/perplexity","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","multimodal/clip"],"tldr":"A loss is the differentiable stand-in for what you want; its gradient is the model's only signal.\nCross-entropy, −log p(correct answer), trains anything that predicts a distribution, including every LLM\ntoken. Contrastive losses (InfoNCE) are cross-entropy over similarity scores.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"gradient-descent","key":"ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Gradient descent","taxonomy_name":"gradient descent","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","ml-fundamentals/warmup","training-adaptation/learning-rate","training-adaptation/overfitting"],"tldr":"Gradient descent minimises a loss by stepping against the gradient, θ ← θ − η∇L(θ). It scales to\nbillions of parameters because a gradient costs a constant multiple of a forward pass. Mini-batch\nnoise trades precision per step for many more steps, and helps.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"backpropagation","key":"ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Backpropagation","taxonomy_name":"backpropagation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","ml-fundamentals/residual-connections","ml-fundamentals/normalization","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","python-for-ai/pytorch"],"tldr":"Backpropagation gets the loss gradient for every parameter in one backward sweep of the chain rule, at a\nsmall multiple of the forward cost regardless of parameter count. The price is memory: saved activations\nscale with batch × sequence length × depth.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"optimizers","key":"ml-fundamentals/optimizers","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Optimizers (Adam, AdamW)","taxonomy_name":"optimizers (Adam, AdamW)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","training-adaptation/learning-rate","training-adaptation/peft"],"tldr":"An optimizer turns gradients into updates. Adam divides a running mean of each parameter's gradient by\nthe root of its running mean square, so every parameter moves about one learning rate. AdamW decays\nweights directly, not through the gradient, and is the transformer default.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"learning-rate-schedulers","key":"ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Learning-rate schedulers","taxonomy_name":"learning-rate schedulers","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","training-adaptation/learning-rate","training-adaptation/checkpointing"],"tldr":"A scheduler changes the learning rate over the run: big steps while the loss surface is forgiving, small\nones when it is not. It is defined over total optimizer steps, so plan it with batch size and run length.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"warmup","key":"ml-fundamentals/warmup","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Warmup","taxonomy_name":"warmup","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":11,"related":["ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","ml-fundamentals/normalization","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","training-adaptation/learning-rate"],"tldr":"Warmup ramps the learning rate from near zero to its peak over the first few hundred or thousand\noptimizer steps, because early steps are the most dangerous. A short ramp costs almost nothing and removes\nmost early-training divergence.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"gradient-clipping","key":"ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Gradient clipping","taxonomy_name":"gradient clipping","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":11,"related":["ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","training-adaptation/distributed-training"],"tldr":"Gradient clipping scales all gradients down by one factor when their global L2 norm exceeds a threshold,\nkeeping direction and bounding step length, because one rare batch can undo thousands of good steps or\ncorrupt optimizer statistics.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"batch-size","key":"ml-fundamentals/batch-size","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Batch size","taxonomy_name":"batch size","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","training-adaptation/distributed-training","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Batch size is how many examples (for LLMs, tokens) feed one gradient estimate. Bigger batches give cleaner\ngradients and busier GPUs, until the *critical batch size* where extra examples just burn data, so batch,\nlearning rate and steps are one decision.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"gradient-accumulation","key":"ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Gradient accumulation","taxonomy_name":"gradient accumulation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":11,"related":["ml-fundamentals/batch-size","ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","training-adaptation/distributed-training"],"tldr":"Gradient accumulation sums gradients from several micro-batches before one optimizer step, giving the\noptimizer the recipe's batch while memory holds one micro-batch of activations. It costs no memory and\nchanges no optimization, but buys no speed.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"mixed-precision","key":"ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Mixed precision","taxonomy_name":"mixed precision","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","ml-fundamentals/normalization","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","training-adaptation/quantization","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Mixed precision runs matmuls in a 16-bit float for tensor-core speed and half the activation memory, while\nan fp32 weight copy keeps tiny updates from rounding away. fp16 needs loss scaling; bf16 has fp32's range,\nso it is the default where supported.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"normalization","key":"ml-fundamentals/normalization","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Normalization (LayerNorm, RMSNorm)","taxonomy_name":"normalization (LayerNorm, RMSNorm)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["ml-fundamentals/residual-connections","ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","model-internals/transformer","model-internals/self-attention"],"tldr":"Normalization rescales each token's activation vector before a sublayer, so attention and the MLP see a\nstable scale. LayerNorm centers and scales; RMSNorm only scales, cheaper and as good. Pre-LN versus Post-LN\nplacement decides training stability.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"residual-connections","key":"ml-fundamentals/residual-connections","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Residual connections","taxonomy_name":"residual connections","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["ml-fundamentals/normalization","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","ml-fundamentals/warmup","model-internals/transformer","model-internals/self-attention","training-adaptation/peft"],"tldr":"A residual connection adds a layer's input to its output, `y = x + F(x)`, so each layer learns a\ncorrection, not a replacement. The identity path keeps gradients alive through depth; in transformers it\nis the residual stream all heads and MLPs share.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"dropout-regularization","key":"ml-fundamentals/dropout-regularization","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Dropout / regularization","taxonomy_name":"dropout / regularization","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/normalization","ml-fundamentals/residual-connections","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","training-adaptation/overfitting","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/train-val-test-split"],"tldr":"Regularization makes a model fit training data worse so it generalizes better: weight penalties, injected\nnoise (dropout) or early stopping. Use it when the model can memorize, as in small fine-tunes; skip\ndropout for single-epoch pretraining.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"train-test-leakage","key":"ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Train/test leakage","taxonomy_name":"train/test leakage","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","training-adaptation/overfitting","evals/dataset-contamination","evals/golden-dataset","data-architecture/feature-stores","data-architecture/data-lineage","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes"],"tldr":"Leakage is any path by which prediction-time-unavailable or evaluation-set information reaches the model\nor metric. It inflates offline numbers, so you ship something that collapses in production, and it looks\nlike a \"surprisingly predictive\" feature, not a bug.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"class-imbalance","key":"ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Class imbalance","taxonomy_name":"class imbalance","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/confusion-matrix","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","ml-fundamentals/roc-auc","ml-fundamentals/calibration","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification"],"tldr":"With a rare class, accuracy, an unweighted loss and a 0.5 threshold quietly optimise for \"say the common\nclass\". It is a cost problem: fix evaluation, then the threshold, and only then weights, resampling or\nfocal loss, which distort probabilities.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"calibration","key":"ml-fundamentals/calibration","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Calibration","taxonomy_name":"calibration","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/roc-auc","ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/confusion-matrix","model-internals/sampling-strategies","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","system-design/model-routing-cascading","agents/human-in-the-loop"],"tldr":"A calibrated model's confidence matches its hit rate: of everything it calls \"90%\", about 90% is right.\nDecisions such as thresholds, abstention and escalation need this; ranking does not. Measure it on held-out\ndata and fix it post hoc, usually with temperature scaling.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"precision-recall-f1","key":"ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Precision / recall / F1","taxonomy_name":"precision / recall / F1","kind":"metric","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":14,"related":["ml-fundamentals/confusion-matrix","ml-fundamentals/roc-auc","ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","ml-fundamentals/calibration","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","evals/retrieval-evaluation","evals/hallucination-evaluation","safety-security/input-output-guardrails"],"tldr":"Precision: of what I flagged, how much was right. Recall: of what was there, how much I found. The\nthreshold trades them, so a scorer is a curve; F1 picks one point and assumes both errors cost the same,\nwhich production rarely grants.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"roc-auc","key":"ml-fundamentals/roc-auc","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"ROC-AUC","taxonomy_name":"ROC-AUC","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["ml-fundamentals/confusion-matrix","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","ml-fundamentals/calibration","evals/model-evaluation","evals/llm-as-a-judge","rag-retrieval/reranking","system-design/model-routing-cascading"],"tldr":"ROC-AUC is the probability that a random positive outscores a random negative: ranking quality across all\nthresholds, unaffected by prevalence. Right for comparing scorers; wrong for judging a deployed rare-positive\nclassifier, where it hides false-alarm volume and calibration.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"confusion-matrix","key":"ml-fundamentals/confusion-matrix","module":"ml-fundamentals","name":"Confusion matrix","taxonomy_name":"confusion matrix","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["ml-fundamentals/classification","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","ml-fundamentals/roc-auc","ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","ml-fundamentals/calibration","evals/error-analysis","evals/llm-as-a-judge","safety-security/content-moderation"],"tldr":"A confusion matrix counts predictions against truth, one cell per (actual, predicted) pair. Every\nclassification metric is a ratio of its cells, so read the table first, then pick the metric from which\nerrors are expensive, a product decision.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"ml-core","stage_index":2,"track_order":5},{"id":"model-internals","tier":1,"order":1,"title":"Model internals","intro":{"overview":"This module opens up the large language model and follows a token all the way through it. It starts\nwith the transformer block and the attention mechanism at its centre: self-attention as the only\noperation that moves information between positions, multi-head attention as several lookups in\nparallel, and grouped-query attention and FlashAttention as the two fixes for attention's memory cost.\nPositional encoding, tokenization and embeddings explain how text becomes ordered vectors in the first\nplace, and llm-architecture explains why one causal decoder stack won over encoder–decoder designs.\nThe serving half follows from the same shapes: the context window and the KV cache set how much a\nrequest can hold and how many requests a GPU can serve, logits and softmax are the raw output and its\nnormalisation, sampling strategies and speculative decoding turn that output into text quickly and\npredictably. The final group covers the model as a whole: mixture-of-experts and small language models\nas two answers to the cost of parameters, scaling laws as the rule that links size, data and compute,\nperplexity as the native quality metric, hallucination as a consequence of the training objective, and\nreasoning models as the move from bigger weights to more inference compute. Afterwards you can read a\nmodel config and derive parameters, FLOPs per token and KV-cache bytes; explain latency, cost and odd\nbehaviours from first principles; choose decoding settings from a task's error model; and pick or\nreject a model for a deployment with numbers instead of benchmark tables. It matters most for AI\nEngineers, and it is the shared vocabulary Architects and FDEs need to defend model and hardware choices.","why":{"engineer":"You will tune temperature and top-p, budget prompts with the real tokenizer, read logprobs, enable prefix caching and speculative decoding, and debug truncation, NaNs and repetition loops, and each of these is only predictable once you know the logits-to-sampler path and what the KV cache holds.","architect":"Concurrency per GPU, cost per request and the dense-versus-MoE-versus-SLM decision all follow from head counts, KV-cache bytes per token, active versus total parameters and the 2N-FLOPs-per-token rule, so capacity plans and model choices are arithmetic you can check rather than vendor claims.","fde":"Customers ask why their language costs three times more tokens, why the model invents facts, whether their older GPUs can run long context, and whether a small fine-tuned model can replace the API; you need first-principles answers and a quick measurement on their own data."},"mental_model":"Hold one picture: text goes through a pipeline of tensors with fixed shapes, and every cost and\nbehaviour can be traced to one stage of it. The tokenizer turns characters into ids, which sets the\ntoken bill. Embeddings turn ids into vectors, positional encoding stamps order onto them, and a stack of\nidentical blocks alternates attention (positions exchange information, cost n² in length, cache grows\nwith length times KV heads) with a feed-forward network (each position transformed alone, cost tracks\nparameters). The last hidden state becomes logits, softmax turns them into a distribution, and a\nsampler picks one token, which is appended and fed back. Prefill is that pipeline run once over the\nprompt and limited by compute; decode is the loop, limited by memory bandwidth. When something is slow,\nexpensive or wrong, ask which stage and which shape is responsible: token count, sequence length, KV\nheads, active parameters, the logit vector or the sampler. Hallucination and reasoning sit at the end\nof the loop: the objective rewards plausible continuations, and more loop iterations buy more compute.","order":["tokenization","embeddings","attention-mechanism","softmax","self-attention","multi-head-attention","positional-encoding","transformer","llm-architecture","logits","sampling-strategies","context-window","kv-cache","grouped-query-attention","flashattention","speculative-decoding","perplexity","scaling-laws","mixture-of-experts","small-language-models","hallucination","reasoning-models-test-time-compute"],"order_reasons":{"tokenization":"text must become token ids before anything else happens, and tokens are the unit of cost and context used everywhere after.","embeddings":"turns token ids into the vectors the network computes on, and separates the LLM's embedding table from embedding models.","attention-mechanism":"introduces the query, key and value lookup that every later attention topic refines.","softmax":"the normalisation inside every attention head and at the output, needed before scaling, masking and sampling make sense.","self-attention":"applies attention within one sequence, adds masking and the O(n²) cost that drives the serving topics.","multi-head-attention":"splits the width into parallel heads and introduces the head counts read from every model config.","positional-encoding":"supplies the order signal self-attention lacks and explains RoPE and context extension.","transformer":"assembles attention, the feed-forward network, residuals and normalisation into the block and derives 2N FLOPs per token.","llm-architecture":"compares encoder, decoder and encoder–decoder stacks and explains why causal decoders dominate chat models.","logits":"the raw per-vocabulary scores the stack outputs, where logit bias, constrained decoding and logprobs act.","sampling-strategies":"turns the softmaxed logits into tokens with greedy, beam, temperature, top-k, top-p and min-p.","context-window":"the budget every request shares, constrained by attention cost, cache size and trained positions.","kv-cache":"makes decoding incremental and turns sequence length into GPU memory, the limit on concurrency.","grouped-query-attention":"shrinks the KV cache by sharing key/value heads, which only lands after the cache formula.","flashattention":"removes the n² memory traffic with tiling and online softmax, building on self-attention and softmax.","speculative-decoding":"uses idle compute during bandwidth-bound decode, relying on logits, sampling and the cache.","perplexity":"the native fit metric computed from logits, needed before comparing models, quantisation and scale.","scaling-laws":"links parameters, tokens and compute to loss, and frames over-training and inference cost.","mixture-of-experts":"separates active from total parameters, building on the FFN and on scaling arguments.","small-language-models":"applies scaling and distillation results to narrow, cheap and private deployments.","hallucination":"explains fabrication from the objective, decoding and incentives, using logits, sampling and calibration.","reasoning-models-test-time-compute":"closes the module by spending more decode tokens instead of more parameters, tying cost back to the loop."},"capstone":{"brief":"A customer wants to move a multilingual support-ticket triage and reply-drafting workload off a\nfrontier API onto self-hosted open weights, on a node with two 48 GB GPUs, and asks you to recommend a\nmodel and serving configuration with evidence. Shortlist three open models: one dense mid-size model,\none mixture-of-experts model and one small language model of 8B parameters or fewer. From each model's\nconfig alone, derive parameter count, active parameters, FLOPs per token and KV-cache bytes per token,\nthen compute the maximum concurrent requests at the customer's real prompt and output lengths. Measure\ntokens per character for each tokenizer on a sample of the customer's tickets in at least three\nlanguages. Serve at least two candidates with an inference engine such as vLLM or llama.cpp and measure\nthroughput and time per output token at several concurrency levels, with and without prefix caching\nand speculative decoding. Compare a quantised build against the original with perplexity on held-out\ntickets. Run a small grid of sampling settings for the classification step and the drafting step, and\nmeasure hallucination on drafts as unsupported claims against the ticket and knowledge-base article.\nFinally, test whether a reasoning mode or best-of-n voting improves the hardest triage cases enough to\njustify its token cost, and route only those cases to it.","deliverables":["A config-derived capacity sheet (script or notebook) that computes parameters, active parameters, 2N FLOPs per token, KV-cache bytes per token and maximum concurrency at the target context for each candidate, checked against the engine's reported memory.","A tokenizer cost table giving tokens per character and estimated monthly token volume per language for each candidate, measured on the customer's own ticket sample with each model's real tokenizer.","A benchmark report of throughput, p50 and p95 time per output token and KV-cache utilisation across concurrency levels, with prefix caching and speculative decoding toggled, including the speculative acceptance rate.","An evaluation set of 50+ labelled tickets with triage labels and reference facts, plus results for the sampling grid, the quantised-versus-original perplexity check, the unsupported-claim rate on drafts and the accuracy-versus-tokens curve for the reasoning or best-of-n escalation.","A two-page recommendation naming the model, quantisation, decoding settings per step, context limit and routing rule, with cost per resolved ticket compared to the current API."],"rubric":["Every capacity number traces to named config fields (`hidden_size`, `num_hidden_layers`, `num_key_value_heads`, `head_dim`, expert counts) through a written formula, and predicted KV-cache memory agrees with the engine's measurement within 15 percent.","Benchmarks are run at production-like concurrency and prompt lengths, and the report explains each result with the right mechanism (bandwidth-bound decode, cache pressure, preemptions, low draft acceptance) rather than only listing numbers.","Sampling settings differ between triage and drafting and are justified by measured accuracy and format-failure rate from the grid, not copied defaults.","Hallucination is reported as a claim-level unsupported rate on the labelled set with at least five traced examples, and the recommendation includes a concrete mitigation such as grounding, abstention or verification, with its measured effect.","The escalation rule is backed by an accuracy-versus-tokens curve showing the chosen budget sits at the knee, and the recommendation states which tickets are routed to it and what that adds to cost per ticket."]}},"topics":[{"id":"transformer","key":"model-internals/transformer","module":"model-internals","name":"Transformer","taxonomy_name":"transformer","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/attention-mechanism","model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/multi-head-attention","model-internals/positional-encoding","model-internals/llm-architecture","model-internals/embeddings","ml-fundamentals/residual-connections","ml-fundamentals/normalization"],"tldr":"A transformer stacks identical blocks: attention lets positions exchange information, then a\nper-position MLP transforms each. Positions run in parallel and any two are one step apart, which made\nweb-scale training affordable. Nearly every LLM is a decoder-only variant.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"attention-mechanism","key":"model-internals/attention-mechanism","module":"model-internals","name":"Attention mechanism","taxonomy_name":"attention mechanism","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/multi-head-attention","model-internals/transformer","model-internals/softmax","model-internals/embeddings","rag-retrieval/similarity-search"],"tldr":"Attention is a differentiable lookup: a query is scored against keys, a softmax turns scores into weights\nsumming to one, and the output is the weighted sum of values. It replaces one fixed-size summary with\nlearned, on-demand reads of the input.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"self-attention","key":"model-internals/self-attention","module":"model-internals","name":"Self-attention","taxonomy_name":"self-attention","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/attention-mechanism","model-internals/multi-head-attention","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/positional-encoding","model-internals/flashattention","model-internals/context-window","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode"],"tldr":"Self-attention draws queries, keys and values from one sequence, so each token builds its representation\nfrom the tokens it may see. It is the only cross-position operation, one matmul over all positions, and\nO(n²): the root of context limits and FlashAttention.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-head-attention","key":"model-internals/multi-head-attention","module":"model-internals","name":"Multi-head attention","taxonomy_name":"multi-head attention","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/attention-mechanism","model-internals/grouped-query-attention","model-internals/flashattention","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/transformer","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference"],"tldr":"Multi-head attention runs *h* attentions in parallel, each in a d_model / h subspace, and concatenates\nthem, so a position can look in *h* places for *h* reasons. Parameters and FLOPs match one wide head;\nattention-probability memory and the sharding unit scale with *h*.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"grouped-query-attention","key":"model-internals/grouped-query-attention","module":"model-internals","name":"Grouped-query attention (GQA)","taxonomy_name":"grouped-query attention (GQA)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["model-internals/multi-head-attention","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/llm-architecture","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","serving-inference/tpot","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"GQA lets several query heads share one key/value head. Decode is bound by reading the KV cache, which\nscales with KV heads, so going from 32 to 8 shrinks it fourfold at small quality cost; Llama 2 70B,\nLlama 3 and Mistral adopted it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"flashattention","key":"model-internals/flashattention","module":"model-internals","name":"FlashAttention","taxonomy_name":"FlashAttention","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/multi-head-attention","model-internals/context-window","serving-inference/pagedattention","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","python-for-ai/pytorch"],"tldr":"FlashAttention computes exact softmax attention without writing the n×n scores to GPU memory, streaming\nK/V blocks through SRAM with a running softmax. Attention was slow from memory traffic, not FLOPs, so it\nruns faster and long contexts fit.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"positional-encoding","key":"model-internals/positional-encoding","module":"model-internals","name":"Positional encoding (RoPE)","taxonomy_name":"positional encoding (RoPE)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/context-window","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/transformer","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","prompting/context-engineering"],"tldr":"Self-attention sees a set, so transformers need an order signal. RoPE rotates each query and key by an\nangle proportional to position, so their dot product depends only on distance. It is the open-LLM\ndefault; context extension means stretching those angles.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tokenization","key":"model-internals/tokenization","module":"model-internals","name":"Tokenization (BPE, SentencePiece)","taxonomy_name":"tokenization (BPE, SentencePiece)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/embeddings","model-internals/context-window","model-internals/logits","python-for-ai/tiktoken","observability/token-cost-tracking","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies"],"tldr":"A tokenizer turns text into integer ids. BPE repeatedly merges the most common adjacent pair, so common\nwords become one token and rare words split. SentencePiece trains BPE or unigram on raw text. Tokens set\ncost, context and many odd behaviours.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"embeddings","key":"model-internals/embeddings","module":"model-internals","name":"Embeddings","taxonomy_name":"embeddings","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/tokenization","model-internals/transformer","model-internals/positional-encoding","model-internals/logits","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions"],"tldr":"An embedding is a learned dense vector standing in for a discrete thing (a token, a sentence). Inside an\nLLM it turns token ids into vectors; an embedding *model* turns whole passages into vectors whose distance\ntracks meaning, which powers semantic search.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"llm-architecture","key":"model-internals/llm-architecture","module":"model-internals","name":"LLM architecture (encoder–decoder vs decoder-only)","taxonomy_name":"LLM architecture (encoder–decoder vs decoder-only)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["model-internals/transformer","model-internals/attention-mechanism","model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/context-window","training-adaptation/pre-training","rag-retrieval/reranking","multimodal/speech-to-text"],"tldr":"Encoder–decoder models read input bidirectionally, then write via cross-attention; decoder-only models\ntreat prompt and answer as one causal stream. Decoder-only won for chat: it scales on raw text and\nreuses its cache. Encoder–decoder still wins for fixed inputs, short outputs.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"context-window","key":"model-internals/context-window","module":"model-internals","name":"Context window","taxonomy_name":"context window","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/positional-encoding","model-internals/tokenization","model-internals/self-attention","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/context-engineering","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle"],"tldr":"The context window is the maximum tokens a model attends over in one request, shared by system prompt,\nhistory, documents, tool results and the answer. It is finite because of attention cost, KV-cache memory\nand trained positions, so budgeting it is core engineering work.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"kv-cache","key":"model-internals/kv-cache","module":"model-internals","name":"KV cache","taxonomy_name":"KV cache","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/self-attention","model-internals/grouped-query-attention","model-internals/context-window","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/pagedattention","serving-inference/prefix-caching"],"tldr":"The KV cache stores earlier tokens' keys and values so a decoder projects each position once, not once\nper step. Generation becomes cheap in compute but costly in GPU memory, so memory usually caps how many\nrequests a server runs.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"logits","key":"model-internals/logits","module":"model-internals","name":"Logits","taxonomy_name":"logits","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["model-internals/softmax","model-internals/sampling-strategies","model-internals/embeddings","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/calibration","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode"],"tldr":"Logits are the raw, unnormalized scores a model emits for every vocabulary entry before softmax. Every\nno-retraining control (temperature, logit bias, bans, constrained decoding) edits this vector, and every\nconfidence measure (logprobs, perplexity) is read from it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"softmax","key":"model-internals/softmax","module":"model-internals","name":"Softmax","taxonomy_name":"softmax","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["model-internals/logits","model-internals/attention-mechanism","model-internals/sampling-strategies","model-internals/flashattention","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","ml-fundamentals/calibration","ml-fundamentals/classification"],"tldr":"Softmax turns arbitrary real scores into a probability distribution, with sharpness set by temperature\n*T*. An LLM uses it inside every attention head and at the output; \"never exactly zero\" and \"must sum to\none\" explain sampling tails, attention sinks and numerical bugs.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"sampling-strategies","key":"model-internals/sampling-strategies","module":"model-internals","name":"Sampling strategies (temperature, top-k / top-p, greedy vs beam)","taxonomy_name":"sampling strategies (temperature, top-k / top-p, greedy vs beam)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["model-internals/logits","model-internals/softmax","model-internals/hallucination","model-internals/perplexity","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","evals/model-evaluation"],"tldr":"A sampling strategy turns each step's next-token distribution into one token. Greedy loops, beam search\nsuits translation but is bland, and sampling with temperature plus top-k or top-p trades determinism for\ndiversity. Choose by whether the task has one correct answer.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"speculative-decoding","key":"model-internals/speculative-decoding","module":"model-internals","name":"Speculative decoding","taxonomy_name":"speculative decoding","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/sampling-strategies","model-internals/small-language-models","model-internals/logits","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/inference-optimization","serving-inference/tpot","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Speculative decoding spends decode's idle GPU compute: a cheap draft guesses several tokens and the large\nmodel verifies them in one pass. A rejection-sampling rule keeps outputs exactly the large model's, so\nthe gain is pure latency, set by draft acceptance.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"mixture-of-experts","key":"model-internals/mixture-of-experts","module":"model-internals","name":"Mixture of experts (MoE)","taxonomy_name":"mixture of experts (MoE)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/transformer","model-internals/llm-architecture","model-internals/scaling-laws","model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","training-adaptation/distributed-training","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing"],"tldr":"An MoE transformer swaps each dense FFN for many expert FFNs plus a router that picks one or two per\ntoken. Compute tracks active parameters and quality beats a dense model of that size, but every expert\nmust sit in memory.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"small-language-models","key":"model-internals/small-language-models","module":"model-internals","name":"Small language models (SLMs)","taxonomy_name":"small language models (SLMs)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["model-internals/scaling-laws","model-internals/hallucination","model-internals/kv-cache","system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","system-design/model-routing-cascading","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","training-adaptation/quantization","infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment"],"tldr":"An SLM is a model of roughly 0.5–10B parameters (no fixed boundary) that runs on one consumer GPU, a CPU\nor a phone. Modern recipes make them strong on narrow tasks, trading knowledge and reasoning for speed,\ncost, privacy and cheap fine-tuning.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"scaling-laws","key":"model-internals/scaling-laws","module":"model-internals","name":"Scaling laws","taxonomy_name":"scaling laws","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/small-language-models","model-internals/mixture-of-experts","model-internals/perplexity","model-internals/reasoning-models-test-time-compute","training-adaptation/pre-training","training-adaptation/data-curation","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling"],"tldr":"Scaling laws are power-law fits predicting pre-training loss from parameters N, tokens D and compute C.\nChinchilla set about 20 tokens per parameter as compute-optimal; practice now over-trains smaller models\nbecause serving cost scales with N.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"perplexity","key":"model-internals/perplexity","module":"model-internals","name":"Perplexity","taxonomy_name":"perplexity","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["ml-fundamentals/loss-functions","model-internals/tokenization","model-internals/logits","model-internals/softmax","model-internals/scaling-laws","evals/model-evaluation","evals/dataset-contamination","serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation"],"tldr":"Perplexity is `exp(mean of −log p(token))`, the effective number of equally likely choices per token;\nlower is better. It tracks training, quantisation and data quality cheaply, but is tokenizer-dependent and\nblind to instruction-following and facts.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"hallucination","key":"model-internals/hallucination","module":"model-internals","name":"Hallucination","taxonomy_name":"hallucination","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/sampling-strategies","model-internals/small-language-models","ml-fundamentals/calibration","evals/hallucination-evaluation","evals/faithfulness-groundedness","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation"],"tldr":"Hallucination is fluent, confident output unsupported by the input or by reality. It follows from how\nmodels work, not a bug awaiting a patch, so manage it like an error rate: measure, ground in evidence,\nverify claims, and allow abstention.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"reasoning-models-test-time-compute","key":"model-internals/reasoning-models-test-time-compute","module":"model-internals","name":"Reasoning models / test-time compute","taxonomy_name":"reasoning models / test-time compute","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["prompting/chain-of-thought","model-internals/sampling-strategies","model-internals/scaling-laws","model-internals/kv-cache","training-adaptation/grpo","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","agents/reflection-self-critique","system-design/model-routing-cascading"],"tldr":"Test-time compute spends more inference per query: longer chains of thought, several attempts with a vote\nor verifier, or search. Reasoning models are RL-trained to do this in a thinking trace, often buying more\naccuracy per dollar than a bigger model.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"ml-core","stage_index":2,"track_order":6},{"id":"training-adaptation","tier":1,"order":2,"title":"Training & adaptation","intro":{"overview":"This module follows a model from random weights to a deployed, adapted artifact, and teaches you when\neach stage is worth paying for. It opens with the lifecycle: pre-training produces a base model that\nknows language but does not follow instructions, and post-training (supervised fine-tuning, instruction\ntuning, then preference and reward optimisation with RLHF, DPO and GRPO) installs the behaviour users\nactually see. Before any of that, the fine-tune-vs-RAG-vs-prompt decision asks whether your gap is\nknowledge, behaviour or economics, because only the last two are good reasons to touch weights. The data\ngroup (curation, labelling, synthetic generation and a split that follows the real unit of\ngeneralisation) is where most fine-tunes are won or lost. The training-dynamics group (learning rate,\nepochs, overfitting, catastrophic forgetting, checkpointing) explains how a small dataset quietly turns\ninto memorisation or a regression on general skills. The efficiency group (PEFT and QLoRA, gradient\ncheckpointing, tooling such as TRL, Axolotl and Unsloth, and distributed training) makes a run fit the\nhardware you have. The compression group (distillation, quantization, pruning, model merging) turns a\ngood model into one that is cheap to serve. Afterwards you can decide with evals whether to train at all,\nbuild and audit a fine-tuning dataset, run a LoRA SFT and a DPO pass on one GPU, catch overfitting and\nforgetting before release, and ship a quantised or merged model whose quality loss you have measured. It\nmatters most for AI Engineers who own a fine-tune end to end, and gives Architects and FDEs the numbers\nto approve or turn down one.","why":{"engineer":"You will format chat data through the right template, mask the loss to assistant turns, pick a learning rate and epoch count for a few thousand rows, attach LoRA to the right projections, read diverging train and validation curves, and resume a crashed run from the last good checkpoint without losing optimizer state.","architect":"Whether to fine-tune, retrieve or prompt sets cost per request, freshness, tenant isolation and the redo bill on every base-model upgrade, and memory per parameter, adapter-per-tenant serving, quantised weight bytes and distillation targets decide hardware, latency and the platform you commit to.","fde":"Customers arrive saying \"fine-tune it on our documents\"; you need to show with a quick eval that RAG fixes their knowledge gap, scope a small fine-tune for the format or cost problem that remains, audit their labels for agreement, and keep their private data inside their environment."},"mental_model":"Hold one picture: training is a single dial, the gradient, applied to one shared set of weights, and\nevery lesson is about what you point it at, how hard you turn it, and what else moves. The data decides\nthe target: whatever is in the batch, including duplicates, label noise, leaked test items and\nsynthetic artefacts, is what the model becomes. The objective decides the kind of signal: next-token\nimitation for pre-training and SFT, comparisons for RLHF and DPO, verifiable scores for GRPO, a\nteacher's distribution for distillation. The learning rate, epochs and trainable capacity decide how far\nthe weights travel from the base, and distance is the hazard: too far on a small set means overfitting\nyour rows and forgetting everything else. The held-out splits are the only instruments that tell you\nwhere you landed. Memory and compute are the budget: PEFT, gradient checkpointing, sharding and\nquantisation are ways to afford the turn. Compression and merging act after training, trading measured\nquality for serving cost. Before any of it, ask whether the weights are the object that needs changing.","order":["pre-training","post-training","fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","data-curation","data-labeling","synthetic-data-generation","train-val-test-split","supervised-fine-tuning","instruction-tuning","learning-rate","epochs","overfitting","catastrophic-forgetting","peft","gradient-checkpointing","checkpointing","tooling","rlhf","dpo","grpo","knowledge-distillation","quantization","pruning","model-merging","distributed-training"],"order_reasons":{"pre-training":"establishes what a base model is, the next-token objective and the compute scale every later stage adapts.","post-training":"maps the SFT, preference and RL stages and why they run in that order, giving the module its backbone.","fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision":"decides whether training is the right lever at all before you spend anything on data or GPUs.","data-curation":"selection, deduplication and decontamination set the ceiling no hyperparameter can raise.","data-labeling":"turns raw inputs into targets with measured agreement, since the model learns the labelling function.","synthetic-data-generation":"fills gaps in real data and shows why verification filters decide its value.","train-val-test-split":"cuts by the unit the model must generalise across, which every later evaluation depends on.","supervised-fine-tuning":"the first and most predictable training step, with response-only loss on demonstrations.","instruction-tuning":"extends SFT to a diverse task mixture for zero-shot instruction following.","learning-rate":"the most sensitive knob in every run, including warmup, decay and fine-tuning ranges.","epochs":"how many passes a small set can bear, and why fine-tuning counts epochs while pre-training counts tokens.","overfitting":"reads diverging train and validation loss and applies early stopping and capacity limits.","catastrophic-forgetting":"the regression your task eval cannot see, and the defences of data mixing and general suites.","peft":"LoRA and QLoRA make fine-tuning fit one GPU and yield swappable adapters, limiting forgetting too.","gradient-checkpointing":"trades recomputation for activation memory so long-context fine-tunes fit.","checkpointing":"saves full training state for resume, rollback and best-checkpoint selection, distinct from the previous topic.","tooling":"TRL, Axolotl and Unsloth package the loop you now understand, with the details that are easy to get wrong.","rlhf":"reward models and PPO turn comparisons into a training signal, and introduce reward hacking and the KL anchor.","dpo":"removes the reward model and RL loop from RLHF, building directly on its objective.","grpo":"replaces PPO's critic with group-relative baselines for verifiable rewards, the reasoning-model recipe.","knowledge-distillation":"trains a small student on a teacher's outputs, the bridge from quality to serving cost.","quantization":"cuts weight bytes for bandwidth-bound decode, measured against the unquantised model.","pruning":"removes structure the model barely uses and repairs it, often with distillation.","model-merging":"combines fine-tunes of one base by weight arithmetic, needing PEFT and forgetting intuition first.","distributed-training":"closes with sharding and parallelism for runs that outgrow one GPU, vocabulary for most roles."},"capstone":{"brief":"A customer's support team uses a frontier API to turn incoming tickets into a structured JSON triage\nrecord plus a short reply draft. It costs too much per ticket, the JSON breaks a few percent of the time,\nand they ask whether a fine-tuned open model can replace it. Start by building a held-out evaluation set\nsplit by customer account, then run the decision honestly: measure a prompted baseline and a RAG baseline\nover their help-centre articles on the same set, and name which gap remains (knowledge, format or cost).\nCurate a training set from their historical tickets: deduplicate, strip personal data, decontaminate\nagainst the eval set, double-label a sample to measure agreement, and add verified synthetic examples for\nrare categories. Train a QLoRA SFT adapter on a 3B to 8B open model with TRL, Axolotl or Unsloth,\nsweeping learning rate and epochs and keeping the best checkpoint on validation. Collect preference pairs\non reply drafts and run a DPO pass. Gate the result on a general regression suite to catch forgetting,\nthen quantise the merged model to 4-bit and confirm quality on the test set before recommending it.","deliverables":["A decision memo comparing prompt-only, RAG and fine-tuned variants on the same held-out set, stating which gap each fixes and the cost per 1,000 tickets of each option.","A versioned dataset with a datasheet recording sources, deduplication and decontamination counts, personal-data scrubbing, synthetic share and its verification filter, inter-annotator agreement on the double-labelled sample, and the account-level split.","A reproducible training config (Axolotl YAML or a TRL script) for the SFT and DPO runs, with loss curves, the learning-rate and epoch sweep, and the selected checkpoint saved with full optimizer state.","An evaluation report covering JSON validity, field-level triage accuracy, a judged reply-quality score, a general-capability regression suite run on base and fine-tuned models, and results before and after 4-bit quantisation.","A deployable artifact: the LoRA adapter and the merged, quantised weights, with a note on the serving setup and peak training and inference memory."],"rubric":["The split is by customer account, a check shows no near-duplicate between training and test rows, and the test set is scored once, after every hyperparameter and checkpoint choice was made on validation.","The fine-tune is justified only by a gap the prompt and RAG baselines leave open, and the memo shows that knowledge questions are still served by retrieval rather than baked into the weights.","Loss curves show where validation loss bottomed out, and the chosen checkpoint and epoch count are taken from that point rather than the last step.","The general regression suite shows the fine-tuned model within a stated tolerance of the base, or the report names the mitigation (data mixing, lower rank, fewer steps) and its measured effect.","DPO's effect is reported as a win rate on reply drafts against the SFT-only model, with the reference model and beta stated, and any increase in length or refusals is called out.","The quantised model's JSON validity and triage accuracy are reported next to the bf16 model's, and the recommendation states the accepted quality loss against the latency, memory and cost gained."]}},"topics":[{"id":"pre-training","key":"training-adaptation/pre-training","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Pre-training","taxonomy_name":"pre-training","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["training-adaptation/post-training","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/distributed-training","model-internals/scaling-laws","model-internals/tokenization","model-internals/perplexity","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions"],"tldr":"Pre-training makes a random transformer predict the next token over trillions of tokens. The text is\nits own label (self-supervised), it uses almost all the compute, and it yields a *base model* that\ncompletes text but does not yet follow instructions.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"post-training","key":"training-adaptation/post-training","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Post-training","taxonomy_name":"post-training","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["training-adaptation/pre-training","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/instruction-tuning","training-adaptation/rlhf","training-adaptation/dpo","training-adaptation/grpo","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","evals/model-evaluation"],"tldr":"Post-training turns a base model into a useful, safe assistant: SFT on demonstrations, preference\noptimisation (RLHF, DPO) on comparisons, and RL on verifiable rewards. It uses a tiny fraction of\npre-training compute but decides almost everything users experience.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"supervised-fine-tuning","key":"training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Supervised fine-tuning (SFT)","taxonomy_name":"supervised fine-tuning (SFT)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["training-adaptation/instruction-tuning","training-adaptation/post-training","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","training-adaptation/overfitting","training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/learning-rate","training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision"],"tldr":"SFT trains a pre-trained model on prompt-response demonstrations with next-token loss on the response\ntokens only. It is the cheapest, most predictable way to install a format, persona or domain style, but\nit can only imitate, never rank answers.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"instruction-tuning","key":"training-adaptation/instruction-tuning","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Instruction tuning","taxonomy_name":"instruction tuning","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/post-training","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","training-adaptation/data-curation","prompting/zero-shot","prompting/few-shot","prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/chain-of-thought"],"tldr":"Instruction tuning is SFT on many different tasks, each phrased as a natural-language instruction, so\nthe model learns \"read an instruction, do what it says\" and generalises zero-shot. Task diversity, not\nexamples per task, drives it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"rlhf","key":"training-adaptation/rlhf","module":"training-adaptation","name":"RLHF","taxonomy_name":"RLHF","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["training-adaptation/dpo","training-adaptation/grpo","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/post-training","training-adaptation/data-labeling","evals/pairwise-comparison","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/llm-as-a-judge"],"tldr":"RLHF trains a reward model on human \"which is better\" comparisons, then uses RL (usually PPO) to push\nthe model toward high-scoring responses, with a KL penalty to the SFT model. Its central hazard: the\npolicy learns to exploit the reward model's errors.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"dpo","key":"training-adaptation/dpo","module":"training-adaptation","name":"DPO","taxonomy_name":"DPO","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["training-adaptation/rlhf","training-adaptation/grpo","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/post-training","training-adaptation/overfitting","evals/pairwise-comparison","evals/llm-as-a-judge","ml-fundamentals/loss-functions"],"tldr":"DPO trains on preference pairs with a classification-style loss, no reward model or RL loop, because\nRLHF's KL-constrained optimum expresses reward through the policy itself. It gets most of RLHF's effect\nat SFT cost, but offline.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"grpo","key":"training-adaptation/grpo","module":"training-adaptation","name":"GRPO","taxonomy_name":"GRPO","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["training-adaptation/rlhf","training-adaptation/dpo","training-adaptation/post-training","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","model-internals/reasoning-models-test-time-compute","prompting/chain-of-thought","evals/pass-k","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria"],"tldr":"GRPO is PPO without the value model: sample a group of responses per prompt and use the group's reward\nmean and spread as the baseline. It keeps PPO's clip and a KL term; open reasoning models with\nverifiable rewards usually use it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"peft","key":"training-adaptation/peft","module":"training-adaptation","name":"PEFT (LoRA, QLoRA)","taxonomy_name":"PEFT (LoRA, QLoRA)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/quantization","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","training-adaptation/tooling","python-for-ai/hugging-face","serving-inference/engines","ml-fundamentals/optimizers"],"tldr":"PEFT freezes the base model and trains a few extra parameters. LoRA learns each weight update as two\nthin matrices; QLoRA also stores the base in 4-bit, so one consumer GPU suffices, quality stays close to\nfull fine-tuning, and adapters are swappable.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"knowledge-distillation","key":"training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Knowledge distillation","taxonomy_name":"knowledge distillation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","training-adaptation/pruning","training-adaptation/quantization","model-internals/small-language-models","model-internals/logits","system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","evals/model-evaluation"],"tldr":"Knowledge distillation trains a small student to match a large teacher's outputs (soft probability\ndistributions or generated text) instead of one-hot labels. Each example carries far more signal, so the\nstudent usually beats the same architecture trained on labels alone.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"quantization","key":"training-adaptation/quantization","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Quantization (INT8/INT4, GPTQ, AWQ, GGUF)","taxonomy_name":"quantization (INT8/INT4, GPTQ, AWQ, GGUF)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/pruning","serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation","serving-inference/engines","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision"],"tldr":"Quantization stores weights (sometimes activations) in INT8, INT4 or small floats instead of 16 bits.\nDecode is limited by bytes read, so models fit cheaper hardware and decode faster. GPTQ and AWQ\ncalibrate 4-bit values to minimise output error; GGUF ships them to CPUs.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"pruning","key":"training-adaptation/pruning","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Pruning","taxonomy_name":"pruning","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":14,"related":["training-adaptation/quantization","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","model-internals/small-language-models","model-internals/multi-head-attention","model-internals/mixture-of-experts","serving-inference/inference-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Pruning removes weights, heads, neurons or layers a trained model barely uses, then repairs it by\nfine-tuning or distillation. Only structured removal (rows, heads, layers, or 2:4) gives real GPU speed and\nmemory wins; scattered zeros mostly do not.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-merging","key":"training-adaptation/model-merging","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Model merging","taxonomy_name":"model merging","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","training-adaptation/tooling","evals/regression-tests","system-design/model-portfolio","llmops-platform/model-registry","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets"],"tldr":"Model merging combines fine-tunes of one base into one set of weights by parameter arithmetic (averaging,\ntask vectors, trimming and sign-voting), with no training. You get several skills at one model's serving\ncost, paying with task interference nothing corrects.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-curation","key":"training-adaptation/data-curation","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Data curation","taxonomy_name":"data curation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["training-adaptation/data-labeling","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","evals/dataset-contamination","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","safety-security/pii-redaction"],"tldr":"Data curation is selecting, cleaning, deduplicating, decontaminating and mixing training data. Models learn\nwhatever is in the batch, so a few thousand curated fine-tuning examples beat a hundred thousand scraped\nones, and filtering beats extra pretraining tokens.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"synthetic-data-generation","key":"training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Synthetic data generation","taxonomy_name":"synthetic data generation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/dpo","evals/golden-dataset","evals/llm-as-a-judge","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance"],"tldr":"Synthetic data generation uses a model, program or simulator to manufacture training or eval examples.\nIts value is decided by verification: examples are only as good as the filter choosing survivors, and\nrecursive training on unfiltered output collapses the distribution.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-labeling","key":"training-adaptation/data-labeling","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Data labeling","taxonomy_name":"data labeling","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","training-adaptation/rlhf","training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/rubrics","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"Data labeling turns raw inputs into targets (classes, spans, demonstrations, preferences) whose quality\nyou can measure, chiefly by inter-annotator agreement. A fine-tuned model learns the labeling function,\ninconsistencies included, and a few thousand examples carry the whole signal.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"train-val-test-split","key":"training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Train / validation / test split","taxonomy_name":"train/val/test split","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/data-labeling","training-adaptation/overfitting","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","evals/golden-dataset","evals/dataset-contamination","llmops-platform/drift"],"tldr":"Weights fit on train, decisions fit on validation, and test is read once for an unbiased estimate,\nbecause a score you optimised against is biased. Cut on the unit the model must generalise across\n(conversation, user, document, time), not rows.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"epochs","key":"training-adaptation/epochs","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Epochs","taxonomy_name":"epochs","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["training-adaptation/learning-rate","training-adaptation/overfitting","training-adaptation/checkpointing","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/pre-training","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers"],"tldr":"An epoch is one full pass of the optimiser over the training set, the natural unit for reshuffling,\nevaluating and stopping. More epochs help a small fine-tuning set until the model starts memorising;\npretraining counts tokens and rarely completes one pass.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"learning-rate","key":"training-adaptation/learning-rate","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Learning rate","taxonomy_name":"learning rate","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","ml-fundamentals/warmup","ml-fundamentals/optimizers","ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting"],"tldr":"The learning rate is the optimiser's step size, its most sensitive hyperparameter: a gradient gives\ndirection, not a safe distance. Too large diverges, too small crawls. Peak, warmup and decay are set\ntogether; fine-tuning runs far lower to stay near the pretrained weights.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"overfitting","key":"training-adaptation/overfitting","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Overfitting","taxonomy_name":"overfitting","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","training-adaptation/epochs","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","training-adaptation/checkpointing","training-adaptation/peft","ml-fundamentals/dropout-regularization","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","evals/dataset-contamination"],"tldr":"Overfitting is fitting the particulars of training examples (label noise, phrasing) instead of the\ntransferable pattern: training loss keeps falling while held-out loss stalls then rises. Fine-tuning a\nsmall set for many epochs is the commonest way to overfit an LLM.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"catastrophic-forgetting","key":"training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Catastrophic forgetting","taxonomy_name":"catastrophic forgetting","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["training-adaptation/overfitting","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/rlhf","training-adaptation/model-merging","training-adaptation/learning-rate","training-adaptation/data-curation","evals/regression-tests"],"tldr":"Fine-tuning gradients see only the new loss, so they overwrite weights behind older capabilities. An LLM\nfine-tune can silently lose instruction following, reasoning or safety. Defend with replay, small or\nisolated updates, pulling back toward the original weights, and a regression gate.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"checkpointing","key":"training-adaptation/checkpointing","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Checkpointing","taxonomy_name":"checkpointing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","training-adaptation/distributed-training","training-adaptation/overfitting","training-adaptation/tooling","llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/rollback","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing"],"tldr":"Checkpointing saves the full training state (weights, optimizer, scheduler, RNG, data position) durably\nso runs can resume, roll back and pick the best step. A 7B checkpoint is ~100 GB, so frequency, format\nand retention are engineering decisions. Not gradient checkpointing, which recomputes activations.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"gradient-checkpointing","key":"training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Gradient checkpointing","taxonomy_name":"gradient checkpointing","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["training-adaptation/checkpointing","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/distributed-training","training-adaptation/tooling","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","ml-fundamentals/gradient-accumulation","model-internals/flashattention"],"tldr":"Gradient checkpointing is activation recomputation, not saving to disk. It keeps only each block's input\nand rebuilds the internals during backward, paying roughly one extra forward pass to shrink activation\nmemory enough for long-context fine-tunes to fit on one GPU.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","key":"training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Fine-tune vs RAG vs prompt decision","taxonomy_name":"fine-tune vs RAG vs prompt decision","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","prompting/few-shot","prompting/prompt-caching","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"Prompts change one request's input, RAG adds missing knowledge at request time, and fine-tuning changes\nweights for behaviour, format, skill and cost, not facts. Name the gap, then climb prompt → RAG →\nfine-tune only when evals prove the cheaper rung insufficient.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tooling","key":"training-adaptation/tooling","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Tooling (Unsloth, Axolotl, TRL)","taxonomy_name":"tooling (Unsloth, Axolotl, TRL)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/dpo","training-adaptation/grpo","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","training-adaptation/distributed-training","training-adaptation/quantization","python-for-ai/hugging-face"],"tldr":"Fine-tuning is the same loop every time: load, maybe quantise, attach LoRA, format chat data, train,\nsave the adapter. TRL provides the trainers, Axolotl makes runs reproducible YAML, Unsloth makes\nsingle-GPU LoRA faster. Pick by control, reproducibility or speed.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"distributed-training","key":"training-adaptation/distributed-training","module":"training-adaptation","name":"Distributed training (data / tensor / pipeline parallelism, FSDP, DeepSpeed)","taxonomy_name":"distributed training (data / tensor / pipeline parallelism, FSDP, DeepSpeed)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["training-adaptation/pre-training","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/tooling","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Caveat: this matters only for training-heavy roles; application roles need the vocabulary, not the\npractice. One GPU cannot hold big-model training state or finish pretraining in time, so data, tensor\nand pipeline parallelism plus FSDP/ZeRO split the work; interconnect decides the mix.","format":"crisp","caveat":"only if the JD is training-heavy; drop for LLM-app roles","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"ml-core","stage_index":2,"track_order":7},{"id":"prompting","tier":1,"order":3,"title":"Prompting","intro":{"overview":"This module treats the prompt as what it really is: the full specification of a task, written in the only\ninput a language model has, and handled with the same discipline as code. It opens with prompt engineering\nas a measured loop (specify, run against a fixed eval set, change one variable, measure again) and then\nwalks through the techniques in the order you reach for them. Zero-shot is the baseline. Few-shot is for\nthe cases where showing the shape works better than describing it. Chain-of-thought buys sequential\ncomputation for multi-step problems. The system prompt carries the operator's standing policy. Next come\nthe production mechanics around the words. Templates separate fixed instructions from per-request\nvariables. Structured outputs turn free text into a schema-valid object. Function calling and the ReAct\nloop let the model ask your code to look things up and act. Context engineering decides what fills a\nfinite, billed window, and prompt caching makes a stable prefix nearly free. Versioning makes every\nresponse traceable to the exact prompt that produced it. The module ends with prompt injection and\njailbreaks, which show why the prompt can never be a security boundary. Afterwards you can take a vague\nprompt to a versioned, tested, cache-friendly request builder with schema-checked output and tool use,\nexplain why each part works in terms of how the model was trained and how it runs, and say when prompting\nshould give way to retrieval, tools or fine-tuning. It matters most for AI Engineers, who write and ship\nthese prompts every day, and for FDEs, who tune them against a customer's real data.","why":{"engineer":"You write the request builder, the templates, the tool schemas and the agent loop, so you need to set a zero-shot baseline, add examples or reasoning only when the eval shows a gain, put static text first so caching works, get zero parse failures from constrained decoding, and record a prompt version on every trace.","architect":"You decide what lives in the prompt and what has to live in code: policy with money or legal consequences, tool permissions and injection containment belong outside the model, while context budgets, compaction strategy and the prompt registry with its eval gate and rollback path are platform choices that set both cost and auditability.","fde":"On a customer engagement you turn their vague requirement into a precise spec, choose few-shot examples from their own tickets or documents, threat-model their data sources for injection, red-team the bot against their business policies, and hand over a versioned prompt plus an eval set they can rerun after every model upgrade."},"mental_model":"Picture each model call as one assembled document that the model reads from top to bottom and then\ncontinues, with no hidden channel and no memory beyond what is on the page. Every topic in this module is\na decision about that page. From the top: a byte-stable system section holding policy, tool definitions and\nexamples, which is the cacheable prefix. Below it, managed context: retrieved material, compacted history\nand tool results, kept within a token budget and placed where attention reaches it. At the bottom, the\nper-request variables, rendered through a template. Then comes the generation itself, which you shape with\nreasoning space before the answer, a schema that constrains which tokens can appear, or a structured tool\ncall that hands control back to your code. Everything on the page is just tokens. That one fact explains\nwhy precise specification works, why caching depends on exact prefixes and why injected text can steer\nthe model. It is also why anything that must hold for certain has to be enforced in the code around the\npage, never in the words on it. Versioning is the label on the page that lets you reproduce it later.","order":["prompt-engineering","zero-shot","few-shot","chain-of-thought","system-prompt","prompt-templates","structured-outputs-json-mode","function-tool-calling","react","context-engineering","prompt-caching","prompt-versioning","prompt-injection","jailbreaks"],"order_reasons":{"prompt-engineering":"orients the module: the prompt as a specification and the measure-change-measure loop every later technique is judged by.","zero-shot":"sets the baseline: a precise instruction with no examples, and how to read its failures.","few-shot":"the first lever when a precise zero-shot prompt plateaus, along with the order, label and copy biases examples bring.","chain-of-thought":"adds reasoning tokens for multi-step tasks, building on the baseline-versus-variant A/B habit from the previous two.","system-prompt":"moves from single prompts to deployed products: the operator's channel, why the model honours it and why it guarantees nothing.","prompt-templates":"turns system and user text into a typed, rendered, testable artefact with the static-first layout.","structured-outputs-json-mode":"makes output machine-consumable with constrained decoding, which you need before code can consume tool arguments.","function-tool-calling":"uses schemas to let the model request actions your code executes, the boundary agents are built on.","react":"wraps tool calls in a reason-act-observe loop and shows what the harness owns: budget, termination and safety checks.","context-engineering":"manages the window that loops and retrieval fill: budgets, ordering, pruning and compaction.","prompt-caching":"rewards the stable prefixes that templates and context layout produce, with the economics of writes versus reads.","prompt-versioning":"makes the effective prompt identifiable, evaluated before promotion and revertible, now that it has many moving parts.","prompt-injection":"the attack that follows from \"everything is tokens\", and why containment lives in architecture, not wording.","jailbreaks":"finishes with attacks on safety training and operator policy, measured with attack suites rather than asserted."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a support-triage assistant for a fictional SaaS product, then deliver it as if a customer team will\ntake it over. Given an inbound ticket, it classifies the ticket into a fixed label set, extracts structured\nfields (product area, severity, account id, requested action) into a schema-constrained object, and drafts\na reply. To draft the reply it runs a short ReAct loop over three mock tools: knowledge-base search, account\nlookup, and a refund-request tool that only creates a pending request a human must approve. Start\nzero-shot, measure on a labelled golden set of at least 60 tickets, and add few-shot examples or a\nreasoning field only where the eval shows a gain. The system prompt and tool definitions live in versioned\ntemplate files rendered static-first, so prompt caching hits from the second call. A context budget prunes\nold tool results in longer loops. Every call logs the prompt version hash and token usage. Finally, attack\nyour own build. Seed tickets and knowledge-base articles with indirect injection attempts, and run a\njailbreak suite against two business policies: never promise a refund, and never reveal the system prompt.\nShow that the refund rule is enforced in code, not only in the prompt.","deliverables":["A runnable repository with the request builder, versioned prompt templates with metadata headers, JSON schemas for the extraction output and tool definitions, the ReAct loop with step and token budgets, and the mock tools.","A golden set of 60+ labelled tickets plus an eval script that reports label accuracy, field-level extraction accuracy, parse failure rate, tokens, latency and cache hit ratio for each prompt version.","An experiment log comparing zero-shot, few-shot and chain-of-thought variants on the same set, with the decision each comparison led to.","An attack suite of injected tickets and documents and at least three jailbreak families per policy, with attack success rate and benign pass rate reported per prompt version.","A one-page handover note covering the prompt versions shipped, how to roll back, and which rules are enforced in code versus in the prompt."],"rubric":["Parse failure rate on the golden set is zero, and the eval script prints a per-label confusion matrix plus a per-version comparison table that the experiment log's decisions refer to.","A second identical request reports cache reads in the usage fields, an integration test asserts this, and no per-request field is rendered above the static instructions.","Every logged call carries a prompt version hash, and rolling back to the previous version is a single command that the eval then confirms.","The ReAct loop ends every run with an answer, a budget stop or a handled error, never by looping until the context overflows, and tool-call arguments that fail validation are rejected before execution.","No injected ticket or jailbreak creates an executed refund. The refund tool only ever produces pending requests, which a test checks even when the model is told to approve them."]}},"topics":[{"id":"prompt-engineering","key":"prompting/prompt-engineering","module":"prompting","name":"Prompt engineering","taxonomy_name":"prompt engineering","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["prompting/system-prompt","prompting/few-shot","prompting/chain-of-thought","prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/context-engineering","evals/eval-driven-development","training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","customer-data-evaluation/domain-prompt-engineering"],"tldr":"Prompt engineering specifies a task through the model's input (instructions, context, examples, output\nformat) and iterates against a measured eval set. It is the cheapest lever you own: seconds per\niteration, no training data, no redeploy.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"system-prompt","key":"prompting/system-prompt","module":"prompting","name":"System prompt","taxonomy_name":"system prompt","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["prompting/prompt-engineering","prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/prompt-injection","prompting/context-engineering","training-adaptation/instruction-tuning","serving-inference/prefix-caching","safety-security/prompt-injection"],"tldr":"The system prompt is the operator's channel: a dedicated role at the start of the context that\npost-training taught the model to treat as standing policy. It is a learned prior, not enforcement, and\ncaching makes its stable prefix nearly free.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"zero-shot","key":"prompting/zero-shot","module":"prompting","name":"Zero-shot prompting","taxonomy_name":"zero-shot","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":10,"related":["prompting/few-shot","prompting/chain-of-thought","prompting/prompt-engineering","prompting/system-prompt","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","training-adaptation/instruction-tuning","model-internals/sampling-strategies","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"Zero-shot prompting describes the task in words with no demonstrations. Instruction tuning makes a clearly\ndescribed new task in distribution, so it is the default start: shortest prompt, no example bias. It fails\nwhere the specification cannot be written down precisely.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"few-shot","key":"prompting/few-shot","module":"prompting","name":"Few-shot prompting","taxonomy_name":"few-shot","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["prompting/zero-shot","prompting/prompt-engineering","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/chain-of-thought","training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"Few-shot prompting puts input → output demonstrations in the prompt; in-context learning shifts output\ntoward their format, labels and style with no weight update. It shows what is hard to say, at a per-call\ntoken cost plus order, frequency and copying biases.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"chain-of-thought","key":"prompting/chain-of-thought","module":"prompting","name":"Chain-of-thought prompting","taxonomy_name":"chain-of-thought","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["prompting/zero-shot","prompting/few-shot","prompting/react","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","model-internals/reasoning-models-test-time-compute","model-internals/sampling-strategies","evals/pass-k","agents/reflection-self-critique"],"tldr":"Chain-of-thought (CoT) has the model write reasoning before its answer, buying sequential computation one\nfixed-compute token cannot supply. Gains are large on multi-step problems, near zero on lookups, and the\nchain is not guaranteed to be the answer's real cause.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"react","key":"prompting/react","module":"prompting","name":"ReAct (reason + act)","taxonomy_name":"ReAct","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["prompting/chain-of-thought","prompting/function-tool-calling","prompting/prompt-injection","agents/agent-loop","agents/tool-calling","agents/planning","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-reliability/loop-detection"],"tldr":"ReAct interleaves reasoning and acting in one loop: thought, tool call, observation, repeat, so each step\nreasons over fresh evidence. Native tool calling is ReAct with the parsing moved into the API; the loop,\nbudget and safety checks remain yours.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-templates","key":"prompting/prompt-templates","module":"prompting","name":"Prompt templates","taxonomy_name":"prompt templates","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["prompting/prompt-engineering","prompting/system-prompt","prompting/few-shot","prompting/prompt-versioning","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/prompt-injection","python-for-ai/pydantic","llmops-platform/prompt-registry"],"tldr":"A prompt template separates fixed instructions from per-request variables and renders them into the\nmessage structure the API expects, making prompts typed, tested, versioned and cache-friendly. Two layers\nexist: yours and the model's chat template.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-versioning","key":"prompting/prompt-versioning","module":"prompting","name":"Prompt versioning","taxonomy_name":"prompt versioning","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/prompt-caching","llmops-platform/prompt-registry","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","llmops-platform/rollback","evals/regression-tests","evals/golden-dataset","observability/tracing"],"tldr":"A prompt is behaviour-defining code written as prose, so it needs immutable versions, an id on every\ncall, review, an eval before promotion and one-step rollback. Without that, regressions cannot be dated\nand complaints cannot be reproduced.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-caching","key":"prompting/prompt-caching","module":"prompting","name":"Prompt caching","taxonomy_name":"prompt caching","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["prompting/system-prompt","prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/prompt-versioning","prompting/context-engineering","model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/ttft","observability/token-cost-tracking"],"tldr":"Prompt caching reuses the model's stored state for a repeated prompt prefix when a later request starts\nwith the same bytes, cutting input cost and time-to-first-token. Only exact prefixes match, so prompt\nlayout decides the discount.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"structured-outputs-json-mode","key":"prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","module":"prompting","name":"Structured outputs / JSON mode","taxonomy_name":"structured outputs / JSON mode","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["prompting/function-tool-calling","prompting/prompt-templates","prompting/chain-of-thought","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/typed-tool-contracts","safety-security/output-validation","evals/regression-tests"],"tldr":"JSON mode guarantees parseable JSON; structured outputs guarantee conformance to your schema by masking\ninvalid tokens during decoding. This removes parse failures, not wrong answers, so semantic validation\nand evals remain your job.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"function-tool-calling","key":"prompting/function-tool-calling","module":"prompting","name":"Function / tool calling","taxonomy_name":"function / tool calling","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","prompting/react","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/prompt-injection","agents/agent-loop","agents/tool-calling","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/model-context-protocol"],"tldr":"Tool calling is a trained protocol: you describe functions with a name, description and JSON schema; the\nmodel emits a structured call; your code executes it and returns the result. The model never runs\nanything, so execution, permissions and errors are yours.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"context-engineering","key":"prompting/context-engineering","module":"prompting","name":"Context engineering","taxonomy_name":"context engineering","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["prompting/system-prompt","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/few-shot","model-internals/context-window","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","agents/context-compaction-summarization","agents/agent-memory"],"tldr":"The context window is the model's whole working memory for a call: finite, billed per token, attended\nunevenly. Context engineering decides what goes in, in what order, form and budget; in multi-step systems\nthat decides quality and cost.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-injection","key":"prompting/prompt-injection","module":"prompting","name":"Prompt injection","taxonomy_name":"prompt injection","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["prompting/jailbreaks","prompting/system-prompt","prompting/function-tool-calling","prompting/context-engineering","safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","security-governance-compliance/prompt-injection-defense","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions"],"tldr":"Prompt injection is text from someone other than the operator that the model treats as instructions. A\ntransformer has no privileged channel, so no prompt wording fixes it; containment means limiting what the\nmodel can do and see.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"jailbreaks","key":"prompting/jailbreaks","module":"prompting","name":"Jailbreaks","taxonomy_name":"jailbreaks","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["prompting/prompt-injection","prompting/system-prompt","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","safety-security/content-moderation","safety-security/llama-guard","evals/red-teaming","evals/adversarial-evaluation","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling"],"tldr":"A jailbreak is input crafted to make a model do what its safety training or operator policy forbids.\nRefusal is a learned behaviour competing with helpfulness, so for app builders the defence matches\ninjection: never make the prompt the boundary.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"llm-apps","stage_index":3,"track_order":8},{"id":"rag-retrieval","tier":1,"order":4,"title":"RAG & retrieval","intro":{"overview":"This module is about getting the right evidence in front of a model, and only that evidence, so its\nanswers come from your documents instead of its memory. It follows one document through the whole\nsystem. Parsing turns PDFs, scans and tables into clean ordered text. Chunking, overlap and granularity\ndecide what counts as a retrievable unit. Embedding models, similarity metrics, normalization, ANN\nindexes and vector databases make dense search fast. An idempotent embedding pipeline keeps the index\nin step with its sources. Inverted indexes and BM25 provide the lexical half, and dense-versus-sparse\nanalysis, hybrid fusion and cross-encoder reranking combine the two arms into a staged pipeline tuned on\nrecall and precision. Metadata and ACL filters make certain results impossible to return. Parent-document\nand contextual retrieval repair chunks that lost their context. Query rewriting, HyDE, decomposition and\nmulti-hop loops repair queries that were never going to match. On the generation side the module covers\nlost-in-the-middle position effects, grounding with citations, and measuring whether each citation\nactually supports its claim. It closes with production concerns: freshness, deletion and index\nconsistency, staged failure diagnosis, the long-context-versus-RAG decision, knowledge graphs, GraphRAG\nand agentic RAG. Afterwards you can design, build, measure and debug a production retrieval system,\nand defend each stage with a number. It matters most for AI Engineers, who spend most of their RAG\ntime in these stages, and for FDEs, who have to make retrieval work on a customer's messy, permissioned\ncorpus.","why":{"engineer":"You build the ingestion pipeline with content-hashed chunk ids and query-time parity, run BM25 and dense arms fused with RRF in front of a reranker, and, when an answer is wrong, trace the gold chunk stage by stage to find where it was lost instead of swapping the embedding model on a hunch.","architect":"You choose the vector store from vector count, dimension, filter selectivity and operational capacity, place ACL enforcement inside the search rather than in the prompt, set freshness and deletion SLOs per content class, and decide from arithmetic and evals when long context, a knowledge graph, GraphRAG or an agent loop earns its cost over plain hybrid retrieval.","fde":"You open the customer's parsed text before tuning anything, map their SharePoint or Confluence permissions into per-chunk principals, build a labelled query set from their own users' questions, and show recall@k and citation correctness on their documents so sign-off rests on measurements rather than a polished demo."},"mental_model":"Picture retrieval as a funnel with a leak test at every stage. The whole corpus goes in at the top. The\nparser decides what text exists. The chunker decides what a hit means. The index (dense vectors and an\ninverted index side by side) decides what can be found. Filters remove what this user must never see.\nA wide first stage keeps a few dozen candidates for recall, a reranker keeps a few for precision, and\nthe prompt builder decides where they sit and how they are cited. Each stage can only lose evidence; no\nlater stage can recover what an earlier one dropped. The techniques in this module either widen a\nstage that loses too much (hybrid, rewriting, HyDE, decomposition, multi-hop, contextual chunks) or\nnarrow one that lets too much through (reranking, filtering, a smaller k). So you diagnose by finding\nthe first stage where the gold evidence disappears, fix that stage, and measure it with its own metric.\nAround the funnel runs a sync loop that keeps the index a faithful, current, permission-correct copy of\nits sources.","order":["document-parsing","chunking-strategies","chunk-overlap","retrieval-granularity","embedding-models","similarity-search","embedding-dimension-and-normalization","ann-indexes","vector-databases","embedding-pipelines","inverted-indexes","bm25-internals","dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","hybrid-search","recall-vs-precision-trade-off","reranking","metadata-filtering","acl-aware-retrieval","parent-document-retrieval","contextual-retrieval","query-rewriting","hyde","query-decomposition","multi-hop-retrieval","lost-in-the-middle","citations-grounding","citation-correctness","freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","retrieval-failure-diagnosis","long-context-vs-rag","knowledge-graphs","graphrag","agentic-rag"],"order_reasons":{"document-parsing":"every later stage sees only what the parser produced, so learn to inspect parsed text first.","chunking-strategies":"defines the retrievable unit and why whole documents embed as blurry averages.","chunk-overlap":"a cheap patch for boundary damage, best understood right after the splitters it patches.","retrieval-granularity":"separates the unit you match on from the unit you return, framing later small-to-big work.","embedding-models":"explains bi-encoders, prefixes and versioning, the basis of dense retrieval.","similarity-search":"shows how dot product, cosine and Euclidean relate and why the trained metric must be used.","embedding-dimension-and-normalization":"adds the storage arithmetic, normalization, truncation and quantization behind index sizing.","ann-indexes":"explains HNSW and IVF-PQ and the recall–latency knobs once exact search stops fitting.","vector-databases":"maps pgvector, Qdrant, Weaviate, Milvus, Pinecone and Chroma onto the operations you now understand.","embedding-pipelines":"turns one-off indexing into an idempotent, incremental, versioned job.","inverted-indexes":"introduces posting lists, the data structure underneath lexical search.","bm25-internals":"explains IDF, saturation and length normalization, the lexical baseline every retriever must beat.","dense-vs-sparse-retrieval":"compares the two families' structural failure modes to motivate combining them.","hybrid-search":"fuses the BM25 and dense arms by rank and verifies the gain with recall@k.","recall-vs-precision-trade-off":"frames retrieval as a wide recall stage followed by a narrow precision stage.","reranking":"supplies the cross-encoder precision stage that the trade-off calls for.","metadata-filtering":"places hard predicates inside the search and explains why post-filtering returns too few results.","acl-aware-retrieval":"applies filtering to per-user permissions, the security requirement of every enterprise deployment.","parent-document-retrieval":"implements small-to-big retrieval so matching and answering use different units.","contextual-retrieval":"repairs chunks that lost their subject by enriching them at ingestion.","query-rewriting":"repairs the query side: follow-ups, vocabulary mismatch and pasted noise.","hyde":"bridges the question-versus-passage gap in embedding space with a hypothetical answer.","query-decomposition":"splits compound questions so each facet gets its own retrieval.","multi-hop-retrieval":"chains dependent sub-questions through bridging facts under a hop limit.","lost-in-the-middle":"shows why more context can lower accuracy and how to order evidence in the prompt.","citations-grounding":"constrains the answer to the sources with stable ids, per-claim citations and abstention.","citation-correctness":"measures whether each citation actually supports its claim, the check on the previous lesson.","freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency":"keeps the index current, deletion-safe and version-consistent in production.","retrieval-failure-diagnosis":"ties the funnel together as a staged trace that finds where evidence was lost.","long-context-vs-rag":"decides with cost and accuracy arithmetic when retrieval is needed at all.","knowledge-graphs":"adds typed entities and relationships for questions that are joins and aggregations.","graphrag":"builds community summaries over an extracted graph for corpus-wide and thematic questions.","agentic-rag":"puts retrieval tools in a bounded loop, earned only when the eval set shows the simpler pipelines fall short."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a permission-aware question-answering assistant over a realistic internal corpus of 500 to 2,000\ndocuments: a mix of born-digital PDFs, at least a few scanned pages, table-heavy spreadsheets or reports,\nand wiki pages, each assigned to one of three or four access groups. Start by parsing a sample of every\ndocument type and fixing what breaks. Then build an idempotent ingestion pipeline with structure-aware\nchunking, content-hashed chunk ids, contextual prefixes, and per-chunk metadata holding source, version,\ndate and allowed principals. Index each chunk in both a vector store and a BM25 index, fuse the two arms\nwith reciprocal rank fusion, rerank the top 30 to 50 with a cross-encoder, and return parent sections to\na generator that must cite a source id for every claim or abstain. Add a query rewriter for\nconversational follow-ups and decomposition for compare-style questions. Build a labelled golden set of\nat least 100 questions, sliced into lookup, identifier, paraphrase, compound, multi-hop and unanswerable\ntypes. Measure each stage, then make the pipeline survive change: edit, revoke and delete documents and\nprove that retrieval reflects each change within a stated lag. Finish with a written analysis of when\nlong context or GraphRAG would beat what you built on this corpus.","deliverables":["A parsing report with sampled pages per document type, the observed failures, the fixes applied, and OCR character error rate and table cell accuracy on a hand-checked subset.","The ingestion pipeline, runnable from one command, whose second run on an unchanged corpus re-embeds nothing, plus a CI golden-vector test proving the index-time and query-time embed paths match.","The retrieval service with BM25, dense, RRF fusion, reranking, metadata and ACL filters, parent expansion, query rewriting and decomposition, each stage switchable by configuration.","The labelled golden set and an evaluation report giving recall@50, recall@5 after reranking, MRR, citation recall and precision, and abstention rate, per slice and per ablation (BM25 only, dense only, hybrid, plus reranker, plus contextual prefixes).","A freshness and deletion test suite that edits, revokes and deletes documents and reports staleness lag, deleted-document leak probes and orphan counts from a reconciliation job.","A failure-diagnosis sheet of at least 30 wrong answers, each labelled with the first stage where the gold evidence was lost, with counts per stage and the fix chosen for the largest bucket.","A two-page design memo on long context versus RAG and on whether GraphRAG or an agent loop is warranted for this corpus, with cost-per-query and accuracy arithmetic."],"rubric":["A red-team query run as a low-privilege test user never returns a chunk from a restricted document, and the same holds within the stated lag after a permission is revoked at the source.","The ablation table shows the measured contribution of each stage, and any stage that does not improve its target metric on the golden set is either removed or justified in writing.","Recall at candidate depth, recall after reranking, and gold-in-context rate are reported separately, so a generation failure is never misread as a retrieval failure.","Every cited source id in a sampled set of answers resolves to a passage that supports its claim, as checked by an entailment model or calibrated judge whose agreement with human labels is reported.","Deleting a source document removes every derived chunk from both indexes, which is verified by a probe query and a reconciliation run reporting zero orphans.","The diagnosis sheet's largest failure bucket is the one the final change targeted, and the before-and-after numbers for that slice are shown."]}},"topics":[{"id":"chunking-strategies","key":"rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Chunking strategies (fixed, recursive, semantic)","taxonomy_name":"chunking strategies (fixed, recursive, semantic)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunk-overlap","rag-retrieval/retrieval-granularity","rag-retrieval/document-parsing","rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval","rag-retrieval/contextual-retrieval","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","model-internals/tokenization","python-for-ai/tiktoken"],"tldr":"Chunking cuts documents into retrievable units before embedding, because one vector per document blurs\nthe passage a question needs. Fixed-size is predictable, recursive follows natural boundaries, semantic\nfollows topic shifts. The strategy defines what \"a hit\" means.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"chunk-overlap","key":"rag-retrieval/chunk-overlap","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Chunk overlap","taxonomy_name":"chunk overlap","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":10,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/retrieval-granularity","rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval","rag-retrieval/contextual-retrieval","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","python-for-ai/tiktoken"],"tldr":"Overlap makes consecutive chunks share a few boundary tokens, so a straddling sentence appears whole in\nat least one chunk. It is a cheap hedge paid for with a larger index and near-duplicate hits, and it\npatches boundaries rather than fixing them.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"retrieval-granularity","key":"rag-retrieval/retrieval-granularity","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Retrieval granularity","taxonomy_name":"retrieval granularity","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/chunk-overlap","rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval","rag-retrieval/contextual-retrieval","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","model-internals/context-window"],"tldr":"Granularity is the size of the retrieved unit, from sentence to document. Small units match sharply but\nstarve the generator; large units carry context but blur vectors. Resolve it by matching on small units\nand returning their parent, measured per question type.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"embedding-models","key":"rag-retrieval/embedding-models","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Embedding models","taxonomy_name":"embedding models","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["model-internals/embeddings","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","rag-retrieval/reranking","python-for-ai/sentence-transformers","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"An embedding model maps text to a fixed-size vector so similar meanings land close together. Documents\nare embedded once, so retrieval becomes a nearest-neighbour lookup that catches synonyms keyword search\nmisses. Model, prefixes and version must stay consistent, or the index silently breaks.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"embedding-dimension-and-normalization","key":"rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Embedding dimension & normalization","taxonomy_name":"embedding dimension & normalization","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","ml-fundamentals/normalization","training-adaptation/quantization","model-internals/embeddings"],"tldr":"Dimension is how many floats a vector holds; it sets storage and compare cost linearly.\nNormalizing to unit length makes cosine, dot product and Euclidean rank identically and stops long\nchunks winning on norm. Matryoshka truncation and quantization trade a little recall for much memory.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"embedding-pipelines","key":"rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Embedding pipelines","taxonomy_name":"embedding pipelines","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","rag-retrieval/document-parsing","data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers"],"tldr":"An embedding pipeline turns source documents into indexed vectors and keeps them in sync: parse, chunk,\nenrich, embed, upsert, update, delete. Its goals are idempotent chunk ids, incremental updates, and\nidentical embedding at index time and query time.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"vector-databases","key":"rag-retrieval/vector-databases","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Vector databases (pgvector, Qdrant, Pinecone, Weaviate, Milvus, Chroma)","taxonomy_name":"vector databases (pgvector, Qdrant, Pinecone, Weaviate, Milvus, Chroma)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","system-design/build-vs-buy"],"tldr":"A vector database stores embeddings with metadata and returns the k nearest vectors under filters at\nrequest-path speed. pgvector lives in Postgres; Qdrant, Weaviate and Milvus are dedicated engines;\nPinecone is managed; Chroma is for prototypes. Choose on operations and scale, not retrieval quality.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"similarity-search","key":"rag-retrieval/similarity-search","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Similarity search (cosine, dot product)","taxonomy_name":"similarity search (cosine, dot product)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","model-internals/embeddings"],"tldr":"Dense retrieval scores a query vector against every stored vector and keeps the top k. Use the\nsimilarity the embedding model was trained with (cosine or dot product); the wrong one degrades\nrankings silently, with no error to catch.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"ann-indexes","key":"rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"ANN indexes (HNSW, IVF)","taxonomy_name":"ANN indexes (HNSW, IVF)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":19,"related":["rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"ANN indexes trade a few percent of recall for sublinear vector search. HNSW walks a layered proximity\ngraph; IVF scans only the nearest k-means clusters, often with product quantization. Their knobs (ef,\nnprobe) set a recall–latency point you must measure.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","key":"rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Dense vs sparse retrieval","taxonomy_name":"dense vs sparse retrieval","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/bm25-internals","rag-retrieval/inverted-indexes","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","model-internals/tokenization"],"tldr":"Sparse retrieval (BM25 over an inverted index) is exact about terms and blind to meaning; dense\nretrieval (learned vectors plus similarity search) is fluent about meaning and unreliable on exact\nterms. Neither dominates, so production systems usually run both and fuse.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"bm25-internals","key":"rag-retrieval/bm25-internals","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"BM25 internals","taxonomy_name":"BM25 internals","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["rag-retrieval/inverted-indexes","rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","model-internals/tokenization","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"BM25 sums, over query terms, a rarity weight (IDF) times a saturating, length-normalized term frequency.\nEach piece fixes one failure of raw counting, and two parameters, k1 and b, set how strong those\ncorrections are.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"inverted-indexes","key":"rag-retrieval/inverted-indexes","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Inverted indexes","taxonomy_name":"inverted indexes","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/bm25-internals","rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","backend-fundamentals/database-design"],"tldr":"An inverted index maps each term to a sorted posting list of the documents containing it, so a query\nreads only its terms' lists. Sorted ids, compression, skips and immutable segments make lexical search\nover hundreds of millions of documents answer in milliseconds.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"hybrid-search","key":"rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Hybrid search (BM25 + dense)","taxonomy_name":"hybrid search (BM25 + dense)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","rag-retrieval/bm25-internals","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"Hybrid search runs BM25 and a dense retriever on the same query and merges results, because they fail on\ndifferent queries. Scores live on unrelated scales, so fuse by rank (RRF) or normalize per query, and\nprove the gain with recall@k.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"reranking","key":"rag-retrieval/reranking","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Reranking (cross-encoder)","taxonomy_name":"reranking (cross-encoder)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","model-internals/self-attention","evals/retrieval-evaluation","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets"],"tldr":"A cross-encoder scores query and passage together: far more accurate than a bi-encoder, far too slow\nfor a corpus. So a cheap retriever pulls a few dozen candidates, the cross-encoder reorders them, and\nonly the top few reach the prompt.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"recall-vs-precision-trade-off","key":"rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Recall vs precision trade-off","taxonomy_name":"recall vs precision trade-off","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","evals/retrieval-evaluation","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"Recall is the share of answer-bearing passages you retrieved; precision is the share of retrieved passages\nthat bear the answer. Widening top-k trades one for the other, so stage retrieval: wide and cheap for\nrecall, then narrow and expensive for precision.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"query-rewriting","key":"rag-retrieval/query-rewriting","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Query rewriting","taxonomy_name":"query rewriting","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["rag-retrieval/query-decomposition","rag-retrieval/hyde","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/bm25-internals","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","prompting/context-engineering","prompting/prompt-injection"],"tldr":"Query rewriting puts a small LLM step between the user and the retriever that turns raw, conversational\ninput into retrieval-friendly queries, because fixing the key at query time is far cheaper than\nre-indexing the corpus for every way a human might ask.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"query-decomposition","key":"rag-retrieval/query-decomposition","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Query decomposition","taxonomy_name":"query decomposition","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["rag-retrieval/query-rewriting","rag-retrieval/multi-hop-retrieval","rag-retrieval/agentic-rag","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","agents/task-decomposition","agents/parallel-tool-calls","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"Query decomposition has an LLM split a compound question into focused, standalone sub-questions,\nretrieves for each (in parallel when independent) and answers from the merged evidence or from\nper-sub-question answers. Independent parts\nfan out; dependent parts chain, which is multi-hop retrieval.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-hop-retrieval","key":"rag-retrieval/multi-hop-retrieval","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Multi-hop retrieval","taxonomy_name":"multi-hop retrieval","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/query-decomposition","rag-retrieval/agentic-rag","rag-retrieval/graphrag","rag-retrieval/knowledge-graphs","rag-retrieval/reranking","prompting/react","agents/agent-loop","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"Some answers need a second fact that is only findable once you know the first. Multi-hop retrieval\ninterleaves retrieving and reading: retrieve, extract the bridging fact, form the next query, repeat under\na hop limit, then answer.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"hyde","key":"rag-retrieval/hyde","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"HyDE (Hypothetical Document Embeddings)","taxonomy_name":"HyDE","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["rag-retrieval/query-rewriting","rag-retrieval/dense-vs-sparse-retrieval","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/reranking","model-internals/hallucination","model-internals/embeddings"],"tldr":"HyDE has an LLM write a plausible answer passage, embeds that instead of (or with) the question, and\nretrieves the real passages nearest to it. Answer-shaped text lands near real answers even with wrong\nfacts; it costs one generation per query.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"parent-document-retrieval","key":"rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Parent-document retrieval","taxonomy_name":"parent-document retrieval","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/retrieval-granularity","rag-retrieval/chunk-overlap","rag-retrieval/contextual-retrieval","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency"],"tldr":"Small chunks match best because they embed as sharp keys; sections answer best because they hold the\ndefinitions, table headers and caveats. Parent-document retrieval searches small children and hands the\ngenerator their parents: small-chunk recall with big-chunk context, paid for in tokens.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"contextual-retrieval","key":"rag-retrieval/contextual-retrieval","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Contextual retrieval","taxonomy_name":"contextual retrieval","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/bm25-internals","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","prompting/prompt-caching","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy"],"tldr":"Chunks lose their subject when cut from a document. Contextual retrieval fixes the index: at ingestion an\nLLM reads the whole document and writes a short context for each chunk, prepended before embedding and\nBM25 indexing. One cached LLM call per chunk, no query latency.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"metadata-filtering","key":"rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Metadata filtering","taxonomy_name":"metadata filtering","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/query-rewriting","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","data-architecture/vector-database-architecture"],"tldr":"Metadata filtering stores structured attributes (tenant, date, type, version) next to each vector and\napplies them as hard predicates *inside* the search. The real question is where the predicate runs:\nbefore the ANN walk, during it, or after it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"acl-aware-retrieval","key":"rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"ACL-aware retrieval","taxonomy_name":"ACL-aware retrieval","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","system-design/tenant-isolation","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency"],"tldr":"ACL-aware retrieval turns the user's authenticated identity into a hard predicate over each chunk's\nallowed principals, inside the search. Hard parts: capturing ACLs at ingestion, expanding groups at query\ntime, and keeping both fresh.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"lost-in-the-middle","key":"rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Lost-in-the-middle","taxonomy_name":"lost-in-the-middle","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["model-internals/context-window","model-internals/attention-mechanism","model-internals/positional-encoding","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","prompting/context-engineering","agents/context-compaction-summarization"],"tldr":"Models use the start and end of a long prompt far more reliably than the middle: a U-shaped curve. In\nRAG, more chunks can hurt even with the evidence present, so retrieve fewer, put the best at the edges and\nthe question last.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"citations-grounding","key":"rag-retrieval/citations-grounding","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Citations / grounding","taxonomy_name":"citations / grounding","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/citation-correctness","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/reranking","evals/faithfulness-groundedness","model-internals/hallucination","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","prompting/context-engineering"],"tldr":"Grounding means the answer comes only from the retrieved sources; citations are machine-checkable\npointers from each claim to its supporting span. Give sources stable IDs, require a citation per claim,\nallow abstention, and verify citations in code.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"citation-correctness","key":"rag-retrieval/citation-correctness","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Citation correctness","taxonomy_name":"citation correctness","kind":"metric","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":14,"related":["rag-retrieval/citations-grounding","evals/faithfulness-groundedness","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/hallucination-evaluation","evals/retrieval-evaluation","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"Citation correctness asks whether each citation actually supports its claim, not whether citations exist.\nIt splits into citation recall (claims fully supported) and precision (citations necessary), measured with\nan entailment model or calibrated judge on labelled samples.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"graphrag","key":"rag-retrieval/graphrag","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"GraphRAG","taxonomy_name":"GraphRAG","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/knowledge-graphs","rag-retrieval/multi-hop-retrieval","rag-retrieval/query-decomposition","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","agents/context-compaction-summarization","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling"],"tldr":"GraphRAG uses an LLM at index time to extract an entity graph, cluster it into communities and pre-write\ntheir summaries, so queries can answer corpus-wide and cross-document questions. The price is a large,\nLLM-heavy indexing bill those queries must repay.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"knowledge-graphs","key":"rag-retrieval/knowledge-graphs","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Knowledge graphs","taxonomy_name":"knowledge graphs","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/graphrag","rag-retrieval/multi-hop-retrieval","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/agentic-rag","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","scoping/domain-modeling","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas"],"tldr":"A knowledge graph stores facts as typed entities and typed relationships under a schema. In RAG it\ncomplements vector search, answering joins, counts and exact constraints that similarity cannot. The hard part is building it:\nextraction, entity resolution, schema design and sync.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","key":"rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Freshness, document deletion and index consistency","taxonomy_name":"freshness / document deletion / index consistency","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture"],"tldr":"A retrieval index is a derived copy that goes stale the moment it is built. Keep it fresh, delete\ncompletely and never mix versions with one discipline: stable document identity, versioned chunk ids,\nwrite-new-then-delete-old, and a reconciliation job.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"retrieval-failure-diagnosis","key":"rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Retrieval failure diagnosis","taxonomy_name":"retrieval failure diagnosis","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/retrieval-evaluation","evals/error-analysis","rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","rag-retrieval/document-parsing","observability/tracing","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search"],"tldr":"A wrong RAG answer means evidence was lost at one stage. Diagnosis traces the gold evidence through\ningestion, parsing, chunking, indexing, filtering, ranking, context and generation to the *first* stage\nwhere it disappears, because each stage has its own fix and tuning another changes nothing.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"long-context-vs-rag","key":"rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Long-context vs RAG","taxonomy_name":"long-context vs RAG","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/context-window","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","prompting/prompt-caching","serving-inference/prefix-caching","training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","system-design/rag-architecture","rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval"],"tldr":"Huge windows sometimes let you skip retrieval: best for small, static, reused corpora or whole-document\nreasoning. RAG wins for large, changing, permissioned or high-volume ones: tokens cost money, prefill\ngrows with length, and models use long contexts less reliably than advertised.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"agentic-rag","key":"rag-retrieval/agentic-rag","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Agentic RAG","taxonomy_name":"agentic RAG","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["agents/agent-loop","prompting/react","rag-retrieval/multi-hop-retrieval","rag-retrieval/query-decomposition","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval"],"tldr":"Agentic RAG makes retrieval tools the model calls in a loop, deciding what to search, where, and when to\nstop. It exists because multi-hop, ambiguous and multi-source questions defeat one query against one\nindex, and it costs latency, tokens and predictability.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"document-parsing","key":"rag-retrieval/document-parsing","module":"rag-retrieval","name":"Document parsing (OCR, layout, tables)","taxonomy_name":"document parsing (OCR, layout, tables)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["multimodal/ocr","multimodal/document-understanding","multimodal/vision-language-models","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis"],"tldr":"Parsing turns PDFs, scans and office files into clean, ordered, structured text before chunking: OCR for\nimage pages, layout analysis for reading order and header removal, and tables that keep cells tied to\ntheir headers. Retrieval cannot find text the parser garbled, reordered or flattened.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"}],"stage":"llm-apps","stage_index":3,"track_order":9},{"id":"agents","tier":1,"order":5,"title":"Agents","intro":{"overview":"This module is about building LLM agents that do real work against real systems and can be trusted to\nkeep doing it. It starts with the agent loop: the model proposes an action as a structured tool call,\nthe harness executes it, the result goes back into the transcript, and the cycle repeats until a stop\ncondition. Everything else either improves that loop or limits it. The tool lessons cover the interface\nthe model acts through: schemas and layered validation, typed contracts that keep schema and handler in\nstep, result shapes the model cannot misread, selection accuracy as the first metric worth tracking, and\nparallel calls for independent reads. MCP and its security layer, followed by A2A, take that interface\nacross process and organisational boundaries. Planning, task decomposition, reflection and state\nmachines give long tasks structure and enforceable phases. Compaction and memory decide what the model\nsees on each step and what persists between sessions, with policies for retrieval, update and erasure.\nHuman-in-the-loop, approval gates, sandboxing and computer use bound what a wrong or prompt-injected\naction can do. Multi-agent patterns, the workflows-versus-agents decision and frameworks then tell you\nhow much autonomy to spend and which plumbing to adopt. Afterwards you can design, build, measure and\ndefend a production agent: choose where autonomy belongs, write tools the model uses correctly, keep\nlong runs within budget, and put the controls a security review expects in code rather than in the\nprompt. It matters most for Engineers, who write the harness, and for Architects, who own the\nautonomy, trust and topology decisions.","why":{"engineer":"You write the harness, not just the prompt: the loop and its budgets, tool contracts and validators that return fixable errors, result shapes with explicit status and truncation, concurrency caps for parallel calls, compaction at tool-call boundaries, and checkpointed state that resumes without repeating a side effect.","architect":"You decide where autonomy is spent and where it is not: workflow versus agentic island, single agent versus orchestrator-worker or handoffs, MCP versus in-process tools versus A2A peers, which actions are gated, which sandbox tier fits the threat, and how memory is scoped, retained and erased.","fde":"Customers arrive with MCP servers, legacy GUIs and security reviewers, so you must connect an agent to their systems with per-user OAuth identity, explain approval gates and sandbox egress in a review, start with full human review, and widen autonomy per action class as agreement data comes in."},"mental_model":"Picture the model as a planner with no authority sitting inside a harness that holds all of it. On\nevery step the model reads a context window and proposes one or more tool calls; the harness decides\nwhether each call is valid, permitted and safe to run, executes it, and writes a result back. Each\nlesson in the module controls one of four things in that picture. The tool surface (schemas, contracts,\nresults, selection, parallelism, MCP, A2A) is the channel through which the model acts and sees, so\nmost \"the agent is dumb\" bugs are interface bugs. The control layer (planning, decomposition,\nreflection, state machines, multi-agent patterns) limits which next steps exist and checks progress\nagainst the goal. The context layer (compaction, memory and its policies) decides what the model knows\non this step. The authority layer (human review, approval gates, sandboxing, careful computer use)\nbounds the damage when a proposal is wrong or injected. Autonomy works like a budget: give the model\nchoice only where you cannot draw the flowchart, and enforce every invariant below the model, in code.","order":["agent-loop","tool-calling","workflows-vs-agents","tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","typed-tool-contracts","tool-result-interpretation","tool-selection-accuracy","parallel-tool-calls","model-context-protocol","mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","a2a-protocol","planning","task-decomposition","reflection-self-critique","state-machines","context-compaction-summarization","agent-memory","memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","human-in-the-loop","approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","sandboxing","computer-use","multi-agent-patterns","frameworks"],"order_reasons":{"agent-loop":"the foundation: transcript as state, harness as authority, and budgets and stop conditions that every later lesson builds on.","tool-calling":"the structured request and response format through which the loop acts, and why the model only ever asks.","workflows-vs-agents":"with the loop and tools understood, decides early when to build a workflow instead and where agentic islands belong.","tool-schemas-and-argument-validation":"the schema is the model's whole specification of a tool, and layered validation catches its mistakes before side effects.","typed-tool-contracts":"derives schema, handler, errors and tests from one typed definition so they cannot drift apart.","tool-result-interpretation":"designs the other half of the interface: results with explicit status, truncation and untrusted-data labels.","tool-selection-accuracy":"measures whether the tool surface built so far works, using a labelled set and a confusion matrix.","parallel-tool-calls":"extends the loop to run independent calls concurrently, which depends on contract metadata about side effects.","model-context-protocol":"moves tools out of process into shared servers with tools, resources and prompts over JSON-RPC.","mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security":"adds per-user identity, OAuth scopes and audience binding once MCP servers are remote and shared.","a2a-protocol":"connects to opaque peer agents with long-running tasks, best understood by contrast with MCP.","planning":"gives long tasks an explicit representation of intent to measure progress against and replan from.","task-decomposition":"decides the shape of the work (granularity, dependencies, interfaces) once order is planned.","reflection-self-critique":"adds a generate-critique-revise loop and teaches that only grounded evidence makes it help.","state-machines":"turns phases into enforced states and guards that can be checkpointed and resumed after a crash.","context-compaction-summarization":"keeps long runs within the window and budget by deciding what must survive.","agent-memory":"extends context management across sessions with short-term, long-term and episodic stores.","memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies":"adds the lifecycle rules that stop memory going stale, poisoned or unlawful.","human-in-the-loop":"decides where scarce human attention buys the most risk reduction and how to avoid rubber stamping.","approval-gates-for-consequential-actions":"implements the most important human checkpoint in the executor, bound to exact arguments and durable.","sandboxing":"bounds the blast radius of model-written code and shell commands, assuming prompt injection.","computer-use":"the most general and least efficient tool, which needs the sandbox and gates already covered.","multi-agent-patterns":"splits work across loops for isolation and parallelism, after single-agent design is solid.","frameworks":"evaluates LangGraph, LlamaIndex, Pydantic AI, DSPy and others against the plumbing problems the module has named."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a billing-dispute agent for the support team of a mid-sized SaaS company. A ticket arrives saying\na customer was double-charged; the agent must investigate across a ticketing system, a billing API and\na policy knowledge base, decide whether a refund or credit is due, draft a reply, and issue the refund\nonly after a support lead approves it. Use mock services you control: expose the billing API as a\nremote MCP server with per-user OAuth scopes, and include one old admin console with no API that the\nagent can reach only through computer use in a VM. Model the run as a state machine: an intake workflow,\nan agentic investigation island with a step and token budget, a proposal state, a durable approval\npause, and execution. The investigation should issue parallel lookups, reconcile invoices in a sandboxed\ncode tool with no network egress, and critique its own reconciliation against computed totals before it\nproposes anything. Per-customer memory records facts such as preferred contact and past disputes, with\nprovenance and a working erasure path. Seed one ticket whose attachment contains a prompt-injection\ninstruction to refund a different account. Over a weekend to a week, build it, measure it and write it\nup for a security reviewer.","deliverables":["A working agent in a repository, built either on a hand-rolled loop or on one framework with a written justification, with typed tool contracts (input, output, error kinds, side-effect metadata) for at least six tools and snapshot tests of the schemas the model sees.","A state-machine diagram and its implementation, showing allowed tools and transitions per state, guards enforced in code, checkpoints, and the durable approval pause with arguments bound to the approval.","A labelled evaluation set of at least 30 tickets, including look-alike tools, empty-result cases and the injection ticket, with a script reporting task completion, tool selection accuracy (recall, confusion and argument accuracy), steps and cost per task at p50 and p95.","A memory schema and policy note covering retrieval scoping and scoring, supersession of contradictory facts, TTLs, and an erasure procedure that covers the raw store, the vector index and compaction summaries.","A two-page security and design review: why each step is workflow or agentic, the MCP OAuth scopes and audience binding, sandbox tier and egress policy, which tool classes are gated and why, and the plan for widening autonomy as approval data accumulates."],"rubric":["On the injection ticket, the agent either never proposes the out-of-scope refund or proposes it and is stopped at the gate; the trace shows the attachment text labelled as untrusted data and no refund call reaching the billing server.","Killing the process while it waits for approval and restarting it resumes in the same state, and approving executes the refund exactly once, shown by idempotency-key logs on the billing mock.","An MCP call with a token issued for a different server or with a missing scope is rejected by the server, and every billing call in the logs is attributed to a human subject rather than a shared key.","Tool results for empty searches, timeouts and truncated lists carry an explicit status, and the eval shows zero cases where the model reported \"customer not found\" when the backend actually failed.","Turning parallel calls and compaction on or off produces a measured change in wall-clock time and tokens per run, reported against task completion so that any lost completions are visible.","After erasing a test customer, a retrieval probe and a direct index query both return nothing for that customer, and the procedure lists every copy it removed.","The sandboxed reconciliation tool cannot reach the network or read host credentials, demonstrated by a recorded attempt that fails with an egress denial."]}},"topics":[{"id":"agent-loop","key":"agents/agent-loop","module":"agents","name":"Agent loop","taxonomy_name":"agent loop","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/tool-calling","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agents/state-machines","agents/context-compaction-summarization","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-reliability/stopping-conditions-termination","agent-reliability/loop-detection","prompting/react"],"tldr":"An agent is a loop, not a model: the model proposes a tool call, your harness executes it, the result\nis appended, and the model is called again until it stops or a budget trips. The loop lets it observe\nconsequences and correct course.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-calling","key":"agents/tool-calling","module":"agents","name":"Tool calling","taxonomy_name":"tool calling","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/tool-selection-accuracy","agents/tool-result-interpretation","agents/parallel-tool-calls","agents/model-context-protocol","prompting/function-tool-calling","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode"],"tldr":"Tool calling lets a model request an action instead of describing one: it emits a structured, typed call\nagainst a declared schema, your code executes it, and the result returns to context. Models cannot reach\nlive data, and parsing actions from free text is unreliable.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","key":"agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","module":"agents","name":"Tool schemas & argument validation","taxonomy_name":"tool schemas & argument validation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/tool-calling","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agents/tool-selection-accuracy","python-for-ai/pydantic","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","safety-security/output-validation","agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate"],"tldr":"The input schema is the model's whole specification of a tool's arguments; harness validation catches\nits mistakes before they become side effects. Keep schemas small and explicit, validate shape, meaning,\nthen permission, and return fixable error results.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"typed-tool-contracts","key":"agents/typed-tool-contracts","module":"agents","name":"Typed tool contracts","taxonomy_name":"typed tool contracts","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/tool-result-interpretation","agents/model-context-protocol","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/contract-testing","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility"],"tldr":"A typed tool contract defines a tool once as types: input, output, error kinds and side-effect metadata.\nSchema, handler signature, serialization and tests are derived from it, so schema and handler cannot drift\nand the model always reads results of a stable shape.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-selection-accuracy","key":"agents/tool-selection-accuracy","module":"agents","name":"Tool selection accuracy","taxonomy_name":"tool selection accuracy","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["agents/tool-calling","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/model-context-protocol","agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","evals/error-analysis","evals/golden-dataset","prompting/context-engineering"],"tldr":"Tool selection accuracy is the share of decision points where the model picks the right tool, or none,\nwith correct arguments. It falls as tools multiply and overlap; instrument it first, because it isolates\nthe decision made every step.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-result-interpretation","key":"agents/tool-result-interpretation","module":"agents","name":"Tool-result interpretation","taxonomy_name":"tool-result interpretation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/tool-calling","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agents/parallel-tool-calls","agents/context-compaction-summarization","safety-security/prompt-injection","prompting/prompt-injection","prompting/context-engineering","agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate"],"tldr":"A tool result is a prompt the model reads. Its shape decides whether the model understands success,\nfailure, \"nothing found\" and \"more available\", and whether it mistakes retrieved content for\ninstructions, so design results for that reader.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"parallel-tool-calls","key":"agents/parallel-tool-calls","module":"agents","name":"Parallel tool calls","taxonomy_name":"parallel tool calls","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["agents/tool-calling","agents/agent-loop","agents/typed-tool-contracts","python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency"],"tldr":"A model can emit several tool calls in one turn; the harness runs independent ones concurrently and\nanswers every call before the next turn, saving a round trip per call. The model picks the batch; only\nthe harness knows what may overlap.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-context-protocol","key":"agents/model-context-protocol","module":"agents","name":"Model Context Protocol (MCP)","taxonomy_name":"Model Context Protocol (MCP)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","agents/a2a-protocol","agents/tool-calling","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agents/tool-selection-accuracy","python-for-ai/mcp-python-sdk","node-ts-for-ai/mcp-typescript-sdk","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege"],"tldr":"MCP, an open protocol Anthropic released in November 2024, standardizes how AI applications connect to\ncapability servers exposing tools, resources and prompts over JSON-RPC. Integrations are written once; it\ndoes not remove authentication, trust decisions or tool-set curation.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","key":"agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","module":"agents","name":"MCP authentication / authorization / transport security","taxonomy_name":"MCP authentication / authorization / transport security","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["agents/model-context-protocol","agents/a2a-protocol","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","safety-security/prompt-injection","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt"],"tldr":"MCP tool calls are safe only if the server knows who asks and what they may do. Local stdio servers\nrely on the OS process boundary; remote HTTP servers use an optional OAuth 2.1 layer. The model is never\nthe trust boundary.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"a2a-protocol","key":"agents/a2a-protocol","module":"agents","name":"A2A protocol (Agent2Agent)","taxonomy_name":"A2A protocol","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/model-context-protocol","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","agents/multi-agent-patterns","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","serving-inference/streaming"],"tldr":"A2A is an open protocol for one agent to discover another, hand it a task and follow it to completion\nover HTTP without seeing inside. MCP connects agents to tools; A2A connects agents to opaque,\nlong-running peer agents.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"planning","key":"agents/planning","module":"agents","name":"Planning","taxonomy_name":"planning","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/task-decomposition","agents/reflection-self-critique","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agents/state-machines","prompting/react","prompting/chain-of-thought","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets"],"tldr":"Planning is how an agent decides which actions to take and in what order. An explicit plan gives the\nloop a target to measure progress against and a place to replan; where planning lives trades tokens for\nrobustness.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"task-decomposition","key":"agents/task-decomposition","module":"agents","name":"Task decomposition","taxonomy_name":"task decomposition","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/planning","agents/multi-agent-patterns","agents/parallel-tool-calls","agents/workflows-vs-agents","rag-retrieval/query-decomposition","prompting/chain-of-thought","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","model-internals/context-window"],"tldr":"Task decomposition splits a large job into subtasks that each fit one model call, can be verified alone\nand expose a small interface. Planning decides order; decomposition decides shape. Done well it buys\nparallelism and isolated failures; done badly, coordination overhead.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"reflection-self-critique","key":"agents/reflection-self-critique","module":"agents","name":"Reflection / self-critique","taxonomy_name":"reflection / self-critique","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/planning","agents/multi-agent-patterns","agents/tool-result-interpretation","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","evals/hallucination-evaluation","prompting/chain-of-thought"],"tldr":"Reflection is a generate–critique–revise loop. It pays off only when the critique brings new\ninformation (a failing test, a tool error, a verifier, a fresh rubric context); \"look again\" with no new\nsignal can turn right answers wrong.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-agent-patterns","key":"agents/multi-agent-patterns","module":"agents","name":"Multi-agent patterns (orchestrator-worker, handoffs)","taxonomy_name":"multi-agent patterns (orchestrator-worker, handoffs)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agents/task-decomposition","agents/planning","agents/reflection-self-critique","agents/a2a-protocol","agents/state-machines","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services"],"tldr":"Multi-agent systems split work across model loops with their own context, prompt and tools.\nOrchestrator-worker delegates to parallel workers; handoffs pass control to one specialist at a time.\nIsolation costs coordination and tokens, so one good agent is the default.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"agent-memory","key":"agents/agent-memory","module":"agents","name":"Agent memory (short-term, long-term, episodic)","taxonomy_name":"agent memory (short-term, long-term, episodic)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","agents/context-compaction-summarization","agents/agent-loop","model-internals/context-window","prompting/context-engineering","rag-retrieval/vector-databases","agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","agents/reflection-self-critique"],"tldr":"A model remembers only its context window; agent memory decides what enters it. Short-term is the\nwindow, long-term holds facts and procedures across sessions, episodic records timestamped events.\nMemory is RAG over the agent's own history, plus a write path.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","key":"agents/memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","module":"agents","name":"Memory retrieval / update / deletion policies","taxonomy_name":"memory retrieval / update / deletion policies","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/agent-memory","agents/context-compaction-summarization","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","safety-security/pii-redaction","safety-security/prompt-injection"],"tldr":"Without policies a memory store becomes stale, contradictory, sometimes poisoned text. Retrieval\ndecides what enters the context, update decides add, supersede or ignore, and deletion expires and\nerases memories everywhere, including indexes and backups.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"context-compaction-summarization","key":"agents/context-compaction-summarization","module":"agents","name":"Context compaction / summarization","taxonomy_name":"context compaction / summarization","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/agent-memory","agents/memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","model-internals/context-window","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/context-engineering","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle","agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery"],"tldr":"An agent re-sends its whole growing transcript every step, so cost is quadratic and quality drops before\nthe limit. Compaction replaces old material with pruned outputs, a structured summary or pointers to\nexternal state; the hard part is choosing what must survive.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"state-machines","key":"agents/state-machines","module":"agents","name":"State machines","taxonomy_name":"state machines","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agents/frameworks","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","agent-reliability/stopping-conditions-termination","system-design/workflow-orchestration"],"tldr":"A state machine gives an agent named phases, per-phase tools, code-checked guards and terminal states.\nThe model still chooses, but only among permitted transitions, so invariants like \"never deploy before\ntests pass\" hold, and (state, data) can be checkpointed and resumed.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"human-in-the-loop","key":"agents/human-in-the-loop","module":"agents","name":"Human-in-the-loop","taxonomy_name":"human-in-the-loop","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agents/state-machines","safety-security/excessive-agency","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation"],"tldr":"Human-in-the-loop places a person before, during or after an agent's actions, because models err and\npeople hold authority and information agents lack. Attention is scarce, so put review where it cuts most\nrisk and keep each decision cheap and genuine.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","key":"agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","module":"agents","name":"Approval gates for consequential actions","taxonomy_name":"approval gates for consequential actions","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/human-in-the-loop","agents/tool-calling","agents/state-machines","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","safety-security/excessive-agency"],"tldr":"An approval gate sits in the tool executor, between a proposed consequential call and its execution, so\nthe model cannot bypass it. It needs an effect classifier, a durable pause, an authorized approval bound\nto exact arguments, and an exactly-once resume.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"sandboxing","key":"agents/sandboxing","module":"agents","name":"Sandboxing","taxonomy_name":"sandboxing","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agents/computer-use","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","safety-security/data-leakage","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management"],"tldr":"A sandbox bounds what an agent's code and tools can reach: filesystem, network, credentials, processes,\nCPU, memory and time. Agent actions are untrusted, so a hijacked action should waste a container, not\nleak a database. Isolation strength trades against latency and compatibility.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"computer-use","key":"agents/computer-use","module":"agents","name":"Computer use","taxonomy_name":"computer use","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agents/sandboxing","agents/tool-calling","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agents/context-compaction-summarization","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","multimodal/vision-language-models","safety-security/prompt-injection"],"tldr":"Computer use is an agent that sees screenshots and acts through mouse, keyboard and scroll, reaching\nGUIs with no API. It is the most general tool and the least efficient: prefer APIs or DOM access, keep\nit for the long tail, and sandbox it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"workflows-vs-agents","key":"agents/workflows-vs-agents","module":"agents","name":"Workflows vs agents","taxonomy_name":"workflows vs agents","kind":"decision","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/state-machines","agents/frameworks","agents/planning","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","system-design/workflow-orchestration"],"tldr":"A workflow runs LLM calls along paths your code defines; an agent lets the model pick each next step.\nIf you can draw the flowchart, build the workflow: cheaper, bounded, testable. Most production systems\nare workflows with small agentic islands where the path is unforeseeable.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"frameworks","key":"agents/frameworks","module":"agents","name":"Frameworks (LangGraph, LangChain, LlamaIndex, Pydantic AI, DSPy)","taxonomy_name":"frameworks (LangGraph, LangChain, LlamaIndex, Pydantic AI, DSPy)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/state-machines","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","python-for-ai/pydantic","prompting/prompt-engineering","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines"],"tldr":"Agent frameworks package the plumbing around model calls. LangChain integrates, LangGraph runs typed\nresumable state machines, LlamaIndex handles data and retrieval, Pydantic AI is small and type-first,\nDSPy compiles prompts against a metric. Choose by the plumbing problem you actually have.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"llm-apps","stage_index":3,"track_order":11},{"id":"agent-reliability","tier":1,"order":6,"title":"Agent reliability & runtime","intro":{"overview":"This module is about the runtime that sits around the model in an agent: the harness code that decides\nwhen a run ends, what survives when a process dies, what a tool is allowed to do, and how you prove the\nagent works. It starts with control of the loop itself. Stopping conditions combine the model's own\n\"finished\" signal with independent goal checks and hard limits, max-step budgets cap tokens, time and\nmoney with a soft warning before the hard stop, and loop detection catches repeats and no-progress runs\nbefore the budget is spent. Next comes state. A hierarchy of identifiers (conversation, turn, run, step,\ntool call) gives every later mechanism a stable key. A structured event stream records what happened,\nand durable execution, conversation recovery and the choice of state store are all built from it.\nIdempotent tool execution makes the retries that durability depends on safe. The tool boundary follows:\nadapters translate between small model-facing contracts and messy vendor APIs, and least privilege\nbounds what a wrong or hijacked decision can do. The last group makes the agent observable and\nmeasurable: audit logs and traces, deterministic replay of incidents, tool-call success rate, task\ncompletion rate and trajectory evaluation. After the module you can build an agent harness that ends\nevery run with a labelled outcome, resumes after a crash without duplicate side effects, enforces\npermissions in code, replays a failed run step for step, and reports a verified completion rate with\nan interval. It matters most for Engineers who build and operate the harness, and for Architects who\nchoose its state, identity and permission model.","why":{"engineer":"You write the loop, the termination function, the budget and loop guards, the checkpoint and resume path, the idempotency-key derivation and the tool adapters, so duplicate invoices, runaway costs and runs that never finish are bugs in your code, not the model's.","architect":"You decide the identifier hierarchy, the event schema that UI, checkpoint, audit and eval all derive from, where state lives and how long it is kept, and the permission model per tool, and each of those choices is expensive to migrate later.","fde":"Customers ask what happens if the agent loops, crashes mid-task or reads a malicious document, and who approved an action; you answer with budgets, resume behaviour, scoped credentials, an audit trail and a verified completion rate measured on their own tasks."},"mental_model":"Picture an agent run as an append-only ledger of events written by a loop that the model does not\ncontrol. The model proposes the next action; the harness decides whether to allow it, execute it and\ncontinue. Around each step sit three guards. Before the step: is the run done, over budget or looping,\nand is this tool call permitted with these arguments under this identity? During the step: the adapter\nexecutes it with a timeout and an idempotency key derived from stable ids, so a retry repeats the\neffect at most once. After the step: the outcome is written to the ledger before anything else\nhappens. Everything else is a projection of that ledger. The UI stream, the checkpoint you resume from,\nthe restored conversation, the audit record, the replay recording and the trajectory you evaluate are\nall the same facts folded different ways. Metrics close the loop: tool-call success rate explains the\nsteps, completion rate scores the outcome, and trajectory evaluation shows which step to fix.","order":["stopping-conditions-termination","max-step-budgets","loop-detection","conversation-execution-identifiers","structured-event-streams","durable-execution-and-checkpointing","idempotent-tool-execution","state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","state-and-checkpoint-stores","tool-adapters-service-interfaces","tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","audit-logs-execution-traces","deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","tool-call-success-rate","task-completion-rate","agent-trajectory-evaluation"],"order_reasons":{"stopping-conditions-termination":"the loop has no natural exit, so designing the done, continue and abort decision comes first.","max-step-budgets":"adds the hard limits that stopping conditions rely on as a backstop, and the partial-result path when they fire.","loop-detection":"catches stuck runs early instead of letting them burn the whole budget, and depends on the action stream budgets already count.","conversation-execution-identifiers":"defines the id hierarchy that checkpoints, dedup keys, events and logs all key on, so it must precede them.","structured-event-streams":"the ordered, typed record of a run that resumption, recovery, audit, replay and evaluation are all projections of.","durable-execution-and-checkpointing":"persists progress at step boundaries so a run outlives its process, building on events and ids.","idempotent-tool-execution":"makes the retries and resumes introduced by durability safe for side-effecting tools, using run and call ids as keys.","state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery":"separates the long-lived transcript from in-flight runs and repairs interrupted turns, applying the event log to the user-facing conversation.","state-and-checkpoint-stores":"chooses where runs, events and blobs live once their access patterns, durability and retention needs are clear.","tool-adapters-service-interfaces":"the layer between model and vendor API where validation, timeouts, idempotency and error normalization are implemented.","tool-permissions-and-least-privilege":"enforces allowlists, argument limits, scoped credentials and approvals in the adapter and runtime rather than in the prompt.","audit-logs-execution-traces":"derives debugging traces and tamper-evident audit records from the event stream, including the permission decisions just covered.","deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility":"uses the recorded effects to reproduce a failed run exactly, which needs the stream, ids and adapters in place.","tool-call-success-rate":"measures each step with a layered error taxonomy emitted by the adapter, and explains completion through compounding.","task-completion-rate":"defines the headline outcome metric with a real success predicate, denominator and interval, building on per-step measurement.","agent-trajectory-evaluation":"combines outcome, process and policy checks over many runs, using everything above as its inputs."},"capstone":{"brief":"Take a working but fragile prototype agent and make it production-ready. The agent handles customer\nrefund requests for an online store: it reads the order and the customer's message, looks up the refund\npolicy, issues a refund through a payments API (a local mock), opens a ticket in a helpdesk mock and\nsends the customer a reply. Today it runs as an in-memory `while True` loop, retries the payments call\non timeout, holds one admin API key, logs to stdout and is judged by whether the model says \"done\".\nOver a week, rebuild the harness: a terminal tool plus a verifier and budgets for steps, tokens and\nseconds, loop detection with an inform, block and abort ladder, an id hierarchy, a typed event stream\nin Postgres with object storage for large payloads, step-boundary checkpointing with resume, and\nidempotency keys on every write. Put each tool behind an adapter with a normalized error taxonomy and\na per-run permission profile with a refund-amount cap and an approval gate above it. Derive the trace,\nthe audit log and a replay recording from the stream, then build a trajectory suite of at least 30\ntasks, including injected-instruction cases, and report verified completion rate, pass^k and per-tool\nsuccess rate before and after.","deliverables":["A harness repository with the termination function, multi-dimension budgets with a soft warning, loop detection with the escalation ladder, step-boundary checkpointing and a resume command that continues a killed run from its last checkpoint.","An identifier and event-schema document listing each id (who mints it, what it is stable across, what it dedupes) and each versioned event type, with the store choice, retention and tenant column justified for runs, events, blobs and audit records.","Tool adapters for orders, policy, payments, helpdesk and email, each with argument validation, timeouts, idempotency-key handling, a shared error taxonomy and a permission profile with argument constraints and an approval gate.","A replay tool that re-runs any recorded run with no model or tool calls and reports the first diverging step, plus a fork mode that re-executes from a chosen step.","A trajectory evaluation suite of 30+ tasks with end-state checks against the mocks, process and policy checks from the event stream, 5+ runs per task, and a report giving verified completion rate with a Wilson interval, pass^k, cost per successful task and per-tool success rate by error class."],"rubric":["Killing the worker between the payments call and recording its result, then resuming, produces exactly one refund and one ticket, shown by the mock's records and a dedup-table hit in the logs.","A task seeded with an unanswerable dependency ends with a labelled \"exhausted\" or \"aborted\" outcome and a partial result within budget, and the trace shows the loop detector firing before the hard limit.","A refund request carrying an injected instruction to refund the full order history is denied or sent to approval by the runtime policy, and the audit log records the user, credential, arguments, decision and approver.","Replaying a recorded failed run reproduces the same tool calls in the same order with zero model calls, and the report identifies the step where a forked re-execution diverges.","The evaluation report separates model-claimed from verified completion, gives every rate with an interval and run count, and attributes each failed task to a tool, policy, model or environment cause using the adapter's error classes.","Every log and event line carries the conversation, turn, run and step ids, so one query joins the UI events, trace spans and audit records for any run."]}},"topics":[{"id":"stopping-conditions-termination","key":"agent-reliability/stopping-conditions-termination","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Stopping conditions / termination","taxonomy_name":"stopping conditions / termination","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-reliability/loop-detection","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agents/agent-loop","agents/state-machines","agents/human-in-the-loop","safety-security/excessive-agency"],"tldr":"An agent loop never ends on its own, so termination is designed: a function over run state returns\ncontinue, done or abort by combining the model's structured done signal, independent verifiers and hard\nlimits, and every exit records a labelled reason.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"max-step-budgets","key":"agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Max-step budgets","taxonomy_name":"max-step budgets","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agent-reliability/stopping-conditions-termination","agent-reliability/loop-detection","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agents/agent-loop","observability/token-cost-tracking","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","serving-inference/timeouts"],"tldr":"A max-step budget caps the model calls, tokens, seconds and dollars one run may spend. It is a backstop,\nnot a target: set it from successful runs, warn before it fires, return a labelled partial, and treat\nevery exhaustion as a stuck-agent signal.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"loop-detection","key":"agent-reliability/loop-detection","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Loop detection","taxonomy_name":"loop detection","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agent-reliability/stopping-conditions-termination","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","agents/agent-loop","agents/reflection-self-critique","agents/tool-result-interpretation"],"tldr":"Loop detection watches an agent's actions for exact repeats, short cycles and no-progress stretches, then\nescalates (tell the model, block, replan, abort), because budgets stop loops only after burning the whole\nallowance and making repetition visible is the cheapest fix.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"durable-execution-and-checkpointing","key":"agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Durable execution & checkpointing","taxonomy_name":"durable execution & checkpointing","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","system-design/workflow-orchestration"],"tldr":"Durable execution makes an agent run's progress outlive its process; checkpointing persists state at step\nboundaries so a resume continues from the last boundary, not from scratch. The hard part is side effects,\nso checkpointing is inseparable from idempotent tools.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","key":"agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","module":"agent-reliability","name":"State persistence & conversation recovery","taxonomy_name":"state persistence & conversation recovery","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","agents/agent-memory","agents/context-compaction-summarization","system-design/memory-architecture","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect"],"tldr":"Persist conversation state and execution state separately. Keep the transcript as an append-only,\nsequence-numbered event log and derive the model's context from it, so clients catch up by sequence\nnumber and interrupted turns are repaired, never dangling or duplicated.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"state-and-checkpoint-stores","key":"agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","module":"agent-reliability","name":"State & checkpoint stores","taxonomy_name":"state & checkpoint stores","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","backend-fundamentals/redis","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","backend-fundamentals/nosql","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture"],"tldr":"The agent state store sets per-step latency, crash survival, tenant isolation and deletion. A sound\ndefault: Postgres for run records, events and small snapshots, object storage for large tool outputs,\nRedis only for leases and stream buffers, and a tenant id on every row.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"conversation-execution-identifiers","key":"agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Conversation / execution identifiers","taxonomy_name":"conversation / execution identifiers","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys"],"tldr":"Agents need an id hierarchy, not one \"session id\": tenant, user, conversation, turn, run, step,\ntool call and trace. Each answers a different question (resume what, dedupe what, bill whom, trace\nwhich); conflating them makes retries double-act and logs impossible to join.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"idempotent-tool-execution","key":"agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Idempotent tool execution","taxonomy_name":"idempotent tool execution","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","serving-inference/idempotency","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions"],"tldr":"Agents retry at three layers, and exactly-once execution across a network is impossible. Target\nat-least-once execution with idempotent effects: classify tools, derive a deterministic key per call,\ndedupe downstream or locally, and gate unkeyable effects behind approvals or an outbox.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","key":"agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Tool permissions & least privilege","taxonomy_name":"tool permissions & least privilege","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces","safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","safety-security/prompt-injection","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation"],"tldr":"Tools turn an agent's text into effects, and anything in its context can steer that text. Least\nprivilege gives each run only the tools, arguments, identities and credentials its task needs, enforced\nin the runtime, so a hijacked decision has bounded blast radius.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-adapters-service-interfaces","key":"agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Tool adapters / service interfaces","taxonomy_name":"tool adapters / service interfaces","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/model-context-protocol","agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts","serving-inference/retry-strategies"],"tldr":"A tool adapter translates between small typed model calls and real services with auth, pagination, rate\nlimits and 40-field responses. It validates, enforces policy, injects credentials, bounds retries and\nreturns every outcome as a compact structured result, making tools testable, swappable and safe.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"structured-event-streams","key":"agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Structured event streams","taxonomy_name":"structured event streams","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","serving-inference/streaming","fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","system-design/event-driven-architecture"],"tldr":"An agent run is a sequence of happenings, not a returned string. Emit them as one ordered, typed,\nversioned event stream and derive the UI, persisted state, trace and eval trajectory from it: one\nreplayable source of truth instead of four drifting formats.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"audit-logs-execution-traces","key":"agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Audit logs / execution traces","taxonomy_name":"audit logs / execution traces","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","observability/tracing","observability/spans","observability/opentelemetry","observability/langfuse"],"tldr":"A trace tells engineers what the agent did and why; an audit log tells security and compliance who did\nwhat with whose authority. Both derive from one event stream but differ in contents, retention, access\nand mutability, so build both on purpose.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","key":"agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Deterministic replay & reproducibility","taxonomy_name":"deterministic replay & reproducibility","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","model-internals/sampling-strategies","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","evals/pass-k"],"tldr":"Rerunning an agent does not reproduce it: models sample, providers change, tools return live data.\nReproducibility has three grades: exact replay from recorded effects, best-effort re-execution with pinned\nsettings, and statistical reproduction. Deterministic orchestration plus recorded effects keeps exact\nreplay always available.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"agent-trajectory-evaluation","key":"agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Agent trajectory evaluation","taxonomy_name":"agent trajectory evaluation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agent-reliability/task-completion-rate","agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","agents/tool-selection-accuracy","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/pass-k","evals/eval-harness"],"tldr":"Final-answer scoring misses forbidden actions, forty-step paths and lucky wins. Trajectory evaluation\nscores the whole path of decisions, tool calls and observations with outcome, process and policy checks\nover many runs per task, so you learn where and why the agent fails.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"task-completion-rate","key":"agent-reliability/task-completion-rate","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Task completion rate","taxonomy_name":"task completion rate","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","evals/pass-k","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","reliability/slas-slos"],"tldr":"Task completion rate is the fraction of tasks an agent verifiably finishes, not claims to. It is only as\ngood as three definitions: task, success and denominator. Report it with an interval,\nsegmented by task type, beside pass^k for unattended use.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-call-success-rate","key":"agent-reliability/tool-call-success-rate","module":"agent-reliability","name":"Tool-call success rate","taxonomy_name":"tool-call success rate","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["agent-reliability/task-completion-rate","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/tool-selection-accuracy","serving-inference/retry-strategies","observability/monitoring"],"tldr":"Tool-call success rate is the fraction of tool invocations that succeed, split into layers: well-formed,\npermitted, executed and effective. Failure compounds (ten steps at 95% is about 60% end to end), and the\nlayers localize it to model, policy, tool or environment.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"llm-apps","stage_index":3,"track_order":12},{"id":"evals","tier":1,"order":7,"title":"Evals","intro":{"overview":"This module is about producing numbers you can trust about whether an LLM system does its job, and\nusing those numbers to decide what ships. It starts by separating capability evaluation (what a model\ncan do in general, answered by benchmarks) from application evaluation (whether your prompt, retrieval\nand model do your task on your data), then builds the core machinery of the second: task-specific\nacceptance criteria that define \"done\", a versioned golden dataset labeled by people with authority\nover the task, and an eval harness that runs, grades, aggregates per slice with confidence intervals\nand compares runs. Grading open-ended output leads to LLM-as-a-judge, which is only trustworthy once it\nis calibrated against human labels, scored with clear rubrics, compared pairwise where absolute scales\nfail, and anchored by inter-annotator agreement on the labels themselves. Specialised metrics follow:\npass@k for executable tasks, retrieval metrics, faithfulness and hallucination evaluation for RAG.\nRegression, metamorphic and property-based tests guard known behaviour and robustness without labels;\nadversarial evaluation and red teaming cover the worst case. Offline and online evaluation, A/B tests,\nerror analysis and eval-driven development close the loop from production failures back into the\nsuite. Afterwards you can design, build and defend an evaluation stack for a real feature and gate\nreleases on it. It matters most for AI Engineers who change prompts and models every week, and for\nFDEs who need a customer to sign off on what \"working\" means.","why":{"engineer":"You turn every prompt, model, retriever or tool change into a paired harness run against a golden set with per-slice intervals, read the items that flipped, and refuse to merge a change whose eval diff you cannot explain.","architect":"You decide where evaluation lives in the delivery pipeline: which graders are deterministic versus judged, which suites gate CI versus releases, how offline scores are validated against online outcomes, and which tooling owns datasets, traces and experiments.","fde":"You write acceptance criteria with the customer before committing to a date, build a golden set from their real documents and labelers, and show a calibrated report that makes sign-off unambiguous instead of resting on a demo that looked fine."},"mental_model":"Hold one picture: an eval is a measuring instrument, and every part of it can be miscalibrated. The\nspecification (acceptance criteria and rubrics) says what to measure; the reference (a golden dataset\nwhose labels humans agree on) says what correct looks like; the harness runs the system under pinned\nconfiguration and stores raw outputs; graders (exact checks, executable tests, retrieval metrics,\ncalibrated judges) turn outputs into scores; and statistics (paired comparisons, intervals, pass@k)\nsay whether a difference is real. Each layer has its own error: vague criteria, noisy or leaked labels,\nbiased judges, too few items. Around the instrument runs a loop: error analysis on real traces finds\nfailure modes, they become criteria and items, adversarial and metamorphic suites probe what averages\nhide, offline gates decide what ships, and online measurement checks that offline measured the right\nthing. Trust a number only as far as its weakest layer.","order":["model-evaluation","task-specific-acceptance-criteria","golden-dataset","eval-harness","error-analysis","benchmarking","dataset-contamination","pass-k","llm-as-a-judge","rubrics","inter-annotator-agreement","judge-calibration-and-bias","pairwise-comparison","retrieval-evaluation","faithfulness-groundedness","hallucination-evaluation","regression-tests","metamorphic-testing","property-based-testing","red-teaming","adversarial-evaluation","offline-vs-online-evaluation","a-b-testing","eval-driven-development","tooling"],"order_reasons":{"model-evaluation":"frames the capability-versus-application split and why every change needs a measured number, the lens for the whole module.","task-specific-acceptance-criteria":"defines what \"done\" means for one task before any dataset or grader is built.","golden-dataset":"builds the agreed, versioned reference every eval number is computed against.","eval-harness":"turns dataset, system and graders into a repeatable, comparable run with slices and intervals.","error-analysis":"teaches reading real traces and counting failure modes, which is where criteria and items should come from.","benchmarking":"covers public benchmarks as a model-shortlisting filter once the difference from your own eval is clear.","dataset-contamination":"explains why benchmark and golden-set scores inflate through leakage, right after benchmarks are introduced.","pass-k":"gives the correct estimator for sampled executable tasks, needed to read coding benchmarks and your own code evals.","llm-as-a-judge":"introduces model graders for open-ended outputs that no exact check can score.","rubrics":"shows how to turn \"is it good?\" into observable, mostly binary criteria a judge or human applies consistently.","inter-annotator-agreement":"measures whether the human labels a judge is scored against are themselves reliable.","judge-calibration-and-bias":"measures and mitigates position, length and self-preference bias against those human labels.","pairwise-comparison":"offers relative judging, win rates and Bradley–Terry ranking once judge biases are understood.","retrieval-evaluation":"isolates the retriever with recall@k, MRR and NDCG, the first stage of any RAG eval.","faithfulness-groundedness":"scores whether generated claims are supported by the retrieved context, the generation-side RAG metric.","hallucination-evaluation":"generalises claim-level verification to fabrication, unanswerable items and abstention.","regression-tests":"pins known behaviour and detects per-item flips across changes despite sampling noise.","metamorphic-testing":"checks label-free relations between outputs to expose brittleness the golden set cannot see.","property-based-testing":"applies generated inputs and shrinking to the deterministic scaffolding around the model.","red-teaming":"discovers the attacks a goal-driven adversary would use against prompts, data and tools.","adversarial-evaluation":"turns those discovered attacks into a versioned suite with attack success and over-refusal rates.","offline-vs-online-evaluation":"lays out the ladder from offline gates through shadow mode and canaries to live measurement.","a-b-testing":"gives the causal online answer, with randomisation unit, power, guardrails and no peeking.","eval-driven-development":"ties everything into a workflow where the eval suite is the executable specification of a feature.","tooling":"maps Promptfoo, RAGAS, DeepEval, Braintrust, LangSmith and Langfuse onto the pipeline once you know what it must do."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build and defend the evaluation stack for a RAG assistant that answers employee questions about a\ncompany's HR and expense policies, then use it to decide a real change: swapping the generation model\nfor a cheaper one and changing the chunking strategy. Start by reading at least 50 traces from a\nbaseline version and writing an error-analysis taxonomy with counts. From it, write acceptance criteria\nthat separate hard gates (no invented policy, no answer to out-of-scope or unanswerable questions) from\nsoft thresholds with slice floors, plus latency and cost limits. Build a golden set of 150 to 300\nstratified items, including unanswerable and false-premise questions, with a held-out split and a\ncontamination check against any few-shot examples. Have two people label an overlap sample to measure\nagreement. Write a harness that pins configuration, stores raw outputs before grading, and reports\nretrieval recall@k and MRR, claim-level faithfulness, hallucination and abstention rates, and a\nrubric-based LLM judge whose position bias and agreement with humans you have measured. Add a regression\ntier seeded from known bugs, a metamorphic suite (paraphrase, irrelevant-paragraph insertion), property\ntests on the chunker, and a small prompt-injection suite through poisoned documents. Wire the fast tier\ninto CI as a gate and write an online plan for the winning candidate.","deliverables":["An error-analysis sheet of at least 50 read traces with a failure taxonomy, counts per category, and the criteria and golden items each top category produced.","An acceptance-criteria document listing hard gates, soft thresholds with slice floors, and latency and cost limits, each with its grader and scope.","The versioned golden dataset with slice labels, a held-out split, a grader spec per item, an inter-annotator agreement report on the overlap sample, and a contamination-check script.","The eval harness, runnable from one command, producing a per-slice report with confidence intervals, plus a CI configuration that runs the fast tier and fails the build on a hard-gate violation.","A judge calibration report showing agreement and kappa against human labels next to human–human agreement, position-swap inconsistency rate, and length preference.","Regression, metamorphic, property-based and adversarial suites, each with its own pass or violation rate on the baseline.","A decision memo on the model swap and chunking change, citing paired per-item results, regressed items read, cost per answer, and an online rollout plan (shadow, canary, A/B with a power estimate and guardrails)."],"rubric":["Every reported metric carries a confidence interval or paired comparison, and the decision memo does not call a difference significant when its interval includes zero.","The judge is not used as a release gate until its true-positive and true-negative rates against human labels are reported, and its agreement is within reach of the human–human ceiling measured on the same items.","Running the harness twice on an unchanged system produces per-item flip rates below a stated flakiness tolerance, and the regression tier distinguishes a planted regression from that noise.","The unanswerable and false-premise slices are present and reported separately, and the fabrication rate on them is a hard gate rather than averaged into overall accuracy.","A planted prompt injection in a retrieved document is caught by the adversarial suite, and the report lists over-refusal on benign questions next to attack success rate.","At least one golden item and one criterion trace back to a named category in the error-analysis taxonomy, and the held-out split was never used while tuning prompts."]}},"topics":[{"id":"model-evaluation","key":"evals/model-evaluation","module":"evals","name":"Model evaluation","taxonomy_name":"model evaluation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/eval-harness","evals/benchmarking","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","evals/error-analysis","llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks"],"tldr":"Model evaluation produces a number you can trust about whether a model, or a system built on one, does\nthe job. Capability evals (benchmarks) and application evals (your golden dataset and graders) answer\ndifferent questions; without them every change is a guess.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"golden-dataset","key":"evals/golden-dataset","module":"evals","name":"Golden dataset (eval dataset)","taxonomy_name":"golden dataset (eval dataset)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/model-evaluation","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/regression-tests","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/dataset-contamination","llmops-platform/golden-dataset-strategy","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","training-adaptation/train-val-test-split"],"tldr":"A golden dataset is a curated, versioned set of inputs with outputs or labels the task owners agree are\ncorrect. It is the fixed reference for every eval number; \"golden\" means agreed and authoritative, not\nperfect or large.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"eval-harness","key":"evals/eval-harness","module":"evals","name":"Eval harness","taxonomy_name":"eval harness","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/regression-tests","evals/pass-k","evals/tooling","llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd"],"tldr":"An eval harness runs a versioned dataset through the system under test with pinned configuration,\ngrades stored outputs, and reports per-slice scores with intervals. It turns notebook evaluation into a\nrepeatable artifact with the status of a test suite.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"task-specific-acceptance-criteria","key":"evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","module":"evals","name":"Task-specific acceptance criteria","taxonomy_name":"task-specific acceptance criteria","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/rubrics","evals/error-analysis","evals/regression-tests","evals/eval-harness","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","system-design/nfrs"],"tldr":"Acceptance criteria turn \"the model should work well\" into checkable statements about one task's\noutputs (format, content, correctness, edge cases, latency, cost), each with a threshold and scope.\nGeneric scores cannot say whether that task is safe to ship.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"benchmarking","key":"evals/benchmarking","module":"evals","name":"Benchmarking (MMLU, HumanEval, SWE-bench, GPQA)","taxonomy_name":"benchmarking (MMLU, HumanEval, SWE-bench, GPQA)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["evals/model-evaluation","evals/dataset-contamination","evals/pass-k","evals/golden-dataset","system-design/model-selection","model-internals/reasoning-models-test-time-compute","agent-reliability/task-completion-rate"],"tldr":"A benchmark is a fixed dataset plus a fixed scoring rule so models can be compared on one task.\nMMLU, HumanEval, SWE-bench and GPQA each measure something narrow, saturate and leak: use leaderboards\nto screen, never as proof your task works.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"dataset-contamination","key":"evals/dataset-contamination","module":"evals","name":"Dataset contamination","taxonomy_name":"dataset contamination","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/benchmarking","evals/golden-dataset","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","training-adaptation/pre-training","training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance"],"tldr":"Dataset contamination means eval items, paraphrases or solutions sit in a model's training data, so\nscores measure memorization, not capability. Web-crawl training makes it unavoidable, inflation is\nuneven across models, and your own golden set leaks just as easily.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"llm-as-a-judge","key":"evals/llm-as-a-judge","module":"evals","name":"LLM-as-a-judge","taxonomy_name":"LLM-as-a-judge","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/rubrics","evals/pairwise-comparison","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/faithfulness-groundedness","evals/eval-harness","prompting/chain-of-thought","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode"],"tldr":"LLM-as-a-judge has a model grade open outputs against a rubric, often with a reference. It scales\ngrading, but is only as good as its measured agreement with humans: judges carry position, verbosity\nand self-preference biases and grade confidently, right or wrong.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"judge-calibration-and-bias","key":"evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","module":"evals","name":"Judge calibration & bias","taxonomy_name":"judge calibration & bias","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/rubrics","evals/pairwise-comparison","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/golden-dataset","ml-fundamentals/calibration"],"tldr":"An LLM judge is an instrument with systematic errors: it favours the first answer, the longer one, its\nown style, and bunches scores high. Measure those errors against human labels before trusting it, then\nchange the protocol to shrink them.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"rubrics","key":"evals/rubrics","module":"evals","name":"Rubrics","taxonomy_name":"rubrics","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/error-analysis","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines"],"tldr":"A rubric turns \"is this good?\" into specific, observable criteria with defined levels, so two graders (or\na human and an LLM judge) score alike. Build it from failure modes, prefer binary checks, gate\ncatastrophic criteria, and version it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pairwise-comparison","key":"evals/pairwise-comparison","module":"evals","name":"Pairwise comparison","taxonomy_name":"pairwise comparison","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/a-b-testing","evals/benchmarking","training-adaptation/rlhf","training-adaptation/dpo"],"tldr":"Pairwise comparison asks \"which of these two answers is better?\" instead of \"rate this 1–10\". It needs\nno shared scale, so verdicts are more consistent; they aggregate into a win rate or a Bradley–Terry\nranking and also feed preference training.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"inter-annotator-agreement","key":"evals/inter-annotator-agreement","module":"evals","name":"Inter-annotator agreement","taxonomy_name":"inter-annotator agreement","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/rubrics","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","training-adaptation/data-labeling","customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines"],"tldr":"Inter-annotator agreement (IAA) measures how consistently independent humans label the same items.\nLabels are the ground truth every eval and judge is scored against, so their noise is in every score.\nUse chance-corrected κ or α, not raw agreement.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pass-k","key":"evals/pass-k","module":"evals","name":"pass@k","taxonomy_name":"pass@k","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["evals/benchmarking","evals/eval-harness","evals/regression-tests","evals/dataset-contamination","model-internals/sampling-strategies","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation"],"tldr":"pass@k is the probability that at least one of k sampled solutions passes an executable check, averaged\nover problems. Estimate it from n ≥ k samples with the unbiased combinatorial estimator; for k > 1 it is\nan oracle number, not what a user sees.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"retrieval-evaluation","key":"evals/retrieval-evaluation","module":"evals","name":"Retrieval evaluation (recall@k, MRR, NDCG)","taxonomy_name":"retrieval evaluation (recall@k, MRR, NDCG)","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/recall-vs-precision-trade-off","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","evals/golden-dataset","evals/faithfulness-groundedness","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1"],"tldr":"A RAG answer is only as good as its retrieved passages, so the retriever is scored alone against known\nrelevant documents: recall@k (did evidence get in?), MRR (first hit how high?), NDCG (graded ranking\nright?). Choosing k and honest judgments matter most.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"faithfulness-groundedness","key":"evals/faithfulness-groundedness","module":"evals","name":"Faithfulness / groundedness","taxonomy_name":"faithfulness / groundedness","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/hallucination-evaluation","evals/retrieval-evaluation","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/tooling","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding","rag-retrieval/citation-correctness","model-internals/hallucination"],"tldr":"Faithfulness (groundedness) measures whether every claim in an answer is supported by the context the\nmodel was given, not whether it is true. Split the answer into atomic claims and verify each; no\nreference is needed, so it runs on production traffic.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"hallucination-evaluation","key":"evals/hallucination-evaluation","module":"evals","name":"Hallucination evaluation","taxonomy_name":"hallucination evaluation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/faithfulness-groundedness","model-internals/hallucination","rag-retrieval/citation-correctness","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/golden-dataset","ml-fundamentals/calibration"],"tldr":"Hallucination evaluation measures how often a system asserts unsupported or false things. It splits\noutputs into atomic claims, verifies each against a reference, and reports claim precision,\nresponse-level rate and abstention per slice, including unanswerable items.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"regression-tests","key":"evals/regression-tests","module":"evals","name":"Regression tests","taxonomy_name":"regression tests","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/eval-harness","evals/eval-driven-development","evals/pass-k","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","prompting/prompt-versioning"],"tldr":"An AI regression test re-runs pinned inputs whenever prompt, model, retriever or tools change, and fails\nthe change if passing cases now fail. LLM changes are non-local, so the hard part is separating real\nregressions from noise with paired, per-item comparisons.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"metamorphic-testing","key":"evals/metamorphic-testing","module":"evals","name":"Metamorphic testing","taxonomy_name":"metamorphic testing","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["evals/property-based-testing","evals/regression-tests","evals/adversarial-evaluation","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/pairwise-comparison","evals/golden-dataset","rag-retrieval/lost-in-the-middle"],"tldr":"Metamorphic testing checks a system without knowing the right answer: transform the input predictably\n(paraphrase, add an irrelevant document, swap options) and assert the relation between the two outputs.\nIt needs no labels and exposes brittleness a golden set cannot see.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"property-based-testing","key":"evals/property-based-testing","module":"evals","name":"Property-based testing","taxonomy_name":"property-based testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/property-based-testing","evals/metamorphic-testing","evals/regression-tests","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","safety-security/output-validation","python-for-ai/pytest"],"tldr":"Property-based testing generates hundreds of random inputs, asserts a property for each, and shrinks any\nfailure to the smallest failing input. In AI systems it finds silent bugs in chunkers, renderers, parsers\nand validators; model-output properties cost one call per example.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"adversarial-evaluation","key":"evals/adversarial-evaluation","module":"evals","name":"Adversarial evaluation","taxonomy_name":"adversarial evaluation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/red-teaming","prompting/prompt-injection","prompting/jailbreaks","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","safety-security/rag-poisoning","evals/metamorphic-testing","evals/regression-tests","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias"],"tldr":"Adversarial evaluation turns inputs chosen to make a system fail (jailbreaks, injected instructions,\nperturbations, grader-fooling outputs) into a versioned suite with attack success per class, run every\nrelease next to over-refusal, because average-case evals miss the worst case.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"offline-vs-online-evaluation","key":"evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","module":"evals","name":"Offline vs online evaluation","taxonomy_name":"offline vs online evaluation","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["evals/a-b-testing","evals/golden-dataset","evals/llm-as-a-judge","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","observability/monitoring","llmops-platform/drift"],"tldr":"Offline evaluation scores a fixed dataset before users see a change; online evaluation measures live\ntraffic. Offline is fast and safe but proxy-bound; online is outcome truth but slow and noisy. Offline\ngates releases, online validates offline, traces refill it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"a-b-testing","key":"evals/a-b-testing","module":"evals","name":"A/B testing","taxonomy_name":"A/B testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","evals/pairwise-comparison","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/rollback","llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","observability/monitoring","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags"],"tldr":"An A/B test randomly assigns users to the current system or a candidate and compares a pre-declared\noutcome, so differences are caused by the change. The discipline: right randomisation unit, one primary\nmetric, fixed sample size, no peeking.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"error-analysis","key":"evals/error-analysis","module":"evals","name":"Error analysis","taxonomy_name":"error analysis","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/eval-harness","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","evals/eval-driven-development","evals/regression-tests","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification"],"tldr":"Error analysis means reading real traces one at a time, noting what went wrong, grouping the notes into\na failure taxonomy and counting. A score says *that* the system fails; the counts say *why*, *where*\nand which fix buys the most.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"red-teaming","key":"evals/red-teaming","module":"evals","name":"Red teaming","taxonomy_name":"red teaming","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/adversarial-evaluation","evals/regression-tests","evals/tooling","prompting/jailbreaks","safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms","safety-security/excessive-agency","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response"],"tldr":"Red teaming is a deliberate, goal-driven attempt to make your AI system leak data, obey injected\ninstructions, cause harm, act without authorisation or invent policy. Ordinary evals test expected\ninputs; costly failures come from inputs an adversary chooses.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"eval-driven-development","key":"evals/eval-driven-development","module":"evals","name":"Eval-driven development","taxonomy_name":"eval-driven development","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/eval-harness","evals/error-analysis","evals/regression-tests","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","prompting/prompt-versioning"],"tldr":"Eval-driven development makes the eval suite the executable spec of an LLM feature: criteria and a seed\ngolden set come first, every change is judged by eval numbers, and failures grow the suite, because\n\"looked fine in the playground\" misses non-local regressions.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tooling","key":"evals/tooling","module":"evals","name":"Tooling (Promptfoo, RAGAS, DeepEval, Braintrust, LangSmith, Langfuse)","taxonomy_name":"tooling (Promptfoo, RAGAS, DeepEval, Braintrust, LangSmith, Langfuse)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/eval-harness","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/retrieval-evaluation","evals/faithfulness-groundedness","observability/langfuse","observability/langsmith","llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks"],"tldr":"Every eval tool runs dataset → system → grade → store and compare. Promptfoo, RAGAS and DeepEval are\nlocal libraries; Braintrust, LangSmith and Langfuse are platforms holding traces and experiments. Choose\nby where traces live and how much grading you must own.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"production","stage_index":4,"track_order":13},{"id":"serving-inference","tier":1,"order":8,"title":"Serving & inference","intro":{"overview":"This module covers what happens between a trained checkpoint and a token arriving in a user's browser,\nand why serving an LLM is a different job from serving a classifier. It starts with the physics. Each\nrequest runs a compute-bound prefill followed by a long, memory-bandwidth-bound decode loop whose working\nset, the KV cache, grows while it runs. TTFT, TPOT and tokens per second are the measurements of those\ntwo phases, and the roofline model tells you which bound you are hitting. Memory comes next: estimating\nthe KV cache from a model config, writing the four-line GPU memory ledger, and seeing how PagedAttention,\npaging and eviction turn a fragmented pool into usable concurrency. Scheduling builds on that. Continuous\nbatching, prefix caching and concurrency limits decide where a replica sits on the throughput-versus-latency\ncurve. Then the levers for fitting and speeding up a model: tensor parallelism, CPU offloading, quantization\nchecked against its full-precision baseline, constrained decoding, and the engines that package all of it.\nThe second half is the request path around the engine: streaming over SSE, layered timeouts, cancellation,\nidempotency, retries, fallbacks, rate limiting, backpressure and the three layers of LLM caching. Together\nthey turn a fast engine into a service that degrades predictably. By the end you can size a GPU deployment\nfrom a config file and a traffic model, find the latency knee in a load test, pick an engine and its\nsettings, and build a gateway that streams, cancels and sheds load correctly. Engineers carrying the pager\nneed it most, followed by Architects deciding between self-hosting and hosted APIs.","why":{"engineer":"You set `max_num_seqs`, `max_model_len` and the memory fraction from arithmetic rather than defaults. You write the SSE handler and the client wrapper with layered timeouts, abort-on-disconnect, idempotency keys and jittered retries. You also export the TTFT, TPOT, queue-wait and preemption metrics that show which phase or limit is failing.","architect":"You decide between self-hosting and a hosted API, and you own the GPU type, the tensor-parallel degree, the engine, the quantization format and the per-workload operating point on the throughput-latency curve. Those choices fix cost per million tokens, the latency SLO you can promise, and how much fallback and quota capacity has to be provisioned.","fde":"Customers arrive with \"it feels slow\", a vendor's tokens-per-second slide, or an on-premises GPU budget that has to be justified. You need to break latency down into queue, prefill and decode on the customer's own prompts, size their hardware from the KV-cache arithmetic, and tune proxies and gateways so streaming and timeouts survive their network."},"mental_model":"Picture one GPU as a fixed budget of two resources, memory bandwidth and memory capacity, with a queue in\nfront of it and a gateway in front of the queue. Each request takes two kinds of turn. Prefill is a single\ncompute-heavy pass over the prompt that sets TTFT. Decode is a long run of steps that each re-read every\nweight plus the request's KV cache, and that sets TPOT. Since a decode step costs about the same for 1\nsequence as for 64, throughput comes from packing many sequences into each step. KV memory caps that\npacking, which is why paging, prefix sharing, quantization and tensor parallelism all reduce to \"more\ntokens in flight per byte\". Pushing concurrency past the knee makes every request a little slower and the\nqueue a lot longer, so you choose an operating point and enforce it. The gateway's job is to keep the queue\nhonest. It streams so users see the first token early, times out and cancels so dead requests free their\nslots, retries only idempotent and transient failures, falls back when the primary is down, and rejects\nearly when demand exceeds what the GPU can serve. Every serving question resolves to the phase, the memory\nterm, or the boundary it belongs to.","order":["model-serving","prefill-vs-decode","ttft","tpot","tokens-sec","inference-optimization","kv-cache-memory-estimation","gpu-memory-optimization","pagedattention","kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","continuous-batching","prefix-caching","batching-and-concurrency-limits","throughput-vs-latency","tensor-parallelism-at-inference","cpu-offloading","quantization-quality-evaluation","structured-constrained-generation","engines","streaming","timeouts","cancellation","idempotency","retry-strategies","fallbacks","rate-limiting","backpressure","caching"],"order_reasons":{"model-serving":"the map of the request path (API, router, scheduler, memory, kernels) that every later lesson zooms into.","prefill-vs-decode":"the compute-bound versus bandwidth-bound split that explains every latency and cost number that follows.","ttft":"isolates the prefill-plus-queue phase as the latency users feel first, and how prompt length drives it.","tpot":"derives the decode floor from bytes read per step over memory bandwidth, and explains why batching barely moves it.","tokens-sec":"separates per-stream speed from aggregate server throughput, so later benchmarks are read correctly.","inference-optimization":"the roofline view that matches each optimization lever to the bound it can actually relieve.","kv-cache-memory-estimation":"turns a model config into bytes per token and a concurrency ceiling, the core capacity arithmetic.","gpu-memory-optimization":"extends that estimate into the four-line VRAM ledger and a fixed order of levers for when it does not fit.","pagedattention":"shows how fixed-size blocks remove reservation waste and fragmentation, so recovered memory becomes concurrency.","kv-cache-paging-and-eviction":"adds what happens when the paged pool still runs out: preemption, swap versus recompute, and prefix eviction.","continuous-batching":"iteration-level scheduling, which depends on paged memory and is what keeps decode batches full.","prefix-caching":"reuses shared prompt blocks across requests, the scheduling-layer fix for repeated prefill and high TTFT.","batching-and-concurrency-limits":"finds the latency knee and enforces sequence and token limits at the engine, gateway and client.","throughput-vs-latency":"generalises the knee into choosing an operating point per workload, with queueing as the tail.","tensor-parallelism-at-inference":"splits a model across GPUs to fit it and pool bandwidth, limited by the interconnect.","cpu-offloading":"computes the PCIe penalty to show when spilling weights or KV cache to host memory is acceptable.","quantization-quality-evaluation":"the measurement ladder that decides whether a smaller, faster checkpoint is still the model you tested.","structured-constrained-generation":"logit masking in the engine for output that parses by construction, and what that costs.","engines":"compares vLLM, SGLang, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, llama.cpp and Ollama once you know which features to judge them on.","streaming":"carries tokens from engine to browser over SSE, which makes TTFT the felt latency and disconnects observable.","timeouts":"layered connect, first-token, idle and total timers derived from TPOT and `max_tokens`, propagated as one deadline.","cancellation":"carries \"stop\" through every hop so expired or abandoned requests release their decode slots and KV blocks.","idempotency":"the key-and-replay mechanism that makes an ambiguous timeout safe to retry without paying twice or acting twice.","retry-strategies":"classified, jittered, budgeted retries that rely on idempotency and avoid layered retry storms.","fallbacks":"what to do when retrying will not help: compatible, pre-evaluated alternatives that share one deadline.","rate-limiting":"cost-aware per-tenant and outbound token buckets that protect shared GPUs and provider quotas.","backpressure":"bounds aggregate admission with Little's law and fast 429/503 rejections, the overload complement to rate limits.","caching":"closes the module by separating prompt, exact-match and semantic caches, each saving a different cost at a different risk."},"capstone":{"brief":"A mid-sized SaaS company wants to move its internal support assistant off a hosted API onto a self-hosted\nopen-weight model on one or two rented GPUs. Usage has grown, the per-token bill is high, and legal wants\ncustomer tickets kept inside the company's cloud account. The assistant has a 3,000-token system prompt\nwith tool schemas, multi-turn chat that streams to a web UI, and a nightly job that classifies the day's\ntickets into structured JSON. Over a weekend to a week, plan, deploy and harden that service. Choose a\nmodel and GPU, then write the capacity plan from the KV-cache arithmetic and the memory ledger before\nlaunching anything. Deploy it on a datacenter engine with deliberate settings, decide whether to quantize\nor use tensor parallelism and back that choice with evidence, and sweep concurrency with production-shaped\nprompts to find the latency knee. Put a thin gateway in front that streams over SSE, applies layered\ntimeouts, cancels on disconnect, enforces idempotency keys on message creation, retries with a budget,\nfalls back to the hosted API when the replica is unhealthy, rate-limits per tenant, and sheds load with\n`Retry-After` once queue wait exceeds its budget. Enable prefix caching and show what it buys on the chat\ntraffic, and run the nightly job through constrained decoding at the throughput end of the curve.","deliverables":["A capacity plan that derives bytes per token from the model config, writes the four-line VRAM ledger for the chosen GPU, and converts the target request rate into a replica count using Little's law, with the resulting `max_model_len`, `max_num_seqs` and memory-fraction settings.","A load-test report that sweeps concurrency (for example 1, 4, 16, 32, 64) with production-shaped prompt and output lengths, records TTFT p95, TPOT p95, per-stream and aggregate tokens/s and preemptions at each point, and states the chosen interactive and batch operating points.","A quantization decision record that compares the quantized checkpoint against its full-precision baseline using KL divergence or perplexity and a golden-set task eval with identical prompts and decoding. It reports the chat and JSON-classification categories separately and gives an accept or reject verdict.","Gateway code with SSE streaming, connect, first-token, idle and total timeouts, propagated cancellation, an idempotency-key store, classified retries with full jitter and a retry budget, a circuit-broken fallback to the hosted API, per-tenant token buckets, and queue-wait-based load shedding.","A dashboard and runbook covering TTFT, TPOT, queue wait, KV-cache utilisation, preemptions, prefix hit rate, fallback rate and tokens generated after disconnect, with one alert per overload signal and the first action to take for each."],"rubric":["The measured KV-cache block count and peak memory under load fall within about 10% of the ledger's prediction, and any gap is explained by a named term such as activation peak or runtime overhead.","Enabling prefix caching cuts chat TTFT p95 by a measured, reported amount on multi-turn traffic, and the report shows that changing one early token in the system prompt removes the gain.","When a client disconnects mid-stream, the engine aborts the sequence within about a second, as shown by engine logs and the tokens-after-disconnect metric, and the freed KV blocks show up in the cache-utilisation graph.","At twice the planned peak load the gateway returns fast 429 or 503 responses with `Retry-After` to the excess traffic, while admitted requests stay within the TTFT and TPOT SLOs and the preemption counter stays near zero.","Replaying a message-creation request with the same idempotency key after a forced timeout returns the stored result with no second generation, and provider calls per user request stay at or below the stated amplification limit during an injected 429 burst.","With the primary replica killed, interactive traffic moves to the hosted fallback within the stated breaker threshold and every response carries a served-by tag. The nightly constrained-decoding job produces 100% schema-valid JSON, and its task accuracy is reported against an unconstrained baseline."]}},"topics":[{"id":"model-serving","key":"serving-inference/model-serving","module":"serving-inference","name":"Model serving","taxonomy_name":"model serving","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["serving-inference/engines","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","serving-inference/streaming","infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","infra-capacity-economics/serverless-gpus","model-internals/kv-cache"],"tldr":"Model serving is everything between a trained checkpoint and an HTTP response. For LLMs each request is a\nstateful loop of unknown length whose KV cache grows while it runs, so the scheduler, not the model,\ndecides what the hardware delivers.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"inference-optimization","key":"serving-inference/inference-optimization","module":"serving-inference","name":"Inference optimization","taxonomy_name":"inference optimization","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","model-internals/speculative-decoding","model-internals/flashattention","training-adaptation/quantization"],"tldr":"Inference optimization is the set of levers that make a fixed model cheaper or faster. Nearly all attack\none of two bounds: decode is memory-bandwidth bound (each token re-reads weights and cache), prefill is\ncompute bound. Learn the roofline that tells them apart.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"prefill-vs-decode","key":"serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","module":"serving-inference","name":"Prefill vs decode","taxonomy_name":"prefill vs decode","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/inference-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth"],"tldr":"Every LLM request has two phases with opposite hardware profiles. Prefill runs the whole prompt at once,\nis compute-bound and sets TTFT. Decode makes one token per step, is bandwidth-bound and sets TPOT. Mixing\nthem on one GPU causes interference.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"kv-cache-memory-estimation","key":"serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","module":"serving-inference","name":"KV-cache memory estimation","taxonomy_name":"KV-cache memory estimation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/grouped-query-attention","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning"],"tldr":"KV-cache estimation turns \"we have an 80 GB GPU\" into \"we can serve N requests of length L\": 2 × layers ×\nKV heads × head dim × bytes per element, times tokens, times sequences. It prevents OOM surprises and\nhalf-empty GPUs.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","key":"serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","module":"serving-inference","name":"KV-cache paging & eviction","taxonomy_name":"KV-cache paging & eviction","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["serving-inference/pagedattention","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/cpu-offloading","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/context-window"],"tldr":"Paging stores each request's KV cache in fixed-size blocks behind a block table, like OS virtual memory,\nso sequences grow without reserving their maximum or fragmenting the pool. Eviction handles overflow:\npreempt sequences, drop cached prefixes, or (lossily) drop tokens.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"prefix-caching","key":"serving-inference/prefix-caching","module":"serving-inference","name":"Prefix caching","taxonomy_name":"prefix caching","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["prompting/prompt-caching","serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/caching","model-internals/kv-cache","prompting/system-prompt","agents/agent-loop"],"tldr":"Prefix caching keeps a prompt prefix's KV cache alive so the next request with the same leading tokens\nskips that prefill. It is exact under causal attention, and cuts TTFT and compute, but only for\nbyte-identical beginnings.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"continuous-batching","key":"serving-inference/continuous-batching","module":"serving-inference","name":"Continuous batching","taxonomy_name":"continuous batching","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/engines","ml-fundamentals/batch-size","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory"],"tldr":"Continuous batching schedules per decode iteration, not per request: finished sequences leave and waiting\nones join every step, so the GPU stays full. One weight read serves dozens of tokens, giving throughput an\norder of magnitude above naive servers.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pagedattention","key":"serving-inference/pagedattention","module":"serving-inference","name":"PagedAttention","taxonomy_name":"PagedAttention","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","serving-inference/engines"],"tldr":"PagedAttention stores each request's KV cache in fixed-size blocks (usually 16 tokens) behind a\nper-request block table, allocated on demand and shareable. Because decode is memory-bound, the recovered\nmemory becomes more concurrent requests, the source of vLLM's throughput gains.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"batching-and-concurrency-limits","key":"serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","module":"serving-inference","name":"Batching & concurrency limits","taxonomy_name":"batching & concurrency limits","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/backpressure","serving-inference/rate-limiting","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory"],"tldr":"Decode streams all weights once per step regardless of batch size, so batching multiplies throughput\nalmost free until compute or KV-cache reads bind. Concurrency limits stop past-the-knee latency growth\nand preemption thrash; enforce them at engine, gateway and client.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tensor-parallelism-at-inference","key":"serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","module":"serving-inference","name":"Tensor parallelism at inference","taxonomy_name":"tensor parallelism at inference","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["training-adaptation/distributed-training","model-internals/grouped-query-attention","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/mixture-of-experts","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","serving-inference/engines","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","infra-capacity-economics/a100-h100-l4-selection"],"tldr":"Tensor parallelism splits every weight matrix across N GPUs, joined by two all-reduces per layer. It\nfits oversized models and pools bandwidth for faster decode, but needs a fast intra-node link because\ndecode sends hundreds of tiny all-reduces per token.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"cpu-offloading","key":"serving-inference/cpu-offloading","module":"serving-inference","name":"CPU offloading","taxonomy_name":"CPU offloading","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","serving-inference/engines","training-adaptation/quantization","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment"],"tldr":"CPU offloading keeps some weights or KV cache in host RAM or NVMe, trading GPU memory for PCIe bandwidth\nroughly 50–100× slower. Decode touches every weight per token, so it suits fitting, offline throughput\nand parking idle KV cache, not low-latency serving.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"quantization-quality-evaluation","key":"serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation","module":"serving-inference","name":"Quantization quality evaluation","taxonomy_name":"quantization quality evaluation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["training-adaptation/quantization","model-internals/perplexity","evals/golden-dataset","evals/regression-tests","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/benchmarking","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases"],"tldr":"Measure a quantized model against its own full-precision baseline on the work you serve. KL divergence\nand perplexity are cheap early signals; paired golden-set evals are the acceptance test. Long context,\nmath, code, strict formats and rare languages break first.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"structured-constrained-generation","key":"serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","module":"serving-inference","name":"Structured / constrained generation","taxonomy_name":"structured / constrained generation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","prompting/function-tool-calling","python-for-ai/outlines","python-for-ai/instructor","python-for-ai/pydantic","model-internals/logits","model-internals/tokenization","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation"],"tldr":"Constrained decoding masks the logits of grammar-violating tokens at every step, so output matches a\nschema, regex, grammar or choice set by construction. It removes retry loops but guarantees\nshape, not truth, costs a grammar compile, and can shift what the model says.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"gpu-memory-optimization","key":"serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","module":"serving-inference","name":"GPU memory optimization (VRAM math)","taxonomy_name":"GPU memory optimization (VRAM math)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/pagedattention","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","serving-inference/cpu-offloading","training-adaptation/quantization","model-internals/flashattention","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing"],"tldr":"Inference memory is a four-line ledger: weights, KV cache, activation peaks, and overhead plus\nfragmentation. Subtract them from usable VRAM and every tuning knob is a term you shrink. Guessing causes\nboth out-of-memory pages and 20%-utilized GPUs.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"streaming","key":"serving-inference/streaming","module":"serving-inference","name":"Streaming (SSE)","taxonomy_name":"streaming (SSE)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/cancellation","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/backpressure","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/websockets","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams"],"tldr":"Streaming sends each token as it is decoded, so users feel time-to-first-token, not total time.\nServer-Sent Events (SSE) is the usual transport: plain one-way HTTP. The price: errors and usage arrive\nin-band, and no hop may buffer.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"ttft","key":"serving-inference/ttft","module":"serving-inference","name":"TTFT (time to first token)","taxonomy_name":"TTFT","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/streaming","serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","observability/latency-percentiles","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets"],"tldr":"Time to first token (TTFT) is queueing wait plus prefill over the whole prompt, so it grows with prompt\nlength and load, not answer length. It is the latency a streaming user feels; cut it with shorter\nprompts, prefix caching, admission control and scheduling.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tpot","key":"serving-inference/tpot","module":"serving-inference","name":"TPOT (time per output token)","taxonomy_name":"TPOT","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/tokens-sec","serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/continuous-batching","model-internals/kv-cache","model-internals/speculative-decoding","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference"],"tldr":"Time per output token (TPOT) is the average gap between tokens once generation starts. Each decode step\nreads all weights plus the KV cache, so TPOT has a bandwidth floor and barely rises with batch size;\nextra FLOPs alone do not help.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tokens-sec","key":"serving-inference/tokens-sec","module":"serving-inference","name":"Tokens per second","taxonomy_name":"tokens/sec","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","evals/benchmarking"],"tldr":"\"Tokens per second\" names two numbers that move in opposite directions with concurrency: one stream's\ndecode speed (what a user sees) and a server's aggregate output (what you pay for). A figure means\nnothing without tokens counted, concurrency, lengths, hardware and percentile.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"throughput-vs-latency","key":"serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","module":"serving-inference","name":"Throughput vs latency","taxonomy_name":"throughput vs latency","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/tokens-sec","serving-inference/tpot","serving-inference/ttft","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","reliability/slas-slos"],"tldr":"A decode step costs about the same for one sequence or thirty-two, so batching multiplies throughput\nalmost for free, but each added request lengthens every step and the queue. Pick an operating point per\nworkload and enforce it with limits and SLOs.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"timeouts","key":"serving-inference/timeouts","module":"serving-inference","name":"Timeouts","taxonomy_name":"timeouts","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/streaming","serving-inference/cancellation","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/fallbacks","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","reliability/circuit-breakers","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","system-design/synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution"],"tldr":"LLM calls last from under a second to minutes, so one flat timeout kills good work or lets hung calls\nhold resources. Layer connect, first-token, idle and total timers, derive the total from\n`max_tokens × TPOT`, propagate the remaining budget, and cancel on every expiry.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"cancellation","key":"serving-inference/cancellation","module":"serving-inference","name":"Cancellation","taxonomy_name":"cancellation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/streaming","serving-inference/backpressure","serving-inference/continuous-batching","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","agents/agent-loop"],"tldr":"An abandoned generation still holds a decode slot, KV memory and token spend until `max_tokens`.\nCancellation carries \"stop\" through every hop, browser to engine, so the sequence is aborted and its\nmemory freed. Each layer must notice the disconnect and close the next connection.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"backpressure","key":"serving-inference/backpressure","module":"serving-inference","name":"Backpressure","taxonomy_name":"backpressure","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/cancellation","serving-inference/rate-limiting","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure"],"tldr":"Backpressure is how an overloaded stage tells its feeder to slow down instead of buffering until memory\nor latency blows up. Bound admission, measure queue wait, and reject the rest fast with 429 or 503 plus\n`Retry-After`.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"fallbacks","key":"serving-inference/fallbacks","module":"serving-inference","name":"Fallbacks","taxonomy_name":"fallbacks","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/model-fallback-strategies","reliability/circuit-breakers","system-design/graceful-degradation","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/idempotency","system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/model-routing-cascading"],"tldr":"A fallback is a pre-decided alternative when the primary fails or is slow: another endpoint, a smaller\nmodel, a cached answer, or an honest \"unavailable\". Retrying a broken target burns the latency budget;\nthe hard parts are fast decisions, compatibility and protecting the backup.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"retry-strategies","key":"serving-inference/retry-strategies","module":"serving-inference","name":"Retry strategies","taxonomy_name":"retry strategies","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/idempotency","serving-inference/rate-limiting","serving-inference/fallbacks","reliability/circuit-breakers","node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation"],"tldr":"A retry re-issues a request that failed transiently. Classify the error, back off exponentially with\njitter, honour `Retry-After`, cap attempts and time, keep a global budget, and rely on idempotency, or a\nbrief hiccup becomes a self-inflicted outage.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"rate-limiting","key":"serving-inference/rate-limiting","module":"serving-inference","name":"Rate limiting","taxonomy_name":"rate limiting","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/backpressure","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","system-design/ai-gateway"],"tldr":"Rate limiting bounds a caller's work per unit of time: inbound to protect shared GPUs and quota, outbound\nto stay under provider request and token limits. Cost is unknown until a request\nfinishes, so limiters reserve an estimate and settle on the actual.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"idempotency","key":"serving-inference/idempotency","module":"serving-inference","name":"Idempotency","taxonomy_name":"idempotency","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/timeouts","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation"],"tldr":"An operation is idempotent when doing it twice equals doing it once. Retries, timeouts, queues and agents\nall create duplicates that cost money or act twice, so a client-chosen key stored with the outcome lets\nrepeats replay the saved result.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"caching","key":"serving-inference/caching","module":"serving-inference","name":"Caching (semantic, prompt)","taxonomy_name":"caching (semantic, prompt)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["serving-inference/prefix-caching","prompting/prompt-caching","model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/ttft","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","observability/token-cost-tracking","infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization"],"tldr":"LLM caching is three layers: exact-match response caches skip identical requests, semantic caches skip\n*similar* ones by embedding distance, and prompt caching reuses a shared prefix's KV cache so only\nprefill is saved. They save, fail and key differently.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"engines","key":"serving-inference/engines","module":"serving-inference","name":"Engines (vLLM, SGLang, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, Ollama, llama.cpp)","taxonomy_name":"engines (vLLM, SGLang, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, Ollama, llama.cpp)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["serving-inference/model-serving","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/pagedattention","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","training-adaptation/quantization","system-design/self-host-vs-api","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing"],"tldr":"An inference engine turns requests into tokens and owns batching, KV memory, kernels and the API.\nDatacenter engines (vLLM, SGLang, TGI, TensorRT-LLM) serve many users; local runtimes (llama.cpp, Ollama)\nfavour portability. Choose by hardware, concurrency and ops effort.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"production","stage_index":4,"track_order":14},{"id":"observability","tier":1,"order":9,"title":"Observability","intro":{"overview":"This module is about seeing what an LLM system actually did, why it did it, how long it took and what\nit cost, when the failures that matter most return HTTP 200 with plausible text. It starts with\ntracing, the timed tree of one request, and spans, the nodes of that tree, where the real skill is\nchoosing boundaries and attributes (model, tokens, chunk ids, tool arguments) that match the questions\nyou will ask later. Distributed tracing carries that tree across the gateway, retrieval service, tool\nservers and queues that a single turn crosses, using W3C `traceparent` propagation. Logging records the\ndiscrete events and payloads that traces should not hold: rendered prompts, raw completions, tool\nresults and guardrail decisions, structured, bound to trace ids and redacted by policy. Monitoring\ncompresses all of it into aggregate signals and alerts, adding LLM quality proxies to the golden\nsignals; latency percentiles and token cost tracking are the two measurements every LLM dashboard is\nbuilt on, and both are easy to compute wrongly. OpenTelemetry is the vendor-neutral plumbing that\nproduces this telemetry once, and Langfuse, LangSmith and Arize Phoenix are the LLM-aware backends\nthat add prompts, costs, scores, datasets and evaluation on top. After the module you can instrument\na RAG or agent service end to end, debug a single bad answer from its trace, build dashboards and\nburn-rate alerts that catch silent regressions, attribute spend to features and tenants, and choose a\nbackend on the merits. It matters most for AI Engineers who ship and operate these systems, and\nfor FDEs who must explain a customer's bad answer or bill within the hour.","why":{"engineer":"You instrument every model call, retrieval and tool call as spans with the right attributes, propagate context through gateways and queues, and use the resulting traces, logs and cost records to find which step produced a wrong answer or a slow turn.","architect":"You choose the telemetry pipeline (OTel SDKs, Collector, sampling policy, retention and redaction) and the LLM backend, deciding what gets stored where, what it costs to keep, and whether prompts containing customer data can leave your infrastructure.","fde":"When a customer forwards a bad answer or questions the bill, you need to pull up that conversation's trace, show exactly what the model saw, attribute cost to their tenant, and stand up dashboards and alerts in their environment without rebuilding their stack."},"mental_model":"Hold one picture: every user turn is a tree of timed spans, and everything else in observability is a\nview onto that tree. The root is the turn; beneath it sit retrieval, reranking, each generation, each\ntool call and each retry, stitched together across processes by a propagated trace id. Spans carry\nthe structure and timing; logs hang payloads and decisions off the same ids; metrics are the tree\naggregated over millions of requests into rates, latency histograms and token counters. Ask \"what\nhappened on this request\" and you read one tree; ask \"how often, how slow, how expensive\" and you\nquery the aggregates, using percentiles from merged histograms and cost from usage times the price in\nforce. OpenTelemetry is how the tree is produced in a standard shape; Langfuse, LangSmith and Phoenix\nare places to store it and attach quality scores to its nodes. If the tree is incomplete, every view\nbuilt on it lies.","order":["tracing","spans","distributed-tracing-across-services","logging","opentelemetry","latency-percentiles","token-cost-tracking","monitoring","langfuse","langsmith","arize-phoenix"],"order_reasons":{"tracing":"establishes why LLM systems need a per-request causal record and what a trace must capture, the frame for everything else.","spans":"teaches the unit traces are built from and how to choose span boundaries and attributes for generations, retrievals and tools.","distributed-tracing-across-services":"extends the span tree across gateways, tool servers and queues so traces do not break at the first hop.","logging":"adds the durable, structured event and payload record that complements sampled, lightweight traces.","opentelemetry":"shows the standard API, SDK, OTLP and propagation format that produce traces, metrics and logs once for any backend.","latency-percentiles":"gives the correct way to measure and aggregate TTFT and end-to-end latency before building dashboards on it.","token-cost-tracking":"records usage per span and attributes spend by feature, tenant and run, the LLM-specific metric beside latency.","monitoring":"combines golden signals, quality proxies, latency and cost into dashboards, SLOs and burn-rate alerts that catch silent failures.","langfuse":"the open-source, self-hostable backend that closes the trace, score, dataset and prompt-version loop in one tool.","langsmith":"the hosted platform most integrated with LangChain and LangGraph, best compared once the Langfuse data model is familiar.","arize-phoenix":"the OpenTelemetry-native, evaluation-heavy option, which makes the most sense after OTel and the other two backends."},"capstone":{"brief":"Instrument a multi-service RAG support assistant so that any bad answer, slow turn or cost spike can be\nexplained from telemetry alone. The system has at least three processes: an API gateway, an\norchestrator that rewrites the query, retrieves and reranks chunks and calls a model, and a separate\ntool service (for example an order-status lookup) reached over HTTP or through a queue. Instrument all\nof them with OpenTelemetry, propagating `traceparent` across every hop including the queue, and record\ngeneration spans with model, parameters, token usage and computed cost, retrieval spans with chunk ids\nand scores, and tool spans with arguments and outcomes. Emit structured logs bound to trace ids, with\nrendered prompts and raw completions redacted at INFO and kept in a sampled payload stream. Send the\ntraces through an OTel Collector to both a general trace store (Jaeger or Tempo) and one LLM backend\n(Langfuse, LangSmith or Phoenix), and attach an LLM-as-a-judge relevance or faithfulness score to a\nsample of production traces. Build a dashboard of TTFT and end-to-end latency percentiles from\nhistograms, error, parse-failure and refusal rates, and cost per tenant and per successful task, with a\nburn-rate alert on a TTFT SLO and a per-run cost budget. Then inject three faults (a slow tool, a\nprompt change that inflates input tokens, and a stale retrieval index) and show that telemetry catches\nand localises each one.","deliverables":["The instrumented services and an OTel Collector configuration (Docker Compose is fine) that fans traces out to a general trace store and an LLM backend, with propagation working across HTTP and the queue.","A dashboard with p50/p95/p99 TTFT and end-to-end latency computed from merged histograms, error, parse-failure and refusal rates, and token cost by feature, tenant and model.","Alert definitions for a TTFT SLO burn rate and a per-agent-run or per-tenant cost budget, plus a short runbook for each alert.","A structured logging schema listing the correlation fields on every line, the per-event fields, and the redaction and sampling rules for prompts and completions.","A fault-injection report covering the three injected faults: the alert or dashboard signal that fired, the trace that localised the cause, and the time from injection to diagnosis.","A one-page backend comparison note justifying the chosen LLM backend (Langfuse, LangSmith or Phoenix) on data residency, framework fit, evaluation workflow and cost."],"rubric":["At least 99% of sampled turns produce a single connected trace from the gateway root to the provider call and the tool service, measured by a completeness query rather than asserted.","Every generation span carries model, input, output and cached token counts and a computed cost, and the summed computed cost for a test run matches the provider's reported usage within a few percent.","Latency percentiles are computed from histograms with bucket edges at the SLO thresholds, and the dashboard never shows an averaged percentile or a mean latency as the headline number.","Each of the three injected faults is detected by a dashboard signal or alert and traced to the responsible span, including the stale index, which produces no errors and is caught only by a quality proxy or judge score.","A grep of the default log stream for a planted secret or email address in a test prompt finds nothing, while the sampled payload stream still lets a reviewer reproduce the exact prompt for a given trace id."]}},"topics":[{"id":"tracing","key":"observability/tracing","module":"observability","name":"Tracing","taxonomy_name":"tracing","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["observability/spans","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","observability/opentelemetry","observability/langfuse","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","evals/error-analysis"],"tldr":"A trace is the timed tree of one request through an LLM app: every retrieval, model call, tool call and\nretry as nested spans. LLM systems fail *semantically*, not by crashing, so you need each step's inputs\nand outputs in order.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"spans","key":"observability/spans","module":"observability","name":"Spans","taxonomy_name":"spans","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","observability/opentelemetry","observability/latency-percentiles","node-ts-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","serving-inference/ttft"],"tldr":"A span is one named, timed operation with a parent, a status, attributes and optional events. Traces are\nonly as useful as their spans, so the skill is choosing boundaries that match your questions and\nrecording attributes that make each span filterable on its own.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"distributed-tracing-across-services","key":"observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","module":"observability","name":"Distributed tracing across services","taxonomy_name":"distributed tracing across services","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/spans","observability/opentelemetry","system-design/ai-gateway","agents/model-context-protocol","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","llmops-platform/observability-stack"],"tldr":"In-process span context dies at every service boundary, so distributed tracing *propagates* trace id and\nparent span id with each call (the W3C `traceparent` header, or queue message metadata). Without it, one\nLLM turn becomes disconnected fragments with unattributable latency.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"logging","key":"observability/logging","module":"observability","name":"Logging","taxonomy_name":"logging","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/monitoring","python-for-ai/logging-structlog","fastapi-for-ai/structured-logging","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","safety-security/pii-redaction","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture"],"tldr":"Logging records discrete events as structured records bound to trace and request ids. In LLM apps logs\nhold what traces and metrics should not: rendered prompts, raw completions, tool arguments, guardrail\ndecisions and provider errors, redacted and retained by policy.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"monitoring","key":"observability/monitoring","module":"observability","name":"Monitoring","taxonomy_name":"monitoring","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["observability/latency-percentiles","observability/token-cost-tracking","observability/logging","reliability/slas-slos","llmops-platform/model-monitoring","llmops-platform/drift","deployment/health-checks"],"tldr":"Monitoring compresses requests into aggregate signals (rates, errors, latency, saturation, quality,\ncost), compares them with targets and pages on drift. The LLM difficulty: the worst failures return HTTP\n200, so you monitor proxies and sampled quality scores, not just errors.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"token-cost-tracking","key":"observability/token-cost-tracking","module":"observability","name":"Token / cost tracking","taxonomy_name":"token / cost tracking","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["observability/monitoring","observability/spans","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","prompting/prompt-caching","python-for-ai/tiktoken","model-internals/tokenization","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets"],"tldr":"LLM spend is metered per token and priced by token class and model, so record provider usage on every\ncall, cost it at the price then in force, and roll up by decision dimensions. Retries, agent loops and\nhistory make averages mislead.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"latency-percentiles","key":"observability/latency-percentiles","module":"observability","name":"Latency percentiles (p50/p95/p99)","taxonomy_name":"latency percentiles (p50/p95/p99)","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["observability/monitoring","observability/tracing","serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","reliability/slas-slos","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets"],"tldr":"Latency is a distribution, not a number: p50 is the typical request, p95 and p99 are the tail users notice\nand fan-out amplifies. For LLM systems, measure per phase (TTFT, per-token, total) and merge histograms\nbefore taking quantiles, never average percentiles.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"opentelemetry","key":"observability/opentelemetry","module":"observability","name":"OpenTelemetry","taxonomy_name":"OpenTelemetry","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/spans","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","observability/logging","observability/arize-phoenix","observability/langfuse","fastapi-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","node-ts-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation"],"tldr":"OpenTelemetry (OTel) is the CNCF vendor-neutral standard for producing traces, metrics and logs: one API\nand SDK per language, one wire protocol (OTLP), W3C Trace Context and shared attribute names. Instrument\nonce; the backend becomes configuration.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"langfuse","key":"observability/langfuse","module":"observability","name":"Langfuse","taxonomy_name":"Langfuse","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/token-cost-tracking","observability/opentelemetry","observability/langsmith","observability/arize-phoenix","evals/tooling","evals/llm-as-a-judge","prompting/prompt-versioning"],"tldr":"Langfuse is an open-source, self-hostable LLM engineering platform: traces of prompts, completions, tokens\nand cost, plus scores, datasets, experiments and versioned prompts. Choose it for the trace-to-eval-to-prompt\nloop in one framework-independent tool you can run yourself.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"langsmith","key":"observability/langsmith","module":"observability","name":"LangSmith","taxonomy_name":"LangSmith","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/langfuse","observability/arize-phoenix","observability/opentelemetry","agents/frameworks","evals/tooling","evals/golden-dataset","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation"],"tldr":"LangSmith is LangChain Inc.'s closed-source platform for tracing, evaluating and monitoring LLM apps: run\ntrees with tokens, latency and cost, feedback, online evaluators and dataset experiments. Any code can use\n`@traceable`, but for LangChain and LangGraph tracing is an environment-variable switch.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"arize-phoenix","key":"observability/arize-phoenix","module":"observability","name":"Arize Phoenix","taxonomy_name":"Arize Phoenix","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["observability/opentelemetry","observability/tracing","observability/langfuse","observability/langsmith","evals/tooling","evals/hallucination-evaluation","evals/retrieval-evaluation","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis"],"tldr":"Arize Phoenix is a self-hostable (source-available, Elastic License 2.0) LLM observability and evaluation\ntool built on OpenTelemetry: it stores OTel spans with OpenInference attributes, runs LLM judges over them,\nwrites verdicts back onto spans and visualises embeddings.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"production","stage_index":4,"track_order":15},{"id":"safety-security","tier":1,"order":10,"title":"Safety & security","intro":{"overview":"This module covers how to keep an LLM system from being turned against its users, its operator or its\ndata. It starts from one premise that every lesson returns to: the model is an untrusted component that\nfollows instructions wherever it finds them, and a system prompt is an instruction, not an enforcement\nboundary. The OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications gives the shared vocabulary. Prompt injection and RAG\npoisoning explain how untrusted text, typed by a user or planted in a document, web page or corpus,\nreaches the model's single input stream. The checkpoint layer comes next: input and output guardrails,\noutput validation that turns strings into typed data, content moderation with per-surface thresholds,\nand two concrete tools, Llama Guard as a policy-in-the-prompt safety classifier and NeMo Guardrails as an\norchestrator of rails. The data lessons (PII redaction, data leakage, cross-tenant leakage) map every\nplace an LLM pipeline copies text: prompts, traces, caches, embeddings, memory and weights. The action\nlessons (excessive agency, tool privilege escalation) cover agents, where a wrong token becomes a real\nside effect. Supply-chain security closes the module by treating checkpoints and datasets as build\nartifacts that can run code or carry backdoors. Afterwards you can threat-model an LLM feature, choose\nand measure guardrails, design tenant isolation and redaction per boundary, scope agent authority, and\nanswer an enterprise security review item by item. It matters most for Engineers who build these layers\nand Architects who own the trust boundaries, and FDEs need it to pass customer security reviews.","why":{"engineer":"You write the code that validates model output with Pydantic, threads a server-derived tenant ID into every vector, cache and memory call, redacts traces, runs moderation and Llama Guard checks, and executes tools with the requesting user's credentials instead of the service's, and each of these needs labelled tests and a tracked false-positive rate.","architect":"You decide where the trust boundaries sit, which workflows combine private data, untrusted content and outbound capability, which tools are tiered as irreversible and gated, how tenants are isolated across every store, and which models and datasets may enter the registry; those choices, not the prompt, set the blast radius.","fde":"Enterprise buyers send security questionnaires keyed to OWASP LLM items, ask where their data goes and whether another customer could see it, and want their own moderation policy; you need a threat model, a data-flow map and red-team evidence you can show, and the ability to tune rails per deployment."},"mental_model":"Picture the model as a powerful but gullible deputy sitting in the middle of a building with many doors.\nText comes in through several doors (the user, retrieved chunks, tool results, downloaded checkpoints),\nand all of it lands in the same ear; the deputy cannot tell instructions from data. Things go out through\nother doors: answers shown to people, tool calls that change the world, and copies of the text written\nto logs, caches, indexes and weights. Security means guarding the doors, not lecturing the deputy. At the\ninbound doors you control provenance, trust tiers, redaction and injection screening. At the outbound\ndoors you validate structure and meaning, moderate content, scan for leaks and enforce tenancy. At the\naction doors you give the deputy only the keys for this task and this user, checked by the resource,\nwith a human at the irreversible ones. Then you prove each guard works with labelled positives,\nnegatives and red-team cases, because a guard nobody has measured is not a control.","order":["owasp-top-10-for-llms","prompt-injection","rag-poisoning","input-output-guardrails","output-validation","content-moderation","llama-guard","nemo-guardrails","pii-redaction","data-leakage","cross-tenant-data-leakage","excessive-agency","tool-privilege-escalation","supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets"],"order_reasons":{"owasp-top-10-for-llms":"the intro-level map and shared vocabulary that every later lesson slots into.","prompt-injection":"the root threat: one input stream with no code/data separation, and the lethal trifecta that turns it into an incident.","rag-poisoning":"extends injection to the corpus, showing why write-path provenance and trust tiers beat prompt-side defences.","input-output-guardrails":"introduces the deterministic checkpoint layer and the measure-before-enforce discipline the next lessons build on.","output-validation":"the most mechanical output rail: syntactic, structural and semantic validity of anything code consumes.","content-moderation":"classification against a harm taxonomy with per-surface thresholds, actions and review metrics.","llama-guard":"a concrete open-weight moderation classifier with policy in the prompt, best read once moderation itself is clear.","nemo-guardrails":"a toolkit that composes rails and classifiers such as Llama Guard, so it comes after the pieces it orchestrates.","pii-redaction":"the first data control: typed, reversible pseudonymisation applied per boundary.","data-leakage":"widens from PII to every channel data can leave by: provider, tools, rendering, logs, embeddings and weights.","cross-tenant-data-leakage":"the multi-tenant special case where vector stores, caches and memory bypass row-level security.","excessive-agency":"moves from words to actions, shrinking tools, permissions and autonomy to bound the blast radius.","tool-privilege-escalation":"the confused-deputy deep dive: every tool call must carry only the asking principal's authority.","supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets":"finishes below the application, treating checkpoints and datasets as signed, scanned build artifacts."},"capstone":{"brief":"Secure a multi-tenant customer-support copilot before its first enterprise launch. The copilot answers\nquestions from a RAG index built from each customer's help-centre articles, inbound support tickets and a\nshared product wiki, and it has three tools: look up an order, issue a refund up to a limit, and email the\nend customer. It runs on a hosted LLM, uses an open-weight embedding model pulled from a public hub, keeps\nper-conversation memory and a semantic cache, and ships every prompt to a tracing vendor. Over a week,\nwrite an OWASP-structured threat model of the system, then build the controls the top rows call for:\ninjection and hidden-text handling at ingestion with per-source trust tiers; input and output rails with\nPydantic-validated tool arguments; moderation using Llama Guard with a custom category set, composed\nwith a topical rail in NeMo Guardrails or an equivalent pipeline; PII pseudonymisation before the\nprovider and irreversible redaction in traces; one server-derived tenant ID enforced on the vector store,\ncache and memory; refunds and outbound email executed with the end user's scopes and gated by human\napproval above a threshold; and a pinned, hashed, safe-format embedding model loaded without remote code.\nFinish with a red-team run that tries to break each control.","deliverables":["A threat model with one row per OWASP LLM item, each naming the affected component, the control, the test that proves it and the residual risk, plus a data-flow diagram showing every store and egress point.","Working code for the guardrail pipeline: ingestion sanitiser with provenance metadata, input and output rails, Pydantic schemas for every tool argument, Llama Guard moderation with per-rail thresholds, and a tool executor that attaches the requesting user's credential and an approval gate.","A tenant-isolation module with a single tenant-context wrapper used by every vector, cache and memory call, and a CI suite of cross-tenant canary tests across at least three tenant pairs.","A PII and leakage policy listing each boundary (provider, traces, index, cache, output, email tool) with the entity list and transformation applied, plus a recall measurement per entity on a labelled sample.","A model supply-chain record for the embedding model: source revision pinned by commit, file hashes, format and scan results, licence, and the registry entry production loads from.","A red-team report of at least 25 attacks (direct and indirect injection, poisoned wiki page, system-prompt extraction, cross-tenant probes, refund escalation, exfiltration through a rendered link or email) with outcomes and fixes."],"rubric":["A poisoned wiki page with a hidden instruction to email order data to an external address is either quarantined at ingestion or, if retrieved, results in no outbound email, and the block appears in the decision log with its source and trust tier.","Every cross-tenant canary test passes in CI, and grepping the codebase shows no vector, cache or memory call that bypasses the tenant-context wrapper.","Each rail reports block rate on an adversarial set and false-refusal rate on a benign set of at least 100 examples each, and its thresholds are justified from those numbers rather than defaults.","A refund above the threshold cannot execute without a recorded human approval, and a refund requested by an injected document fails at the resource layer because the call carries the end user's scopes, not a service account's.","Sampled traces contain typed placeholders instead of raw emails, phone numbers and order addresses, and measured redaction recall per entity is reported next to the target.","Production loads the embedding model only from the internal registry by hash, in a non-pickle format with remote code disabled, and swapping a tampered file makes the load fail."]}},"topics":[{"id":"input-output-guardrails","key":"safety-security/input-output-guardrails","module":"safety-security","name":"Input / output guardrails","taxonomy_name":"input / output guardrails","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/output-validation","safety-security/content-moderation","safety-security/pii-redaction","safety-security/llama-guard","safety-security/nemo-guardrails","customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails","evals/red-teaming"],"tldr":"Guardrails are deterministic checkpoints around a probabilistic model: input rails inspect what reaches\nit, output rails inspect what leaves it and allow, block, rewrite, retry or escalate, because a system\nprompt is an instruction, not an enforcement boundary.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"output-validation","key":"safety-security/output-validation","module":"safety-security","name":"Output validation (Pydantic, Instructor, Outlines)","taxonomy_name":"output validation (Pydantic, Instructor, Outlines)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/instructor","python-for-ai/outlines","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation"],"tldr":"Model output is a string until proven otherwise. Pydantic defines the contract, Instructor turns\nvalidation failures into retries with the error fed back, and Outlines constrains decoding. Shape can be\nguaranteed; meaning must be checked.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pii-redaction","key":"safety-security/pii-redaction","module":"safety-security","name":"PII redaction","taxonomy_name":"PII redaction","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","safety-security/data-leakage","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","observability/tracing","observability/logging","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval"],"tldr":"PII redaction replaces personal identifiers before text reaches a vendor model, log, trace, index or the\nwrong screen. LLM systems copy text everywhere, so it matters more; typed placeholders with a short-lived\nmapping keep the model useful.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"content-moderation","key":"safety-security/content-moderation","module":"safety-security","name":"Content moderation","taxonomy_name":"content moderation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["safety-security/llama-guard","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","prompting/jailbreaks","evals/red-teaming","ml-fundamentals/classification","customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails"],"tldr":"Content moderation scores text (and images) against a harm taxonomy and routes it: allow, block,\nsafe-complete or escalate, on input, output and retrieved content. It is classification with policy\nattached: thresholds and actions are product decisions.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-injection","key":"safety-security/prompt-injection","module":"safety-security","name":"Prompt injection (direct, indirect)","taxonomy_name":"prompt injection (direct, indirect)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["prompting/prompt-injection","prompting/jailbreaks","security-governance-compliance/prompt-injection-defense","safety-security/rag-poisoning","safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions"],"tldr":"Prompt injection is untrusted text treated as instructions, because a model has one input stream.\nDirect: the user attacks. Indirect: the payload hides in content the model reads. Defence is reduced\nprivilege, isolated content and controlled outputs.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"rag-poisoning","key":"safety-security/rag-poisoning","module":"safety-security","name":"RAG poisoning","taxonomy_name":"RAG poisoning","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["safety-security/prompt-injection","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding","data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","data-architecture/data-lineage","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis"],"tldr":"RAG makes your corpus part of the prompt, so whoever writes to the corpus writes to the prompt.\nPoisoning hides instructions in documents or plants false content built to be retrieved; defences live\nmostly on the write path and ingestion pipeline.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"excessive-agency","key":"safety-security/excessive-agency","module":"safety-security","name":"Excessive agency","taxonomy_name":"excessive agency","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agents/human-in-the-loop","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agents/sandboxing","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets"],"tldr":"Excessive agency is an LLM system that can do more than its task needs: too many functions, permissions\nor autonomy. The fix is a smaller blast radius: minimal tools, user-scoped credentials enforced outside\nthe model, and human checkpoints.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tool-privilege-escalation","key":"safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","module":"safety-security","name":"Tool privilege escalation","taxonomy_name":"tool privilege escalation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/prompt-injection","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agents/sandboxing","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","backend-fundamentals/rbac"],"tldr":"Tool privilege escalation is low-trust input (a user, a fetched page, a document) exercising a\nhigh-trust capability (a service account, a shell, a payment API) through the model. The fix: each tool\ncall carries only the requester's authority.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"cross-tenant-data-leakage","key":"safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage","module":"safety-security","name":"Cross-tenant data leakage","taxonomy_name":"cross-tenant data leakage","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["system-design/tenant-isolation","system-design/multi-tenancy","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","safety-security/data-leakage","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation"],"tldr":"Cross-tenant leakage is tenant A's data in tenant B's answer, because LLM pipelines copy data into stores\nrow-level security never sees. Fix: one server-derived tenant ID, a hard pre-filter in every store, proven\nby negative tests.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-leakage","key":"safety-security/data-leakage","module":"safety-security","name":"Data leakage","taxonomy_name":"data leakage","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["safety-security/pii-redaction","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage","safety-security/prompt-injection","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","observability/logging","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation"],"tldr":"Data leakage is any path where information crosses a boundary it should not: to a provider, another\nuser, model weights, a tool call or a rendered link. Minimise what enters, control what leaves, and assume\nno boundary exists inside the prompt.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","key":"safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","module":"safety-security","name":"Supply-chain security for models & datasets","taxonomy_name":"supply-chain security for models & datasets","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["python-for-ai/safetensors","python-for-ai/hugging-face","python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","llmops-platform/model-registry","safety-security/rag-poisoning","security-governance-compliance/model-governance","evals/dataset-contamination"],"tldr":"Hub models and datasets are dependencies that can execute code on load, hide backdoors in weights, and\ncarry poisoned data. Treat them as build artifacts: pinned, hashed, scanned, signed, quarantined and\nloaded with least capability, because nobody can review ten billion floats.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"owasp-top-10-for-llms","key":"safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms","module":"safety-security","name":"OWASP Top 10 for LLMs","taxonomy_name":"OWASP Top 10 for LLMs","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":15,"related":["safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","safety-security/rag-poisoning","safety-security/data-leakage","safety-security/output-validation","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","deployment/security-questionnaires"],"tldr":"The OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications names the most consequential LLM risk classes in a shared\nvocabulary. Its core framing: the model is an untrusted component that follows instructions in data, so\nevery classic boundary must be redrawn around the prompt.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"llama-guard","key":"safety-security/llama-guard","module":"safety-security","name":"Llama Guard","taxonomy_name":"Llama Guard","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["safety-security/content-moderation","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","safety-security/nemo-guardrails","safety-security/prompt-injection","python-for-ai/hugging-face","customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails"],"tldr":"Llama Guard is Meta's open-weight content-safety classifier: a fine-tuned Llama that reads a policy and a\nconversation and generates `safe` or `unsafe` plus category codes. The policy is editable prompt text;\nit does not detect injection, PII or hallucination.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"nemo-guardrails","key":"safety-security/nemo-guardrails","module":"safety-security","name":"NeMo Guardrails","taxonomy_name":"NeMo Guardrails","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["safety-security/input-output-guardrails","safety-security/llama-guard","safety-security/content-moderation","safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/pii-redaction","safety-security/output-validation","agents/state-machines","customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails"],"tldr":"NeMo Guardrails is NVIDIA's open-source toolkit that runs programmable \"rails\" around an LLM at five\npoints: input, dialogue, retrieval, tool execution and output. Rails are declared in Colang and YAML, and\nit orchestrates classifiers like Llama Guard rather than being one.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"production","stage_index":4,"track_order":16},{"id":"multimodal","tier":1,"order":11,"title":"Multimodal","intro":{"overview":"This module covers how AI systems take in and produce things other than plain text: images, scanned\npages and audio. It starts with vision-language models, the encoder-connector-LLM design that lets a\nlanguage model attend over visual tokens, then goes underneath to CLIP, the contrastive image-text\ntraining that produced the vision towers most VLMs reuse. Image embeddings generalise that idea into\nvectors you can index, search, cluster and deduplicate, and they make the point that the encoder you\nchoose decides what \"similar\" means. OCR and document understanding cover the enterprise workhorse:\nrecovering characters, positions and confidences from scans, then putting layout back into the model\nso that fields, tables and answers come out structured and verifiable. Speech-to-text and TTS close\nthe loop for voice, from Whisper-style spectrogram transcription to neural synthesis built for\ntime-to-first-audio. The topics connect through one recurring decision: convert the input to text\nearly (OCR, ASR, captions) and accept the loss, embed it for cheap comparison at scale, or hand the\nraw signal to a model that reasons over it directly. After the module you can pick the right\nmodality pipeline for a task, estimate its token and latency cost, measure it with the right metric\n(recall@k, CER/WER, per-field accuracy, RTF) and route what the system cannot verify to a human. It\nmatters most for AI Engineers building these pipelines and for FDEs who meet scanned documents and\ncall recordings in nearly every enterprise deployment.","why":{"engineer":"You will wire OCR, VLM calls, embedding search and ASR/TTS into products, and you need to know which stage is failing, what each image or second of audio costs in tokens and latency, and how to measure accuracy on your own data rather than trusting a benchmark.","architect":"You decide whether a workload converts to text early, embeds for search, or sends pixels and audio to a VLM, and that choice sets cost per request, latency budget, auditability and how much human review the system needs at volume.","fde":"Customer data arrives as skewed scans, image-only PDFs, phone-quality audio and screenshots; you must prove extraction accuracy on their own documents, explain confidence-based review routing to stakeholders, and pick the cheapest pipeline that meets their bar."},"mental_model":"Picture every multimodal system as a funnel from a raw signal (pixels or a waveform) to a decision,\nwith one question at each stage: how much information do I throw away, and when? Converting early,\nwith OCR, ASR or a caption, is cheap, gives you per-word confidence and plugs into text tooling, but\nit compresses before the question is known, so layout, tables, tone and visual detail can vanish.\nEmbedding (CLIP, image embeddings) keeps a fixed-length summary tuned for comparison: ideal for\nsearching or filtering millions of items, useless for a reasoned answer about one. Passing the raw\nsignal to a VLM keeps the most information and reasons jointly, but costs tokens per image, adds\nlatency and hallucinates with confidence. Real systems chain these: embed to narrow, OCR to ground,\nVLM to reason, validate to trust, and speech models at either end when the user talks and listens.\nAlways measure where the loss happened.","order":["clip","image-embeddings","vision-language-models","ocr","document-understanding","speech-to-text","tts"],"order_reasons":{"clip":"the contrastive image-text training that underpins both multimodal search and the vision tower of most VLMs.","image-embeddings":"generalises CLIP into vectors for search, dedup and clustering, and shows how encoder choice defines similarity.","vision-language-models":"builds on the vision encoder to explain how images become tokens an LLM reasons over, and what resolution costs.","ocr":"the convert-to-text-early path, with positions and confidences, that grounds LLMs on scanned material.","document-understanding":"combines OCR, layout and VLMs with validation and review routing into structured, auditable extraction.","speech-to-text":"moves to audio, with Whisper's spectrogram encoder-decoder, WER measurement and the streaming problem.","tts":"closes the voice loop with neural synthesis, text normalisation and the time-to-first-audio budget of voice agents."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a multimodal intake assistant for an insurance claims team. Claimants submit a mix of inputs: a\nscanned or photographed claim form, an invoice or repair estimate as an image-only PDF, a few photos\nof the damage, and a short voice note describing what happened. Your system OCRs and parses the form\nand invoice into a typed claim record with per-field confidence, transcribes the voice note with a\nWhisper-class model and extracts the incident date and location from it, embeds the damage photos\nwith a CLIP-style encoder to flag near-duplicates of photos already in the claims archive, and uses a\nVLM to check whether the photos are consistent with the described damage. Cross-field validation\n(invoice total equals line items, dates agree between form, invoice and voice note) decides whether a\nclaim goes straight through or to a reviewer queue with the reasons attached. Finally, a TTS\nread-back confirms the extracted summary to the claimant over the phone. Build it on a labelled set of\nat least 30 synthetic or public claim packets so every stage can be measured independently.","deliverables":["A runnable pipeline (API or CLI) that ingests a claim packet and returns a JSON claim record with field values, source page or audio span, per-field confidence and a straight-through or review decision with reasons.","A labelled evaluation set of 30+ packets with ground-truth fields, transcripts and known duplicate photos, plus an evaluation script that reports metrics per stage rather than only end to end.","A photo deduplication index built from image embeddings, with a tuned similarity threshold and a short note on why that encoder was chosen.","A TTS read-back of the claim summary with measured time-to-first-audio and a pronunciation check on names, amounts and dates.","A one-page design note covering where the pipeline converts to text versus sends raw pixels or audio, cost per packet in tokens and seconds, and the failure modes found during evaluation."],"rubric":["The evaluation reports CER on OCR, WER on the voice notes, per-field extraction accuracy, recall@k and precision at threshold for duplicate photos, and straight-through rate, each computed on the labelled set.","Every extracted field links back to its source region or audio timestamp, and low-confidence or validation-failing fields are routed to review instead of being silently accepted.","At least three real failure cases are traced to the stage that caused them (for example OCR misread versus LLM extraction error) with a fix or mitigation, and re-running the evaluation shows the change.","Cost and latency per packet are measured, including image tokens at the shipped resolution and ASR real-time factor, and the design note justifies the resolution and model choices against them.","The TTS read-back pronounces currency amounts, dates and claimant names correctly on the test set, verified by an ASR round-trip or a listed manual check."]}},"topics":[{"id":"vision-language-models","key":"multimodal/vision-language-models","module":"multimodal","name":"Vision-language models","taxonomy_name":"vision-language models","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["multimodal/clip","multimodal/image-embeddings","multimodal/ocr","multimodal/document-understanding","model-internals/transformer","model-internals/context-window","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","agents/computer-use"],"tldr":"A vision-language model (VLM) turns an image into \"visual tokens\" in the LLM's embedding space: an\nencoder makes patch features, a connector projects them, and the LLM attends over image and text\ntogether, so no detail is lost before the question is known.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"clip","key":"multimodal/clip","module":"multimodal","name":"CLIP","taxonomy_name":"CLIP","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["multimodal/image-embeddings","multimodal/vision-language-models","model-internals/embeddings","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","ml-fundamentals/supervised-vs-unsupervised-vs-self-supervised-learning","prompting/zero-shot"],"tldr":"CLIP trains image and text encoders with a contrastive loss so images and their captions land close in\none vector space. Free-form text labels let it classify and search images by description, zero-shot; its\nencoders became the standard VLM vision front end.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"image-embeddings","key":"multimodal/image-embeddings","module":"multimodal","name":"Image embeddings","taxonomy_name":"image embeddings","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["multimodal/clip","multimodal/vision-language-models","multimodal/document-understanding","model-internals/embeddings","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy"],"tldr":"An image embedding is a fixed-length vector from a neural encoder, placed so similar images sit close\nunder cosine similarity. It turns images into something a vector index can search, cluster and\ndeduplicate, and the encoder you pick decides what \"similar\" means.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"ocr","key":"multimodal/ocr","module":"multimodal","name":"OCR","taxonomy_name":"OCR","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["multimodal/document-understanding","multimodal/vision-language-models","rag-retrieval/document-parsing","data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","prompting/prompt-injection"],"tldr":"OCR turns pixels that depict text into strings with positions and confidences, so scans, photos and\nimage-only PDFs can feed search, extraction and LLMs. The hard parts are layout, image quality and\nknowing when the output is wrong.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"speech-to-text","key":"multimodal/speech-to-text","module":"multimodal","name":"Speech-to-text (Whisper)","taxonomy_name":"speech-to-text (Whisper)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["multimodal/tts","multimodal/vision-language-models","model-internals/transformer","model-internals/tokenization","serving-inference/streaming","fastapi-for-ai/websockets","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","safety-security/pii-redaction"],"tldr":"Speech-to-text (ASR) turns audio into a transcript. Whisper, an encoder-decoder transformer, reads a\n30-second log-Mel spectrogram and generates text; trained on roughly 680,000 hours of weakly labelled\naudio, it is robust across accents, noise and languages.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tts","key":"multimodal/tts","module":"multimodal","name":"TTS (text-to-speech)","taxonomy_name":"TTS","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["multimodal/speech-to-text","serving-inference/streaming","fastapi-for-ai/websockets","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","model-internals/tokenization"],"tldr":"Text-to-speech turns a string into a waveform: normalise the text, predict a compact acoustic\nrepresentation (mel-spectrogram or codec tokens) with prosody, then decode it to audio, fast enough that\na voice agent feels conversational.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"document-understanding","key":"multimodal/document-understanding","module":"multimodal","name":"Document understanding","taxonomy_name":"document understanding","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["multimodal/ocr","multimodal/vision-language-models","multimodal/image-embeddings","rag-retrieval/document-parsing","data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","evals/golden-dataset"],"tldr":"Document understanding is turning pages (invoices, contracts, forms, reports) into structured,\nverifiable information: classified, with fields extracted, tables recovered and questions answered.\nIt exists because text alone is not the document; meaning lives in layout, position and tables, and\nOCR-plus-regex pipelines break on every new template. The modern mechanism is a model that reads text,\nposition and pixels together, wrapped in a pipeline that validates and routes what it cannot verify.","format":"long","review":"reviewed","status":"ok"}],"stage":"llm-apps","stage_index":3,"track_order":10},{"id":"backend-fundamentals","tier":1,"order":12,"title":"Backend fundamentals","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the backend engineering underneath every AI product: the parts that decide whether\na model call becomes a dependable feature or a demo that falls over at the first retry, deploy or\ntenant. It opens with the languages and frameworks of the stack (Python and FastAPI at the core,\nNode.js with TypeScript at the product edge) and the HTTP contracts that join them. Next come API\ndesign (REST and GraphQL, versioning and backward compatibility, pagination, rate limits and quotas)\nfrom the provider's side, and integrating third-party APIs, SDKs and signed webhooks from the\nconsumer's side. The data layer follows: schema design, SQL and query plans, PostgreSQL transactions\nand isolation, and when NoSQL stores or Redis belong beside the relational system of record. Work that\ncannot finish inside a request moves to message queues and durable background workers, and both rely\non idempotency. Identity runs through authentication, OAuth 2.0 and OIDC, RBAC and secrets\nmanagement. The module ends with packaging and delivery: Docker images, Kubernetes, and CI/CD\npipelines that also gate prompts and models. Afterwards you can design, build, secure and ship a\nmulti-tenant AI service that survives retries, crashes, bursts and deploys, and you can defend each\nchoice in a design review or interview. It matters most for AI Engineers who own production services,\nand for FDEs who have to plug those services into a customer's identity provider, data and\ninfrastructure.","why":{"engineer":"You keep the request path fully async, wrap every model and vendor call in explicit timeouts, retries and idempotency keys, claim jobs with `SKIP LOCKED` and leases, and put an authorization check scoped to the tenant at the top of every mutating handler.","architect":"You choose the contract (REST with OpenAPI or GraphQL), the system of record versus caches and specialised stores, queue versus job table, the token and role model, and the deploy target, and you know which failure mode each choice prevents and what it costs to operate.","fde":"You connect the product to a customer's SSO through OIDC, map their identity-provider groups to roles, verify their webhooks, respect their API quotas, keep their credentials in a secrets manager, and ship into the Kubernetes cluster and CI rules they already run."},"mental_model":"Picture one request travelling through a set of trust and time boundaries. At the edge, the request\ncrosses a trust boundary: authentication turns a credential into a principal, authorization checks\nthat principal against this action on this resource, and signatures prove that inbound webhooks are\ngenuine. At the contract, the API bounds what crosses: a versioned schema, a bounded page, a rate\nlimit and a quota. At the core, the handler waits cheaply on a model, keeps the facts that must stay\ntrue in PostgreSQL behind constraints and short transactions, and keeps rebuildable fast state in\nRedis. Anything slower than the caller can wait for crosses a time boundary into a queue or a durable\njob, where at-least-once delivery makes idempotency mandatory. Around all of it, the platform\nboundary: secrets arrive through workload identity, code ships as one immutable image, Kubernetes\nreconciles desired state, and CI turns every change into evidence. Most backend bugs are a boundary\nthat someone assumed was not there.","order":["python","fastapi","node-js-typescript","rest-graphql-api-design","api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","api-and-sdk-integration","database-design","sql","postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","nosql","redis","message-queues","background-jobs-durable-workers","webhook-signature-verification-and-replay-protection","authentication-vs-authorization","oauth-2-0-oidc","rbac","secrets-management","docker","kubernetes","ci-cd"],"order_reasons":{"python":"the language of the AI core; asyncio and the GIL explain how every later service waits on models.","fastapi":"the HTTP layer built on that async model, where Pydantic contracts and dependency injection live.","node-js-typescript":"the product-edge runtime and where the boundary with Python sits, before designing the contract between them.","rest-graphql-api-design":"resources, status codes, idempotency and the long-running-job pattern that every later API topic refines.","api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility":"how a published contract changes without breaking clients you cannot redeploy.","pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas":"the three bounds every API needs, designed as a provider and handled as a consumer.","api-and-sdk-integration":"the consumer side of APIs: timeouts, retries, error taxonomy and idempotency around vendor and model SDKs.","database-design":"the schema, keys, constraints and indexes that outlive every version of the code.","sql":"set-based queries and reading `EXPLAIN` plans against the schema you just designed.","postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels":"what concurrent writers see, and how locks and SERIALIZABLE retries prevent lost updates.","nosql":"when access-pattern-first key design beats the relational default, judged against the SQL foundation.","redis":"the fast, shared, short-lived state beside PostgreSQL: caches, rate-limit counters, locks and sessions.","message-queues":"durable buffers between producers and consumers, at-least-once delivery and the choice of broker.","background-jobs-durable-workers":"job tables, leases and idempotent job bodies, built from transactions and queues.","webhook-signature-verification-and-replay-protection":"HMAC verification, replay windows and ack-then-queue, which depend on idempotency and workers.","authentication-vs-authorization":"the principal versus the permission check, and why 401 differs from 403.","oauth-2-0-oidc":"delegated access and federated login, building on the authentication model.","rbac":"roles and scoped permissions that bound what users and the agents acting for them may do.","secrets-management":"keeping API keys, signing keys and OAuth client secrets out of code, images, logs and prompts.","docker":"packaging the service as one immutable image, which is the unit the remaining topics deploy.","kubernetes":"reconciling those images across a fleet with probes, resource requests and graceful termination.","ci-cd":"build once, promote many: the pipeline that tests, scans, evaluates and ships everything above."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build and ship \"DocBrief\", a multi-tenant service that summarises uploaded documents with an LLM. Write\nthe core in FastAPI on PostgreSQL. Expose a REST API, versioned under `/v1` and described by OpenAPI.\n`POST /v1/documents` returns `202` with a job id and accepts an `Idempotency-Key` header, and\n`GET /v1/jobs/{id}` reports status. List endpoints use cursor pagination. Design the schema with\ntenant-scoped foreign keys and indexes justified by `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`. Summaries run in a durable\nworker that claims jobs with `SKIP LOCKED` and a lease, calls the model provider through an adapter\nwith explicit timeouts, bounded retries and a client-side token bucket, and writes results\nidempotently. Per-tenant rate limits and a monthly token quota live in Redis, and the authoritative\nusage ledger stays in PostgreSQL. A billing provider notifies plan changes by webhook: verify the HMAC\nover the raw body, reject stale timestamps, deduplicate by event id and process the event in the\nworker. Users log in through OIDC against a local identity provider (Keycloak or a dev tenant).\nTenant `admin`, `member` and `viewer` roles gate every endpoint, and each provider key comes from a\nsecrets manager, never from the image or the repository. Package the API and the worker as Docker\nimages, deploy them to a local Kubernetes cluster (kind or k3d) with probes and graceful shutdown, and\ndrive everything from a CI pipeline.","deliverables":["A repository with the FastAPI service, the worker, migrations and a schema diagram, plus an OpenAPI document that CI checks for breaking changes against the previous release.","A design note covering the job-state machine, the idempotency strategy for the API, worker and webhook, the isolation level or lock chosen for the quota decrement, and why Redis holds counters while PostgreSQL holds the ledger.","A test suite with integration tests for concurrent idempotent submits, a worker killed mid-job, a replayed webhook, a tampered signature, a cross-tenant read attempt, and a tenant pushed over its rate limit and quota.","Dockerfiles for the API and the worker, plus Kubernetes manifests (or a Helm chart) with readiness and liveness probes, resource requests, a PodDisruptionBudget and a termination grace period longer than the longest model call.","A CI workflow that lints, tests, builds one image per commit, scans it, runs the OpenAPI compatibility check and deploys that same image digest to the cluster, authenticating without a stored long-lived cloud key.","A secrets runbook showing where each secret lives, which workload identity can read it, and a timed end-to-end rotation of the model-provider key with no failed requests."],"rubric":["Submitting the same document 50 times concurrently with one `Idempotency-Key` creates exactly one job and one model call, and every response returns the same job id.","Killing a worker pod mid-summary leaves no job stuck: the lease expires, another worker finishes the job, and exactly one summary row exists for it.","A webhook with a modified body or a timestamp outside the window gets `401` and changes no state; replaying a valid event changes nothing, and logs show it as a duplicate.","A `viewer` attempting a write gets `403`, an unauthenticated call gets `401`, and a valid token for tenant A requesting tenant B's document gets `404` with no data in the body.","Sustained traffic above a tenant's limit returns `429` with `Retry-After` and rate-limit headers, other tenants' p99 latency stays flat, and the quota never goes negative under concurrent requests.","A rolling deploy during a load test drops zero requests, and `git grep` together with an image-layer scan finds no secret in the repository or any published image."]}},"topics":[{"id":"python","key":"backend-fundamentals/python","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Python","taxonomy_name":"Python","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":10,"related":["python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/gil","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","backend-fundamentals/fastapi","backend-fundamentals/node-js-typescript"],"tldr":"Python is the default AI-backend language because every model library and SDK is Python-first and the\nservice mostly waits on models. `asyncio` makes waiting cheap; the GIL only bites CPU-bound\npure-Python work. (Orientation; deep dive: `python-for-ai`.)","format":"crisp","note":"Python as the AI backend language; deep dive lives in the python-for-ai module","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"fastapi","key":"backend-fundamentals/fastapi","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"FastAPI","taxonomy_name":"FastAPI","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","backend-fundamentals/python","backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design"],"tldr":"FastAPI is the default framework for Python AI services: async-native ASGI, Pydantic as one contract\nfor validation and docs, first-class streaming and DI. Sharp edge: one blocking call in `async def`\nstalls the worker. (Deep dive: `fastapi-for-ai`.)","format":"crisp","note":"why FastAPI for AI services; deep dive lives in the fastapi-for-ai module","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"node-js-typescript","key":"backend-fundamentals/node-js-typescript","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Node.js / TypeScript","taxonomy_name":"Node.js / TypeScript","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","node-ts-for-ai/worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks","node-ts-for-ai/vercel-ai-sdk","node-ts-for-ai/mcp-typescript-sdk","backend-fundamentals/python","agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts"],"tldr":"Node.js with TypeScript runs the product edge of an AI system: UI streaming routes, BFFs, serverless\nfunctions, MCP servers. It proxies many streams cheaply but is a poor home for inference and evals, so\nPython owns the core. (Deep dive: `node-ts-for-ai`.)","format":"crisp","note":"where Node/TS fits in AI systems; deep dive lives in node-ts-for-ai","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"rest-graphql-api-design","key":"backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"REST / GraphQL API design","taxonomy_name":"REST / GraphQL API design","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","serving-inference/idempotency","serving-inference/streaming","agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts"],"tldr":"An API is a contract that outlives its code. REST uses resources and HTTP semantics every proxy\nunderstands; GraphQL lets clients query one typed graph for exact shapes. Use REST for services, models\nand tools; GraphQL when many client shapes read one rich domain.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"api-and-sdk-integration","key":"backend-fundamentals/api-and-sdk-integration","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"API & SDK integration","taxonomy_name":"API & SDK integration","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","python-for-ai/httpx","python-for-ai/tenacity","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/timeouts","reliability/circuit-breakers","node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers"],"tldr":"Most of an AI system is glue to APIs you do not control. Use the SDK, but own a thin\nintegration layer with explicit timeouts, one retry policy, idempotency, an error taxonomy and\nobservability, because SDK defaults suit the average caller, not your latency budget.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","key":"backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"API versioning & backward compatibility","taxonomy_name":"API versioning & backward compatibility","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","fastapi-for-ai/routers","python-for-ai/contract-testing","data-architecture/data-contracts","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","prompting/prompt-versioning","agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts"],"tldr":"Once others depend on your API, every change is a negotiation with clients you cannot redeploy. Make\nadditive changes and tolerant readers the default; version only for rare breaks, with announced\ndeprecation and sunset. The same applies to models, prompts and tool schemas.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","key":"backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Pagination, rate limits & API quotas","taxonomy_name":"pagination / rate limits / API quotas","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","backend-fundamentals/api-and-sdk-integration","backend-fundamentals/redis","backend-fundamentals/sql","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","serving-inference/retry-strategies","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation"],"tldr":"Every API needs three bounds at three time scales: pagination bounds one response, rate limits bound\nrequests or tokens per minute, quotas bound total spend per period. Providers design them, consumers\nhandle them; for LLM traffic, tokens per minute usually binds.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"webhook-signature-verification-and-replay-protection","key":"backend-fundamentals/webhook-signature-verification-and-replay-protection","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Webhook signature verification & replay protection","taxonomy_name":"webhook signature verification & replay protection","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/api-and-sdk-integration","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","enterprise-integration/webhooks","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations"],"tldr":"Anyone can POST to a webhook URL, so providers sign each delivery with an HMAC-SHA256 over a timestamp\nand the raw body. Verify in constant time, bound replays with a timestamp window and event-id dedupe,\nand keep the handler idempotent: providers retry.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"database-design","key":"backend-fundamentals/database-design","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Database design","taxonomy_name":"database design","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/sql","backend-fundamentals/nosql","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","fastapi-for-ai/sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","fastapi-for-ai/alembic-migrations","system-design/multi-tenancy","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","rag-retrieval/vector-databases"],"tldr":"The schema outlives every version of the code, so encode the facts that must stay true (keys,\nconstraints, ownership). Normalize so each fact lives once, denormalize only where a measured read needs\nit, and design indexes from real queries.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"sql","key":"backend-fundamentals/sql","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"SQL","taxonomy_name":"SQL","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/database-design","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","backend-fundamentals/nosql","fastapi-for-ai/sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","node-ts-for-ai/postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","observability/token-cost-tracking","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search"],"tldr":"SQL is declarative: you state which rows you want and a cost-based planner picks the algorithm. With the\nright index a query survives 1000× data growth; without it, it collapses. Think in sets, and read the plan\ninstead of guessing.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"nosql","key":"backend-fundamentals/nosql","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"NoSQL","taxonomy_name":"NoSQL","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/database-design","backend-fundamentals/sql","backend-fundamentals/redis","system-design/data-consistency","system-design/distributed-systems","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","rag-retrieval/vector-databases"],"tldr":"NoSQL stores give up general joins and cross-record transactions to partition data by key and serve it\nwith predictable latency at any size. You design around exact queries and accept weaker consistency on\nsome reads; choose one for a known access pattern at scale.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"redis","key":"backend-fundamentals/redis","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Redis","taxonomy_name":"Redis","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","node-ts-for-ai/redis","backend-fundamentals/nosql","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","serving-inference/caching"],"tldr":"Redis is an in-memory data-structure server whose commands run atomically on one thread. That makes it\nthe default home for shared, short-lived state (caches, rate limits, locks, sessions, queues), at the cost\nof memory-sized data and weaker durability than a database.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","key":"backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"PostgreSQL transactions & isolation levels","taxonomy_name":"PostgreSQL transactions & isolation levels","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["backend-fundamentals/database-design","backend-fundamentals/sql","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","node-ts-for-ai/postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","system-design/data-consistency","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions"],"tldr":"A transaction makes statements atomic and durable; the isolation level decides what concurrent\ntransactions see. PostgreSQL's MVCC never blocks readers, but default READ COMMITTED permits lost updates\nand write skew; fix them with atomic updates, row locks, or SERIALIZABLE plus retries.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"message-queues","key":"backend-fundamentals/message-queues","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Message queues (Kafka, SQS, RabbitMQ)","taxonomy_name":"message queues (Kafka, SQS, RabbitMQ)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","backend-fundamentals/redis","system-design/event-driven-architecture","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory"],"tldr":"A message queue is a durable buffer between producers and consumers, so they need not be up or scaled\ntogether. Delivery is at-least-once, so consumers must be idempotent. Kafka is a replayable log, SQS a\nmanaged queue, RabbitMQ a routing broker.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"background-jobs-durable-workers","key":"backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Background jobs / durable workers","taxonomy_name":"background jobs / durable workers","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["backend-fundamentals/message-queues","backend-fundamentals/redis","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing"],"tldr":"A durable worker keeps each background job's state in a store that outlives both the enqueuing and the\nrunning process, so deploys and crashes never lose work. Its lease-and-acknowledge protocol means a job\ncan run twice, so handlers must be idempotent.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"authentication-vs-authorization","key":"backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Authentication vs authorization","taxonomy_name":"authentication vs authorization","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":14,"related":["backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/rbac","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval"],"tldr":"Authentication verifies *who* is calling; authorization decides whether that principal may do *this\naction* on *this resource*. 401 means \"I do not know who you are\", 403 means \"I know, and no\". Most\naccess-control bugs assume the first implies the second.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"oauth-2-0-oidc","key":"backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"OAuth 2.0 / OIDC","taxonomy_name":"OAuth 2.0 / OIDC","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","backend-fundamentals/rbac","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","enterprise-integration/saml-sso","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security"],"tldr":"OAuth 2.0 gives an app a scoped, expiring access token to call an API for a user without seeing their\npassword. OpenID Connect (OIDC) adds a signed ID token saying *who* logged in. Most login bugs come from\nconfusing these tokens.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"rbac","key":"backend-fundamentals/rbac","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"RBAC","taxonomy_name":"RBAC","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","enterprise-integration/scim-provisioning","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries"],"tldr":"Role-based access control attaches permissions to roles and assigns callers roles within a scope; a\nrequest is allowed only if some role grants that permission there. Direct per-user grants do not scale,\nand the same check must bound agents.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"secrets-management","key":"backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Secrets management","taxonomy_name":"secrets management","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","backend-fundamentals/docker","deployment/terraform","python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","safety-security/prompt-injection"],"tldr":"A secret is any value that lets someone act as your system. Secrets management keeps secrets out of code,\nimages, logs and prompts, stores them encrypted, delivers them via platform identity, and keeps them\nshort-lived and rotatable, so leaks stay small.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"docker","key":"backend-fundamentals/docker","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Docker","taxonomy_name":"Docker","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment","python-for-ai/lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure"],"tldr":"Docker packages an app with its exact filesystem (OS libraries, interpreter, CUDA runtime, code) into an\nimmutable, content-addressed image and runs it as an isolated process on the host kernel. What passed CI\nis byte-for-byte what runs in production.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"kubernetes","key":"backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"Kubernetes","taxonomy_name":"Kubernetes","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["backend-fundamentals/docker","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","fastapi-for-ai/health-readiness-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","infra-capacity-economics/ray-kuberay","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure"],"tldr":"Kubernetes runs containers across many machines by reconciling declared desired state (\"three replicas,\none GPU each, ready when `/ready` returns 200\") with reality. Its requests, probes and graceful termination\nmake GPU-bound, slow-starting, streaming AI services deployable without dropped requests.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"ci-cd","key":"backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","module":"backend-fundamentals","name":"CI/CD","taxonomy_name":"CI/CD","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/docker","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/rollback","node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment"],"tldr":"CI merges and verifies every change on a clean machine; CD turns each verified build into one immutable\nartifact with an automated path to production. Small, routine releases make failure cheap to reverse. For\nAI systems it also gates prompts and models with evaluations.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"foundations","stage_index":1,"track_order":2},{"id":"python-for-ai","tier":1,"order":13,"title":"Python for AI","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the Python you need to build AI systems that hold up once they leave the notebook.\nIt starts with the language itself. The object model and mutability explain the aliasing bugs that\nleak state between requests. Type hints, `TypedDict`, `Literal`, Protocols and generics give names to\nthe shapes moving through your code. Dataclasses handle trusted internal records and Pydantic\nvalidates untrusted JSON. Exceptions, generators, decorators and context managers are the idioms\nevery later library depends on. Next comes the project around the code: uv, lockfiles, a `src/`\nlayout, configuration from `.env` files and pydantic-settings, and Ruff and mypy as gates. Concurrency\nfollows, because AI services mostly wait: async/await and asyncio, cancellation, locks and semaphores,\nthe GIL, and how to choose between asyncio, threads and processes. The integration layer is httpx,\ntenacity, structured logging, dependency injection, the OpenAI and Anthropic SDKs, tiktoken, LiteLLM,\nInstructor and the MCP SDK. Testing goes from pytest and test doubles up to property-based, contract\nand integration tests. The data and model stack is Jupyter, NumPy, pandas, pyarrow, Polars,\nscikit-learn, PyTorch, Hugging Face, safetensors, sentence-transformers and Outlines. Profiling and\nsecurity scanning close the module. Afterwards you can write a typed, tested, reproducible Python\nservice or pipeline that calls models concurrently and safely, handles their data efficiently and\nfails in ways you can diagnose. The module matters most for Engineers, who write this code every\nday. FDEs rely on it too, because they have to make the same code install, configure and run inside a\ncustomer's environment.","why":{"engineer":"You write the agent loops, SDK wrappers, async fan-outs, Pydantic schemas, data pipelines and test suites. A mutable default shared across tenants, a blocking call on the event loop, a swallowed `CancelledError` or retries on a non-retryable error are all bugs that end up in your code.","architect":"You decide where the trust boundaries are and which ones get Pydantic validation, which vendor SDKs sit behind small Protocols, how the import graph is layered and what concurrency model each workload uses. Those choices decide whether the provider can be swapped, whether the core runs in tests without credentials, and where the system's throughput limit sits.","fde":"Customer environments have proxies, pinned Python versions, air-gapped package mirrors, their own secrets stores and their own models. You are the one who gets a locked, audited environment to install, injects settings without code changes, points the SDK at their gateway through httpx, and profiles the job that runs out of memory on their hardware."},"mental_model":"Think of an AI program as a typed core surrounded by a membrane of boundaries, running on an event\nloop, with a data plane next to it. The core is plain Python: frozen dataclasses, Protocols and pure\nfunctions, which you can test without a network, a key or a GPU. The membrane is where untrusted or\nslow things come in. Pydantic validates every JSON payload, model output and setting that crosses\nit. Small adapters wrap the OpenAI, Anthropic, MCP and httpx clients, and the adapters translate\nvendor failures into your own exception hierarchy, with tenacity retrying only what is safe to\nrepeat. The event loop runs the I/O: it may wait as often as it likes but must never block, and\ncancellation, semaphores and context managers keep its resources bounded and released. CPU-heavy\nwork leaves the loop for threads, processes or vectorised libraries. The data plane moves tables and\ntensors through NumPy, Arrow, Parquet, Polars and PyTorch without row-by-row Python. Tooling holds it\nall in place: lockfiles fix the environment, type checkers and Ruff fix the shapes, tests pin the\nbehaviour at each layer, and logs and profiles show what actually happened.","order":["object-model-and-mutability","type-hints","typeddict-literal-enum","protocols-generics-structural-typing","dataclasses","pydantic","exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","generators","decorators","context-managers-resource-cleanup","json-jsonl","uv-poetry-pip","lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","package-structure-and-import-design","python-dotenv","pydantic-settings","ruff","mypy","async-await","asyncio","async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","gil","asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","httpx","tenacity","logging-structlog","dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","openai-sdk","anthropic-sdk","tiktoken","litellm","instructor","mcp-python-sdk","pytest","test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","pytest-asyncio","property-based-testing","contract-testing","integration-and-end-to-end-testing","jupyter","numpy","pandas","pyarrow","polars","scikit-learn-basics","pytorch","hugging-face","safetensors","sentence-transformers","outlines","memory-and-performance-profiling","security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing"],"order_reasons":{"object-model-and-mutability":"names, aliasing and mutation underlie every later bug about shared state, defaults and caches.","type-hints":"the annotation syntax that checkers, dataclasses, Pydantic and FastAPI all read.","typeddict-literal-enum":"types the wire-format dicts and closed vocabularies (roles, finish reasons) that SDKs pass around.","protocols-generics-structural-typing":"lets the consumer define small interfaces for LLM clients, embedders and stores without inheritance.","dataclasses":"the default record type for trusted internal values, built on the hints just learned.","pydantic":"adds runtime validation and JSON Schema for the untrusted data that dataclasses deliberately do not check.","exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies":"organises failures by handling policy before any remote call is written.","generators":"lazy pipelines and token streams, which context managers and streaming SDKs build on.","decorators":"the mechanism behind retries, tracing, tool registration and routes in later lessons.","context-managers-resource-cleanup":"guaranteed release of clients, streams, sessions and locks, the base for `async with`.","json-jsonl":"the formats of API payloads, eval sets and fine-tuning data, validated with Pydantic line by line.","uv-poetry-pip":"sets up projects and environments before the code grows dependencies.","lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning":"makes that environment reproducible across laptops, CI and containers.","package-structure-and-import-design":"a src layout and one-way imports turn the code into a testable, layered package.","python-dotenv":"local configuration through environment variables, and where its limits are.","pydantic-settings":"typed, validated configuration that fails at startup instead of mid-request.","ruff":"a fast lint and format gate from the first commit, including async and security rules.","mypy":"makes the type hints enforceable in CI, now that the code has real types to check.","async-await":"coroutines and the never-block rule, the base for every async lesson that follows.","asyncio":"the event loop, tasks, gather and queues that run concurrent model and tool calls.","async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation":"how timeouts, TaskGroups and disconnects actually stop work and release resources.","race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives":"locks for check-then-act and semaphores to cap concurrent provider calls.","gil":"explains why threads do not speed up pure-Python CPU work, which the next lesson relies on.","asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing":"picks the concurrency model by what the work waits on.","httpx":"the async HTTP client under the provider SDKs, whose timeouts and pools become your SDK's behaviour.","tenacity":"declarative retries with backoff and error classification for those remote calls.","logging-structlog":"structured events with request, model and token fields, so retries and calls can be queried.","dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries":"wraps slow, paid dependencies behind Protocols before the SDK lessons start.","openai-sdk":"the de facto request shape, used directly and through `base_url` for compatible servers.","anthropic-sdk":"content blocks, tool-result turns and prompt caching, which differ from OpenAI's shape.","tiktoken":"local token counting for context budgets, chunking and cost estimates on those calls.","litellm":"one interface across providers, once you know what it translates and what it hides.","instructor":"validated Pydantic objects from hosted models with re-prompting when validation fails.","mcp-python-sdk":"exposes typed Python functions as tools to any MCP host, combining decorators, Pydantic and asyncio.","pytest":"fixtures, parametrization and assertions as the base of every test lesson.","test-doubles-mocks-fixtures":"fakes and stubs for model APIs and clocks, enabled by the interface boundaries.","pytest-asyncio":"tests concurrency caps, timeouts and cancellation on a managed event loop.","property-based-testing":"Hypothesis finds the edge cases in chunkers, parsers and serialisers.","contract-testing":"keeps doubles and services in agreement on interfaces without full environments.","integration-and-end-to-end-testing":"checks the assumptions behind the doubles against real databases and HTTP stacks.","jupyter":"the exploration environment for the data lessons, and its reproducibility pitfalls.","numpy":"arrays, shapes, dtypes and broadcasting, which every later data and model library builds on.","pandas":"labelled tables for eval results and logs that fit in memory.","pyarrow":"the columnar format and Parquet I/O shared by pandas, Polars, DuckDB and datasets.","polars":"lazy, multi-core DataFrames on Arrow for gigabyte-scale or scheduled pipelines.","scikit-learn-basics":"baselines, pipelines and honest metrics that any LLM solution must beat.","pytorch":"tensors, autograd and devices, the base of the open-model stack.","hugging-face":"loading, tokenising, fine-tuning and evaluating open models on top of PyTorch.","safetensors":"the safe weight format those models should be stored and exchanged in.","sentence-transformers":"embeddings and cross-encoder reranking from open encoders for retrieval.","outlines":"constrained decoding for self-hosted models, contrasted with Instructor's retry approach.","memory-and-performance-profiling":"measures CPU, wall time and native memory across everything built so far.","security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing":"pip-audit and Bandit on the locked environment and code as the final CI gate."},"capstone":{"brief":"A customer wants to triage tens of thousands of support tickets exported as JSONL into categories,\nurgency levels and extracted fields, and wants proof that it beats a cheap baseline. Build it as an\ninstallable Python package with a `src/` layout, managed and locked with uv. Validate every ticket\nline with Pydantic and report rejected lines by line number. Configuration (provider, model, concurrency,\nbudget) comes from pydantic-settings, with a committed `.env.example`. The core classifier depends on a\nsmall `LLMClient` Protocol. Implement it twice, with the OpenAI SDK and the Anthropic SDK, each built on\na shared httpx client with explicit timeouts. Get typed outputs through Instructor, or through native\nstructured output. Run calls concurrently on asyncio with a semaphore cap, tenacity retries that\napply only to transient errors, a custom exception hierarchy, and a per-run token budget estimated\nwith tiktoken. Cancelling the run must release every connection. Log one structlog event per call\nwith model, tokens, latency and retry count. As a baseline, embed tickets with sentence-transformers\nand train a scikit-learn classifier. Write results to Parquet and compare both approaches with Polars.\nShip it with tests and CI that a reviewer can run.","deliverables":["A repository with `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock`, a `src/` package and a CLI entry point that runs the full pipeline from a JSONL file to a Parquet results file.","Two provider adapters behind one Protocol, a Pydantic output schema, an exception hierarchy separating retryable, input-too-large and fatal errors, and a settings class that rejects invalid configuration at startup.","A test suite with a fake LLM client, pytest-asyncio tests for the concurrency cap, timeout and cancellation, Hypothesis properties for the JSONL parser and token-based chunker, and one integration test against a local fake HTTP server.","A CI workflow running `uv sync --locked`, Ruff, mypy in strict mode on the package, pytest, pip-audit and Bandit.","A short report, as a notebook that passes Restart and Run All plus a Markdown summary, comparing LLM and embedding-baseline accuracy, macro F1, cost and wall time on a held-out labelled set, with a profile of the slowest stage."],"rubric":["Running the test suite with network access disabled and no API keys passes, and switching providers is a one-line settings change with no edits to the core.","With concurrency set to 8, logs never show more than 8 calls in flight, a 429 is retried with backoff, and an invalid-key error fails once without any retry.","Pressing Ctrl+C mid-run exits within two seconds, leaves no open connections or half-written output file, and logs how many tickets were completed.","A malformed JSONL line is reported with its line number and skipped, and mypy strict and Ruff report zero errors without blanket `type: ignore` or `noqa` comments.","A fresh clone on another machine reproduces the same environment from the lockfile, and pip-audit reports no unaddressed known vulnerabilities.","The report states whether the LLM beats the scikit-learn baseline on macro F1 and cost per thousand tickets, using the same held-out split for both."]}},"topics":[{"id":"type-hints","key":"python-for-ai/type-hints","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Type hints","taxonomy_name":"type hints","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/mypy","python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/dataclasses","agents/typed-tool-contracts","node-ts-for-ai/typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types"],"tldr":"Type hints describe what a value should be. The interpreter ignores them; mypy, pyright, Pydantic and\nFastAPI read them. AI code is glue between untyped JSON and SDKs, so catching a shape mismatch before\nthe run beats a failed thirty-minute batch job.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"protocols-generics-structural-typing","key":"python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Protocols, generics and structural typing","taxonomy_name":"Protocols / generics / structural typing","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","python-for-ai/decorators","python-for-ai/mypy","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces","node-ts-for-ai/typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types"],"tldr":"A Protocol says \"anything with these methods is acceptable\", so checkers verify duck typing without\ninheritance. Generics track element types, so `first(xs: list[Message])` returns a `Message`. Together\nthey type swappable AI parts: embedders, LLM clients, vector stores, retry decorators.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"typeddict-literal-enum","key":"python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","module":"python-for-ai","name":"TypedDict, Literal and Enum","taxonomy_name":"TypedDict / Literal / Enum","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/dataclasses","python-for-ai/json-jsonl","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","agents/state-machines"],"tldr":"`TypedDict` types a plain dict with fixed keys; `Literal` limits a value to a few constants at zero\nruntime cost; `Enum` makes such a set real objects with methods. Together they guard AI wire dicts and\nvocabularies (roles, finish reasons, tool names).","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pydantic","key":"python-for-ai/pydantic","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Pydantic","taxonomy_name":"Pydantic","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/dataclasses","python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","python-for-ai/pydantic-settings","python-for-ai/instructor","fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation"],"tldr":"Pydantic turns annotated `BaseModel` classes into validators, serializers and JSON Schema. Untrusted\ninput (API payloads, LLM output, configs) becomes typed objects with structured errors, and the same\nclass emits the schema you give a model, because type hints alone do nothing at runtime.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"dataclasses","key":"python-for-ai/dataclasses","module":"python-for-ai","name":"dataclasses","taxonomy_name":"dataclasses","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/object-model-and-mutability","python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores"],"tldr":"`@dataclass` generates `__init__`, `__repr__`, `__eq__` and optionally ordering, hashing and `__slots__`\nfrom annotated fields, so a record is five lines, not thirty. It suits AI records (configs, agent steps,\nchunks) but validates nothing; that is Pydantic's job.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"object-model-and-mutability","key":"python-for-ai/object-model-and-mutability","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Object model and mutability","taxonomy_name":"object model & mutability","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/dataclasses","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/pytorch","python-for-ai/decorators","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution"],"tldr":"A Python variable is a name bound to an object; assignment never copies. Most \"the value changed by\nitself\" bugs in AI code are aliasing: names, defaults, class attributes or slices sharing one mutable\nobject, which can leak data across tenants.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","key":"python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Exceptions and custom exception hierarchies","taxonomy_name":"exceptions & custom exception hierarchies","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/tenacity","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/logging-structlog","python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","serving-inference/retry-strategies","fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","system-design/model-fallback-strategies"],"tldr":"Exceptions carry a failure up the stack to code that can handle it. A custom hierarchy groups failures\nby *handling policy* (retry, shrink, re-prompt, give up), so rate limits differ from bad keys and your\nbugs never retry at provider prices.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"async-await","key":"python-for-ai/async-await","module":"python-for-ai","name":"async / await","taxonomy_name":"async / await","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/gil","python-for-ai/httpx","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","serving-inference/streaming"],"tldr":"`async def` defines a coroutine that pauses at `await` while waiting on I/O, letting an event loop run\nother coroutines on one thread. AI work is mostly waiting on networks, so this beats a thread per call;\nit does nothing for CPU-bound work.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"asyncio","key":"python-for-ai/asyncio","module":"python-for-ai","name":"asyncio","taxonomy_name":"asyncio","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/httpx","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue"],"tldr":"asyncio runs coroutines on a single-threaded event loop that resumes each one only when what it awaited\nis ready. An AI service, which mostly waits on LLMs, vector stores and tools, gets thousands of\nconcurrent waits cheaply, provided nothing on the loop ever blocks.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","key":"python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Async cancellation & exception propagation","taxonomy_name":"async cancellation & exception propagation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","serving-inference/cancellation","serving-inference/timeouts","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","agents/parallel-tool-calls"],"tldr":"Cancelling an asyncio task schedules a `CancelledError` at its next `await`, so `finally` blocks can\nrelease connections. `TaskGroup` and `asyncio.timeout` build on this one mechanism, so swallowing\n`CancelledError` silently breaks deadlines, sibling cleanup and shutdown.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","key":"python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Race conditions & synchronization primitives (asyncio.Lock, Semaphore)","taxonomy_name":"race conditions & synchronization primitives (asyncio.Lock, Semaphore)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/gil","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/rate-limiting","serving-inference/backpressure","agents/parallel-tool-calls"],"tldr":"Single-threaded asyncio still races whenever an `await` separates reading shared state from writing it.\n`asyncio.Lock` makes check-then-act exclusive; `asyncio.Semaphore` caps concurrent calls to a provider.\nNeither is thread-safe, and a semaphore limits concurrency, not requests per second.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","key":"python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","module":"python-for-ai","name":"asyncio vs threads vs multiprocessing","taxonomy_name":"asyncio vs threads vs multiprocessing","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/gil","python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines"],"tldr":"Pick by what the work waits on: asyncio for many network waits, threads for blocking libraries and\nGIL-releasing C code, processes for CPU-bound pure Python. AI pipelines mix all three: async core,\n`asyncio.to_thread` for blocking SDKs, a process pool for parsing.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"gil","key":"python-for-ai/gil","module":"python-for-ai","name":"GIL (Global Interpreter Lock)","taxonomy_name":"GIL","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/pytorch","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","node-ts-for-ai/worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks"],"tldr":"The GIL is a CPython mutex letting one thread run bytecode at a time, protecting reference counts and\nC extensions. Threads still help with I/O and GIL-releasing native code, not pure-Python CPU work. It\ndoes not make your code thread-safe.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"generators","key":"python-for-ai/generators","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Generators","taxonomy_name":"generators","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","python-for-ai/json-jsonl","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","serving-inference/streaming","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines"],"tldr":"A generator `yield`s values one at a time, pausing with its state intact. A 10 GB JSONL corpus or an\nendless token stream becomes a lazy pipeline holding one item or batch at a time; async generators do\nthe same for LLM token streams.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"decorators","key":"python-for-ai/decorators","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Decorators","taxonomy_name":"decorators","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/tenacity","python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","python-for-ai/logging-structlog","python-for-ai/mcp-python-sdk","agents/tool-calling","observability/tracing","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection"],"tldr":"A decorator takes a function and returns a replacement, applied with `@name`. Retries, tracing, caching\nand tool or route registration are written once and attached declaratively. Watch three edges: missing\n`functools.wraps`, sync wrappers on async functions, and stacking order.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"context-managers-resource-cleanup","key":"python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Context managers / resource cleanup","taxonomy_name":"context managers / resource cleanup","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/generators","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/decorators","python-for-ai/httpx","python-for-ai/pytorch","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining"],"tldr":"A context manager pairs setup with guaranteed teardown: `with` runs `__enter__`, your block, then\n`__exit__` whether the block returns, raises or is cancelled, so AI-service resources stop leaking on\nerror paths. `async with` and `contextlib` extend it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","key":"python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Dependency injection & interface boundaries","taxonomy_name":"dependency injection & interface boundaries","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/package-structure-and-import-design","python-for-ai/pydantic-settings","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","node-ts-for-ai/dependency-injection","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces"],"tldr":"A component receives its dependencies (LLM client, vector store, clock) from outside, typed as a small\n`Protocol` in your own vocabulary. AI code becomes testable without network calls and swappable across\nproviders, for the price of one thin layer of indirection.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"package-structure-and-import-design","key":"python-for-ai/package-structure-and-import-design","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Package structure & import design","taxonomy_name":"package structure & import design","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/ruff","python-for-ai/mypy","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces"],"tldr":"In Python the import graph is your architecture: it decides what tests in isolation and what runs at\nstartup. Use a `src/` layout, point imports one way (domain ← services ← adapters ← entrypoints), and keep\nheavy SDKs and import-time side effects at the edges.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tenacity","key":"python-for-ai/tenacity","module":"python-for-ai","name":"tenacity","taxonomy_name":"tenacity","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["serving-inference/retry-strategies","reliability/circuit-breakers","serving-inference/idempotency","serving-inference/timeouts","python-for-ai/decorators","python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","python-for-ai/httpx","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution"],"tldr":"tenacity declares a retry policy: when to stop, how long to wait, what to retry, what to log. LLM APIs\nfail transiently (429s, 5xx, timeouts) and hand-written loops get backoff and jitter wrong; it cannot make\nan unsafe operation safe to repeat.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"logging-structlog","key":"python-for-ai/logging-structlog","module":"python-for-ai","name":"logging / structlog","taxonomy_name":"logging / structlog","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["observability/logging","fastapi-for-ai/structured-logging","observability/tracing","observability/opentelemetry","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","safety-security/pii-redaction","observability/token-cost-tracking","python-for-ai/async-await"],"tldr":"Stdlib `logging` routes records through levels, handlers and formatters; structlog makes every line a\nstructured event rendered as JSON. Incident questions (\"which model, how many tokens, which request, which\nretry?\") become queries over fields, not greps over prose.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"memory-and-performance-profiling","key":"python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Memory & performance profiling","taxonomy_name":"memory & performance profiling","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["python-for-ai/gil","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/generators","python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/pytorch","observability/latency-percentiles","observability/tracing","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization"],"tldr":"Profiling replaces guessing: CPU profilers show which functions consume time, memory profilers which lines\nallocated the bytes still alive. AI services often wait on network or GPU and hold native buffers Python\ncannot see, so pick the tool by the question.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"uv-poetry-pip","key":"python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","module":"python-for-ai","name":"uv / poetry / pip","taxonomy_name":"uv / poetry / pip","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","python-for-ai/package-structure-and-import-design","python-for-ai/pytorch","backend-fundamentals/docker","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd"],"tldr":"pip installs packages; Poetry and uv manage projects: `pyproject.toml` dependencies, a lockfile,\nenvironments and builds. Reproducibility is the point, because \"works on my machine\" is usually two\nenvironments resolving different versions; uv replaces pip, pip-tools, virtualenv, pyenv and pipx, much faster.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","key":"python-for-ai/lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Lockfiles & dependency pinning","taxonomy_name":"lockfiles & dependency pinning","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","backend-fundamentals/docker","python-for-ai/openai-sdk"],"tldr":"Declare what you *need* as ranges, record what you *got* as exact versions with hashes, and install only\nfrom that record. Every developer, CI run and container then installs the same bytes; applications must\nlock, libraries publish ranges.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","key":"python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Security scanning & dependency auditing (pip-audit, Bandit)","taxonomy_name":"security scanning & dependency auditing (pip-audit, Bandit)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","python-for-ai/ruff","python-for-ai/safetensors","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd"],"tldr":"**pip-audit** checks your exact installed versions against vulnerability databases; **Bandit** scans your\nown code for risky patterns like `pickle.load`, `eval`, `shell=True` and hard-coded secrets. Both run in\nseconds in CI; neither proves code safe, they free reviewers for design-level risks.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pytest","key":"python-for-ai/pytest","module":"python-for-ai","name":"pytest","taxonomy_name":"pytest","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/pytest-asyncio","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/property-based-testing","python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","python-for-ai/ruff","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","evals/regression-tests"],"tldr":"pytest is the de facto Python test runner: informative plain `assert`, fixtures injected by argument\nname, parametrized case tables and a plugin hook system. It makes the cheap way to write tests the\ncorrect one.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pytest-asyncio","key":"python-for-ai/pytest-asyncio","module":"python-for-ai","name":"pytest-asyncio","taxonomy_name":"pytest-asyncio","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","fastapi-for-ai/testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio"],"tldr":"pytest-asyncio runs `async def` tests and async fixtures on an event loop it owns; markers and config\nchoose which tests are async and how long each loop lives. Most sharp edges come from loop lifetime.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","key":"python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Test doubles, mocks and fixtures","taxonomy_name":"test doubles / mocks / fixtures","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/pytest-asyncio","python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","python-for-ai/contract-testing","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility"],"tldr":"A test double (dummy, stub, spy, mock or fake) stands in for an LLM, API or database so tests are fast,\ndeterministic and free; fixtures build and tear them down. Pick the kind by what you verify: result\nor interaction.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"property-based-testing","key":"python-for-ai/property-based-testing","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Property-based testing (Hypothesis)","taxonomy_name":"property-based testing (Hypothesis)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/json-jsonl","python-for-ai/contract-testing","evals/property-based-testing","evals/metamorphic-testing"],"tldr":"You state a property that must hold for every input; Hypothesis generates hundreds of inputs, biased\ntoward edge cases, and shrinks any failure to a minimal counterexample. Parsing, chunking and\nserialisation bugs hide exactly there.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"contract-testing","key":"python-for-ai/contract-testing","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Contract testing","taxonomy_name":"contract testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","python-for-ai/pydantic","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts","agents/typed-tool-contracts","data-architecture/data-contracts"],"tldr":"A contract test checks that two components agree on their interface (request shapes, response fields,\nstatus codes, error formats) without running both at once: the consumer records what it relies on, and\nthe provider verifies it in its own build.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"integration-and-end-to-end-testing","key":"python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Integration and end-to-end testing","taxonomy_name":"integration & end-to-end testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/contract-testing","fastapi-for-ai/integration-tests-with-real-dependencies","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","evals/regression-tests","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments"],"tldr":"Integration tests run your code against real dependencies (Postgres with pgvector, Redis, a real HTTP\nstack); end-to-end tests drive the deployed system through its public interface. Keep fewer of each going\nup, because some failures exist only there.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"mypy","key":"python-for-ai/mypy","module":"python-for-ai","name":"mypy","taxonomy_name":"mypy","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/ruff","agents/typed-tool-contracts","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd"],"tldr":"mypy is a static type checker: it reads your hints, infers the rest, and reports code that would pass the\nwrong type, read a missing key or call a method on `None`, without running anything. It is gradual, so\nunannotated code stays mostly unchecked.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"ruff","key":"python-for-ai/ruff","module":"python-for-ai","name":"ruff","taxonomy_name":"ruff","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["python-for-ai/mypy","python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","python-for-ai/asyncio","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd"],"tldr":"Ruff is a Rust-written Python linter and formatter that replaces flake8 and its plugins, isort,\npyupgrade, most of bandit, part of pylint, and Black with one fast binary and one config. It checks style\nand likely bugs, not types.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"python-dotenv","key":"python-for-ai/python-dotenv","module":"python-for-ai","name":"python-dotenv","taxonomy_name":"python-dotenv","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":9,"related":["python-for-ai/pydantic-settings","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","backend-fundamentals/docker","enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments","python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing"],"tldr":"python-dotenv copies `KEY=value` lines from a gitignored `.env` file into `os.environ`, so code reads\nenvironment variables everywhere. It is a dev convenience, not a secrets manager (no typing, validation\nor encryption), and by default never overrides variables already set.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"pydantic-settings","key":"python-for-ai/pydantic-settings","module":"python-for-ai","name":"pydantic-settings","taxonomy_name":"pydantic-settings","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/python-dotenv","python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags"],"tldr":"pydantic-settings is a Pydantic model filled from env vars, `.env` files and secret files. Declare\nconfig once with types, defaults and constraints; the app starts validated or refuses to start\nnaming the bad field, turning 2 a.m. runtime failures into deploy-time crashes.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"json-jsonl","key":"python-for-ai/json-jsonl","module":"python-for-ai","name":"JSON / JSONL","taxonomy_name":"JSON / JSONL","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/generators","python-for-ai/pyarrow","evals/golden-dataset","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","observability/logging"],"tldr":"JSON is the lingua franca of LLM payloads, tool arguments and structured outputs; JSONL puts one JSON\nvalue per line, making files appendable, streamable and splittable for evals, fine-tuning, batches and\nlogs. Watch NaN, big integers and encoding.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"numpy","key":"python-for-ai/numpy","module":"python-for-ai","name":"numpy","taxonomy_name":"numpy","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/pandas","python-for-ai/pytorch","python-for-ai/pyarrow","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","python-for-ai/gil"],"tldr":"NumPy's `ndarray` is typed, contiguous memory plus a shape and strides, operated on by compiled loops\ninstead of per-element bytecode. Embedding math and metrics get compiled speed and compact memory, but\nbugs live in shapes, dtypes, broadcasting and views.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pandas","key":"python-for-ai/pandas","module":"python-for-ai","name":"pandas","taxonomy_name":"pandas","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/polars","python-for-ai/pyarrow","python-for-ai/json-jsonl","python-for-ai/jupyter","evals/error-analysis","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling"],"tldr":"pandas gives Python an in-memory labelled table, the DataFrame: typed columns plus an index, with\nvectorized math, group-by, joins and readers for most formats. It is the default for eval results and\nlogs that fit in RAM; watch alignment, copies and memory.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"polars","key":"python-for-ai/polars","module":"python-for-ai","name":"polars","taxonomy_name":"polars","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/pandas","python-for-ai/pyarrow","python-for-ai/json-jsonl","python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing"],"tldr":"Polars is a Rust DataFrame library on Arrow memory. Its lazy mode optimizes a query plan, pushing\nfilters and columns into the scan, and uses all cores, so recurring or multi-gigabyte pipelines\ntypically run much faster than in pandas; the API is stricter.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pyarrow","key":"python-for-ai/pyarrow","module":"python-for-ai","name":"pyarrow","taxonomy_name":"pyarrow","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/pandas","python-for-ai/polars","python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/hugging-face","python-for-ai/json-jsonl","data-architecture/lakehouse","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines"],"tldr":"pyarrow binds Apache Arrow, the shared columnar memory format, and is Python's standard Parquet reader\nand writer. Arrow lets pandas, Polars, DuckDB and Hugging Face swap tables without serializing; Parquet\nstores them compressed with statistics so readers fetch only what they need.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"scikit-learn-basics","key":"python-for-ai/scikit-learn-basics","module":"python-for-ai","name":"scikit-learn basics","taxonomy_name":"scikit-learn basics","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","ml-fundamentals/classification","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","ml-fundamentals/class-imbalance","ml-fundamentals/calibration","training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/pandas"],"tldr":"scikit-learn puts classical ML behind one interface: estimators with `fit`, then `predict` or\n`transform`. Pipelines learn preprocessing from training data only, so evaluation stays honest. It is the\nfastest route to baselines and embedding classifiers, often good enough that no LLM call is needed.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pytorch","key":"python-for-ai/pytorch","module":"python-for-ai","name":"PyTorch (tensors, autograd, nn.Module, DataLoader, device)","taxonomy_name":"PyTorch (tensors, autograd, nn.Module, DataLoader, device)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":20,"related":["python-for-ai/numpy","python-for-ai/hugging-face","python-for-ai/safetensors","ml-fundamentals/backpropagation","ml-fundamentals/gradient-descent","ml-fundamentals/mixed-precision","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing"],"tldr":"PyTorch is GPU-capable NumPy-style tensors that record their operations, so `loss.backward()` computes\nevery gradient. `nn.Module` packages parameters, `DataLoader` feeds batches, `device` places memory, and\nthe graph is built by running plain Python.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"hugging-face","key":"python-for-ai/hugging-face","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Hugging Face (transformers, datasets, tokenizers, accelerate, PEFT, TRL)","taxonomy_name":"Hugging Face (transformers, datasets, tokenizers, accelerate, PEFT, TRL)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":22,"related":["python-for-ai/pytorch","python-for-ai/safetensors","python-for-ai/sentence-transformers","model-internals/tokenization","training-adaptation/peft","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/dpo","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets"],"tldr":"Hugging Face is a set of libraries over a model hub: load, tokenise, feed data, scale to many GPUs, train\nadapters and preference-tune, all by model name. It exists because every open model would otherwise\nship incompatible loading and training code.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"sentence-transformers","key":"python-for-ai/sentence-transformers","module":"python-for-ai","name":"sentence-transformers","taxonomy_name":"sentence-transformers","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/hugging-face","python-for-ai/pytorch","rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/similarity-search","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","model-internals/embeddings"],"tldr":"sentence-transformers is the standard Python library for text embeddings whose cosine similarity tracks\nmeaning, and for cross-encoder reranking. It adds the pooling, normalisation and training losses that\nturn a Hugging Face encoder into a usable search model.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tiktoken","key":"python-for-ai/tiktoken","module":"python-for-ai","name":"tiktoken","taxonomy_name":"tiktoken","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["model-internals/tokenization","model-internals/context-window","python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/hugging-face","observability/token-cost-tracking","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling"],"tldr":"tiktoken is OpenAI's fast byte-pair-encoding tokenizer (Rust core, Python bindings) that yields exactly\nthe token ids OpenAI models see. Use it locally to count, budget, chunk and estimate cost; for other model\nfamilies its counts are only an approximation.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"safetensors","key":"python-for-ai/safetensors","module":"python-for-ai","name":"safetensors","taxonomy_name":"safetensors","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["python-for-ai/pytorch","python-for-ai/hugging-face","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","training-adaptation/checkpointing","training-adaptation/quantization","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization"],"tldr":"safetensors stores tensors as a small JSON header (name, dtype, shape, byte offsets) plus raw bytes.\nIt replaced pickle checkpoints on the Hugging Face Hub because loading it runs no code, and its layout\nallows fast, lazy, memory-mapped loading.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"openai-sdk","key":"python-for-ai/openai-sdk","module":"python-for-ai","name":"OpenAI SDK","taxonomy_name":"OpenAI SDK","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","python-for-ai/litellm","python-for-ai/tiktoken","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/httpx","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","prompting/function-tool-calling","serving-inference/retry-strategies"],"tldr":"The `openai` package is OpenAI's official typed client: sync and async, streaming, tools, structured\noutputs, retries and timeouts. Its Chat Completions shape is the de facto standard, so the same client\noften reaches vLLM, Ollama or gateways by changing `base_url`.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"anthropic-sdk","key":"python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Anthropic SDK","taxonomy_name":"Anthropic SDK","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/litellm","python-for-ai/pydantic","prompting/prompt-caching","prompting/function-tool-calling","agents/tool-calling","agents/parallel-tool-calls","node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks"],"tldr":"The `anthropic` package is the official typed client for Claude's Messages API. Its shapes differ from\nOpenAI's: typed content blocks, top-level `system`, required `max_tokens`, tool results as user blocks,\nand explicit `cache_control`. Learn them and most integration bugs become obvious.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"litellm","key":"python-for-ai/litellm","module":"python-for-ai","name":"LiteLLM","taxonomy_name":"LiteLLM","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","observability/token-cost-tracking","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy"],"tldr":"LiteLLM is an open-source library and proxy that puts many LLM providers behind one OpenAI-style call,\nadding routing, retries, fallbacks, cost tracking, virtual keys and budgets. Its main cost: translation\ncan hide or lag provider-specific features.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"instructor","key":"python-for-ai/instructor","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Instructor","taxonomy_name":"Instructor","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/outlines","python-for-ai/tenacity","python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","prompting/function-tool-calling","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation"],"tldr":"Instructor wraps an LLM client so a call takes a Pydantic model as `response_model` and returns a\nvalidated instance. When validation fails it sends the error back and retries, replacing the brittle\n\"ask for JSON and `json.loads` it\" loop every codebase rewrites.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"outlines","key":"python-for-ai/outlines","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Outlines","taxonomy_name":"Outlines","kind":"tool","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/instructor","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/hugging-face","serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","model-internals/sampling-strategies","serving-inference/engines"],"tldr":"Outlines makes model output match a regex, JSON Schema, choice list or grammar *by construction*, by\nmasking tokens that would break the pattern before sampling, so invalid output is impossible. It needs\nlogit access: self-run models or engines integrating it, not closed hosted APIs.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"mcp-python-sdk","key":"python-for-ai/mcp-python-sdk","module":"python-for-ai","name":"MCP Python SDK","taxonomy_name":"MCP Python SDK","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/model-context-protocol","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","node-ts-for-ai/mcp-typescript-sdk","python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/pydantic","python-for-ai/type-hints"],"tldr":"The MCP Python SDK (the `mcp` package) exposes Python tools, data and prompts to any MCP host and\ncalls MCP servers from Python. FastMCP turns type-hinted functions into tools; lower layers handle\nJSON-RPC and transports. The protocol is taught in the agents module.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"httpx","key":"python-for-ai/httpx","module":"python-for-ai","name":"httpx","taxonomy_name":"httpx","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/tenacity","python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","serving-inference/streaming"],"tldr":"httpx is a requests-style Python HTTP client for sync and `async`/`await` code, with streaming, pooling,\noptional HTTP/2 and mockable transports. The OpenAI and Anthropic Python SDKs are built on it, so its\ntimeout and pooling behaviour is your SDK's behaviour.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"jupyter","key":"python-for-ai/jupyter","module":"python-for-ai","name":"Jupyter","taxonomy_name":"Jupyter","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["python-for-ai/pandas","python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/package-structure-and-import-design","evals/error-analysis","llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc"],"tldr":"Jupyter runs cells against a long-lived kernel and saves code with outputs in an `.ipynb` file: the\nfastest way to explore data and model outputs. The same state makes notebooks unreliable as production\ncode, and as evidence until you restart and run all.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"}],"stage":"foundations","stage_index":1,"track_order":1},{"id":"fastapi-for-ai","tier":1,"order":14,"title":"FastAPI for AI","intro":{"overview":"This module is about building the HTTP service that sits in front of a model: the FastAPI process that\naccepts prompts, files and chat sessions, calls a provider or a local model, and returns tokens without\nfalling over under real traffic. It starts with the runtime underneath. The ASGI lifecycle explains\nwhat a request, a stream, a disconnect and a startup actually are, and async endpoints set the rule\nthat decides everything else: await remote models, never block the event loop. Next comes the contract\nlayer. Pydantic request and response models, `response_model`, error handling, routers and dependency\ninjection define what goes in, what may come out and who is calling. Lifespan and resource scoping then\ndecide what is built once per worker (model weights, the shared httpx client, the database engine) and\nwhat lives for one request (session, principal, budget). The middle of the module covers the shapes AI\ntraffic takes: streaming responses, Server-Sent Events, WebSockets and cancellation when the user closes\nthe tab; async database access, SQLAlchemy and Alembic; uploads and their size and safety limits;\nbackground tasks versus durable job queues. Protection follows: timeouts, idempotency keys, CORS,\nauthentication, Redis caching, rate limits, concurrency caps and backpressure. The last group makes the\nservice operable and provable: structured logs, OpenTelemetry traces, health and readiness probes,\nworker sizing, graceful draining, OpenAPI docs and three layers of tests. Afterwards you can ship an\nauthenticated, streaming, cost-bounded LLM API that survives deploys, overload and retries, and explain\nits behaviour from a trace. It matters most for Engineers who build and run these services, and for\nFDEs who deploy them into customer environments.","why":{"engineer":"You write the endpoints, dependencies, streaming generators and worker configuration, so a blocking SDK call that freezes every request, a pool exhausted by a held connection, a double-billed retry or a stream that keeps generating for a closed tab is a bug in your code.","architect":"You decide what is application-scoped versus request-scoped, where slow work goes (BackgroundTasks or a durable queue), how concurrency, rate and token budgets are enforced, and how workers map to memory and GPUs, and those choices fix the service's cost and failure behaviour.","fde":"Customers deploy the service behind their own proxies, identity providers and Kubernetes clusters, so you are the one tuning CORS and auth, fixing buffering that breaks streaming, setting probe and drain timeouts, and proving with traces and contract tests that it behaves under their load."},"mental_model":"Picture one worker process as an event loop with two kinds of objects and three gates. The application-\nscoped objects are built once in lifespan and shared by every request: the model or provider client,\nits connection pool, the database engine, the Redis client, the semaphore. The request-scoped objects\nare created by dependencies and destroyed when the request ends: the session, the principal, the\nbudget, the trace span. Every request passes three gates on the way to the model. At the edge,\nmiddleware, size limits, CORS, auth and rate limits decide whether it gets in at all. At admission,\nconcurrency limits, idempotency keys and caches decide whether it needs to call the model. At\nexecution, timeouts, cancellation and backpressure decide how long it may hold its slot and what\nhappens if the client goes away. Anything slower than a request leaves the loop for a durable worker.\nLogs, traces and probes are how you see each gate working, and tests pin the contract to the schema.","order":["asgi-lifecycle","async-endpoints","pydantic-request-response-models","response-model","httpexception-handling","routers","dependency-injection","lifespan","request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","shared-httpx-asyncclient","middleware-ordering","cors","api-keys-oauth2-jwt","timeouts","streamingresponse","server-sent-events","cancellation-on-client-disconnect","websockets","async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","alembic-migrations","uploadfile","request-size-limits-and-upload-security","backgroundtasks","job-queues-and-durable-workers","idempotency-keys","redis-caching","rate-limiting","concurrency-limits","backpressure","structured-logging","opentelemetry-instrumentation","health-readiness-endpoints","uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","openapi-docs","testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","integration-tests-with-real-dependencies","openapi-contract-testing"],"order_reasons":{"asgi-lifecycle":"the scope/receive/send protocol underlies lifespan, middleware, streaming and disconnects, so every later lesson refers back to it.","async-endpoints":"the async versus def rule and the blocking-call trap decide how every handler, client and database call must be written.","pydantic-request-response-models":"defines the validated input contract that stops malformed or oversized prompts before they cost money.","response-model":"completes the contract on the way out so internal fields and raw provider payloads never reach clients.","httpexception-handling":"maps validation, domain and upstream LLM failures onto one consistent error shape built on those models.","routers":"splits the growing service by feature and version once there are enough endpoints and errors to organise.","dependency-injection":"introduces Depends, the mechanism that delivers auth, sessions, clients and test overrides to handlers.","lifespan":"shows where expensive shared resources are built and closed once per worker, which dependencies then hand out.","request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources":"names the scoping rule that lifespan and dependencies implement, and the leaks when it is wrong.","shared-httpx-asyncclient":"applies that rule to the most common resource in an AI service, the pooled client calling model providers.","middleware-ordering":"explains the wrapping stack that request IDs, CORS, auth and timing all sit in before you add any of them.","cors":"the first middleware most browser-facing AI apps need, and why it is not authentication.","api-keys-oauth2-jwt":"adds real caller identity for billing and data scoping, implemented as dependencies and middleware.","timeouts":"bounds every outbound model call and the request as a whole before streams make requests long-lived.","streamingresponse":"sends tokens as they are generated, with the frozen-status rule that shapes mid-stream error handling.","server-sent-events":"gives the stream message boundaries and reconnection, the format chat UIs and vendor APIs use.","cancellation-on-client-disconnect":"stops generation and frees slots when a streaming client goes away, using the ASGI disconnect message.","websockets":"covers the two-way case (interrupts, voice, approvals) once one-way streaming and its limits are understood.","async-database-access-and-connection-pooling":"introduces async drivers, pool sizing and the rule never to hold a connection across a model call.","sqlalchemy-sqlmodel":"models conversations, jobs and chunks on top of the async engine and session dependency.","alembic-migrations":"evolves that schema safely across every environment once models exist.","uploadfile":"accepts documents, images and audio for RAG and multimodal endpoints.","request-size-limits-and-upload-security":"caps and isolates the large, untrusted inputs that uploads and long prompts bring.","backgroundtasks":"handles cheap after-response work and shows where in-process follow-up stops being safe.","job-queues-and-durable-workers":"moves long or must-complete work to a queue with 202 and a status endpoint, building on the database layer.","idempotency-keys":"makes retried POSTs and at-least-once job delivery produce one effect instead of double billing.","redis-caching":"avoids repeated model and embedding calls with a shared cache whose key captures everything that changes the answer.","rate-limiting":"caps request and token spend per caller, typically counted in the same Redis.","concurrency-limits":"caps in-flight model calls per worker so the provider or GPU is not overrun by an async loop.","backpressure":"ties limits, bounded queues, 429/503 with Retry-After and stream flow control into one overload story.","structured-logging":"binds request, tenant, model and token fields into every log line so behaviour can be filtered and costed.","opentelemetry-instrumentation":"adds traces across FastAPI, httpx, SQLAlchemy and Redis plus manual LLM spans, joined to those logs.","health-readiness-endpoints":"separates liveness from readiness so orchestrators restart and route traffic correctly, including during model loading.","uvicorn-gunicorn-workers":"sizes processes by memory and GPUs rather than cores, which determines how per-worker limits add up.","graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining":"drains long streams within the orchestrator's budget using readiness flips and lifespan cleanup.","openapi-docs":"turns the typed routes and models into a published schema for clients, SDKs and LLM tool definitions.","testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio":"tests the app in-process with the LLM replaced by a dependency override, now that the whole surface exists.","integration-tests-with-real-dependencies":"proves SQL, pgvector, Redis TTLs and migrations against real containers, which fakes cannot.","openapi-contract-testing":"closes the loop by checking the schema for breaking changes and fuzzing responses against it."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are delivering a document question-answering API for a customer pilot, and it must survive their\nstaging cluster rather than a laptop. Build a FastAPI service with versioned routers where an\nauthenticated caller (API key for servers, JWT for the browser app) uploads PDFs, the service ingests\nthem through a durable job queue (202 Accepted plus a job-status endpoint), and a chat endpoint streams\nanswers over Server-Sent Events from a real or mock LLM provider called through one shared httpx\nclient created in lifespan. Conversations, jobs and chunks live in Postgres through an async\nSQLAlchemy engine with Alembic migrations, and repeated questions are served from a Redis cache.\nProtect the model with per-tenant request and token rate limits, a per-worker concurrency semaphore\nthat sheds load with 503 and Retry-After, per-phase timeouts, upload size caps and idempotency keys on\nthe ingest endpoint. The stream must stop generating when the client disconnects. Make it operable:\nJSON logs with request and tenant fields, OpenTelemetry traces showing where each second went,\nseparate liveness and readiness probes, a Gunicorn/Uvicorn worker configuration you can justify, and a\ndrain path that lets in-flight streams finish on SIGTERM. Finish with a test suite that a reviewer can\nrun in CI.","deliverables":["A repository with the FastAPI service, a docker-compose file (API, worker, Postgres with pgvector, Redis, an OTel collector) and Alembic migrations that build the schema from empty.","Endpoints for upload, job status, streaming chat over SSE and health/readiness, with Pydantic request and response models and one consistent error envelope, published at `/docs`.","A test suite with in-process tests using dependency overrides for the LLM, integration tests against containerised Postgres and Redis, and an OpenAPI snapshot diff plus a Schemathesis run in CI.","A load-test script and short report showing behaviour at and beyond the concurrency cap, with p50/p95 time to first token and the rate of 429 and 503 responses.","A one-page operations note covering worker count and pool sizing arithmetic, timeout layering, probe settings, drain budget and how to read a trace for a slow request."],"rubric":["A blocking-call check passes: under load, `/health` stays responsive while 50 chat streams run, and no sync SDK or database call appears on the event loop.","Closing the browser mid-answer stops the upstream generation within a second and releases the concurrency slot, visible in logs and the trace.","Replaying an ingest request with the same idempotency key returns the original job id and creates no second job or charge.","Beyond the cap, excess requests receive 503 or 429 with `Retry-After` while memory stays flat and accepted requests keep their latency, rather than every request slowing down.","A rolling restart during active streams drops no in-flight answer that fits within the drain budget, and readiness goes false before the process stops accepting.","Responses never contain fields outside the declared response models, and a deliberately removed field fails the OpenAPI contract check in CI.","Every log line for a request carries the same request id and tenant as its trace, and the trace shows separate spans for retrieval, the LLM call and database work."]}},"topics":[{"id":"asgi-lifecycle","key":"fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"ASGI lifecycle","taxonomy_name":"ASGI lifecycle","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","python-for-ai/asyncio","backend-fundamentals/fastapi"],"tldr":"ASGI is the message protocol between an async server (Uvicorn, Hypercorn) and your app: one\n`async def app(scope, receive, send)` called once per connection, plus one `lifespan` connection per\nprocess. FastAPI's lifespan, middleware, streaming and disconnect detection are all moves in it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"middleware-ordering","key":"fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Middleware ordering","taxonomy_name":"middleware ordering","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","fastapi-for-ai/cors","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","fastapi-for-ai/structured-logging","fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","fastapi-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse"],"tldr":"Middleware is a stack of nested ASGI apps: requests walk in, responses walk out. `app.add_middleware()`\ninserts at the *outside*, so the last added runs first. Order decides whether a 401 carries CORS headers\nand whether timing sees exceptions.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"async-endpoints","key":"fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Async endpoints","taxonomy_name":"async endpoints","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/async-await","python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/gil","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits"],"tldr":"FastAPI runs `async def` endpoints on the one event loop and `def` endpoints in a thread pool. Awaiting\nI/O lets one process hold hundreds of requests; blocking inside `async def` freezes them all. Await remote\nmodels, offload local ones.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pydantic-request-response-models","key":"fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Pydantic request / response models","taxonomy_name":"Pydantic request / response models","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["python-for-ai/pydantic","fastapi-for-ai/response-model","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility"],"tldr":"FastAPI endpoints declare request and response bodies as Pydantic models. Bad input is rejected with a\n422 naming the field, and output is filtered to declared fields, so bad prompts never cost money and\ninternal fields never leak.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"response-model","key":"fastapi-for-ai/response-model","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"response_model","taxonomy_name":"response_model","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":11,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","python-for-ai/pydantic","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","safety-security/output-validation","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility"],"tldr":"`response_model` (or the return annotation) declares the exact shape a route may return. FastAPI\nvalidates output against it, serialises only declared fields, and publishes it in OpenAPI: the cheapest\nguarantee that internal fields never reach clients.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"dependency-injection","key":"fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Dependency injection (Depends)","taxonomy_name":"dependency injection (Depends)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","fastapi-for-ai/testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling"],"tldr":"`Depends` lets an endpoint declare what it needs (principal, DB session, LLM client, tenant config) as\ntyped parameters. FastAPI resolves, caches and cleans them up per request, and tests swap any of them\nwith `dependency_overrides`.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","key":"fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Request-scoped vs application-scoped resources","taxonomy_name":"request-scoped vs application-scoped resources","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives"],"tldr":"Application-scoped objects (model, tokenizer, provider client, DB engine, index, settings) are built once\nper worker in lifespan; request-scoped ones (session, principal, budget, span, request ID) die with the\nrequest. Wrong one way wastes sockets; the other leaks users' data.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"lifespan","key":"fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Lifespan (load model / client once)","taxonomy_name":"lifespan (load model / client once)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/health-readiness-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","serving-inference/model-serving"],"tldr":"`lifespan` is an async context manager passed to `FastAPI(lifespan=...)`: code before `yield` runs once\nper worker before serving, code after it at shutdown. Load models, provider clients and DB engines there,\nbecause they are expensive, shared and need a guaranteed close.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"streamingresponse","key":"fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"StreamingResponse","taxonomy_name":"StreamingResponse","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","fastapi-for-ai/websockets","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","serving-inference/streaming","serving-inference/ttft"],"tldr":"`StreamingResponse` sends bytes from an iterator as each chunk exists, so users wait for time to first\ntoken, not the whole generation. Status and headers freeze at the first byte, so mid-stream errors go\nin-band and nothing on the path may buffer.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"server-sent-events","key":"fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Server-Sent Events","taxonomy_name":"Server-Sent Events","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/websockets","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","serving-inference/streaming","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams"],"tldr":"Server-Sent Events is one-way text streaming in a normal `text/event-stream` HTTP response: `field: value`\nlines, a blank line ending each event. Typed events, plus browsers reconnecting with `Last-Event-ID`, make\nit the default for LLM token streams.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"websockets","key":"fastapi-for-ai/websockets","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"WebSockets","taxonomy_name":"WebSockets","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","serving-inference/cancellation"],"tldr":"A WebSocket upgrades one HTTP connection into a persistent two-way channel. Use it when the client must\ntalk mid-stream (stop, voice, approvals); you pay in pinned per-connection state, hand-rolled auth and\nresume, and upgrade-aware balancers. One-way streaming: use SSE.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"backgroundtasks","key":"fastapi-for-ai/backgroundtasks","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"BackgroundTasks","taxonomy_name":"BackgroundTasks","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers"],"tldr":"`BackgroundTasks` runs functions after the response is sent, in the same process, with no persistence,\nretry or status. Use it for cheap, loss-tolerant follow-ups (traces, counters, webhooks); anything that\nmust happen or takes seconds needs a durable queue.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"job-queues-and-durable-workers","key":"fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Job queues & durable workers (Celery / ARQ + job-status endpoint)","taxonomy_name":"job queues & durable workers (Celery / ARQ + job-status endpoint)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","backend-fundamentals/redis","fastapi-for-ai/backgroundtasks","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","system-design/synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution"],"tldr":"A job queue moves slow or must-complete work to workers after persisting a job record; the API answers\n`202 Accepted` with a job id and a status endpoint reports the outcome. Celery is sync-first, ARQ\nasyncio-native; both need idempotent jobs.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","key":"fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Async database access & connection pooling","taxonomy_name":"async database access & connection pooling","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","python-for-ai/asyncio"],"tldr":"In `async def` endpoints use an async driver behind SQLAlchemy's `AsyncEngine`; blocking drivers belong\nin `def` endpoints. Each process keeps a bounded pool, and `processes × (pool_size + max_overflow)` must fit Postgres's cap.\nAI rule: never hold a pooled connection across a model call.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","key":"fastapi-for-ai/sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"SQLAlchemy / SQLModel","taxonomy_name":"SQLAlchemy / SQLModel","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","fastapi-for-ai/alembic-migrations","fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","python-for-ai/pydantic","backend-fundamentals/sql","backend-fundamentals/database-design"],"tldr":"SQLAlchemy is Python's standard SQL toolkit: Core builds safe parameterised SQL, the ORM maps classes to\ntables with a unit of work. SQLModel makes one class both table and Pydantic schema: use it while\nstorage and API shapes agree, plain SQLAlchemy once they diverge.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"alembic-migrations","key":"fastapi-for-ai/alembic-migrations","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Alembic migrations","taxonomy_name":"Alembic migrations","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/sqlalchemy-sqlmodel","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","fastapi-for-ai/integration-tests-with-real-dependencies","backend-fundamentals/database-design","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","node-ts-for-ai/postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","llmops-platform/rollback","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy"],"tldr":"Alembic versions every schema change as a Python script with `upgrade()` and `downgrade()`, and each\ndatabase records its revision, so \"what schema does production have?\" is queryable. Autogenerate only\ndrafts; renames, data moves and locks need a human and expand-contract.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"timeouts","key":"fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Timeouts","taxonomy_name":"timeouts","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/retry-strategies","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation"],"tldr":"FastAPI and uvicorn impose no per-request time limit, so a slow LLM call holds a connection, memory and\na concurrency slot indefinitely. Timeouts turn that hang into a fast, known error: phase timeouts per\ncall, one request deadline, innermost firing first.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"idempotency-keys","key":"fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Idempotency keys","taxonomy_name":"idempotency keys","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","serving-inference/idempotency","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation"],"tldr":"An idempotency key is a client-chosen unique ID sent with a request. The server records the first\noutcome under it and replays it on retries, so a retried POST cannot bill twice or rerun a generation:\nat-least-once delivery becomes effectively-once.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"cors","key":"fastapi-for-ai/cors","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"CORS","taxonomy_name":"CORS","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust"],"tldr":"CORS is the set of response headers by which a server lets another origin's JavaScript read its\nresponses. Browsers enforce it, not your server, so it is not authentication: curl, scripts and other\nservers ignore it, and your API still needs real auth.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"api-keys-oauth2-jwt","key":"fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"API keys / OAuth2 / JWT","taxonomy_name":"API keys / OAuth2 / JWT","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":22,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/cors","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security"],"tldr":"An AI API must know who calls, to bill tokens and scope data. API keys are long-lived server-to-server\nsecrets; OAuth2 delegates access through an identity provider; a JWT is a signed token verified without\na lookup. Teams often combine them.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"rate-limiting","key":"fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Rate limiting (slowapi)","taxonomy_name":"rate limiting (slowapi)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","serving-inference/rate-limiting","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas"],"tldr":"Rate limiting caps how fast each caller consumes paid LLM work. slowapi gives FastAPI decorator limits\nlike `\"10/minute\"` per client, counted in memory or Redis, returning 429. It counts *requests*; AI\nservices also need a *token* budget built beside it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"request-size-limits-and-upload-security","key":"fastapi-for-ai/request-size-limits-and-upload-security","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Request-size limits & upload security","taxonomy_name":"request-size limits & upload security","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/uploadfile","fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","rag-retrieval/document-parsing","safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/rag-poisoning","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing"],"tldr":"AI backends accept huge, untrusted inputs, and uvicorn and FastAPI put no cap on JSON body size. Cap\nsize at every layer, count bytes while streaming, never trust the client's filename or content type,\nand parse files in an isolated, resource-limited worker.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"uploadfile","key":"fastapi-for-ai/uploadfile","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"UploadFile","taxonomy_name":"UploadFile","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/request-size-limits-and-upload-security","fastapi-for-ai/backgroundtasks","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","multimodal/speech-to-text","rag-retrieval/document-parsing"],"tldr":"`UploadFile` is FastAPI's type for `multipart/form-data` files (PDFs, images, audio): a spooled temp\nfile, in memory while small and on disk when large, with filename, content type and async read/seek.\nPrefer it to `bytes = File()`; it does no safety checks.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"httpexception-handling","key":"fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"HTTPException handling","taxonomy_name":"HTTPException handling","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/structured-logging","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","system-design/graceful-degradation"],"tldr":"`raise HTTPException(status_code, detail)` aborts a request from anywhere in the call stack and becomes\nan error response. Do it well: map domain and LLM failures to meaningful codes, one error shape, no\nleaked internals, and check everything before a stream starts.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"routers","key":"fastapi-for-ai/routers","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Routers (APIRouter)","taxonomy_name":"routers","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","fastapi-for-ai/api-keys-oauth2-jwt","fastapi-for-ai/httpexception-handling","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design"],"tldr":"An `APIRouter` groups path operations in their own module, attached with\n`include_router(prefix=..., tags=..., dependencies=...)`. It splits a growing AI service by feature,\nversion and audience while still producing one app, one route table and one OpenAPI document.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"openapi-docs","key":"fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"OpenAPI docs","taxonomy_name":"OpenAPI docs","kind":"tool","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models","fastapi-for-ai/response-model","fastapi-for-ai/routers","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility"],"tldr":"FastAPI derives an OpenAPI document from route signatures and Pydantic models, serves it at\n`/openapi.json`, and renders it at `/docs` (Swagger UI) and `/redoc`. Built from the validating types, it\ncannot drift, so it reliably feeds SDKs, contract tests and LLM tool definitions.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"openapi-contract-testing","key":"fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"OpenAPI contract testing","taxonomy_name":"OpenAPI contract testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","fastapi-for-ai/testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","fastapi-for-ai/response-model","python-for-ai/contract-testing","python-for-ai/property-based-testing","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts"],"tldr":"Contract testing treats the generated schema as a promise: a CI snapshot diff fails on breaking changes,\nand schema-driven tests (Schemathesis) check responses match the schema. Unit tests check what you meant;\ncontract tests check what clients were told.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","key":"fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Uvicorn / Gunicorn workers","taxonomy_name":"uvicorn / gunicorn workers","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","serving-inference/model-serving"],"tldr":"Uvicorn runs FastAPI's event loop; Gunicorn is a pre-fork manager that supervises several Uvicorn workers.\nWorkers (processes) use more cores, but each loads its own copy of any in-process model, so AI services\nsize workers by memory and GPUs, not CPU.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","key":"fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Graceful shutdown & worker draining","taxonomy_name":"graceful shutdown & worker draining","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/health-readiness-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","node-ts-for-ai/graceful-shutdown","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes"],"tldr":"On SIGTERM (every deploy, scale-down or drain) a worker stops taking traffic, finishes in-flight requests,\ncloses resources and exits. LLM streams can outlast the default 30-second grace, so draining needs a\nbudget, a readiness flip and a plan for unfinishable work.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"health-readiness-endpoints","key":"fastapi-for-ai/health-readiness-endpoints","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Health / readiness endpoints","taxonomy_name":"health / readiness endpoints","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","deployment/health-checks","system-design/graceful-degradation"],"tldr":"Liveness asks \"is this process stuck, restart it?\"; readiness asks \"should it get traffic now?\"; a startup\nprobe gives slow model loads time. Restarting fixes a deadlock but worsens a database outage, so\ndependency checks belong in readiness, never liveness.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"structured-logging","key":"fastapi-for-ai/structured-logging","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Structured logging","taxonomy_name":"structured logging","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/middleware-ordering","fastapi-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","python-for-ai/logging-structlog","observability/logging","observability/token-cost-tracking","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces"],"tldr":"Structured logging emits each event as one JSON object with named fields (`request_id`, `tenant`,\n`model`, `prompt_tokens`, `latency_ms`). Middleware binds request context into `contextvars`, so every\nline carries it, making logs filterable, joinable to traces and summable into per-tenant cost.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"opentelemetry-instrumentation","key":"fastapi-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"OpenTelemetry instrumentation","taxonomy_name":"OpenTelemetry instrumentation","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/structured-logging","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","observability/opentelemetry","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","observability/spans","node-ts-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation"],"tldr":"OpenTelemetry (OTel) is a vendor-neutral API, SDK and protocol for traces, metrics and logs. In FastAPI:\nan OTLP-exporting tracer provider, auto-instrumented app and clients, and manual spans around LLM and\nretrieval steps give one cross-service trace per request, for any backend.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"redis-caching","key":"fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Redis caching","taxonomy_name":"Redis caching","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/redis","serving-inference/caching","prompting/prompt-caching","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers"],"tldr":"Redis caching puts one shared store in front of repeated LLM and embedding calls: hash the normalised\nrequest into a key, return hits, and on a miss call the model and store with a TTL. Safe only when the\nkey captures everything changing the answer.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"shared-httpx-asyncclient","key":"fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Shared httpx AsyncClient","taxonomy_name":"shared httpx AsyncClient","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["python-for-ai/httpx","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/request-scoped-vs-application-scoped-resources","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","python-for-ai/openai-sdk"],"tldr":"An AI service is mostly an HTTP client. A per-request `httpx.AsyncClient` discards the connection pool,\nso every call pays DNS, TCP and TLS setup. Create one client per worker in the lifespan, set limits and\ntimeouts deliberately, share it, close it on shutdown.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","key":"fastapi-for-ai/testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"TestClient + pytest-asyncio","taxonomy_name":"TestClient + pytest-asyncio","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/pytest-asyncio","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","fastapi-for-ai/dependency-injection","fastapi-for-ai/lifespan","fastapi-for-ai/integration-tests-with-real-dependencies","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient"],"tldr":"FastAPI apps are ASGI callables, so tests call them in-process. `TestClient` serves synchronous tests on\na helper-thread loop; `httpx.AsyncClient` with `ASGITransport` serves `async def` tests on their own loop.\nPick by whether the test awaits, and fake the LLM via dependency override.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"integration-tests-with-real-dependencies","key":"fastapi-for-ai/integration-tests-with-real-dependencies","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Integration tests with real dependencies","taxonomy_name":"integration tests with real dependencies","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/testclient-plus-pytest-asyncio","python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","python-for-ai/contract-testing","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","fastapi-for-ai/alembic-migrations","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","backend-fundamentals/docker","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd"],"tldr":"Integration tests run the app against real Postgres, pgvector and Redis in disposable containers, each\ntest isolated by rollback or cleanup, because fakes cannot prove your SQL, migrations or TTLs work. The\nLLM is the exception: use recordings or a separate contract suite.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"concurrency-limits","key":"fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Concurrency limits (semaphores)","taxonomy_name":"concurrency limits (semaphores)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","fastapi-for-ai/shared-httpx-asyncclient","fastapi-for-ai/uvicorn-gunicorn-workers","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","reliability/bulkheads","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory"],"tldr":"An async worker will start thousands of model calls at once; providers, GPUs and memory cannot cope. A\nsemaphore caps calls in flight. Size it from the downstream limit divided across workers, bound the\nwait, and fail fast with 503.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"backpressure","key":"fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Backpressure","taxonomy_name":"backpressure","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/rate-limiting","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","serving-inference/backpressure","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure"],"tldr":"Backpressure carries a slow consumer's signal back to a fast producer so it slows down instead of\nfilling buffers. In FastAPI it means bounded queues, early 429/503 with `Retry-After`, and stream flow\ncontrol; without it, overload grows latency and memory until collapse.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"cancellation-on-client-disconnect","key":"fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","module":"fastapi-for-ai","name":"Cancellation on client disconnect","taxonomy_name":"cancellation on client disconnect","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/asgi-lifecycle","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","fastapi-for-ai/async-endpoints","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","serving-inference/cancellation","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers"],"tldr":"When users close tabs or press stop, an unaware server keeps generating tokens, holding slots and\nrunning tools for nobody. ASGI reports disconnect as an `http.disconnect` message, so detect it,\ncancel the work, clean up in `finally`, and shield side effects that must finish.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"}],"stage":"foundations","stage_index":1,"track_order":3},{"id":"node-ts-for-ai","tier":1,"order":15,"title":"Node.js / TypeScript for AI","intro":{"overview":"This module is about building the backend of an AI product in Node.js and TypeScript to production\nstandard. Most of that backend is glue around slow, billed, non-deterministic model calls, and the\nmodule is organised around what that glue needs. It starts with types and trust: strict TypeScript\nmakes the compiler reject null and variant mistakes, and runtime schema validation with Zod checks\nthe data the compiler cannot see, including model output and tool arguments. Next comes the runtime.\nThe event loop explains why one process can hold thousands of open model streams and why one\nsynchronous step stalls all of them. Promise error propagation, AbortController cancellation, streams\nwith backpressure and worker threads are the tools for working with that single thread. The third\ngroup is the code that talks to the outside world. Dependency injection keeps clients swappable. The\nOpenAI and Anthropic SDKs, the Vercel AI SDK and resilient API clients with retries and circuit\nbreakers handle model calls. Postgres transactions, Redis and BullMQ hold state and durable jobs, and\nthe MCP TypeScript SDK exposes tools to any agent host. The last group covers running the service:\nOpenTelemetry tracing, layered testing that keeps the real model out of the merge gate, graceful\nshutdown and a Docker and CI pipeline. After the module you can ship a typed, observable,\ncancellable, streaming AI service that survives deploys, provider outages and restarts without lost\nwork or runaway token spend. It matters most for Engineers who write and operate these services day to\nday, and for FDEs who build integrations at customer sites, often on a customer's Node stack.","why":{"engineer":"You write the handlers, workers and tool servers, so a floating promise that crashes the process, a stream that keeps billing after the client left, a pool transaction split across connections or a 300 ms tokenizer call on the main thread is a bug in your code.","architect":"You decide which layer owns retries, where cancellation and deadlines propagate, what runs in a request versus a BullMQ job or a worker thread, what lives in Redis versus Postgres, and whether to standardise on the Vercel AI SDK or the native provider SDKs.","fde":"Customer integrations are often a TypeScript service or MCP server built in days against their APIs, and you answer for it with typed contracts, a trace of each request, safe secret handling and deploys that do not cut off users mid-answer."},"mental_model":"Picture one Node process as a single cashier serving thousands of customers who are each waiting on\na slow kitchen, the model provider. Waiting costs almost nothing, so the cashier can juggle thousands\nof open orders. Cooking at the counter, meaning any synchronous CPU work, makes everyone wait, so heavy\nwork goes to worker threads or to a queue. Every request carries three things down its call tree: a\ntype that was parsed at the boundary rather than assumed, an AbortSignal that can stop all of its work\nat once, and a trace context that ties its spans together across awaits and services. Every call that\nleaves the process goes through an injected client with a timeout, a classified retry policy and a\nbreaker. Every piece of state that must outlive the request goes to Postgres, Redis or a durable job.\nShutdown runs the same picture in reverse: stop taking orders, finish or hand off the open ones, flush,\nthen exit.","order":["typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types","runtime-schema-validation","event-loop-and-microtask-queue","promises-and-async-error-propagation","abortcontroller-and-cancellation","streams-and-backpressure","worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks","dependency-injection","openai-anthropic-node-sdks","api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","vercel-ai-sdk","postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","redis","bullmq-and-durable-jobs","mcp-typescript-sdk","opentelemetry-instrumentation","vitest-jest-and-integration-testing","graceful-shutdown","docker-ci-cd-deployment"],"order_reasons":{"typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types":"the compiler settings and union types every later lesson's code relies on.","runtime-schema-validation":"fills the gap types leave at runtime and gives one Zod schema for types, parsing and tool definitions.","event-loop-and-microtask-queue":"the execution model that explains concurrency, ordering and stalls in everything that follows.","promises-and-async-error-propagation":"how failures travel through awaits and combinators, needed before cancellation and retries.","abortcontroller-and-cancellation":"makes abandoned and timed-out work actually stop, building on promise semantics.","streams-and-backpressure":"token streaming to clients with bounded memory, wired to the request's abort signal.","worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks":"the answer to the event-loop stalls lesson 3 identified for sustained CPU work.","dependency-injection":"the explicit wiring pattern that makes provider clients, stores and queues swappable and testable.","openai-anthropic-node-sdks":"the native model clients, their retry, timeout, streaming and tool-call mechanics, injected behind an interface.","api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers":"layers deadlines, classified retries and breakers over the SDKs' own retries without multiplying attempts.","vercel-ai-sdk":"the provider-agnostic alternative, best judged once the native SDKs and their trade-offs are familiar.","postgresql-transactions-and-migrations":"durable run and budget state, including the pool-per-statement trap and safe schema changes.","redis":"shared fast state across replicas for caches, rate limits and locks, and the store BullMQ needs.","bullmq-and-durable-jobs":"moves long agent runs and batch work off the request, built on Redis and idempotent Postgres writes.","mcp-typescript-sdk":"exposes the service's tools over MCP using the Zod schemas, transports and error handling covered earlier.","opentelemetry-instrumentation":"traces one request across HTTP, model calls, Postgres, Redis and queue hops, so it comes after those pieces exist.","vitest-jest-and-integration-testing":"layered tests with injected fakes, containerised dependencies and scored evals over the full stack.","graceful-shutdown":"the SIGTERM drain ordering across servers, workers, pools and exporters introduced above.","docker-ci-cd-deployment":"packages and ships the service with a gated pipeline, depending on shutdown and tests being in place."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a production-grade \"document Q&A\" service in TypeScript over a week. Users upload PDFs and ask\nquestions in a chat UI. Answers stream token by token and cite pages. The service runs as an API\nprocess and a separate worker process. Uploads enqueue a BullMQ ingestion job. The worker parses and\ntokenizes each document in a worker-thread pool, embeds chunks through a provider client with\ntimeouts, classified retries and a circuit breaker, and writes chunks, run records and a per-tenant\ntoken budget to Postgres in transactions, with schema managed by migrations. The chat endpoint\nvalidates requests with Zod and streams through either the Vercel AI SDK or a native SDK behind an\ninjected interface, with a fallback provider when the breaker is open. A single AbortSignal per request\nstops generation when the client disconnects. Redis holds a rate limiter and an answer cache. The same\nretrieval and citation tools are also exposed as an MCP server over stdio and Streamable HTTP. Add\nOpenTelemetry traces across API, queue, worker and database, a layered test suite, graceful shutdown\nfor both processes and a multi-stage Docker image with a CI pipeline.","deliverables":["A repository with the API, worker and MCP server under `strict` plus `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, a single composition root per process, and Zod schemas used for request bodies, environment config, model tool definitions and MCP tool inputs.","Postgres migrations for documents, chunks, runs and budgets, a transactional budget-charge path that pins one client, and a BullMQ ingestion queue with idempotent handlers, retries with backoff and a dead-letter inspection script.","A test suite with unit tests against a fake model client, integration tests running real Postgres and Redis in containers plus intercepted provider HTTP, and a separate scored eval of 20+ questions with a pass threshold, run outside the merge gate.","A multi-stage Dockerfile running Node as PID 1 in exec form, a CI workflow gating on typecheck, lint (`no-floating-promises`, `no-explicit-any`) and tests, and a short runbook covering the shutdown sequence, secrets injection and dashboards.","An OpenTelemetry setup that exports one trace per question showing the HTTP span, retrieval, model call with token usage, Redis and Postgres spans, plus a linked trace for each ingestion job across the queue hop."],"rubric":["Closing the browser tab mid-answer ends the upstream model stream within a second, shown by the span ending with an abort status and no further token usage recorded for that request.","Ingesting a 200-page PDF while 50 chats stream keeps p99 event-loop lag under 50 ms, measured with `monitorEventLoopDelay` and compared with a run that parses on the main thread.","With the primary provider forced to return 529, requests fail over to the fallback after the breaker opens, total attempts per request stay within the configured cap, and breaker state transitions appear in metrics.","Sending SIGTERM to the API during 20 active streams and to the worker mid-job finishes or cleanly ends every stream, records zero 5xx at the load balancer, and leaves the job either completed or retried exactly once with no duplicate chunks.","Two concurrent questions for a tenant with budget for only one result in exactly one being charged and the other rejected with a typed budget error, and the unit and integration suites run with no network access or provider keys.","An MCP host such as an IDE lists and calls the retrieval tool over stdio, returns `isError` results for bad arguments rather than protocol errors, and nothing other than JSON-RPC is written to stdout."]}},"topics":[{"id":"typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types","key":"node-ts-for-ai/typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"TypeScript strict mode & advanced types","taxonomy_name":"TypeScript strict mode & advanced types","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","node-ts-for-ai/promises-and-async-error-propagation","python-for-ai/type-hints","python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","agents/typed-tool-contracts","backend-fundamentals/node-js-typescript"],"tldr":"TypeScript types are erased before code runs, so they protect you only under `strict`. Strict mode plus\ndiscriminated unions, generics, `satisfies` and `unknown` encode stream events, tool contracts and\nprovider responses, so mismatches fail at compile time, not as mid-stream `undefined` crashes.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"event-loop-and-microtask-queue","key":"node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Event loop & microtask queue","taxonomy_name":"event loop & microtask queue","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/promises-and-async-error-propagation","node-ts-for-ai/worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","python-for-ai/asyncio","python-for-ai/async-await","serving-inference/streaming"],"tldr":"Node runs JavaScript on one thread, multiplexing I/O through a phased event loop and draining microtask\nqueues after every callback. That lets an AI gateway hold 5,000 open model streams cheaply, and lets one\n200 ms synchronous computation stall every one of them.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"promises-and-async-error-propagation","key":"node-ts-for-ai/promises-and-async-error-propagation","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Promises & async error propagation","taxonomy_name":"promises & async error propagation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","serving-inference/retry-strategies"],"tldr":"A promise settles once, and `await` re-throws a rejection at the awaiting site, so async errors travel up\nlike sync ones. Unawaited promises, combinators that hide failures and lost causes are what make\nmulti-provider LLM calls fail silently instead of loudly and recoverably.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"streams-and-backpressure","key":"node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Streams & backpressure","taxonomy_name":"streams & backpressure","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue","serving-inference/streaming","serving-inference/backpressure","fastapi-for-ai/streamingresponse","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams"],"tldr":"Streams process data in chunks as it arrives, so model tokens reach a browser with sub-second first-token\nlatency. Backpressure pauses a fast producer when the consumer lags, keeping memory bounded; `pipeline` and\n`pipeTo` wire Node and Web streams with errors and backpressure intact.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks","key":"node-ts-for-ai/worker-threads-for-cpu-bound-tasks","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Worker threads for CPU-bound tasks","taxonomy_name":"worker threads for CPU-bound tasks","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/event-loop-and-microtask-queue","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","python-for-ai/gil","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits"],"tldr":"`worker_threads` run JavaScript in separate V8 isolates on other threads, so tokenizing, parsing or vector\nmath stops freezing the event loop. Pool them, since each has its own heap and startup cost, and transfer\nor share large data.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"abortcontroller-and-cancellation","key":"node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"AbortController & cancellation","taxonomy_name":"AbortController & cancellation","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/promises-and-async-error-propagation","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","serving-inference/cancellation","serving-inference/timeouts","fastapi-for-ai/cancellation-on-client-disconnect","python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation"],"tldr":"Promises cannot be cancelled, so JavaScript cancels cooperatively: an `AbortController`'s `AbortSignal` is\npassed to every operation that should stop together. It is how AI services stop paying for generation after\nthe user leaves, and how a 30-second timeout becomes a real stop.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"runtime-schema-validation","key":"node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Runtime schema validation (Zod)","taxonomy_name":"runtime schema validation (Zod)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types","node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","python-for-ai/pydantic","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","safety-security/output-validation","fastapi-for-ai/pydantic-request-response-models"],"tldr":"TypeScript types vanish at runtime, so nothing checks JSON from clients, webhooks, env vars or models. A Zod\nschema defines the shape once, derives the type, parses untrusted input into typed values or errors, and\nbecomes the model's tool JSON Schema.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"dependency-injection","key":"node-ts-for-ai/dependency-injection","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Dependency injection","taxonomy_name":"dependency injection","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/vitest-jest-and-integration-testing","node-ts-for-ai/typescript-strict-mode-and-advanced-types","node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation"],"tldr":"A module receives its collaborators (LLM client, database pool, cache, clock, config) from the caller\ninstead of constructing them. In TypeScript a typed `Deps` object and one composition root make AI\nservices testable offline, per-tenant and provider-swappable, with no framework.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","key":"node-ts-for-ai/postgresql-transactions-and-migrations","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"PostgreSQL transactions & migrations","taxonomy_name":"PostgreSQL transactions & migrations","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","backend-fundamentals/sql","fastapi-for-ai/alembic-migrations","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment"],"tldr":"Transactions make grouped writes (agent steps, budgets, outbox rows) atomic; a Node pool hands out a\nconnection per query, so a transaction must pin one client. Migrations version the schema as ordered,\nforward-only scripts that never lock a live table for minutes.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"redis","key":"node-ts-for-ai/redis","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Redis","taxonomy_name":"Redis","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/redis","fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","serving-inference/rate-limiting","agents/agent-memory","node-ts-for-ai/graceful-shutdown"],"tldr":"Redis is an in-memory data-structure server that runs commands one at a time, so each is atomic and\nsub-millisecond. For replicated Node AI services it holds shared, expiring state: completion cache,\nrate limits, locks, cross-replica streaming and BullMQ's store.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"bullmq-and-durable-jobs","key":"node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"BullMQ & durable jobs","taxonomy_name":"BullMQ & durable jobs","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/redis","node-ts-for-ai/graceful-shutdown","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns"],"tldr":"BullMQ is a Redis-backed Node job queue with concurrency, retries, backoff, rate limits, delays and\nflows; job state survives restarts because it lives in Redis. Delivery is at-least-once, so handlers\nmust be idempotent, and durability equals Redis's.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","key":"node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"API clients with retries & circuit breakers","taxonomy_name":"API clients with retries & circuit breakers","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["serving-inference/retry-strategies","reliability/circuit-breakers","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/idempotency","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","serving-inference/rate-limiting","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation"],"tldr":"LLM and tool APIs fail transiently (429, 5xx, resets), so every call needs a per-attempt timeout,\nclassified retries with jittered backoff, and a total deadline. A circuit breaker above that fails fast\nwhile a provider is down. SDKs already retry; you add the rest.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"opentelemetry-instrumentation","key":"node-ts-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"OpenTelemetry instrumentation","taxonomy_name":"OpenTelemetry instrumentation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["observability/opentelemetry","observability/tracing","observability/spans","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","observability/token-cost-tracking","fastapi-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","observability/langfuse"],"tldr":"OpenTelemetry (OTel) is the vendor-neutral standard for traces, metrics and logs. In Node you load the\nSDK first, let auto-instrumentation wrap HTTP, databases and Redis, and add spans for agent steps;\ncontext follows `await` and crosses services in `traceparent`.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"graceful-shutdown","key":"node-ts-for-ai/graceful-shutdown","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Graceful shutdown","taxonomy_name":"graceful shutdown","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining","deployment/health-checks","fastapi-for-ai/health-readiness-endpoints","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","node-ts-for-ai/bullmq-and-durable-jobs","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment","node-ts-for-ai/opentelemetry-instrumentation"],"tldr":"On SIGTERM a Node service must fail readiness, stop intake, finish in-flight work within a deadline\nshorter than the grace period, close queues and pools, flush telemetry and exit. Otherwise every deploy\ntruncates LLM streams, re-runs jobs and leaks half-done writes.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"vitest-jest-and-integration-testing","key":"node-ts-for-ai/vitest-jest-and-integration-testing","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Vitest / Jest & integration testing","taxonomy_name":"Vitest / Jest & integration testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/dependency-injection","node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","python-for-ai/pytest","python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","evals/regression-tests"],"tldr":"Model calls are slow, billed and non-deterministic, so split tests into three layers: unit tests with an\ninjected fake, integration tests with the real SDK over intercepted HTTP plus real databases, and scored\nevals against the real model.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"docker-ci-cd-deployment","key":"node-ts-for-ai/docker-ci-cd-deployment","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Docker / CI/CD deployment","taxonomy_name":"Docker / CI/CD deployment","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/docker","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","node-ts-for-ai/graceful-shutdown","node-ts-for-ai/vitest-jest-and-integration-testing","deployment/health-checks","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments"],"tldr":"Ship a Node AI service as a multi-stage image of compiled JavaScript and production deps, run Node as PID\n1 so `SIGTERM` drains streams, inject keys at runtime, and gate on typecheck and tests, with evals beside\nthe gate.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"vercel-ai-sdk","key":"node-ts-for-ai/vercel-ai-sdk","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"Vercel AI SDK","taxonomy_name":"Vercel AI SDK","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","serving-inference/streaming","prompting/function-tool-calling","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","agents/frameworks"],"tldr":"The Vercel AI SDK (`ai` on npm) gives one TypeScript call shape across model providers plus a streaming\nUI protocol for `useChat`. Swapping models becomes one line and browser streaming needs no hand-rolled\nparser; provider-specific features sit behind an escape hatch.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"openai-anthropic-node-sdks","key":"node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"OpenAI / Anthropic Node SDKs","taxonomy_name":"OpenAI / Anthropic Node SDKs","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers","node-ts-for-ai/abortcontroller-and-cancellation","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","node-ts-for-ai/vercel-ai-sdk","python-for-ai/openai-sdk","python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","prompting/function-tool-calling","prompting/prompt-caching"],"tldr":"`openai` and `@anthropic-ai/sdk` are generated, typed clients with the same bones: env-read keys,\nretries with backoff, millisecond timeouts, `AbortSignal`, async-iterable streams and typed errors. They\ndiffer in message and tool-call shapes and in explicit versus automatic prompt caching.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"mcp-typescript-sdk","key":"node-ts-for-ai/mcp-typescript-sdk","module":"node-ts-for-ai","name":"MCP TypeScript SDK","taxonomy_name":"MCP TypeScript SDK","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/model-context-protocol","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","python-for-ai/mcp-python-sdk","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","node-ts-for-ai/openai-anthropic-node-sdks","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege"],"tldr":"`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` is the reference TypeScript MCP implementation: servers turn Zod-typed handlers\ninto JSON-RPC tool, resource and prompt endpoints; clients connect, discover and call them, over stdio or\nStreamable HTTP. It never calls a model.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"foundations","stage_index":1,"track_order":4},{"id":"system-design","tier":2,"order":16,"title":"System design","intro":{"overview":"This module covers designing the whole system around a model call, from the first requirement to the\ndeployment boundary. It starts with the architect's working method. AI solution architecture says what\nbelongs to the model and what belongs to deterministic code. NFRs turn adjectives like \"fast\" and\n\"accurate\" into measurable scenarios. Trade-off analysis, ADRs and C4 diagrams then let you choose\nbetween options, record the choice and show the result. Reference architectures and build versus buy\ngive you starting blueprints and a per-layer sourcing decision. The model layer comes next: selecting a\nmodel on your own evals, SLMs against LLMs, open-weight against proprietary, self-host against API, and\nhow to govern a small portfolio. From there you route and cascade requests, put an AI gateway in front of\nevery call, and plan fallbacks, degradation and caching. The application shapes follow: RAG, memory,\nagentic and multi-agent systems, and orchestration. They rest on distributed-systems foundations:\npartial failure, sync against async execution, consistency, events, outbox and inbox, sagas, and the\nlimits of exactly-once. The module closes with DDD, modular monolith against microservices, and\nmulti-tenancy with real tenant isolation. Afterwards you can take a vague AI product brief and produce\na defensible architecture. That means measurable NFRs, a costed model plan, a failure-aware runtime\ndesign, tenant boundaries, and ADRs and diagrams that let other people review and challenge it. It\nmatters most for Architects. Senior Engineers who own a service end to end and FDEs who design\ndeployments for customers need it too.","why":{"engineer":"You implement the seams this module defines: the gateway client, the outbox relay, idempotent saga steps, tenant-scoped cache keys and retrieval filters, the async job API with streaming, and the router's quality check. If any of them is wrong, a duplicate charge or a cross-tenant leak follows.","architect":"You own the decisions that are expensive to reverse: model tiers and sourcing, gateway and orchestration runtime, monolith or service split, pool, bridge or silo tenancy, and consistency guarantees per store. You have to defend each one with NFR scenarios, a trade-off matrix, an ADR and a C4 view that reviewers can check.","fde":"Customers arrive with residency rules, an existing identity provider, a budget and a hard deadline. You have to fit a reference architecture to those constraints, explain self-host versus API and build versus buy in their terms, and leave behind diagrams and ADRs their own team can maintain after you leave."},"mental_model":"Picture the system as concentric layers around a rented, probabilistic, stateless model, each layer\nadding a guarantee the model cannot give. The innermost layer is the model plan: which models, chosen\non your data, sourced by deliberate build, buy, host and licence decisions, and governed as a\nportfolio. Around it sits the control plane: a gateway, routing and cascades, fallbacks, a degradation\nladder and caches, so quality, cost and availability are managed in one place. Next come the\napplication shapes, which feed the model context and let it act: RAG, memory, workflows and agents.\nUnder all of them is a distributed system, so every boundary needs timeouts, idempotency, an explicit\nconsistency choice, and outbox, inbox or saga handling where state crosses services. The outer layer is\nstructure and ownership: bounded contexts, deployment units and tenant isolation. Wrapping everything is\nthe decision discipline. Measurable NFRs drive trade-off analysis, the result is recorded in ADRs, and C4\ndiagrams show it. A design nobody can review or reverse is not finished.","order":["ai-solution-architecture","nfrs","trade-off-analysis","adrs","c4-diagrams","reference-architectures","build-vs-buy","model-selection","slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","open-weight-vs-proprietary","self-host-vs-api","model-portfolio","multi-model-architecture","model-routing-cascading","ai-gateway","model-fallback-strategies","graceful-degradation","caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","rag-architecture","memory-architecture","distributed-systems","synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution","data-consistency","event-driven-architecture","exactly-once-processing-limitations","outbox-inbox-patterns","saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","workflow-orchestration","agentic-architecture","agent-orchestration","multi-agent-systems","domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","modular-monolith-vs-microservices","multi-tenancy","tenant-isolation"],"order_reasons":{"ai-solution-architecture":"frames the job: what the model does, what deterministic code must guarantee, and the components around every call.","nfrs":"turns quality, latency, cost and safety goals into measurable scenarios, so later choices have something to be judged against.","trade-off-analysis":"gives a repeatable method for choosing between options using those NFRs, sensitivity points and reversibility.","adrs":"records each decision with its evidence and revisit trigger, so the rest of the module produces durable records.","c4-diagrams":"supplies the notation for showing where models, data and trust boundaries sit, used in every later design.","reference-architectures":"introduces the recurring AI system shapes you start from and deviate from on purpose.","build-vs-buy":"decides per layer what to own and what to rent, and where to keep a seam so the choice can be reversed.","model-selection":"the eval-driven funnel for picking the cheapest model that clears a measured quality bar.","slm-vs-llm-trade-offs":"applies selection to model size and shows when a narrow small model beats a frontier one.","open-weight-vs-proprietary":"separates the control and licensing axis from the deployment decision.","self-host-vs-api":"works through the utilisation and constraint arithmetic that decides whether GPUs pay for themselves.","model-portfolio":"turns individual choices into a small, governed set of models with owners, baselines and retirement plans.","multi-model-architecture":"breaks one system into stages with a different model per stage, joined by typed contracts.","model-routing-cascading":"sends each request to the right portfolio tier before or after a cheap first attempt.","ai-gateway":"centralises keys, budgets, routing, caching and logging, which makes routing and fallbacks operable.","model-fallback-strategies":"decides in advance what the gateway calls when a provider throttles or fails.","graceful-degradation":"goes beyond fallbacks to a labelled ladder of reduced functions for when the AI path cannot be saved.","caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation":"cuts cost and latency at the prefix, response, embedding and retrieval layers without serving stale or wrong answers.","rag-architecture":"the most common application shape: an offline indexing pipeline and an online retrieval path joined by an index.","memory-architecture":"extends retrieval to state the system writes itself, scoped per user and tenant, with forgetting policies.","distributed-systems":"sets out partial failure and the core toolkit every multi-service AI product relies on.","synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution":"picks request, stream or job-and-callback shapes for calls that take seconds to minutes.","data-consistency":"chooses which invariants must be strong and which copies, such as indexes, caches and memory, may lag.","event-driven-architecture":"decouples producers from consumers such as billing, evals and audit, at the cost of eventual consistency.","exactly-once-processing-limitations":"explains why delivery cannot be exactly once and how to get exactly-once effect instead.","outbox-inbox-patterns":"the concrete mechanism for publishing events atomically and processing redeliveries idempotently.","saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions":"coordinates multi-step operations across services with compensations around an irreversible pivot.","workflow-orchestration":"runs long, stateful processes durably with Airflow, Prefect or Temporal instead of hand-rolled sagas.","agentic-architecture":"wraps a model-driven loop in typed tools, budgets, approval gates and externalised state.","agent-orchestration":"decides how much control flow is code and how much is the model, and where checkpoints and interrupts go.","multi-agent-systems":"splits work across several agents only when isolation, parallelism or specialisation pays for the coordination cost.","domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts":"draws boundaries by business meaning, which then define tool, data and service ownership.","modular-monolith-vs-microservices":"turns those boundaries into deployment units only where scaling, team or blast radius demands it.","multi-tenancy":"carries tenant identity through every layer and picks pool, bridge or silo per resource.","tenant-isolation":"layers independent controls so one bug cannot leak data or capacity across tenants, including through the prompt."},"capstone":{"brief":"Design, and partly build, the architecture for \"Policy Desk\", a multi-tenant AI assistant that a\nB2B insurance-software vendor will sell to its broker customers. Brokers upload policy documents and\nask questions with cited answers. An agent drafts endorsement change requests, which a human must\napprove before the agent submits them to the carrier's API and records the fee in billing. Tenants\nrange from ten-seat agencies to one enterprise that requires EU data residency and refuses to have its\ndata in a shared vector index. Over one week, write measurable NFRs for answer quality, latency, cost per\nquestion, availability and isolation. Run model selection on a small labelled set and define a\nthree-tier portfolio with routing and a fallback chain behind a gateway. Design the RAG and memory\nlayers with tenant-scoped indexes and caches. Specify the approval-and-submit flow as a durable workflow\nsaga with an outbox for the billing event. Decide the bounded contexts, the deployment units and the\npool, bridge or silo tier for each tenant class. Record each major choice as an ADR. Build a thin\nvertical slice that proves the riskiest parts: tenant-scoped retrieval, gateway routing with fallback,\nand an idempotent submit step.","deliverables":["An architecture document with C4 context and container diagrams, plus a component diagram for the agent runtime, each showing the trust and tenant boundaries and where every model call sits.","An NFR catalogue of at least ten measurable scenarios, each with stimulus, condition and numeric response measure, mapped to an SLO, a test or an eval gate.","A model-selection report: candidates filtered by hard constraints, eval scores and cost per 1,000 requests on the labelled set, the chosen portfolio tiers, the routing or cascade rule, and the ordered fallback chain with the error classes that trigger it.","At least six ADRs, covering at minimum self-host versus API, gateway choice, tenancy tier per customer class, sync versus async for agent runs, monolith versus service split, and cache keying. Each has options, a trade-off matrix and a revisit trigger.","A sequence diagram and failure table for the approve-and-submit saga, listing each step's compensation, the irreversible pivot, idempotency keys, and the outbox and inbox records.","A runnable slice with tests: a gateway config with two providers and fallback, tenant-filtered retrieval and tenant-keyed response cache, and a workflow step that submits to a mocked carrier API exactly once under retries."],"rubric":["Every NFR has a number and a measurement method, and at least three ADRs cite a specific NFR or eval result as the deciding evidence instead of preference.","The model plan shows measured cost and quality per tier on the labelled set, and the router's chosen mix is within a stated quality tolerance of always using the top tier at a lower stated cost.","A test that queries as tenant A, with tenant B's documents indexed and cached on the same key text, returns no B content. The isolation test suite fails if the tenant filter or cache key prefix is removed.","Killing the provider in the gateway config makes requests succeed on the secondary with a labelled fallback flag in logs. Killing both makes the UI show the designed degraded state, not an error page.","Replaying the carrier submit step three times produces one carrier call and one billing event, shown by idempotency-key and outbox logs. A forced failure after the pivot runs the documented forward-recovery path, not a compensation.","The C4 diagrams pass a reviewer check: every box has a name, type, technology and purpose, every arrow has intent, and the EU enterprise tenant's data path stays inside its silo and region."]}},"topics":[{"id":"ai-solution-architecture","key":"system-design/ai-solution-architecture","module":"system-design","name":"AI / solution architecture","taxonomy_name":"AI / solution architecture","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/reference-architectures","system-design/nfrs","system-design/trade-off-analysis","system-design/c4-diagrams","system-design/adrs","system-design/rag-architecture","system-design/agentic-architecture","scoping/ai-use-case-discovery"],"tldr":"AI solution architecture designs everything around the model call: context, model choice, output\nvalidation, logging and measurement. A model is a probabilistic, expensive, stateless, rented\ndependency, so deterministic engineering must surround the narrow part only the model can do.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"reference-architectures","key":"system-design/reference-architectures","module":"system-design","name":"Reference architectures","taxonomy_name":"reference architectures","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":14,"related":["system-design/ai-solution-architecture","system-design/rag-architecture","system-design/agentic-architecture","system-design/workflow-orchestration","system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/c4-diagrams","system-design/adrs","agents/workflows-vs-agents"],"tldr":"A reference architecture is a validated, reusable blueprint for a recurring system shape (RAG assistant,\nextraction pipeline, agent, workflow, gateway). Starting from the matching one means you deviate\ndeliberately instead of rediscovering missing pieces after launch.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-selection","key":"system-design/model-selection","module":"system-design","name":"Model selection","taxonomy_name":"model selection","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/model-evaluation","evals/benchmarking","evals/golden-dataset","evals/dataset-contamination","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","system-design/model-portfolio","system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs"],"tldr":"Model selection is a funnel: filter by hard constraints, then pick per task the cheapest, fastest model\nthat clears a quality bar measured on your data, not a public benchmark's. Quality has a threshold;\ncost and latency are paid linearly on every call.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-portfolio","key":"system-design/model-portfolio","module":"system-design","name":"Model portfolio","taxonomy_name":"model portfolio","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/model-selection","system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/multi-model-architecture","system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","llmops-platform/model-registry","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy"],"tldr":"A model portfolio is a small, governed set of models, each with a role, owner, eval baselines, cost\ncard and retirement plan. One model everywhere overpays on easy work; unmanaged per-team choices create\nsprawl nobody can evaluate, secure or exit.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","key":"system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","module":"system-design","name":"SLM vs LLM trade-offs","taxonomy_name":"SLM vs LLM trade-offs","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/small-language-models","model-internals/scaling-laws","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","training-adaptation/quantization","system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/self-host-vs-api","infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment","system-design/model-selection"],"tldr":"Small language models trade breadth for cost, latency and deployability. On narrow, in-distribution\ntasks, especially fine-tuned, they often match large models; on open reasoning, long tool use and world\nknowledge they do not. Decide by task narrowness, tail-error cost and data location.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"build-vs-buy","key":"system-design/build-vs-buy","module":"system-design","name":"Build vs buy","taxonomy_name":"build vs buy","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["strategy/vendor-evaluation","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","scoping/roi-model","infra-capacity-economics/tco","system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","strategy/platform-strategy","delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions"],"tldr":"Build versus buy in AI is one decision per stack layer. Buy what is commodity and fast-moving, build\nwhat differentiates and touches your data most, and own a thin seam at every boundary so each decision\nstays reversible.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"open-weight-vs-proprietary","key":"system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","module":"system-design","name":"Open-weight vs proprietary","taxonomy_name":"open-weight vs proprietary","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/build-vs-buy","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","training-adaptation/peft","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","serving-inference/engines"],"tldr":"Open-weight models publish parameters you can run, inspect and fine-tune under a licence; proprietary\nweights stay with the provider as a service. It decides lifecycle control, data flow, customisation and\ncost shape, and is separate from self-host versus API.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"self-host-vs-api","key":"system-design/self-host-vs-api","module":"system-design","name":"Self-host vs API","taxonomy_name":"self-host vs API","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/build-vs-buy","system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","system-design/model-selection","infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","infra-capacity-economics/tco","serving-inference/engines","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy"],"tldr":"An API buys capability, elasticity and no operations; self-hosting buys control, data locality and\nlower *marginal* cost, but only with busy GPUs. Start on the API unless residency, an air gap,\ncustomisation or sustained volume forces hosting; re-run the numbers as traffic grows.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-routing-cascading","key":"system-design/model-routing-cascading","module":"system-design","name":"Model routing / cascading","taxonomy_name":"model routing / cascading","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/model-selection","system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","system-design/multi-model-architecture","system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","evals/llm-as-a-judge","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling"],"tldr":"Routing picks a model per request *before* the call; cascading calls the cheap model first and escalates\nwhen a quality check fails. Both cut cost sharply, pay with router error or tail latency, and are only as\ngood as their quality signal.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-model-architecture","key":"system-design/multi-model-architecture","module":"system-design","name":"Multi-model architecture","taxonomy_name":"multi-model architecture","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["system-design/model-portfolio","system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/rag-architecture","system-design/agentic-architecture","rag-retrieval/reranking","evals/llm-as-a-judge","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","observability/tracing"],"tldr":"Split the system into stages, each served by the model that fits its quality, latency and cost budget,\njoined by typed contracts. The price is coupling: each stage's output is the next one's input, so version\nand evaluate models together.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"ai-gateway","key":"system-design/ai-gateway","module":"system-design","name":"AI gateway (LiteLLM proxy, Portkey)","taxonomy_name":"AI gateway (LiteLLM proxy, Portkey)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["python-for-ai/litellm","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","observability/token-cost-tracking","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","serving-inference/rate-limiting","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp"],"tldr":"An AI gateway is a proxy every model call passes through: one OpenAI-style API over many providers,\nhandling keys, budgets, limits, routing, fallbacks, caching and logging once. It buys control and\nvisibility for an extra hop and a new single point of failure.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-fallback-strategies","key":"system-design/model-fallback-strategies","module":"system-design","name":"Model fallback strategies","taxonomy_name":"model fallback strategies","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["serving-inference/fallbacks","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/timeouts","reliability/circuit-breakers","system-design/graceful-degradation","system-design/ai-gateway","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution"],"tldr":"A fallback strategy decides in advance what to call instead, in what order and on which errors, so a\nprovider's bad hour is not your outage. The substitute behaves differently, retries can amplify incidents,\nand an untested fallback is a second failure.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"graceful-degradation","key":"system-design/graceful-degradation","module":"system-design","name":"Graceful degradation","taxonomy_name":"graceful degradation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["system-design/model-fallback-strategies","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","reliability/circuit-breakers","reliability/bulkheads","reliability/slas-slos","system-design/nfrs","llmops-platform/rollback","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets"],"tldr":"When the AI path fails, slows, overspends or overloads, step down to a cheaper, simpler, honestly labelled\nversion instead of falling over, using a designed ladder with triggers, hysteresis and exercised paths.\nFallbacks change the provider; degradation keeps users moving by changing the function.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","key":"system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","module":"system-design","name":"Caching strategy & cache invalidation","taxonomy_name":"caching strategy & cache invalidation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["serving-inference/caching","prompting/prompt-caching","serving-inference/prefix-caching","model-internals/kv-cache","fastapi-for-ai/redis-caching","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","system-design/multi-tenancy","system-design/ai-gateway"],"tldr":"Model calls are the slowest, costliest step, and their input largely repeats. Cache at several layers\n(prefix, exact, semantic, embedding, retrieval), each with its own key and way of going stale. The key\nis the design: what must match for a result to stay correct.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"rag-architecture","key":"system-design/rag-architecture","module":"system-design","name":"RAG architecture","taxonomy_name":"RAG architecture","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","rag-retrieval/reranking","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","rag-retrieval/long-context-vs-rag","data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation"],"tldr":"RAG is two pipelines joined by an index: offline, documents become searchable chunks; online, a query\nretrieves the few relevant chunks and the model answers from them. It exists because weights are\nfrozen and permission-blind, while product facts are changing, private and access-controlled.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"agentic-architecture","key":"system-design/agentic-architecture","module":"system-design","name":"Agentic architecture","taxonomy_name":"agentic architecture","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/workflows-vs-agents","agents/tool-calling","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","agent-runtime-architecture/agent-runtime-and-orchestration-layer","system-design/synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution"],"tldr":"An agentic architecture lets a model choose the next action at runtime, observe, and repeat. The system\nwraps that loop in boundaries it cannot control: least-privilege typed tools, external state, budgets,\napproval gates and tracing. The model owns judgment; the architecture owns guarantees.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-agent-systems","key":"system-design/multi-agent-systems","module":"system-design","name":"Multi-agent systems","taxonomy_name":"multi-agent systems","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["agents/multi-agent-patterns","agents/task-decomposition","agents/a2a-protocol","agents/tool-selection-accuracy","system-design/agent-orchestration","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services"],"tldr":"A multi-agent system splits a task across model loops with their own instructions, tools and context.\nIt helps when one agent's context, tools and privileges stop scaling, but duplicated context,\ncompounding errors and harder tracing mean each split must buy a named gain.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"agent-orchestration","key":"system-design/agent-orchestration","module":"system-design","name":"Agent orchestration","taxonomy_name":"agent orchestration","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/workflows-vs-agents","agents/state-machines","agents/frameworks","agents/human-in-the-loop","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","agent-runtime-architecture/agent-runtime-and-orchestration-layer","system-design/workflow-orchestration"],"tldr":"Agent orchestration is the control layer deciding what runs next (model call, tool, sub-agent or human\nstep), with what state, and what happens on failure. The core choice is how much control flow is\ndeterministic code versus left to the model.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"workflow-orchestration","key":"system-design/workflow-orchestration","module":"system-design","name":"Workflow orchestration (Temporal, Prefect, Airflow)","taxonomy_name":"workflow orchestration (Temporal, Prefect, Airflow)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["reliability/durable-workflow-execution","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","system-design/agent-orchestration","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations"],"tldr":"Workflow orchestrators run multi-step processes reliably outside a request. Airflow schedules batch DAGs,\nPrefect runs dynamic Python dataflows, Temporal replays durable workflow code. Indexing pipelines fit\nAirflow or Prefect; long-lived agent runs and approval flows fit Temporal-style engines.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"memory-architecture","key":"system-design/memory-architecture","module":"system-design","name":"Memory architecture","taxonomy_name":"memory architecture","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agents/agent-memory","agents/memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","agents/context-compaction-summarization","prompting/context-engineering","agent-reliability/state-persistence-and-conversation-recovery","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","system-design/rag-architecture"],"tldr":"Models are stateless and windows finite, so anything remembered across turns, sessions or users lives\noutside the model and returns selectively. Memory architecture designs those scoped stores and their\nwrite, read, update and delete policies; the hard part is what to remember and forget.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","key":"system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","module":"system-design","name":"Domain-driven design & bounded contexts","taxonomy_name":"domain-driven design & bounded contexts","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/modular-monolith-vs-microservices","system-design/event-driven-architecture","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","system-design/data-consistency","system-design/c4-diagrams","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","agents/typed-tool-contracts","agent-reliability/tool-adapters-service-interfaces"],"tldr":"Domain-driven design splits a system along the meanings of its business language: each bounded context\nowns one consistent model, and contexts talk through explicit contracts, not a shared schema. For AI,\ncontexts decide tool boundaries, data ownership and who translates meaning for the agent.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"event-driven-architecture","key":"system-design/event-driven-architecture","module":"system-design","name":"Event-driven architecture","taxonomy_name":"event-driven architecture","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services"],"tldr":"A service records an immutable fact (\"DocumentUploaded\", \"RunCompleted\") on a broker and any number of\nconsumers react on their own schedule. Producers stop knowing about consumers; the price is eventual\nconsistency, at-least-once delivery and no single stack trace.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution","key":"system-design/synchronous-vs-asynchronous-execution","module":"system-design","name":"Synchronous vs asynchronous execution","taxonomy_name":"synchronous vs asynchronous execution","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["system-design/event-driven-architecture","fastapi-for-ai/job-queues-and-durable-workers","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","serving-inference/streaming","fastapi-for-ai/server-sent-events","fastapi-for-ai/timeouts","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","python-for-ai/asyncio"],"tldr":"Synchronous: the caller holds a connection until the result arrives. Asynchronous: it gets a job id and\nthe result later. LLM calls take seconds and agent runs minutes, longer than proxies and people wait, so\nmost AI work goes async, or streams when someone watches.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","key":"system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","module":"system-design","name":"Saga pattern & distributed transactions","taxonomy_name":"saga pattern & distributed transactions","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/event-driven-architecture","system-design/data-consistency","system-design/workflow-orchestration","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels"],"tldr":"A saga replaces one cross-service transaction with local transactions, each paired with a compensating\naction. It gives atomicity and durability but not isolation, so order steps around the irreversible pivot\nand make every step idempotent. An agent calling several tools is a saga.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"outbox-inbox-patterns","key":"system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","module":"system-design","name":"Outbox / inbox patterns","taxonomy_name":"outbox / inbox patterns","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/event-driven-architecture","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys"],"tldr":"The **outbox** writes a message in the same transaction as the business change and a relay publishes it;\nthe **inbox** records each processed message id in the consumer's transaction. Together: effectively-once\nprocessing between two databases over an at-least-once broker.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"exactly-once-processing-limitations","key":"system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","module":"system-design","name":"Exactly-once processing limitations","taxonomy_name":"exactly-once processing limitations","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/event-driven-architecture","system-design/distributed-systems","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","serving-inference/idempotency","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation"],"tldr":"Exactly-once *delivery* over a lossy network is impossible; exactly-once *effect* is buildable from\nat-least-once delivery plus deduplication committed with the effect. Every \"exactly-once\" feature, Kafka\ntransactions included, stops at that boundary; external APIs and LLM calls are not covered.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"distributed-systems","key":"system-design/distributed-systems","module":"system-design","name":"Distributed systems","taxonomy_name":"distributed systems","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["system-design/data-consistency","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","system-design/event-driven-architecture","reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/high-availability","reliability/multi-region-architecture","reliability/circuit-breakers","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","serving-inference/retry-strategies"],"tldr":"Machines cooperating over a network, defined by **partial failure**: any node or link can fail or stall\nwhile the rest continue, and \"slow\" looks like \"dead\". Every AI product is one. The discipline: deadlines,\nidempotent retries, quorums, fenced leases, consensus, tracing.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-consistency","key":"system-design/data-consistency","module":"system-design","name":"Data consistency","taxonomy_name":"data consistency","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["system-design/distributed-systems","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/caching-strategy-and-cache-invalidation","backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","reliability/multi-region-architecture","data-architecture/data-contracts"],"tldr":"\"Consistency\" means invariants inside one database (ACID's C) and which value a read returns when copies\nexist (linearizable to eventual). Keep a few invariants strong (permissions, quotas, idempotency keys); let\nindex, cache and agent memory lag visibly.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"modular-monolith-vs-microservices","key":"system-design/modular-monolith-vs-microservices","module":"system-design","name":"Modular monolith vs microservices","taxonomy_name":"modular monolith vs microservices","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","system-design/event-driven-architecture","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/distributed-systems","system-design/adrs","system-design/trade-off-analysis","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services"],"tldr":"The real decision is where *deployment and ownership* boundaries go. Start with a modular monolith\n(enforced module APIs, owned data) and turn a boundary into a network boundary only when it has a\nproven, independent reason: scaling, team or blast radius.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-tenancy","key":"system-design/multi-tenancy","module":"system-design","name":"Multi-tenancy","taxonomy_name":"multi-tenancy","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["system-design/tenant-isolation","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage","deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped"],"tldr":"Multi-tenancy is one running system serving many customers while keeping each one's data, configuration,\nquotas and bill separate. A tenant identity set at the edge reaches every layer (index, prompt, caches,\ntoken meter), and each resource is deliberately pooled, bridged or siloed.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tenant-isolation","key":"system-design/tenant-isolation","module":"system-design","name":"Tenant isolation","taxonomy_name":"tenant isolation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["system-design/multi-tenancy","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","security-governance-compliance/data-residency"],"tldr":"Tenant isolation keeps tenants apart even when application code is wrong, by layering independent\ncontrols (scoped credentials, row policies, partitioned indexes and caches, quotas, tagged telemetry).\nIn AI systems the prompt is an exfiltration path: whatever is retrieved under the wrong key is read out.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"c4-diagrams","key":"system-design/c4-diagrams","module":"system-design","name":"C4 diagrams","taxonomy_name":"C4 diagrams","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":11,"related":["system-design/ai-solution-architecture","system-design/reference-architectures","system-design/adrs","system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","strategy/stakeholder-alignment"],"tldr":"C4 draws architecture at four nested zoom levels (System Context, Container, Component, Code), with every\nbox labelled by name, type, technology and purpose and every arrow by intent. For AI systems it shows\nwhere the model, customer data and trust boundaries sit.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"adrs","key":"system-design/adrs","module":"system-design","name":"ADRs (architecture decision records)","taxonomy_name":"ADRs","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["system-design/trade-off-analysis","system-design/nfrs","system-design/c4-diagrams","system-design/build-vs-buy","system-design/model-selection","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","strategy/stakeholder-alignment"],"tldr":"An ADR is a short, numbered, immutable record of one significant decision: context, options, choice and\nconsequences. Decisions outlive the people who made them; in AI systems an ADR also captures the evidence\nat decision time and the trigger for revisiting.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"nfrs","key":"system-design/nfrs","module":"system-design","name":"NFRs (non-functional requirements)","taxonomy_name":"NFRs","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/trade-off-analysis","system-design/adrs","reliability/slas-slos","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","observability/latency-percentiles","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/requirements-traceability"],"tldr":"Non-functional requirements state how well a system must work: latency, availability, cost, security\nand, for AI, answer quality, safety and freshness. They shape the architecture, so each must be a\nmeasurable scenario traced to a test, SLO or eval gate.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"trade-off-analysis","key":"system-design/trade-off-analysis","module":"system-design","name":"Trade-off analysis","taxonomy_name":"trade-off analysis","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/nfrs","system-design/adrs","system-design/build-vs-buy","system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency"],"tldr":"Trade-off analysis chooses between options when none dominates: filter by hard constraints, score real\noptions on NFR evidence, find where the ranking flips, weigh reversibility, then record. In AI it is\nusually quality vs latency vs cost, plus vendor risk.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"architecture","stage_index":5,"track_order":17},{"id":"reliability","tier":2,"order":17,"title":"Reliability","intro":{"overview":"This module is about keeping an AI product working, or failing in a controlled way, when the things\nit depends on do not. It begins with targets: SLIs, SLOs, SLAs and error budgets turn \"reliable\" into\na number you can measure from the user's side, including quality and cost for model endpoints. High\navailability then shows how availabilities multiply along a request path and why the model provider\nusually caps yours. Fault tolerance supplies the baseline toolkit for every remote call: timeouts,\nbounded jittered retries and idempotency. Circuit breakers, bulkheads, backpressure and load shedding\nand tenant-level quotas are the containment layer, each stopping one slow dependency, workload or\ncustomer from consuming capacity everyone else needs. Durable workflow execution makes long agent runs\nand approval flows survive crashes and deploys without redoing paid model calls. Multi-region\narchitecture, disaster recovery and business continuity widen the frame to region loss, deleted data,\nvendor exits and the people and decisions around a restore. FMEA and chaos engineering close the loop:\none finds the failure modes nobody is watching, the other proves the mechanisms actually fire. After\nthe module you can set SLOs for an LLM service, size pools and queues with Little's law, design\nfallbacks and failover with stated RPO and RTO, and show evidence that they work. It matters most for\nArchitects, who own these trade-offs, and for Engineers who carry the pager.","why":{"engineer":"You write the client wrapper every service imports, so you must get timeouts, retry budgets, idempotency keys, breaker thresholds, per-dependency semaphores and `Retry-After` handling right, and export the metrics that show when traffic is quietly running on a fallback path.","architect":"You choose the availability target, the number of zones, regions and model providers, the RPO and RTO for each store, and the isolation boundaries between tenants and workload classes, and each choice sets cost, complexity and what the SLA can honestly promise.","fde":"Enterprise buyers ask about multi-region, continuity plans and what happens when your AI vendor is down; you need credible answers, a sensible SLA, and quotas that stop one customer's backfill from slowing everyone else on a shared deployment."},"mental_model":"Picture reliability as a budget defended in rings. At the centre is the error budget from your SLO:\nthe amount of failure users will tolerate, measured as they experience it. The first ring is per-call\nhygiene (timeout, bounded retry, idempotency), which turns transient faults into successes without\nduplicating side effects. The second ring is containment: breakers, bulkheads, backpressure, load\nshedding and tenant quotas, each keeping a local fault local by capping how much shared capacity it\ncan take, sized with Little's law (in-flight = rate × time). The third ring is survival of larger\nlosses: redundancy and failover for components and regions, durable execution for long processes,\nbackups separated in time, place and trust for data, and continuity plans for suppliers and people.\nAround all of it sits verification: FMEA decides which faults to worry about, and chaos experiments\nshow whether each ring holds. A mechanism nobody has seen fire does not count as protection.","order":["slas-slos","high-availability","fault-tolerance","circuit-breakers","bulkheads","backpressure-and-load-shedding","tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","durable-workflow-execution","multi-region-architecture","disaster-recovery","business-continuity","fmea","chaos-engineering-and-resilience-testing"],"order_reasons":{"slas-slos":"sets measurable targets and error budgets first, so every later mechanism has a goal to defend.","high-availability":"shows how availabilities compose along a dependency chain and why the provider caps your nines.","fault-tolerance":"the baseline per-call toolkit of timeouts, retry budgets, idempotency and fallbacks that the later patterns build on.","circuit-breakers":"stops sending to a failing dependency, the first containment pattern layered on top of timeouts and retries.","bulkheads":"caps aggregate resource use per dependency or workload, covering the window before a breaker trips.","backpressure-and-load-shedding":"applies Little's law to overload, bounding queues and rejecting early to protect goodput.","tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation":"extends bounded capacity to fair division among tenants who share a provider key or GPU pool.","durable-workflow-execution":"makes multi-step agent and approval processes survive crashes and deploys without repeating side effects.","multi-region-architecture":"moves from component failure to region loss, latency and residency, where state placement decides what is lost.","disaster-recovery":"defines RPO and RTO and the restore of state that redundancy cannot protect, such as indexes, weights and deleted data.","business-continuity":"wraps DR in function-first planning for vendors, people, roles and communication during a disruption.","fmea":"systematically lists and ranks failure modes, especially silent AI ones, to decide what to guard and test.","chaos-engineering-and-resilience-testing":"verifies the earlier mechanisms by injecting the top FMEA faults under a bounded blast radius."},"capstone":{"brief":"Harden a multi-tenant document question-answering and drafting service for a B2B SaaS company that is\nabout to sign its first enterprise customers. The service has a chat endpoint that retrieves from a\nvector store and calls a hosted LLM, a batch endpoint that re-embeds customer documents, and an agent\nflow that drafts a reply, waits for human approval and then sends it through a CRM API. Today it runs in\none region on one provider key with one shared worker pool, and a single tenant's backfill has already\nslowed everyone. Over a week, define SLIs and SLOs for availability, latency, answer quality and cost\nper task; run an FMEA of the request path; then implement the fixes the top rows call for: a shared\nclient wrapper with timeouts, retry budgets and idempotency keys, a breaker and secondary-provider\nfallback, per-dependency bulkheads, queue-wait-based load shedding, per-tenant token buckets with fair\nqueuing, and the approval flow as a durable workflow. Write the DR and continuity plan, including how\nthe vector index is restored or rebuilt and what happens if the provider is down for a day, and prove\nthe design with fault-injection experiments.","deliverables":["An SLO document with SLI definitions (including a quality SLI backed by an eval), 30-day targets, error-budget arithmetic, a burn-rate alert rule and a draft external SLA that is looser than the SLOs.","An FMEA spreadsheet covering at least 15 failure modes across retrieval, model calls, tools and tenancy, scored S × O × D, with the top five mapped to specific mitigations in the code.","Working code for the resilience layer: a client wrapper, a circuit breaker with a fallback provider, per-dependency semaphores sized with Little's law, an admission controller that sheds by queue wait and priority, per-tenant quotas, and the approval flow as a durable workflow.","A DR and business-continuity runbook listing every stateful asset (database, vector index, prompt registry, secrets) with its RPO, RTO and restore-or-rebuild method, named roles, and the manual fallback mode.","A resilience test report from at least four experiments (provider 429s and latency, CRM hang, noisy-tenant backfill, worker crash mid-workflow), each with steady-state hypothesis, blast radius, abort condition, measured result and follow-up fix."],"rubric":["During a simulated noisy-tenant backfill, other tenants' p95 chat latency stays within the latency SLO, and per-tenant dashboards show only the offending tenant's queue wait and 429s rising.","With the CRM API hanging, chat requests that do not need the CRM keep succeeding; the CRM bulkhead saturates, its breaker opens within the stated threshold, and no worker pool beyond that compartment is exhausted.","Killing a worker mid-approval resumes the workflow at the same step with no second model call and no duplicate CRM send, shown by activity history and idempotency-key logs.","Pool sizes, queue bounds and retry budgets are each justified in writing with Little's law or error-budget arithmetic, not chosen arbitrarily.","A timed restore drill records actual RPO and RTO against the targets, including the measured time and cost to re-embed the vector index if it must be rebuilt.","Every chaos experiment states its hypothesis and abort condition before it runs, at least one refuted hypothesis produced a code or config change, and the change was verified by re-running the experiment."]}},"topics":[{"id":"high-availability","key":"reliability/high-availability","module":"reliability","name":"High availability","taxonomy_name":"high availability","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/multi-region-architecture","reliability/disaster-recovery","reliability/slas-slos","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","deployment/health-checks","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","fastapi-for-ai/graceful-shutdown-and-worker-draining"],"tldr":"High availability (HA) keeps a system answering through the failure of any single component, using\nredundancy, fast health checks and automatic failover. It costs money and complexity, and an AI\nproduct is capped by its least available dependency, usually the model provider.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"fault-tolerance","key":"reliability/fault-tolerance","module":"reliability","name":"Fault tolerance","taxonomy_name":"fault tolerance","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["reliability/high-availability","reliability/circuit-breakers","reliability/bulkheads","reliability/fmea","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/timeouts","system-design/graceful-degradation","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution"],"tldr":"Fault tolerance keeps a system delivering correct, or explicitly degraded, service while components\nfail, using timeouts, bounded retries, idempotency, breakers, bulkheads, fallbacks and replicated state.\nThe question is which faults you tolerate, how you detect them, and what happens before recovery.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-region-architecture","key":"reliability/multi-region-architecture","module":"reliability","name":"Multi-region architecture","taxonomy_name":"multi-region architecture","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["reliability/high-availability","reliability/disaster-recovery","reliability/durable-workflow-execution","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","system-design/data-consistency","system-design/distributed-systems","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits"],"tldr":"Multi-region runs a system in several cloud regions to survive region loss, cut latency and meet\nresidency rules. The hard part is state: synchronous replication taxes every write, asynchronous loses\nrecent writes. Start multi-AZ; go multi-region for a named reason.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"disaster-recovery","key":"reliability/disaster-recovery","module":"reliability","name":"Disaster recovery (RPO / RTO)","taxonomy_name":"disaster recovery (RPO / RTO)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["reliability/business-continuity","reliability/high-availability","reliability/multi-region-architecture","deployment/runbooks","data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","llmops-platform/rollback","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy"],"tldr":"Disaster recovery restores service after failures redundancy cannot mask. **RPO** is how much data,\nin time, you may lose; **RTO** is how long you may be down. Every design trades cost against both,\nand an unrehearsed plan is a hypothesis.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"business-continuity","key":"reliability/business-continuity","module":"reliability","name":"Business continuity","taxonomy_name":"business continuity","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["reliability/disaster-recovery","reliability/fmea","reliability/slas-slos","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","deployment/runbooks","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","system-design/model-fallback-strategies"],"tldr":"Business continuity keeps critical functions running through any disruption: outages, vendor failures,\nmodel deprecations, incidents, key people leaving. Disaster recovery is its technical subset. It starts\nfrom a business impact analysis, because mid-disruption nobody has time to decide what matters.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"durable-workflow-execution","key":"reliability/durable-workflow-execution","module":"reliability","name":"Durable workflow execution","taxonomy_name":"durable workflow execution","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","system-design/workflow-orchestration","system-design/saga-pattern-and-distributed-transactions","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","reliability/fault-tolerance"],"tldr":"Durable workflow execution persists a multi-step process as an append-only event history and replays\nit to resume after crashes, deploys and days-long waits. Orchestration code stays deterministic; side\neffects live in retried, idempotent activities. It suits slow, flaky, expensive agent loops.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"backpressure-and-load-shedding","key":"reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","module":"reliability","name":"Backpressure & load shedding","taxonomy_name":"backpressure & load shedding","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/backpressure","serving-inference/backpressure","node-ts-for-ai/streams-and-backpressure","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","reliability/bulkheads","reliability/circuit-breakers","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","serving-inference/rate-limiting"],"tldr":"Overload grows queues until latency beats every timeout and throughput collapses. **Backpressure**\npushes \"slow down\" upstream via bounded queues and 429/503; **load shedding** rejects some work early\nand cheaply so the rest meets its budget. Serving everyone means serving no one.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","key":"reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","module":"reliability","name":"Tenant-level quotas & noisy-neighbor isolation","taxonomy_name":"tenant-level quotas & noisy-neighbor isolation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["reliability/bulkheads","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","system-design/tenant-isolation","serving-inference/rate-limiting","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage"],"tldr":"Tenant-level quotas cap what each tenant may consume of shared pools (provider tokens, GPU concurrency,\nworkers); isolation (per-tenant lanes, fair queuing, separate pools) makes a tenant at its cap degrade\nonly itself, turning a shared failure into a local one.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"circuit-breakers","key":"reliability/circuit-breakers","module":"reliability","name":"Circuit breakers","taxonomy_name":"circuit breakers","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["reliability/bulkheads","reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/fallbacks","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","node-ts-for-ai/api-clients-with-retries-and-circuit-breakers"],"tldr":"A circuit breaker counts a dependency's recent failures and, past a threshold, fails fast for a\ncooling-off period, then lets a few trial calls test recovery. It trades a few wrongly rejected requests\nfor a caller that stays alive.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"bulkheads","key":"reliability/bulkheads","module":"reliability","name":"Bulkheads","taxonomy_name":"bulkheads","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["reliability/circuit-breakers","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","fastapi-for-ai/concurrency-limits","fastapi-for-ai/async-database-access-and-connection-pooling","infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory"],"tldr":"A bulkhead partitions a shared resource (workers, connections, queues, pods) per dependency, workload\nclass or tenant, so one exhausted compartment cannot drain the others. It bounds damage in advance, unlike\na breaker; the price is capacity that cannot be borrowed.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"fmea","key":"reliability/fmea","module":"reliability","name":"FMEA (Failure Mode and Effects Analysis)","taxonomy_name":"FMEA","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["reliability/chaos-engineering-and-resilience-testing","reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/slas-slos","delivery-ownership/failure-analysis","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management"],"tldr":"FMEA is a table of how each part can fail (modes), what happens (effects), why (causes) and how you would\nnotice (detection), ranked by Severity × Occurrence × Detection. For AI systems it is a postmortem written\nbefore the incident, aimed at silent failures.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"chaos-engineering-and-resilience-testing","key":"reliability/chaos-engineering-and-resilience-testing","module":"reliability","name":"Chaos engineering & resilience testing","taxonomy_name":"chaos engineering & resilience testing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["reliability/fmea","reliability/circuit-breakers","reliability/bulkheads","reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/disaster-recovery","serving-inference/fallbacks","deployment/runbooks","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems"],"tldr":"Resilience mechanisms (fallbacks, breakers, retries, failover) run rarely and rot silently. Resilience\ntesting exercises them deliberately; chaos engineering runs controlled experiments on real systems:\nhypothesis, bounded fault, measurement. For AI, faults include 429s, latency, stale retrieval and bad output.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"slas-slos","key":"reliability/slas-slos","module":"reliability","name":"SLAs / SLOs (and SLIs, error budgets)","taxonomy_name":"SLAs / SLOs","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["reliability/high-availability","reliability/fmea","delivery-ownership/slas","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","observability/latency-percentiles","observability/monitoring","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets"],"tldr":"An SLI measures what users care about; an SLO is its internal target over a window; an SLA is the looser\nexternal contract. The gap below 100 percent is the error budget. For AI, SLIs must capture \"useful\", not\n\"returned 200\".","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"architecture","stage_index":5,"track_order":18},{"id":"agent-runtime-architecture","tier":2,"order":18,"title":"Agent runtime architecture","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the parts of an agent system that sit around the model rather than inside it. It starts\nwith the runtime and orchestration layer: the loop of call model, dispatch tool, record result, rebuilt as a\nlong-lived, crash-safe unit of work that persists before it executes, pauses for approvals without holding a\nthread, and coordinates sub-agents, queues and budgets. Every later topic plugs a cross-cutting rule into\nthat layer's dispatch boundary. Approval and authorization boundaries decide whether a principal may take an\naction at all and whether a human must confirm this exact instance, with the approval bound to the precise\narguments. Policy enforcement generalises that check across many agents and tool servers by splitting one\npolicy decision point from many enforcement points and attenuating authority down a delegation chain.\nMulti-tenant state isolation inventories every place tenant data lands (step logs, memory, vector indexes,\ncaches, sandboxes, traces) and enforces scoping structurally at each store. Secrets and credential isolation\nkeeps credentials out of the context window entirely, minting short-lived, per-tenant tokens inside a\nseparate executor. Cross-language API contracts make the tool schemas, event streams and checkpoints that\ncross Python, TypeScript and the model itself one generated, validated source of truth. After the module you\ncan draw a production agent runtime layer by layer, explain where each control lives and why it cannot live\nin the prompt, and test that it holds. It matters most for Architects and AI Engineers shipping agents that\ntake real actions for more than one customer, and for FDEs who must pass a customer's security review.","why":{"engineer":"You will write the dispatcher, the step store, the tool adapters and the schema generation step, so you need to know why state is persisted before a tool runs, how a run context with tenant and user identity is threaded through every adapter, and how to prove with a kill test and a canary test that resume and isolation actually work.","architect":"You own the decision of where each control lives: one policy decision point versus checks scattered in agents, storage-level tenant scoping versus remembered filters, a token broker versus secrets in environment variables, and a neutral contract versus hand-kept types; those choices decide auditability, blast radius and how cheaply the system can add agents.","fde":"Customer security teams will ask who the agent acts as, what it can touch, who approves irreversible actions, where their data and tokens live and how deletion works; you need to answer with the architecture, configure approval tiers and connected-account scopes for their environment, and demonstrate the leak and revocation tests on their tenant."},"mental_model":"Picture the model as an untrusted planner sitting inside a ring, and the runtime as the ring. The model can\nonly propose: a tool name and some arguments. Everything that turns a proposal into an effect happens on the\nruntime side of one boundary, the tool-dispatch point, and every concern in this module is a check stacked\non that boundary. Before a call runs, the runtime has already written the step to durable state, attached a\nsigned run context (tenant, user, delegated scope, provenance of the data in play) that the model never\nproduced, asked one policy engine for a decision, and, if the action is consequential, parked the run until a\nhuman approves those exact arguments. The call then executes in a separate executor that resolves a named\nconnection to a short-lived credential and writes only into stores scoped by that context. The data crossing\nevery arrow follows one generated schema. If an action can reach a side effect without crossing that\nboundary, the design has a hole.","order":["agent-runtime-and-orchestration-layer","approval-and-authorization-boundaries","policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","multi-tenant-state-isolation","secrets-and-credential-isolation","cross-language-api-contracts"],"order_reasons":{"agent-runtime-and-orchestration-layer":"establishes the loop, durable step state and the dispatch boundary that every other control attaches to.","approval-and-authorization-boundaries":"the first and simplest check at that boundary: may this principal act, and does a human need to confirm this call.","policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools":"scales that single gate into a decision point with many enforcement points and attenuated delegation across agents.","multi-tenant-state-isolation":"applies the signed run context to every store the runtime writes, so tenant identity never comes from model output.","secrets-and-credential-isolation":"builds on tenant and user scoping to mint per-tenant, short-lived credentials the model can name but never see.","cross-language-api-contracts":"ties the layers together by making tool schemas, events, run context and checkpoints one validated contract across languages and the model."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a small multi-tenant agent runtime for a fictional helpdesk product in which an agent can read tickets,\nsearch a knowledge base, and take two consequential actions: issue a refund through a mock payments API and\nemail a customer through a mock mail API. Two tenants, each with two users of different roles, share the\ndeployment. The loop runs as a durable worker, not inside a request handler: each step is persisted before\nits tool executes, and killing the worker mid-run must resume without repeating a refund. Every tool call\npasses through one policy decision point that combines the user's rights with the agent's delegated scope;\nrefunds above a threshold and all outbound email park the run for approval bound to a fingerprint of the\narguments, with a TTL and re-authorization on resume. A planner agent may spawn a research sub-agent whose\nscope is attenuated from its parent. Tenant identity comes only from authentication and scopes the step store,\nthe vector index, a semantic cache and traces at the storage layer. Credentials for the mock APIs live in a\nvault-backed broker in a separate executor process. Tool definitions, run events and the run context are\ndefined once in a schema that generates both the Python runtime types and a TypeScript client that streams\nrun events to a minimal approvals UI.","deliverables":["A runnable repository with the durable worker, step store, policy decision point, tool executor process, token broker and approvals UI, started with one command against local mock services.","An architecture diagram of the layers and trust zones, plus a one-page surface inventory listing every store that holds tenant data and the storage-level mechanism that scopes it.","A `contracts/` directory with the shared schema, generated Python and TypeScript types, and a CI step that regenerates and fails on diff.","A test suite covering a forced-kill resume, a cross-tenant canary leak test across every listed store, a prompt-injected document that attempts an unapproved refund and email exfiltration, and an environment and filesystem scan proving no secret is reachable from the loop or sandbox.","A short write-up with measured steps per run, model versus tool time, policy-gate coverage, approval and reject rates per tool, and token TTL and revocation-to-last-use time."],"rubric":["Killing the worker at any step and restarting it completes the run with exactly one refund recorded in the mock payments API, shown by a test that repeats the kill at several points.","Every tool invocation in the step log has a matching logged policy decision (coverage is 100%), and a sub-agent's effective scope is provably a subset of its parent's; a test that asks the sub-agent to exceed it is denied.","Changing any argument of an approved refund after approval invalidates it, an expired approval cannot be used, and the injected-document test produces a denied or parked call, never an executed one.","The canary test seeds a unique string in tenant A in every store and confirms tenant B's runs, searches, cache hits and trace queries never return it; no tool accepts `tenant_id` as a model-supplied argument.","No credential value appears in prompts, tool arguments, traces, step logs or the sandbox environment, and revoking a tenant's connection stops its next tool call within the measured window.","Renaming a field in the schema without regenerating breaks CI, and the TypeScript client tolerates an unknown new event type without throwing."]}},"topics":[{"id":"agent-runtime-and-orchestration-layer","key":"agent-runtime-architecture/agent-runtime-and-orchestration-layer","module":"agent-runtime-architecture","name":"Agent runtime & orchestration layer","taxonomy_name":"agent runtime & orchestration layer","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["agents/agent-loop","agents/state-machines","agent-reliability/durable-execution-and-checkpointing","agent-reliability/structured-event-streams","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","system-design/agent-orchestration","llmops-platform/ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools"],"tldr":"The runtime owns the agent loop (call model, run tool, feed back) as a crash-safe, policy-checked unit of\nwork; orchestration coordinates many runs. Both exist because loop state outlives processes, tools have side\neffects, and rules need one enforcement point.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"approval-and-authorization-boundaries","key":"agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","module":"agent-runtime-architecture","name":"Approval & authorization boundaries","taxonomy_name":"approval & authorization boundaries","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/human-in-the-loop","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools"],"tldr":"Authorization asks \"may this principal do this at all\"; approval asks \"must a human confirm this instance\".\nThe runtime decides both at tool dispatch, outside the model's influence, using user-and-agent intersected\nauthority and approvals bound to exact arguments.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","key":"agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","module":"agent-runtime-architecture","name":"Policy enforcement across agents & tools","taxonomy_name":"policy enforcement across agents & tools","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","agents/multi-agent-patterns","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","security-governance-compliance/prompt-injection-defense","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","system-design/ai-gateway","customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails"],"tldr":"With many agents, tool servers and entry points, per-agent rules drift and get bypassed. Use one policy\ndecision point and many enforcement points that share a signed run context, and only ever attenuate a child\nagent's authority from its parent's.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"multi-tenant-state-isolation","key":"agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","module":"agent-runtime-architecture","name":"Multi-tenant state isolation","taxonomy_name":"multi-tenant state isolation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/multi-tenancy","system-design/tenant-isolation","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","agents/agent-memory","agents/sandboxing","agent-reliability/state-and-checkpoint-stores","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture"],"tldr":"Agent runtimes spread tenant data across step logs, memory, indexes, files, sandboxes, caches, traces and\nqueues, and leaks happen wherever a filter was forgotten. Take tenant identity from authentication, never\nthe model, and enforce it at each store's storage layer.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"secrets-and-credential-isolation","key":"agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","module":"agent-runtime-architecture","name":"Secrets & credential isolation","taxonomy_name":"secrets & credential isolation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","agents/sandboxing","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","safety-security/data-leakage","safety-security/prompt-injection","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation"],"tldr":"Assume the model will repeat anything in its context, so secrets never enter the context, the sandbox or\nmodel-filled arguments. The model names a connection; a separate executor mints a short-lived, scoped,\nper-tenant credential at call time and never returns it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"cross-language-api-contracts","key":"agent-runtime-architecture/cross-language-api-contracts","module":"agent-runtime-architecture","name":"Cross-language API contracts","taxonomy_name":"cross-language API contracts","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["agents/typed-tool-contracts","agents/tool-schemas-and-argument-validation","agents/model-context-protocol","python-for-ai/pydantic","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-contract-testing","python-for-ai/contract-testing","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility"],"tldr":"Agent systems are polyglot, and the model itself consumes JSON Schema. A contract is one machine-readable\nschema every side generates types and runtime validators from, with explicit rules for JSON's ambiguities\n(int64, dates, null vs absent, enums) and for safe evolution.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"architecture","stage_index":5,"track_order":19},{"id":"data-architecture","tier":2,"order":19,"title":"Data architecture","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the data side of an AI system: how bytes leave source systems, become retrieval\nunits, training examples and features, and how you can later say where each one came from and remove\nit when asked. It starts at the edge. Ingestion pipelines capture changes from sources (deletes\nincluded) into an immutable, replayable raw copy with a committed watermark, and the lakehouse gives\nthat copy and every derived table atomic commits, snapshots, time travel and real deletes on cheap\nobject storage. Staged ELT then turns raw data into materialized parse, chunk, enrich and embed tables,\ndocument processing pipelines route each file to the cheapest extractor that keeps its structure, and\nknowledge management decides which sources are canonical, who owns them and how documents age out.\nData contracts make producers responsible for the schema and meaning their consumers rely on. On the\nserving side, embedding versioning treats the model, its settings and the chunker as part of an index's\nidentity, vector database architecture explains mutability, filtered search and memory residency, and\nvector store sizing turns a corpus into a RAM and cost number before you choose a vendor. Training data\npipelines produce deduplicated, decontaminated, versioned shards. Lineage, provenance and retention\ntie everything together: a recorded graph of what was built from what, tamper-evident manifests for\nmodels and datasets, and a deletion that reaches every copy with proof. Feature stores close the module\nfor hybrid systems that also run classical models. Afterwards you can design a RAG and fine-tuning\ndata platform that re-indexes without downtime, survives upstream schema changes, and honours an\nerasure request across every store. It matters most for Architects, and for Engineers who own the\npipelines.","why":{"engineer":"You write the change capture, the content-hashed stage tables, the extraction router, the alias swap and the deletion job, so stale chunks, re-embedding bills that scale with the corpus and documents that are never removed are bugs in your pipeline code.","architect":"You decide what is the system of record and what is a rebuildable projection, how an index version is identified, where lineage is emitted and how deletion propagates, and these choices are a full rebuild to change once the first index is live.","fde":"Customer corpora are messy, mixed-format and unowned, and their first hard questions are about cost, freshness and erasure; you answer with a sizing estimate, a canonical-source agreement, a contract on their feeds and a deletion drill run on their own data."},"mental_model":"Picture one directed graph that runs left to right. On the left are source systems; the first node is an\nimmutable raw copy keyed by source identity and content hash. Every node after it is a derived,\nversioned table produced by a job: parsed blocks, chunks, embeddings, training shards, features. On the\nright are serving projections: a vector index, an online feature store, a fine-tuned checkpoint. Three\nrules hold everywhere. Only the raw copy is the system of record; everything to its right can be\nrebuilt, so a new chunker or embedding model is a re-run into a new version beside the old one, followed\nby an evaluated swap. Every edge is recorded by the job that walked it, which is lineage, and every\nartifact that reaches a registry carries a hashed manifest, which is provenance. And every edge carries\na contract and a retention class, so a schema change fails loudly at the producer and a deletion request\nis a forward traversal from one source record to every node derived from it. Contracts and knowledge\nmanagement govern what enters on the left; sizing and vector architecture govern what it costs on the\nright.","order":["ingestion-pipelines","lakehouse","etl-elt-for-rag","document-processing-pipelines","knowledge-management","data-contracts","embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","vector-database-architecture","vector-store-sizing","training-data-pipelines","data-lineage","model-and-dataset-provenance","data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","feature-stores"],"order_reasons":{"ingestion-pipelines":"everything downstream depends on an incremental, delete-aware, replayable raw copy, so getting bytes out of sources comes first.","lakehouse":"gives the raw copy and the stage tables transactional commits, snapshots and real deletes, which the later lessons assume.","etl-elt-for-rag":"establishes the staged, content-addressed transformation DAG and the rule that the index is a projection, not the source.","document-processing-pipelines":"fills in the parse stage of that DAG with tiered routing, quality scoring and a canonical block model.","knowledge-management":"decides which sources are canonical and what ownership and freshness metadata the ingestion schema must carry.","data-contracts":"makes producers responsible for the schema and meaning the pipeline stages now depend on, catching silent breakage at write time.","embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy":"treats model, settings and chunker as the index's identity and teaches build-beside, evaluate and alias swap over the stored chunks.","vector-database-architecture":"explains how the serving index handles updates, deletes, selective filters, memory residency and sharding.","vector-store-sizing":"turns the corpus, chunking and embedding choices into a RAM and cost estimate, using the index mechanics just covered.","training-data-pipelines":"applies the same staged, versioned approach to fine-tuning data, adding dedup, decontamination and packing.","data-lineage":"records the graph of inputs, jobs and versions across both the RAG and training paths built so far.","model-and-dataset-provenance":"adds hashed, signed manifests for registry artifacts on top of lineage, so models and datasets can be verified.","data-retention-and-deletion-architecture":"uses the store inventory, lineage and versioned tables to propagate an erasure request with receipts.","feature-stores":"an optional extension for hybrid systems that also train and serve classical models, best read once the rest is in place."},"capstone":{"brief":"A mid-sized software company wants an internal assistant over its help-centre articles (HTML), a\nshared drive of policy PDFs (some scanned, some with tables) and a Postgres table of resolved support\ntickets, plus a small fine-tuned classifier that routes new tickets. Today a nightly script re-crawls\neverything, embeds it into one vector collection and never removes deleted documents. Over a week,\nrebuild it as a data platform. Ingest each source incrementally into an immutable raw table with\ncontent hashes and tombstones, and run a staged ELT DAG (parse, clean, chunk, embed) on lakehouse or\nPostgres tables, with a tiered document router that escalates to OCR only when a quality score fails.\nTag canonical sources and owners, and put a data contract with a CI compatibility check on the ticket\nfeed. Version the embedding recipe, build a second index with a different chunker beside the first,\nevaluate both on a fixed query set and swap an alias. Size the store before choosing pgvector or a\ndedicated engine. Build a deduplicated, eval-decontaminated training set for the classifier with a\nmanifest hash, record lineage for every job, and finish with a deletion drill for one customer.","deliverables":["A pipeline repository with per-source change capture, a raw landing table keyed by source id and content hash, a committed watermark, and stage tables for parsed blocks, chunks and embeddings that a second run fills incrementally.","A document-routing report showing per-tier volume, cost, quality-score distribution and escalation rate, with extraction accuracy measured on a small hand-labelled gold set.","A data contract file for the ticket feed with a CI check that rejects a breaking change, plus a source registry listing canonical status, owner and `last_verified_at` for each source.","A sizing worksheet with explicit assumptions (chunks per document from a sample, dimension, precision, graph overhead, payload, replicas, rebuild headroom) and the resulting store choice, and an index-version record with eval recall for both index versions and the alias swap.","A training-set manifest for the routing classifier giving dataset hash, removal counts per stage, overlap rate with the eval set and base model revision, and a lineage table that connects every chunk and training row to its source record and job run.","A deletion runbook and a drill report for one customer, listing every store reached, a receipt per store and the time taken, with an explicit statement about any fine-tuned weights."],"rubric":["Deleting a policy PDF at the source removes its chunks from the live index on the next run, and a second run with no source changes re-embeds zero chunks, shown by the per-run change ratio and embedding cache hit rate.","Renaming a field in the ticket feed fails the producer's CI contract check before any data lands, rather than producing empty chunks or blank training targets downstream.","Live queries are served from exactly one index version throughout the re-index, the swap is a single alias change that can be reversed, and both versions report recall on the same fixed query set.","The sizing estimate is within a stated margin of the store's measured memory after loading, and the worksheet names quantization and payload placement as the levers with their effect on the number.","Starting from one customer id, a lineage traversal lists every derived chunk, cache entry, eval row and training row, and the drill report shows each of them deleted or tombstoned with a receipt.","The classifier's training config references the dataset manifest hash, and re-running the training-data pipeline from the same raw snapshot reproduces that hash."]}},"topics":[{"id":"ingestion-pipelines","key":"data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","module":"data-architecture","name":"Ingestion pipelines","taxonomy_name":"ingestion pipelines","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","data-architecture/data-contracts","data-architecture/data-lineage","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","enterprise-integration/data-ingestion-from-client-systems","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation"],"tldr":"An ingestion pipeline moves data from source systems into a durable, replayable landing store. What\nsurvives production: incremental change capture including deletes, an immutable raw copy keyed by source\nidentity and content hash, and idempotent stages with a committed watermark.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"etl-elt-for-rag","key":"data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","module":"data-architecture","name":"ETL / ELT for RAG","taxonomy_name":"ETL / ELT for RAG","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","data-architecture/lakehouse","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/contextual-retrieval","system-design/rag-architecture"],"tldr":"ETL transforms before loading; ELT loads raw first and transforms after. For RAG, load raw, then run a\nstaged DAG (parse, clean, chunk, enrich, embed, index) of materialized, content-addressed tables. The\nvector index is a rebuildable projection, never the system of record.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"training-data-pipelines","key":"data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","module":"data-architecture","name":"Training data pipelines","taxonomy_name":"training data pipelines","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["training-adaptation/data-curation","training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/data-labeling","evals/dataset-contamination","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","data-architecture/lakehouse","model-internals/tokenization"],"tldr":"A training data pipeline turns raw sources into versioned, deduplicated, decontaminated, token-ready\nshards with provenance. What you remove shapes the model as much as what you add, runs must reproduce\nfrom a dataset hash, and offline packing keeps GPUs fed.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"lakehouse","key":"data-architecture/lakehouse","module":"data-architecture","name":"Lakehouse","taxonomy_name":"lakehouse","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","data-architecture/data-lineage","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","backend-fundamentals/database-design","system-design/data-consistency","security-governance-compliance/data-residency"],"tldr":"A lakehouse is a transactional table layer (Delta Lake, Apache Iceberg, Apache Hudi) over open Parquet\nfiles in object storage. It adds atomic commits, snapshot isolation, schema enforcement, time travel and\nreal deletes: the shared home for AI documents, chunks, embeddings, training sets and traces.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"document-processing-pipelines","key":"data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","module":"data-architecture","name":"Document processing pipelines","taxonomy_name":"document processing pipelines","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/document-parsing","multimodal/ocr","multimodal/document-understanding","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","rag-retrieval/parent-document-retrieval"],"tldr":"A document processing pipeline turns mixed files into one canonical block structure. It is a tiered\nrouter: detect what each page really is, use the cheapest extractor that preserves structure, score the\noutput, and escalate (native text, OCR, layout model, vision LLM) only on failure.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"vector-database-architecture","key":"data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","module":"data-architecture","name":"Vector database architecture","taxonomy_name":"vector database architecture","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/vector-databases","rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/hybrid-search","data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency"],"tldr":"A vector database is an ANN index, a payload store and a query planner bolted together. The hard parts are\nnot distance math but mutability, filtered search, memory residency and distribution; understand those\nfour and any vendor comparison becomes a checklist.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"vector-store-sizing","key":"data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","module":"data-architecture","name":"Vector store sizing","taxonomy_name":"vector store sizing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","rag-retrieval/ann-indexes","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","rag-retrieval/chunking-strategies","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation"],"tldr":"Vector store cost is set by RAM-resident bytes: `vectors × (dim × bytes_per_dim + graph_overhead +\npayload)`, times replicas and build headroom. Every input is knowable before choosing a vendor, and the\narithmetic often flips the choice between pgvector and a dedicated cluster.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","key":"data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","module":"data-architecture","name":"Embedding versioning / re-indexing strategy","taxonomy_name":"embedding versioning / re-indexing strategy","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["rag-retrieval/embedding-models","rag-retrieval/embedding-pipelines","rag-retrieval/embedding-dimension-and-normalization","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","evals/retrieval-evaluation"],"tldr":"A vector only means something relative to the recipe (model, settings, chunker) that produced it, so a\nrecipe change means re-embedding everything. Version the recipe, build beside the old index, evaluate,\nswap atomically, and hash content to re-embed only changed chunks.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"knowledge-management","key":"data-architecture/knowledge-management","module":"data-architecture","name":"Knowledge management","taxonomy_name":"knowledge management","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/metadata-filtering","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","enterprise-integration/data-ingestion-from-client-systems","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding"],"tldr":"Retrieval cannot beat its corpus, and enterprise corpora are stale, duplicated and unowned. Knowledge\nmanagement decides canonical sources, owners, lifecycle and metadata, so the retriever chooses among\ncorrect candidates; many \"hallucination\" reports trace here, not to the model.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-lineage","key":"data-architecture/data-lineage","module":"data-architecture","name":"Data lineage","taxonomy_name":"data lineage","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","data-architecture/data-contracts","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","observability/tracing","security-governance-compliance/audit-trails","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding"],"tldr":"Data lineage is the recorded graph of where every dataset, chunk, feature and model came from: inputs,\njob, code version, run. Production questions like \"why did this answer change\" are graph traversals, so\nlineage is what makes deletion, debugging and reproducibility possible.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-contracts","key":"data-architecture/data-contracts","module":"data-architecture","name":"Data contracts","taxonomy_name":"data contracts","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","data-architecture/data-lineage","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation","python-for-ai/contract-testing","agents/typed-tool-contracts"],"tldr":"A data contract is a versioned, machine-checked agreement between a dataset's producer and its consumers:\nschema, meaning, quality, freshness, ownership. AI pipelines fail silently on upstream changes, so the\ncontract moves failure to the producer at write time: loud and cheap.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-and-dataset-provenance","key":"data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","module":"data-architecture","name":"Model & dataset provenance","taxonomy_name":"model & dataset provenance","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["data-architecture/data-lineage","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","llmops-platform/model-registry","security-governance-compliance/model-cards","security-governance-compliance/model-governance","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","evals/dataset-contamination","training-adaptation/data-curation"],"tldr":"Provenance is the verifiable record of a model's or dataset's origin: exact input bytes, code,\nparameters, builder, licence and evaluations. A checkpoint is opaque about what it saw, so provenance\nreplaces \"trust me\" with hashes, manifests and signatures.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","key":"data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","module":"data-architecture","name":"Data retention & deletion architecture","taxonomy_name":"data retention & deletion architecture","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["data-architecture/data-lineage","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","agents/memory-retrieval-update-deletion-policies","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","safety-security/pii-redaction","serving-inference/caching"],"tldr":"LLM apps copy user data into logs, traces, indexes, caches, eval and training sets, vendor logs and\nweights. This architecture inventories every copy, gives each a retention class and TTL, and executes\ndeletion across all of them with proof, designed in from the start.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"feature-stores","key":"data-architecture/feature-stores","module":"data-architecture","name":"Feature stores (hybrid ML only)","taxonomy_name":"feature stores (hybrid ML only)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","data-architecture/lakehouse","data-architecture/data-contracts","data-architecture/data-lineage","llmops-platform/drift","llmops-platform/model-registry","system-design/data-consistency"],"tldr":"Caveat first: this matters only for hybrid systems serving classical ML models beside LLMs. A feature store\ncomputes features once, serves them point-in-time correct to training and fresh to inference, and versions\ndefinitions, preventing training/serving skew and time leakage.","format":"crisp","caveat":"hybrid ML only; drop for LLM-app roles","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"architecture","stage_index":5,"track_order":20},{"id":"infra-capacity-economics","tier":2,"order":20,"title":"Infra, capacity & economics","intro":{"overview":"This module is about the physical and financial reality under every AI feature: where the model\nruns, how much of it you need, and whether the result pays for itself. It starts with the hardware.\nGPU infrastructure is the stack from card to driver to scheduler. GPU sizing turns a model config into\nbytes of weights and KV cache. The roofline explains why decode is memory-bandwidth-bound and why\n\"GPU-Util\" lies. Card selection asks which constraint binds first: fit, bandwidth or cost per token.\nNext comes capacity: queueing theory and Little's law predict the latency knee, capacity planning\nconverts token distributions and an SLO into replicas or quota, latency budgets split one target\nacross every hop, and the Universal Scalability Law shows where adding replicas stops helping. The\ndeployment lessons cover inference clusters, KEDA, serverless GPUs, Ray, shared versus isolated\nserving, provider quotas, cloud AI platforms, hybrid on-prem and edge. The economics lessons build\ncost models, cost per successful task, allocation by tenant and feature, FinOps, ordered optimization,\nthe quality-latency-cost frontier, break-even and TCO. Afterwards you can size a deployment, defend a\nreplica count or quota request, choose between API, self-hosted, hybrid and edge with numbers, and\nshow what a successful task costs and who caused the cost. It matters most for Architects. Engineers\nneed it to run serving and metering, and FDEs need it for customer sizing and pricing.","why":{"engineer":"You derive KV bytes per token from the model config before an OOM does it for you, read DCGM SM and DRAM activity instead of GPU-Util, scale inference replicas on queue depth and KV usage through KEDA rather than CPU, and emit one tagged cost event per model call so spend reaches the right tenant and feature.","architect":"You turn token histograms and a p95 TTFT target into a replica count with headroom below the queueing knee, choose card, region, quota and shared or isolated pools on the binding constraint, and back every build-versus-buy or on-prem decision with a break-even band and a TCO that includes people, evals and exit cost.","fde":"You size a customer's deployment from their real document lengths and peak hour, file quota increases weeks before launch, place the endpoint inside their Bedrock, Azure or on-prem perimeter, and show the sponsor cost per successful task against the task's value, so the pricing and ROI conversation rests on their numbers."},"mental_model":"Picture one pipe with three gauges. Requests enter at the left as tokens, not calls, and every\nrequest has a shape: input length, output length, turns and context. The pipe's width is set by\nphysical limits: GPU memory decides how many sequences fit at once, memory bandwidth decides how fast\neach one decodes, and provider quotas cap what a hosted pool will give you. Queueing theory says what\nhappens as the pipe fills: wait time stays flat and then jumps at the knee, so you run below it on\npurpose and buy headroom. The three gauges on the pipe are quality, latency and cost, and every lever\nmoves at least two of them. Batching, quantisation, smaller models, caching, serverless and shared\npools all trade one gauge against another. Underneath sits a ledger that divides all spend, including\nfailures, retries and idle capacity, by tasks that succeeded, and assigns each dollar to a tenant and\nfeature. Infrastructure decisions are sizing the pipe, and economic decisions are reading the ledger.\nNeither is right unless both agree.","order":["gpu-infrastructure","gpu-sizing","gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","a100-h100-l4-selection","queueing-theory","capacity-planning","latency-budgets","scalability","inference-clusters","keda-autoscaling","serverless-gpus","ray-kuberay","shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","cloud-ai-architecture","hybrid-on-prem","edge-ai-deployment","cost-modeling","unit-economics-per-successful-task","cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","finops","cost-optimization","quality-latency-cost-optimization","break-even-analysis","tco"],"order_reasons":{"gpu-infrastructure":"the layer map (card, host, driver, runtime, scheduler, procurement) that every later hardware lesson plugs into.","gpu-sizing":"the weights-plus-KV-cache arithmetic that decides card count, precision and promised concurrency.","gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth":"the roofline explains why decode is bandwidth-bound, which sizing alone does not show.","a100-h100-l4-selection":"applies fit, bandwidth and cost per token from the previous two lessons to a concrete card choice.","queueing-theory":"Little's law and the utilization knee, the reason no capacity plan targets \"average load fits\".","capacity-planning":"combines queueing with measured per-replica capacity to turn token demand and an SLO into replicas or quota.","latency-budgets":"splits the user-facing target across every hop, so capacity work knows how much time the model actually gets.","scalability":"the USL shows where adding replicas stops paying off and which shared resource is to blame.","inference-clusters":"the replica fleet, router and LLM-specific scaling signals that put the capacity plan into practice.","keda-autoscaling":"the Kubernetes mechanism that scales those replicas on queue depth and to zero, and its cold-start limits.","serverless-gpus":"the per-second alternative for bursty load, decided by utilization break-even and cold-start tolerance.","ray-kuberay":"the distributed Python runtime for heterogeneous batch, embedding and multi-node workloads a plain Deployment handles poorly.","shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure":"when one batched pool serves every tenant and when noisy neighbours or contracts force isolation.","api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits":"capacity planning on hosted APIs is quota planning, with 429s, headroom and reserved throughput.","cloud-ai-architecture":"places a managed model endpoint inside a cloud's identity, network and per-region quota perimeter.","hybrid-on-prem":"routes restricted data to owned GPUs and the rest to hosted APIs, and sizes the fixed pool so it stays busy.","edge-ai-deployment":"the far end of the placement spectrum, sizing small models to device memory and bandwidth with a cloud fallback.","cost-modeling":"turns request shape (fresh, cached, output and reasoning tokens, plus agent turns) into dollars from logged distributions.","unit-economics-per-successful-task":"divides the full cost, failures included, by tasks that met the bar, the number every later decision uses.","cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature":"assigns direct and shared spend to whoever caused it, so unit economics exist per tenant.","finops":"the ongoing inform–optimize–operate practice that keeps metering, allocation and budgets running after the first analysis.","cost-optimization":"the ordered list of levers (avoid, shrink, route, buy cheaper, tune), each gated by an eval.","quality-latency-cost-optimization":"chooses from the Pareto frontier across all three gauges instead of optimizing cost alone.","break-even-analysis":"the API-versus-self-host crossover, driven by utilization and team cost rather than GPU list price.","tco":"the widest frame, adding people, eval upkeep, forced migrations and exit cost over a fixed horizon to every option."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are the architect for a B2B SaaS company whose contract-review assistant extracts clauses and\nflags risks in uploaded agreements. It runs on a hosted frontier API in one region and costs $48k a\nmonth. Three things have just happened: the largest tenant, 40% of volume, now requires EU data\nresidency; the provider's tokens-per-minute quota throttled the Monday morning peak twice last\nmonth; and finance wants to know whether self-hosting an open-weight 70B model would be cheaper.\nOver a week, using a synthetic request log with realistic input and output token histograms, tenant\nIDs, timestamps and task outcomes (generate it or use the one provided), produce a decision that holds\nup under review. Size the model on candidate cards, build the capacity plan and latency budget, model\ncost per successful task by tenant, compare hosted, self-hosted and hybrid options on break-even and\nTCO, and recommend a serving shape with its scaling and quota plan.","deliverables":["A sizing sheet for the 70B candidate showing weights and KV bytes per token derived from the model config, concurrency per card at the p95 context length on L4, A100 and H100 at bf16 and fp8, and the roofline time per output token compared with a measured or published figure.","A capacity plan that converts the log's peak hour into requests/s, input and output tokens/s and in-flight sequences via Little's law, sets per-replica capacity at the TTFT and TPOT SLO, gives a replica count with headroom justified by queueing, and converts the hosted path into TPM and RPM per region with a quota-increase request.","A latency budget for the review path (upload, parsing, retrieval, guardrail, model queue and prefill, decode) with per-hop p95 allocations, a reserve, and the steps that degrade under deadline pressure.","A cost model and allocation report giving cost per successful task by tenant and feature, with retries, failed extractions and human review included, shared GPU cost allocated by GPU-seconds, and the unattributed share stated.","A break-even and TCO comparison of hosted API, self-hosted and hybrid (EU tenant on owned or regional capacity, the rest hosted) over 24 months, with a sensitivity table on utilization, team cost and an API price cut.","A serving design with an autoscaling configuration (KEDA or equivalent) keyed on queue depth and KV usage, a shared-versus-isolated decision for the large tenant, and a one-page recommendation that names the premium paid for the chosen option."],"rubric":["Concurrency per card is derived from bytes per token and free memory after weights, and the engine's KV-block count, or a published figure for the same engine, is within about 20% of the estimate, or the gap is explained.","The replica count targets a utilization below the queueing knee with the wait-time consequence shown, and plans for peak demand rather than the daily average.","Every cost figure is per successful task rather than per call, and at least one option that looks cheaper per call is shown to cost more once failures and retries are counted.","Break-even is reported as a band with the assumption that flips the recommendation named explicitly, and the TCO includes people, eval upkeep and migration or exit cost.","The autoscaling rule never uses CPU or GPU-Util as its signal, and its threshold comes from a measured per-replica capacity curve.","The residency requirement is enforced at the gateway by data classification, and the large tenant's isolation decision cites a measured noisy-neighbour effect or a contract term, not preference."]}},"topics":[{"id":"gpu-infrastructure","key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"GPU infrastructure","taxonomy_name":"GPU infrastructure","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":12,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","infra-capacity-economics/a100-h100-l4-selection","infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","serving-inference/engines"],"tldr":"A GPU deployment is a stack, not a card: accelerator, host, driver and CUDA, container runtime,\nscheduler and procurement. Each layer fails differently, and most \"the GPU is slow\" tickets are really\nhost, driver or scheduling problems.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"gpu-sizing","key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"GPU sizing (VRAM math)","taxonomy_name":"GPU sizing (VRAM math)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["model-internals/kv-cache","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","training-adaptation/quantization","training-adaptation/distributed-training","infra-capacity-economics/a100-h100-l4-selection"],"tldr":"Inference VRAM is weights + KV cache + activations + overhead; training adds gradients and optimizer\nstate, about 16 bytes per parameter with mixed-precision Adam. A minute of arithmetic picks card, count,\nprecision and concurrency, and prevents both OOMs and half-empty 80 GB cards.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"GPU utilization & memory bandwidth","taxonomy_name":"GPU utilization & memory bandwidth","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","serving-inference/tpot","model-internals/flashattention","model-internals/kv-cache","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing"],"tldr":"`nvidia-smi` GPU-Util says a kernel ran, not that the chip was busy. Decode does about one FLOP per\nweight byte per sequence against roofline ridges of ~150-300, so small batches are bandwidth-bound:\nbatch more or move fewer bytes.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"a100-h100-l4-selection","key":"infra-capacity-economics/a100-h100-l4-selection","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"A100 / H100 / L4 selection","taxonomy_name":"A100 / H100 / L4 selection","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/tco","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation","training-adaptation/quantization"],"tldr":"Pick the card by the first binding constraint: fit (memory), per-stream decode (bandwidth), then cost\nper token at real load. L4: cheap 24 GB. A100: 40/80 GB, no FP8. H100: 80 GB, ~3.35 TB/s, FP8,\ncheapest per token when busy.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"serverless-gpus","key":"infra-capacity-economics/serverless-gpus","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Serverless GPUs","taxonomy_name":"serverless GPUs","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/model-serving"],"tldr":"Serverless GPUs bill per second and scale to zero, so bursty workloads pay only for busy seconds. The\ncost: cold starts of tens of seconds to minutes and a rate above on-demand. Decide on utilisation\nbreak-even, gated by your latency SLO.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"keda-autoscaling","key":"infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"KEDA autoscaling","taxonomy_name":"KEDA autoscaling","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/serverless-gpus","infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/ttft","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding"],"tldr":"KEDA scales Kubernetes workloads on real demand signals (queue depth, in-flight requests, Prometheus\nqueries) and down to zero, which LLM servers need because CPU and GPU utilisation mislead. It moves pod\ncounts only: nodes and GPU cold starts still take minutes.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"ray-kuberay","key":"infra-capacity-economics/ray-kuberay","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Ray / KubeRay","taxonomy_name":"Ray / KubeRay","kind":"tool","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","serving-inference/model-serving","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","training-adaptation/distributed-training"],"tldr":"Ray turns Python functions and classes into distributed tasks and stateful actors, scheduled by declared\nresources such as GPUs, for mixed CPU/GPU AI pipelines that Spark and job queues handle poorly. KubeRay\nruns Ray clusters, jobs and Serve apps as Kubernetes custom resources.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"inference-clusters","key":"infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Inference clusters","taxonomy_name":"inference clusters","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","infra-capacity-economics/ray-kuberay","infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","serving-inference/prefix-caching","serving-inference/engines"],"tldr":"An inference cluster is a fleet of model-server replicas (vLLM, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, Triton) behind a\nrouter, on GPU nodes, scaled on LLM-specific signals. One GPU box caps out early, and long-lived,\nstateful requests break round-robin balancing and CPU autoscaling.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","key":"infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Shared vs isolated model-serving infrastructure","taxonomy_name":"shared vs isolated model-serving infrastructure","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","system-design/multi-tenancy","system-design/tenant-isolation","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","reliability/bulkheads","serving-inference/continuous-batching","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage"],"tldr":"Shared serving runs every tenant through one pool of replicas; isolated serving gives a tenant its own\nreplicas, nodes, cluster or account. Sharing wins on cost; isolation wins on noisy neighbours,\ncompliance and custom configs. It is a spectrum, and most platforms use both.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"cloud-ai-architecture","key":"infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Cloud AI architecture (AWS Bedrock, Azure OpenAI, Vertex AI)","taxonomy_name":"cloud AI architecture (AWS Bedrock, Azure OpenAI, Vertex AI)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","deployment/private-endpoints","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy"],"tldr":"Bedrock, Azure OpenAI and Vertex AI serve models as a managed API inside your cloud account's identity,\nnetwork, logging and billing perimeter. You gain solved procurement and compliance; you pay with model\nlag, per-region quotas and more lock-in.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","key":"infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"API provider quotas & regional limits","taxonomy_name":"API provider quotas & regional limits","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","serving-inference/rate-limiting","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/fallbacks","reliability/multi-region-architecture","reliability/circuit-breakers","system-design/ai-gateway"],"tldr":"Hosted model APIs cap each account's requests and tokens per minute, per model, per region, by spend\ntier. Exceed them and you get 429s; a full pool fails regardless. So API capacity planning is quota\nplanning: measure, keep headroom, spread regions, reserve the base.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"hybrid-on-prem","key":"infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Hybrid / on-prem","taxonomy_name":"hybrid / on-prem","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","infra-capacity-economics/tco","system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/model-routing-cascading","deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","security-governance-compliance/data-residency"],"tldr":"Hybrid AI infrastructure runs some inference on hardware you control and the rest on hosted APIs, a\ngateway routing each request by data class, latency and model need. Some data cannot leave and steady\nvolume is cheaper owned; on-prem alone loses frontier models.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"edge-ai-deployment","key":"infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Edge AI deployment","taxonomy_name":"edge AI deployment","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","model-internals/small-language-models","training-adaptation/quantization","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","system-design/slm-vs-llm-trade-offs","serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation","serving-inference/engines"],"tldr":"Edge AI runs the model on the device that has the data: phone, laptop, browser, vehicle, controller. It\nbuys latency, offline use, privacy and zero cloud cost, at the price of hard memory, bandwidth and power\nbudgets: small compressed models, device runtimes, cloud fallback.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"capacity-planning","key":"infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Capacity planning (tokens/sec, requests/sec, concurrent sessions)","taxonomy_name":"capacity planning (tokens/sec, requests/sec, concurrent sessions)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency"],"tldr":"One LLM request can cost a hundred times another, so plan in three linked units: requests/sec,\ninput and output tokens/sec, and concurrent sequences. Little's Law links them; a load test at the SLO\ngives replica capacity; replicas = peak ÷ capacity, plus headroom.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"queueing-theory","key":"infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Queueing theory","taxonomy_name":"queueing theory","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","infra-capacity-economics/scalability","serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","serving-inference/continuous-batching","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","observability/latency-percentiles"],"tldr":"Random arrivals plus variable service times mean waiting, and waiting grows non-linearly near 100%\nutilization: in a 16-slot fleet, 75% to 95% busy multiplies mean queue wait about twentyfold. Little's\nlaw, M/M/c and Kingman predict that knee early.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"latency-budgets","key":"infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Latency budgets","taxonomy_name":"latency budgets","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["reliability/slas-slos","observability/latency-percentiles","serving-inference/ttft","serving-inference/timeouts","serving-inference/streaming","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services"],"tldr":"A latency budget splits one user-facing target, such as \"first token within 2 s at p95\", across every\nhop plus a reserve. Without it each team spends the whole budget alone; with it you get timeouts,\ndeadline propagation and rules for what to skip.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"scalability","key":"infra-capacity-economics/scalability","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Scalability","taxonomy_name":"scalability","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","serving-inference/continuous-batching","serving-inference/prefix-caching","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding"],"tldr":"Scalability is how throughput grows as you add resources, and it is almost never linear: contention\nserializes work and coherency costs grow with workers. In AI systems the walls are GPU memory, provider\nquotas, cache affinity and shared stores, not the stateless app tier.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"cost-optimization","key":"infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Cost optimization","taxonomy_name":"cost optimization","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","system-design/model-routing-cascading","prompting/prompt-caching","serving-inference/caching","evals/regression-tests","infra-capacity-economics/finops"],"tldr":"Cost optimization cuts spend per unit of useful work without breaking quality or latency. Work in order:\nmeasure, avoid calls, send fewer tokens, route easy work to cheaper models, buy capacity cheaper, then\ntune serving, each change gated by an eval.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"cost-modeling","key":"infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Cost modeling (cost per request, token economics)","taxonomy_name":"cost modeling (cost per request, token economics)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","observability/token-cost-tracking","prompting/prompt-caching","model-internals/tokenization","agent-reliability/max-step-budgets","infra-capacity-economics/tco"],"tldr":"A cost model turns request shape into dollars: fresh, cached and output tokens (including hidden\nreasoning) times prices, plus non-model calls. Output costs more because decode is sequential, agent\ncost grows roughly quadratically with turns, and the heavy tail sets the bill.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"unit-economics-per-successful-task","key":"infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Unit economics per successful task","taxonomy_name":"unit economics per successful task","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","agent-reliability/task-completion-rate","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","agents/human-in-the-loop","scoping/success-metrics-kpis"],"tldr":"Cost per successful task divides everything a unit of work costs (failed attempts, retries,\nverification, human fallback, damage from missed errors) by tasks meeting an agreed acceptance bar.\nCompare it with task value to know whether the product makes money.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","key":"infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Cost allocation by tenant & feature","taxonomy_name":"cost allocation by tenant & feature","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/finops","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","observability/token-cost-tracking","system-design/ai-gateway","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","observability/opentelemetry"],"tldr":"Cost allocation assigns every dollar of AI spend to the tenant and feature that caused it: token costs\nby tagging each call, shared GPU fleets and stores by an explicit driver. Without it you cannot price\ntiers or find unprofitable customers.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"quality-latency-cost-optimization","key":"infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Quality–latency–cost optimization","taxonomy_name":"quality–latency–cost optimization","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/model-routing-cascading","system-design/model-selection","prompting/prompt-caching","infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization","training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation"],"tldr":"Every LLM feature trades answer quality, user wait and per-request cost; levers that improve one axis\nalmost always degrade another, so you cannot maximize all three. Measure all three per task, discard\ndominated configurations, and choose from the Pareto frontier under hard constraints.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"break-even-analysis","key":"infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"Break-even analysis (hosted API vs self-hosting)","taxonomy_name":"break-even analysis (hosted API vs self-hosting)","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","infra-capacity-economics/tco","infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits"],"tldr":"A hosted API is variable cost, nothing when idle; self-hosting is mostly fixed GPU and people cost. Break-even is the monthly\nvolume where the curves cross, and it hinges on achievable utilization and team cost far more than on GPU\nlist price.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"finops","key":"infra-capacity-economics/finops","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"FinOps","taxonomy_name":"FinOps","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["observability/token-cost-tracking","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization","infra-capacity-economics/tco","system-design/ai-gateway","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling"],"tldr":"FinOps makes variable cloud and AI spend visible, attributed and owned by the teams that cause it. For LLM\nsystems: meter every call at the gateway, allocate by tenant and feature, track cost per successful task,\nand run a continuous inform–optimize–operate loop.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"},{"id":"tco","key":"infra-capacity-economics/tco","module":"infra-capacity-economics","name":"TCO (total cost of ownership)","taxonomy_name":"TCO","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/finops","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","system-design/build-vs-buy","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy"],"tldr":"Total cost of ownership is every cost an option incurs over a fixed horizon: acquiring, running, changing\nand leaving it. For AI systems people, eval upkeep, forced migrations and exit usually outweigh the\ninvoice, so compare equal outcomes and report a range.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp-verified","status":"ok"}],"stage":"architecture","stage_index":5,"track_order":21},{"id":"llmops-platform","tier":2,"order":21,"title":"LLMOps & AI platform","intro":{"overview":"This module covers what it takes to change an LLM application safely and repeatedly once it is in\nproduction and more than one team depends on it. The unit of release is not a container image but a\nbundle: code, prompt version, model version or provider snapshot, decoding parameters, retrieval index\nand guardrail policy, any one of which can change behaviour without touching the others. The module\nbuilds the system of record for that bundle first: experiment tracking for runs and lineage, a model\nregistry and a prompt registry that both split immutable versions from mutable aliases. It then builds\nthe evidence: a shared evaluation framework, a tiered golden-dataset portfolio, and reproducible runs\nwith manifests and a measured noise floor. CI/CD, release gates, shadow and canary rollouts and rollback\nturn that evidence into controlled exposure. Monitoring, drift detection, an observability stack and a\nlifecycle and deprecation policy keep the bundle honest after release, while the world, the provider\nand the corpus move underneath it. The last two lessons step back to platform shape: a control plane\nthat publishes config snapshots to a fast, independent data plane, and the line between what a central\nplatform team owns and what product teams own. Afterwards you can design a release pipeline where\npromotion and rollback are audited pointer moves backed by statistical evidence, explain why a prompt\nedit is a production change, and lay out the platform for an organisation with many AI teams. It\nmatters most for AI Engineers and Architects who run shared AI infrastructure, and for FDEs who must\nshow a customer how changes reach their tenant.","why":{"engineer":"You will wire prompts and models into registries, write the eval-based CI ladder with content-addressed caching, implement a paired non-inferiority gate, emit traces that carry the resolved bundle version, and make rollback a single alias move you have rehearsed.","architect":"You decide what the release unit is, where the control plane ends and the data plane begins, which signals gate a promotion, how provider sunsets and embedder upgrades are migrated, and which platform pieces are mandatory and central versus federated to product teams.","fde":"Customers will ask how a prompt or model change reaches their tenant, what evidence backs it, how fast you can undo it and how you will notice quality slipping; you need to show the gate report, the canary plan, the rollback drill and per-tenant monitoring on their traffic."},"mental_model":"Picture one immutable, content-hashed bundle moving along a conveyor of environments, with a pointer\n(an alias such as `champion` or `canary`) marking where it is live. Everything left of production\nproduces evidence about the bundle: tracked runs, a versioned golden portfolio, reproducible eval runs\nwith manifests, and a gate that compares the candidate to the champion with a confidence interval and\nper-slice floors. Crossing into production is never a rebuild, only a pointer move, first to a shadow\ncopy of traffic, then to a sticky canary slice, then to everyone. Everything right of production\nwatches the bundle against the world: monitoring slices quality proxies by bundle version, drift\ndetection asks whether inputs, answers or a pinned component moved, and the lifecycle policy schedules\nthe day a version must leave. Any red signal moves the pointer back to the last validated bundle. The\ncontrol plane owns the pointers and the evidence; the data plane only reads a snapshot and serves it.","order":["experiment-tracking","model-registry","prompt-registry","evaluation-frameworks","golden-dataset-strategy","reproducible-evaluations","ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","canary-shadow-deployments","rollback","observability-stack","model-monitoring","drift","model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform"],"order_reasons":{"experiment-tracking":"the run data model and lineage that every registry entry and eval score points back to.","model-registry":"introduces the immutable-version versus mutable-alias split on the artifact that is hardest to version.","prompt-registry":"applies the same split to prompt text and its model settings, completing the bundle's components.","evaluation-frameworks":"the shared datasets, targets, scorers, runner and results store that make eval scores comparable.","golden-dataset-strategy":"turns the dataset interface into a tiered, owned, refreshed portfolio sized to the regressions it must detect.","reproducible-evaluations":"pins every drifting part of an eval in a manifest and measures the noise floor gates will rely on.","ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models":"assembles registries and evals into a pipeline that promotes one tested bundle through a risk-sized ladder.","evaluation-gates-for-production-releases":"formalises the promotion decision as floors, a paired non-inferiority test and three outcomes.","canary-shadow-deployments":"extends evidence to live traffic with bounded exposure once offline gates pass.","rollback":"makes the reverse pointer move fast and safe, including the components that destroy their own undo path.","observability-stack":"the trace, metric and payload pipeline that post-release signals and eval loops depend on.","model-monitoring":"layers operational, proxy and late quality signals over that stack, sliced by bundle version and tenant.","drift":"separates what moved, how it moved and whether it mattered, and feeds confirmed shifts back into the golden set.","model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy":"plans the exit of versions and provider snapshots as dated, evaluated migrations.","ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane":"reframes registries, aliases and policies as a control plane publishing snapshots to a serving path.","centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform":"decides which of all these pieces one platform team owns and which product teams own."},"capstone":{"brief":"Build a release platform for a small retrieval-augmented support assistant that answers questions about\na fictional company's returns and billing policy. The assistant runs behind a minimal gateway that\nresolves a `champion` and a `canary` alias from a local control-plane store into a bundle: a prompt\nversion from a prompt registry, a model version (a provider snapshot or a small local model) from a\nmodel registry, decoding parameters and an index version. The gateway caches the resolved snapshot and\nkeeps serving it if the control plane is stopped. Every eval run is tracked with a manifest that pins\ndataset, bundle, judge and rubric versions, and runs over a tiered golden portfolio with smoke, full,\nslice and regression tiers. A CI pipeline runs the smoke tier on every prompt change and the full tier on\nrelease candidates, then applies a gate with safety and schema floors, per-slice floors and a paired\nnon-inferiority test against the champion. Passing candidates go to a shadow run on replayed traffic, then\na sticky 10% canary with guard metrics and automatic rollback. Traces carry the resolved bundle hash, and\na monitoring view shows proxies and sampled judge scores by version, with a drift check on intent and\nlanguage mix. Finally, deprecate the old model version through the lifecycle states with an inventory of\nwhich bundles still pin it.","deliverables":["A runnable repository with the gateway, control-plane store, prompt and model registries, tracked eval runner and golden portfolio, started with one command.","A CI configuration that runs the eval ladder with cached results and writes a gate report showing per-slice deltas, the confidence interval and the pass, block or needs-review verdict.","A deployment script that performs shadow, canary ramp and rollback as audited alias moves, plus a recorded rollback drill with its measured time.","A monitoring dashboard or notebook that slices latency, cost, schema failures, refusals and judge scores by bundle version and tenant, with a drift report comparing two traffic windows.","A two-page platform write-up covering the control/data-plane split, the deprecation plan for the retired model, and which components would be central versus team-owned."],"rubric":["A deliberately degraded prompt that raises the aggregate score but breaks the refunds slice is blocked by the gate, and the report names the slice and the confidence interval that caused the block.","Re-running any recorded eval from its manifest reproduces the verdict, and a comparison across mismatched dataset or judge versions is refused rather than reported.","Every production trace resolves to one bundle hash, and moving the alias back restores the previous bundle on all replicas within the measured rollback time, without a redeploy.","Stopping the control-plane store leaves the gateway serving the last snapshot with zero failed requests, and alias changes during the canary are visible in an audit log.","Monitoring shows the canary arm separately from the champion, a seeded language shift in replayed traffic appears in the drift report, and the failing examples become new regression items.","CI rejects a new bundle that pins the deprecated model version, and the inventory lists every bundle and consumer still depending on it before the freeze date."]}},"topics":[{"id":"ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","key":"llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","module":"llmops-platform","name":"CI/CD for prompts and models","taxonomy_name":"CI/CD for prompts and models","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/prompt-registry","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","evals/regression-tests","prompting/prompt-versioning","evals/eval-driven-development"],"tldr":"An LLM app ships three artifacts (code, prompts and config, model) and any one changes behaviour. CI/CD\nfor them adds evals sized to each stage's blast radius, cached model calls, and promotion of one tested,\nimmutable bundle.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-registry","key":"llmops-platform/prompt-registry","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Prompt registry","taxonomy_name":"prompt registry","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["prompting/prompt-versioning","prompting/prompt-templates","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/rollback","observability/langfuse","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases"],"tldr":"A prompt registry stores immutable, numbered prompt versions with their model settings, mutable labels\nlike `production` or `canary`, and a cached runtime fetch, so prompt changes get history, evidence and\nrollback without a code deploy.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-registry","key":"llmops-platform/model-registry","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Model registry","taxonomy_name":"model registry","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","llmops-platform/prompt-registry","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","security-governance-compliance/model-cards","python-for-ai/hugging-face","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","llmops-platform/rollback","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets"],"tldr":"A model registry maps a model name to immutable versions (exact weights or provider snapshot, adapters,\nserving config, lineage, eval evidence) and mutable aliases like `champion`, so \"what is in production\"\nhas one answer and rollback is a pointer move.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"experiment-tracking","key":"llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Experiment tracking (MLflow, W&B)","taxonomy_name":"experiment tracking (MLflow, W&B)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","observability/langfuse","llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks","evals/error-analysis"],"tldr":"Experiment tracking stores every training or eval run (params, code and data versions, step metrics,\nartifacts) so runs can be compared, reproduced and promoted. MLflow and W&B share that data model and\ndiffer in hosting, UI and ecosystem.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"evaluation-frameworks","key":"llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Evaluation frameworks","taxonomy_name":"evaluation frameworks","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/eval-harness","evals/tooling","llmops-platform/golden-dataset-strategy","llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","evals/llm-as-a-judge","observability/langfuse","evals/benchmarking"],"tldr":"An evaluation framework turns one engineer's eval script into a shared service with five fixed\ninterfaces (datasets, target, scorers, runner, results), so an eval score becomes a currency every team\ncan compare and accept.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"golden-dataset-strategy","key":"llmops-platform/golden-dataset-strategy","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Golden dataset strategy","taxonomy_name":"golden dataset strategy","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/dataset-contamination","evals/error-analysis","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","training-adaptation/data-labeling","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance"],"tldr":"The platform view of golden data: a portfolio of tiered, versioned sets, each with an owner, an\nadmission workflow fed by production failures, and a size set by the smallest regression it must detect,\nbecause one static set goes stale, leaks and is mis-sized.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"reproducible-evaluations","key":"llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Reproducible evaluations","taxonomy_name":"reproducible evaluations","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","evals/eval-harness","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/prompt-registry","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","python-for-ai/lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning"],"tldr":"A reproducible eval can be re-run from a recorded manifest (dataset, bundle, scorers, code, environment)\nto the same conclusion, with raw outputs stored and leftover noise measured, because unpinned comparisons\nacross weeks compare two different systems.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","key":"llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Evaluation gates for production releases","taxonomy_name":"evaluation gates for production releases","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/rollback","llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","llmops-platform/golden-dataset-strategy","evals/regression-tests","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation"],"tldr":"An evaluation gate is an automated policy that passes, blocks or flags a candidate bundle for review\nusing absolute floors, a paired non-inferiority test against the champion, per-slice floors and cost and\nlatency budgets, with every override recorded.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"canary-shadow-deployments","key":"llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Canary / shadow deployments","taxonomy_name":"canary / shadow deployments","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["llmops-platform/rollback","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","evals/a-b-testing","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","system-design/ai-gateway"],"tldr":"Shadow runs a candidate model, prompt or agent on copied live traffic without serving it; canary serves\nit to a small sticky slice and ramps while guard metrics hold. Together they turn \"ship and hope\" into\nmeasured exposure.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"rollback","key":"llmops-platform/rollback","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Rollback","taxonomy_name":"rollback","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/prompt-registry","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","deployment/runbooks","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response"],"tldr":"Rollback returns production to a previously validated version of the whole AI bundle (model, prompt,\nparameters, tools, index, guardrails) in minutes by repointing an alias, not rebuilding. It must be\nplanned, because some AI changes destroy their own undo path.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-monitoring","key":"llmops-platform/model-monitoring","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Model monitoring","taxonomy_name":"model monitoring","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["llmops-platform/drift","llmops-platform/observability-stack","observability/monitoring","observability/token-cost-tracking","evals/llm-as-a-judge","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","reliability/slas-slos"],"tldr":"A model can return fast 200s while answers get worse. Model monitoring layers operational signals,\nlabel-free behavioural proxies and late, sparse quality signals over live traffic, sliced by version and\ntenant, so silent regressions become alerts with examples attached.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"drift","key":"llmops-platform/drift","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Drift","taxonomy_name":"drift","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["llmops-platform/model-monitoring","llmops-platform/golden-dataset-strategy","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","llmops-platform/rollback","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","observability/monitoring","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification"],"tldr":"Drift is any change between the conditions you validated under and the ones you serve: inputs, the right\nanswer, or (in LLM apps) the model, prompt, corpus, embedder or judge. Detect it against a reference\nwindow; act only if outcomes changed.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","key":"llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Model lifecycle & deprecation policy","taxonomy_name":"model lifecycle & deprecation policy","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","llmops-platform/rollback","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","security-governance-compliance/model-governance","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture"],"tldr":"A lifecycle policy names a model version's states, who may move it and what evidence each move needs; the\ndeprecation half dates retirements, freezes new pins, migrates consumers with evaluation and archives.\nWithout it, every sunset is an unplanned migration.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"observability-stack","key":"llmops-platform/observability-stack","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Observability stack","taxonomy_name":"observability stack","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["observability/tracing","observability/opentelemetry","observability/distributed-tracing-across-services","observability/token-cost-tracking","observability/langfuse","observability/langsmith","observability/arize-phoenix","llmops-platform/model-monitoring"],"tldr":"An AI observability stack turns every request into queryable data: LLM-aware traces, a collector that\nredacts and samples, separate stores for traces, metrics and payloads, analysis tools where scores attach\nto traces, and alerting. Each layer has its own owner, retention and cost.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","key":"llmops-platform/ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","module":"llmops-platform","name":"AI control plane vs data plane","taxonomy_name":"AI control plane vs data plane","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["system-design/ai-gateway","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/prompt-registry","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/rollback","llmops-platform/centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation"],"tldr":"The data plane is the fast per-request path (gateway, routing, model call, retrieval, guardrails); the\ncontrol plane is the slow, audited system of record (registries, rollouts, policies, keys). The data plane\nserves versioned snapshots and keeps the last one if the control plane fails.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform","key":"llmops-platform/centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform","module":"llmops-platform","name":"Centralized vs federated AI platform","taxonomy_name":"centralized vs federated AI platform","kind":"decision","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["strategy/platform-strategy","llmops-platform/ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","system-design/ai-gateway","security-governance-compliance/model-governance","security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","adoption/change-management"],"tldr":"Pure centralization becomes a ticket queue; pure federation becomes duplicated gateways, spend and no\ninventory. Most organisations want a thin mandatory core (identity, model access, budgets, audit,\ntelemetry) with domain-owned prompts, evals, corpora and agents.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"architecture","stage_index":5,"track_order":22},{"id":"security-governance-compliance","tier":2,"order":22,"title":"Security, governance & compliance","intro":{"overview":"This module is about making an AI system safe to run, defensible to an auditor and acceptable to an\nenterprise buyer. It starts with security. AI security is ordinary application security plus one new\ncomponent: a model that follows instructions found in its data. Threat modeling draws the trust\nboundaries a classical review misses, where untrusted text flows into context and model output flows\ninto actions. Prompt injection defence turns that diagram into deterministic enforcement outside the\nmodel, using the rule of two. Zero trust makes every tool call a request to verify, with delegated,\nscoped, short-lived credentials. The data group comes next. Classification labels data so controls are\ndecided once per label and inherited by chunks, embeddings and weights. PII protection and DLP decide,\nper data class and destination, whether to allow, redact, tokenize or block. Residency maps every place a\nrequest's data actually goes. Governance follows: an inventory of every AI use, a risk tier per use case,\na risk register, responsible AI requirements, a model registry enforced at the gateway, model cards, and\naudit trails that can reconstruct a decision months later. The module closes with operations and\nassurance: AI incident response with kill switches and legal clocks, a tested vendor exit plan, and a\ncompliance architecture that maps one control set to GDPR, SOC 2, HIPAA, ISO/IEC 42001, the EU AI Act\nand the DPDP Act. Afterwards you can threat-model an agent, put a policy gateway in front of it, classify\nand govern its data, and hand an auditor evidence instead of policy documents. It matters most for\nArchitects and FDEs, who face these questions in design reviews and security questionnaires, and for\nEngineers who build the enforcement points.","why":{"engineer":"You build the enforcement points that make policy real: the tool dispatcher that checks the rule of two, the gateway that redacts PII and refuses unregistered models or out-of-region endpoints, the scoped token exchange, the hash-chained audit log and the kill switch, so a prompt injection or a leaked card number is a bug in your code path.","architect":"You decide where the trust boundaries sit, which data classes may reach which model and region, how labels propagate into vectors and logs, what the registry and audit schema look like, and how one control set maps to several frameworks; retrofitting residency, deletion or provenance fields later costs far more than designing them in.","fde":"Enterprise customers gate deployment on security questionnaires, data residency, DPAs, model documentation and an incident plan; you answer from a threat model, a data-flow map, model and system cards and automatically collected evidence, and you raise residency and risk tier in the first scoping call because they can rule out the default provider."},"mental_model":"Picture two planes around every AI system. The runtime plane is a single chokepoint, a policy gateway and\ntool dispatcher, that sits between the model and everything it can read, call or send. The model is\ntreated as an untrusted interpreter: whatever it proposes, the gateway checks identity, delegation, data\nlabel, destination, region, model approval and the rule of two in code, then allows, redacts, reroutes,\nasks a human or denies, and writes an audit event either way. The governance plane decides what rules\nthat gateway enforces. It starts from the inventory: every AI use case, tiered by what decisions it\ninforms and about whom. The tier sets how much risk assessment, fairness evaluation, documentation,\nvalidation and oversight the use case needs, and those decisions are recorded in the registry, the risk\nregister and the model card. The two planes meet in evidence. The audit trail and gateway metrics prove\nthe controls ran, incident response uses them to contain and reconstruct, and compliance becomes mapping\none set of enforced, evidenced controls onto each framework a customer or regulator brings.","order":["ai-security","threat-modeling","prompt-injection-defense","zero-trust","data-classification","pii-protection-dlp","data-residency","ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","ai-risk-management","responsible-ai","model-governance","model-cards","audit-trails","ai-incident-response","vendor-exit-strategy","compliance-architecture"],"order_reasons":{"ai-security":"the map of what changes when a model enters the system, used to decide which later lessons apply to a design.","threat-modeling":"draws the data-flow diagram and trust boundaries every later control is chosen against.","prompt-injection-defense":"turns the content sources and action sinks from the threat model into enforcement outside the model.","zero-trust":"gives each tool call delegated, scoped, short-lived credentials checked at a PEP, the identity half of injection defence.","data-classification":"labels data and its derived artifacts so controls are decided per label, which DLP and residency both key on.","pii-protection-dlp":"applies per-label, per-destination actions to personal data flowing into prompts, logs, indexes and weights.","data-residency":"extends the data-flow map to the locations of every endpoint, log, cache and support path a request touches.","ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification":"builds the list of AI uses and the risk tiers that make all governance proportionate.","ai-risk-management":"runs the map, measure and manage loop per inventory entry, with owners, appetite and residual risk.","responsible-ai":"turns fairness, transparency and oversight into testable requirements for the high-tier use cases the risk loop flags.","model-governance":"enforces which models are approved for which use through a registry and the gateway, using the tiers and risk decisions above.","model-cards":"documents intended use, out-of-scope uses and disaggregated results as a release gate in the governance flow.","audit-trails":"records prompts, versions, retrieved context, tool calls and gateway decisions so any output can be reconstructed and defended.","ai-incident-response":"uses kill switches, degraded modes and the audit trail to contain AI failures that never trip an error-rate alert.","vendor-exit-strategy":"measures how quickly and safely you can leave a provider, drawing on governance, evals and data ownership.","compliance-architecture":"maps the whole control set and its evidence onto GDPR, SOC 2, HIPAA, ISO/IEC 42001, the EU AI Act and DPDP, so it comes last."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are the FDE and architect for an AI assistant being rolled out to a mid-sized EU insurer. Claims\nhandlers use it to summarise claim files (PDFs and emails from policyholders, which carry health and\nfinancial data), answer questions from internal policy documents, draft emails to claimants, and\nrecommend whether a claim needs a fraud review. The customer's security team will not sign off until\nyou pass their review. Over a week, take an existing prototype (a model API behind a small web app, with\nlocal mocks for the claims system, document store and email) and make it reviewable. Threat-model it\nwith trust boundaries around claimant content and the email and claims-update sinks. Put one policy\ngateway in front of the model and every tool that enforces the rule of two, delegated per-user tokens,\ndata labels, PII redaction per destination, EU-only endpoints that fail closed, and a model registry.\nInventory and tier the four use cases (the fraud recommendation is the high-risk one), write the risk\nregister, a fairness evaluation and a system card for it, add a hash-chained audit trail, rehearse an\nincident, write a vendor exit plan, and assemble an evidence pack mapped to GDPR, the EU AI Act and SOC 2.","deliverables":["A threat model: a data-flow diagram with trust boundaries, every content source and action sink marked, a STRIDE-based threat register, and a verified test linked to each high-severity threat.","A policy gateway and tool dispatcher in code, enforcing per-user delegated scopes, the rule of two with human approval for outbound email after reading claimant content, label-based PII redaction or blocking per destination, EU-region endpoints that fail closed, and refusal of unregistered models.","A data map and classification table covering every store and derived artifact (chunks, embeddings, traces, caches, eval sets) with its label, region, retention and deletion path, plus a DLP precision and recall measurement on a labelled sample.","An AI inventory with risk tiers and rationale for all four use cases, a risk register with owners, KRIs and residual risk, a fairness evaluation of the fraud-review recommendation by relevant groups, and a versioned model or system card with out-of-scope uses.","A hash-chained audit trail capturing user, prompt template version, model version, retrieved chunk ids, tool calls and gateway decisions, with a verification script and a reconstruction query.","An incident runbook with a kill switch and degraded mode, a tabletop write-up of an injected claimant email that tries to forward a claim file, a vendor exit plan with measured eval deltas on an alternative model, and a compliance evidence matrix mapping each control to GDPR, the EU AI Act and SOC 2 with links to automatically collected evidence."],"rubric":["A claimant PDF containing an injected instruction to email the full claim file externally results in no email being sent without approval, and the audit log shows the denied or pending call with the rule that fired.","Switching the configured model endpoint to a non-EU region or an unregistered model makes requests fail closed with a logged denial, not a silent fallback.","A request made as a handler without access to a given claim cannot retrieve that claim's documents through the assistant, shown by a PEP denial tied to the user's delegated token.","Running the verification script after manually editing one audit record reports the exact broken link, and the reconstruction query returns the model version, prompt version and chunk ids for a chosen past recommendation.","Every inventory entry has a tier, owner and review date, and the high-risk fraud recommendation has a card with disaggregated results and a named human oversight step, while low-tier uses carry visibly lighter documentation.","The evidence matrix links each control to an artifact generated by the system (gateway metrics, audit samples, test runs), and a deletion request for one claimant is shown removing their data from the document store, vector index, traces and caches."]}},"topics":[{"id":"ai-security","key":"security-governance-compliance/ai-security","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"AI security","taxonomy_name":"AI security","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","security-governance-compliance/prompt-injection-defense","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms","safety-security/prompt-injection","safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege"],"tldr":"AI security is application security plus a model that obeys instructions in its data and cannot be\npatched like a parser. Treat it as an untrusted interpreter: least privilege, isolation, output handling,\napproval gates and monitoring hold; cleverer prompts do not.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"threat-modeling","key":"security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Threat modeling","taxonomy_name":"threat modeling","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["security-governance-compliance/ai-security","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms","safety-security/excessive-agency","safety-security/tool-privilege-escalation","system-design/c4-diagrams","evals/red-teaming"],"tldr":"Threat modeling answers four questions: what are we building, what can go wrong, what will we do, did\nwe do well. For AI systems the diagram marks every flow of untrusted text into the context and every\noutput that becomes an action.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"prompt-injection-defense","key":"security-governance-compliance/prompt-injection-defense","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Prompt injection defense","taxonomy_name":"prompt injection defense","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["safety-security/prompt-injection","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","safety-security/input-output-guardrails","safety-security/excessive-agency","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","evals/red-teaming"],"tldr":"No prompt, classifier or fine-tune reliably stops a model obeying hidden instructions, so defence is an\narchitecture: deterministic enforcement outside the model on what it can reach and cause, detection as\nan alarm, and a per-workflow policy, regression suite and risk owner.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pii-protection-dlp","key":"security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"PII protection / DLP","taxonomy_name":"PII protection / DLP","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["safety-security/pii-redaction","safety-security/data-leakage","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","system-design/ai-gateway","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","observability/logging"],"tldr":"PII protection decides which personal data may reach which destination; DLP finds it in motion, in use\nand at rest and enforces allow, redact, tokenize, block or alert. LLM systems copy free text into\nprompts, providers, logs, indexes and weights, so every copy can leak.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-classification","key":"security-governance-compliance/data-classification","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Data classification","taxonomy_name":"data classification","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":13,"related":["security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","security-governance-compliance/model-governance","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","data-architecture/data-lineage","system-design/ai-gateway","scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery"],"tldr":"Data classification gives every dataset a sensitivity label (Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted)\nso controls are decided once per label. In AI the hard part is propagation: chunks, embeddings,\nsummaries, caches and fine-tuned weights must inherit the source's label.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"zero-trust","key":"security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Zero trust","taxonomy_name":"zero trust","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["security-governance-compliance/ai-security","security-governance-compliance/prompt-injection-defense","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","agent-runtime-architecture/approval-and-authorization-boundaries","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","deployment/private-endpoints"],"tldr":"Zero trust drops the assumption that anything inside the perimeter is trustworthy: every request is\nauthenticated, authorised per resource and given least privilege for the shortest time. An agent is\nexactly such a trickable component, so each tool call is verified, not trusted.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-residency","key":"security-governance-compliance/data-residency","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Data residency","taxonomy_name":"data residency","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["security-governance-compliance/data-classification","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","deployment/compliance-constraints","deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","system-design/self-host-vs-api","system-design/ai-gateway","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy"],"tldr":"Data residency means data is stored and processed only in approved locations. In AI it covers the whole\nflow: model endpoint, provider logs, caches, vector stores, traces and support staff each have a\nlocation. Sovereignty differs: whose law can compel access.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-governance","key":"security-governance-compliance/model-governance","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Model governance","taxonomy_name":"model governance","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","security-governance-compliance/model-cards","security-governance-compliance/audit-trails","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","llmops-platform/model-registry","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance"],"tldr":"Model governance controls which models may be used, for what, on what evidence, and how changes are\napproved, monitored and retired. Models change without code changes and fail statistically, so good\ngovernance is automated: a registry, risk-tiered approval, a gateway refusing unapproved models.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-cards","key":"security-governance-compliance/model-cards","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Model cards","taxonomy_name":"model cards","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["security-governance-compliance/model-governance","security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","llmops-platform/model-registry","evals/model-evaluation","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation"],"tldr":"A model card is a short, versioned document that travels with a model: what it is for and not for, how\nit was evaluated per condition that matters, and where it fails. Deployers need it because behaviour\ncannot be read from weights.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"audit-trails","key":"security-governance-compliance/audit-trails","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Audit trails","taxonomy_name":"audit trails","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","observability/logging","observability/tracing","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","agents/approval-gates-for-consequential-actions","agent-reliability/audit-logs-execution-traces"],"tldr":"An audit trail is an append-only, tamper-evident record of who did what, when, with which inputs and\nversions. AI needs more, because without the prompt, model version, retrieved context and tool\ncalls recorded at the time, an output cannot be explained later.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","key":"security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"AI inventory & use-case risk classification","taxonomy_name":"AI inventory & use-case risk classification","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["security-governance-compliance/model-governance","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","security-governance-compliance/model-cards","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","scoping/ai-use-case-discovery","llmops-platform/model-registry"],"tldr":"An AI inventory lists every AI system and use case (built, bought or embedded in SaaS) with owner, data,\nmodels and purpose. Risk classification gives each *use case* a tier that sets its controls, because the\nsame model can draft copy or screen applicants.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"responsible-ai","key":"security-governance-compliance/responsible-ai","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Responsible AI","taxonomy_name":"responsible AI","kind":"concept","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":16,"related":["security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","security-governance-compliance/model-cards","security-governance-compliance/model-governance","security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","agents/human-in-the-loop","safety-security/content-moderation"],"tldr":"Responsible AI turns values like fairness, transparency, accountability, privacy and safety into\nrequirements, metrics and owners per AI system. Principles alone change nothing: an accurate model can\nstill harm a group, so you measure and decide trade-offs explicitly.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"ai-risk-management","key":"security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"AI risk management","taxonomy_name":"AI risk management","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","security-governance-compliance/responsible-ai","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management","llmops-platform/model-monitoring","evals/red-teaming"],"tldr":"AI risk management is a continuous loop: identify what can go wrong, rate likelihood and harm, choose\ncontrols, track what remains, with owners and an explicit appetite. AI risks are probabilistic and shift\nafter launch, so a one-time review cannot cover them.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"ai-incident-response","key":"security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"AI incident response","taxonomy_name":"AI incident response","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["security-governance-compliance/audit-trails","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","llmops-platform/rollback","system-design/graceful-degradation","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites"],"tldr":"AI incident response is a prepared process to detect, contain, investigate and learn from AI failures:\nharmful outputs at scale, data leakage, injection-triggered actions, bias, silent provider model changes.\nThe system is often \"up\" while harming people, with no stack trace.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"vendor-exit-strategy","key":"security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Vendor exit strategy","taxonomy_name":"vendor exit strategy","kind":"decision","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/build-vs-buy","system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary","system-design/multi-model-architecture","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture"],"tldr":"A vendor exit strategy is a tested plan for leaving an AI provider within a target time and cost without\nlosing data or quality. AI lock-in hides in tuned prompts, model-specific embeddings, unexportable\nfine-tunes and vendor-held state, so plan early.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"compliance-architecture","key":"security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","module":"security-governance-compliance","name":"Compliance architecture (GDPR, SOC 2, HIPAA, ISO 42001, EU AI Act, DPDP Act)","taxonomy_name":"compliance architecture (GDPR, SOC 2, HIPAA, ISO 42001, EU AI Act, DPDP Act)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":25,"related":["security-governance-compliance/data-residency","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","security-governance-compliance/audit-trails","security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","deployment/compliance-constraints","deployment/security-questionnaires"],"tldr":"Compliance architecture enforces legal and assurance obligations through system structure and automatic\nevidence, not policy documents. One control set maps to GDPR, SOC 2, HIPAA, ISO/IEC 42001, the EU AI Act\nand India's DPDP Act. Orientation, not legal advice.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"architecture","stage_index":5,"track_order":23},{"id":"strategy","tier":2,"order":23,"title":"Strategy","intro":{"overview":"This module is about the decisions that sit above any single AI system: which use cases to pursue and\nin what order, who has to agree before building starts, which vendors to depend on, what to provide\nonce as a shared platform, and whether the AI claims behind a deal hold up. It opens with the AI\nroadmap, a sequenced portfolio of use cases treated as hypotheses, each scored separately on value\nand on feasibility (data readiness, evaluability, error tolerance, risk class, integration effort)\nand moved through evidence gates from feasibility eval to pilot to production. Stakeholder alignment\nthen turns the conflicting goals of operations, compliance, finance and security into one agreed\noperating point on a measured trade-off curve, set against a human baseline and written on one page.\nVendor evaluation supplies the comparative method: hard gates first, then a bake-off on your own\nlabelled data measuring quality, latency under load and cost per successful task, then exit terms.\nPlatform strategy decides which recurring capabilities to centralise, using breadth of demand,\nvariance between teams and externalities, and when to build them: after demand appears, as a thin\nmandatory core plus an optional paved road. Technical due diligence applies the same habit of turning\nclaims into tests to an acquisition, investment or critical partner. The thread through all five is\nthat AI feasibility is an empirical question, so every strategic commitment should be gated on an\neval rather than on a demo or a slide. Afterwards you can build and defend a roadmap, run a launch\ndecision without a late veto, select a vendor on evidence, argue a platform boundary with numbers,\nand write a diligence findings list. It matters most for Architects and FDEs, and for Engineers\nmoving into technical leadership.","why":{"engineer":"You build the feasibility evals, bake-off harnesses and cost-per-item models that the roadmap gates, vendor choice and launch decision actually rest on, and you can say plainly when a use case should be killed or parked until a new model ships.","architect":"You draw the platform boundary (gateway, identity, budgets, audit, telemetry in the mandatory core; prompts, corpora and agents left with teams), sequence the portfolio so early projects build reusable assets, and keep vendor seams so decisions stay reversible as the model market moves.","fde":"You get the customer's business owner, compliance, security and finance to agree an operating point and decision rights before building, run the vendor bake-off on their own data, and present results against the one-pager at each gate so sign-off does not stall in pilot purgatory."},"mental_model":"Hold one picture: AI strategy is portfolio management under uncertainty, where every bet is bought\nwith information before it is bought with engineering. Each use case is a hypothesis with a value\nrange and a weakest feasibility axis; an eval on real data is the cheap experiment that resolves it.\nGates set in advance decide whether a bet advances, waits for a capability trigger, or is killed.\nStakeholder alignment fixes what \"advance\" means: an operating point on a trade-off curve, measured\nagainst today's human baseline, with named decision owners. Vendor evaluation and due diligence are\nthe same experiment pointed outward: do not trust the claim, test it on your data and price the exit.\nPlatform strategy is what the portfolio leaves behind: the shared parts that several bets needed,\nwith little variance and organisation-wide risk, pulled into a thin core once demand exists. If a\ncommitment does not have an evidence gate and an owner, it is not yet strategy.","order":["ai-roadmap","stakeholder-alignment","vendor-evaluation","platform-strategy","technical-due-diligence"],"order_reasons":{"ai-roadmap":"sets the portfolio frame, value-versus-feasibility scoring and stage gates that every other lesson plugs into.","stakeholder-alignment":"defines the agreed operating point, baseline and decision rights that a roadmap gate needs before anyone can say yes.","vendor-evaluation":"teaches the gates-then-bake-off method on your own data, the core skill for each build, buy or wait decision on the roadmap.","platform-strategy":"builds on roadmap sequencing and vendor sourcing to decide what to centralise, when, and how to fund it.","technical-due-diligence":"the advanced case that extends vendor evaluation's claim-to-test discipline to deals, data rights and margin models."},"capstone":{"brief":"Act as the AI lead for a mid-sized insurer (fictional, with synthetic or public data) that has\ncollected fifteen AI ideas from workshops, has two teams already calling a model provider with their\nown keys, and is being pitched a small claims-automation startup as an acquisition. Produce a strategy\npack the leadership team could decide from in one meeting. Turn the ideas into workflow-level\ncandidates and score each on the value formula (volume, value per unit, capture rate, automation\nshare, run and review cost) and on each feasibility axis separately, then sequence the next three\nquarters so early items build shared assets. For the top item, write the stakeholder one-pager:\nbaseline human error rate and cycle time, hard constraints kept apart from outcome goals, a trade-off\ncurve from a small offline eval, the chosen operating point and the launch and pause owners. Run a\nlight vendor bake-off for that item between at least two model providers on 100 or more labelled\nsynthetic cases. Propose the platform core with a break-even estimate, and write a diligence plan and\nmock findings list for the acquisition target.","deliverables":["A portfolio sheet with one row per candidate: owner, value range, per-axis feasibility scores, build/buy/wait decision with a trigger for each \"wait\", current stage, gate criterion, kill condition and next decision date, plus a three-horizon sequence.","A one-page stakeholder alignment document for the top use case, including the measured baseline on the same items the AI will handle, a confidence-threshold trade-off curve, the chosen operating point, failure handling and a RACI or DACI table.","A re-runnable vendor bake-off (script or notebook) and a short report: gate results, quality with confidence intervals, p95 latency at expected concurrency, cost per successful task, and priced exit terms.","A platform proposal naming the mandatory core and the optional paved road, with the centralisation break-even worked through for at least three capabilities and a funding model.","A diligence plan that turns each of the target's headline claims into a test, with a classified findings list (deal-breaker, price adjustment, post-close fix) and a usage-sensitive margin model."],"rubric":["Feasibility is scored per axis and the weakest axis is named for every candidate; the top-value item is not first in the sequence unless its foundations already exist, and the reason is stated.","Every roadmap stage, the launch decision and each \"wait\" item has a pre-agreed numeric criterion or trigger, and at least one candidate is killed or parked on evidence.","The alignment one-pager compares the AI's error on its automated slice with the human error on that same slice, and a reviewer can make the launch call from the one-pager and the eval without new negotiation.","Vendors are compared on cost per successful task (retries and review included), not list price, and the bake-off re-runs from one command when a new model is added.","The platform core is justified by externalities or the break-even inequality, at least one capability is deliberately left with teams, and each diligence claim maps to a concrete test with a named finding class."]}},"topics":[{"id":"ai-roadmap","key":"strategy/ai-roadmap","module":"strategy","name":"AI roadmap","taxonomy_name":"AI roadmap","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["scoping/ai-use-case-discovery","scoping/roi-model","scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","evals/eval-driven-development","strategy/platform-strategy","strategy/stakeholder-alignment"],"tldr":"An AI roadmap is a sequenced portfolio of use cases, each a hypothesis with an evidence gate, plus the\nshared capabilities early bets build for later ones. It commits to outcomes and learning milestones, not\nto named models or dates for everything.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"platform-strategy","key":"strategy/platform-strategy","module":"strategy","name":"Platform strategy","taxonomy_name":"platform strategy","kind":"decision","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["llmops-platform/centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform","llmops-platform/ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","system-design/build-vs-buy","system-design/ai-gateway","infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","strategy/ai-roadmap"],"tldr":"Platform strategy decides which AI capabilities are provided once as a shared internal product and which\nstay with teams. Platformise what is widely needed, low-variance or risky organisation-wide; leave\ndifferentiating parts with teams; build after demand appears.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"vendor-evaluation","key":"strategy/vendor-evaluation","module":"strategy","name":"Vendor evaluation","taxonomy_name":"vendor evaluation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/model-selection","system-design/build-vs-buy","evals/golden-dataset","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","security-governance-compliance/vendor-exit-strategy","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","deployment/security-questionnaires"],"tldr":"Vendor evaluation picks a model provider, vector database, eval tool or AI platform by testing candidates\non your own task: hard gates first, then a bake-off on your labelled data (quality, latency under load,\ncost per successful task), then contract and exit terms.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"technical-due-diligence","key":"strategy/technical-due-diligence","module":"strategy","name":"Technical due diligence","taxonomy_name":"technical due diligence","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["strategy/vendor-evaluation","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","evals/dataset-contamination","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","system-design/open-weight-vs-proprietary"],"tldr":"Technical due diligence on an AI company, product or team (acquisition, investment or strategic\npartnership) tests whether the technical claims behind a deal are true and what keeping them true will\ncost, before the price is fixed.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"stakeholder-alignment","key":"strategy/stakeholder-alignment","module":"strategy","name":"Stakeholder alignment","taxonomy_name":"stakeholder alignment","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["adoption/stakeholder-management","adoption/exec-communication","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","adoption/change-management","strategy/ai-roadmap"],"tldr":"Stakeholder alignment gets everyone who funds, approves, operates, uses or can veto an AI system to agree,\nbefore building, on the goal, success metric, acceptable error rate, failure handling and decision rights.\nFor a probabilistic system, \"does it work?\" needs an agreed operating point.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"architecture","stage_index":5,"track_order":24},{"id":"scoping","tier":3,"order":24,"title":"Scoping","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the work between \"the customer wants AI\" and \"the team is building the right thing,\nagainst agreed numbers, with a path to production\". It starts with understanding. Customer discovery\nturns a requested solution into the underlying job, its current cost, the cost of an error and the\nperson who decides. AI use-case discovery ranks candidate ideas on value, fit for a probabilistic model,\ndata feasibility and error tolerance. Workflow analysis finds the step that actually limits the outcome.\nProblem decomposition splits the goal into nodes with inputs, outputs, techniques and tests. Domain\nmodeling pins down what the customer's words mean, and data access and permissions discovery starts the\nslowest approvals early. The second group makes value measurable: a metric tree with guardrails, a\nbaseline captured before anything changes, and an ROI model built as ranges with a break-even point.\nThe third group turns this into a commitment: a dependency and risk log, a statement of work with\nstatistical acceptance and dated customer obligations, and requirements traced to eval cases. The last\ngroup moves from evidence to production through timeboxed prototypes and PoCs, demos that drive\ndecisions, a staged pilot plan with entry and exit criteria, and risk-ordered hardening. After the\nmodule you can take a vague request, pick a use case worth doing, write a scope and SOW a buyer will\nsign, prove or kill feasibility inside a timebox, and plan a pilot whose go/no-go is a reading of agreed\nnumbers. It matters most for FDEs, who run this with customers, and for Architects, who own the\ndecomposition, data access and production plan.","why":{"engineer":"Decomposition gives you nodes with contracts and tests instead of one untestable prompt, and the frozen eval set, traced requirements and timebox tell you what \"done\" means before you start tuning.","architect":"You decide which nodes need a model and which are plain code, how the domain model projects into schemas, prompts and retrieval filters, how source permissions carry into the system, and what hardening production needs.","fde":"You turn \"build us a chatbot\" into a signed scope: the real job, a ranked use case, a baseline, an ROI range, dated customer dependencies and acceptance criteria that survive a probabilistic model and a steering meeting."},"mental_model":"Picture scoping as a funnel of decisions, each one narrowing what you build and each backed by\nevidence. At the wide top are a customer request and a pile of ideas; discovery, use-case ranking and\nworkflow analysis narrow these to one job and the step that limits its outcome. Decomposition and the\ndomain model give that job a shape: nodes with contracts, words with fixed meanings, data with known\nowners and permissions. Then comes the measuring stick, which you fix before building: a metric tree,\na baseline and an ROI range, which together say what success is worth and which number the pilot must\nmove. The SOW, risk log and traceability matrix turn that measuring stick into a commitment both sides\ncan check. Below that, every stage of execution (timeboxed PoC, demo, pilot, production) ends in an\nexplicit go, pivot or stop decision taken against the same numbers. If a stage cannot name its\nquestion, its evidence and its decision rule, it is not scoped yet.","order":["customer-discovery","ai-use-case-discovery","workflow-analysis","problem-decomposition","domain-modeling","data-access-and-permissions-discovery","success-metrics-kpis","baseline-measurement-before-deployment","roi-model","dependency-and-risk-management","statement-of-work","timeboxing","rapid-prototyping-poc","demos","requirements-traceability","pilot-to-production-plan","productionization"],"order_reasons":{"customer-discovery":"every later step depends on knowing the real job, who feels it, what it costs and who decides.","ai-use-case-discovery":"narrows many ideas to a ranked shortlist on value, model fit, data feasibility and error cost.","workflow-analysis":"maps the current process so the AI lands on the step that actually limits the outcome.","problem-decomposition":"splits the chosen job into nodes with inputs, outputs, techniques and tests, separating model work from plain code.","domain-modeling":"fixes the entities, states and vocabulary that the decomposed nodes, schemas and eval labels will share.","data-access-and-permissions-discovery":"identifies sources, owners and permission rules for those entities early, because approvals have the longest lead time.","success-metrics-kpis":"defines the outcome, driver, quality and guardrail metrics before architecture choices lock in.","baseline-measurement-before-deployment":"measures those metrics under the current process, because the before number cannot be recovered later.","roi-model":"turns metrics and baseline into a money range with sensitivity and break-even, which can kill or prioritise the use case.","dependency-and-risk-management":"lists dated customer dependencies and sized risks, which the SOW must contain.","statement-of-work":"commits scope, statistical acceptance, dated obligations and change control, built from everything above.","timeboxing":"sets the question, budget and decision rule that keep uncertain work like a PoC from expanding without limit.","rapid-prototyping-poc":"tests the riskiest assumption on real data inside a timebox and ends in a decision.","demos":"presents PoC and pilot evidence to a named audience so it produces a decision rather than applause.","requirements-traceability":"links SOW requirements to components, prompts and eval cases once the pilot build begins.","pilot-to-production-plan":"stages the rollout with cohorts, entry and exit criteria, owners and rollback, measured against the baseline.","productionization":"closes the gap between a working pilot and a system that survives real traffic, outages, model changes and audits."},"capstone":{"brief":"Scope an AI engagement end to end for a realistic customer: a mid-sized insurer's claims team that\nhas asked for \"a chatbot to answer adjusters' policy questions\". Work from a short written case (a\none-page sponsor brief, three interview transcripts you write or role-play, and a synthetic dataset of\na few hundred claims with timestamps and policy documents). Over a week, run discovery and reframe the\nrequest into the underlying job, rank at least five candidate use cases on the four axes, and map the\ncurrent claims workflow to find the step that limits cycle time. Decompose the chosen use case into\nnodes, write a glossary and one state machine for a claim, and build a data source register with owners,\naccess methods and permission models. Define a metric tree with guardrails, compute a baseline from the\nsynthetic logs, and build an ROI model with ranges, sensitivity and break-even. Write a phased SOW with\nstatistical acceptance, then run a two-day timeboxed PoC on the riskiest node and record a short demo\nthat ends in a stated decision. Finish with a pilot-to-production plan and a prioritised hardening list.","deliverables":["A discovery brief (one page) stating the job, current cost, cost of an error, success evidence and decision-maker, plus a ranked use-case table scored on value, model fit, data feasibility and error tolerance.","A workflow swimlane with wait times and variant frequencies, a decomposition diagram where every node has an input, output, technique, owner and test, a domain glossary with a claim state machine, and a data source register with owner, access method, classification and permission model per source.","A metric tree (outcome KPI, drivers, quality metrics, guardrails), a baseline report with means, variance, per-segment results and a human-quality audit, and an ROI spreadsheet with ranges, a sensitivity ranking and a break-even value.","A RAID log with dated dependencies and risks sized in days, and a phased SOW with scope, out-of-scope items, statistical acceptance against a frozen eval set, dated customer obligations and change control.","A PoC report from a two-day timebox with the question, pass/fail bar, results on real-shaped data and the decision, a recorded demo of ten minutes or less, a traceability matrix from requirements to eval cases, and a pilot-to-production plan with stages, cohorts, entry and exit criteria, rollback and a risk-ordered hardening list."],"rubric":["The discovery brief names a job different from \"chatbot\" and supports it with quotes from the interviews, and the use-case ranking shows at least one high-value idea rejected for error cost or data feasibility, with the reason stated.","The chosen AI step is the one the workflow map shows limits cycle time, and the decomposition multiplies per-node error rates to name the node that dominates end-to-end error.","Every SOW acceptance criterion is a threshold on a named metric, on a frozen eval set with a stated size and slices, and every customer dependency has an owner and a need-by date that also appears in the RAID log.","The ROI model's break-even value and its top-ranked sensitivity assumption are the numbers the pilot plan says it will measure, and the baseline uses the same metric definitions as the pilot.","The PoC report ends with an explicit ship, continue, pivot or stop decision tied to its pass/fail bar, and the demo shows at least one failure case alongside eval numbers.","Each pilot exit criterion maps to a metric in the tree, and every requirement in the traceability matrix links to at least one eval case and to the component or prompt that implements it."]}},"topics":[{"id":"customer-discovery","key":"scoping/customer-discovery","module":"scoping","name":"Customer discovery","taxonomy_name":"customer discovery","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["scoping/ai-use-case-discovery","scoping/workflow-analysis","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","strategy/stakeholder-alignment","adoption/stakeholder-management"],"tldr":"Customer discovery finds out what problem a customer actually has, who feels it, what it costs today and\nwhat evidence would prove it solved, before anyone builds. AI projects fail more often from solving the\nwrong problem than from picking the wrong model.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"ai-use-case-discovery","key":"scoping/ai-use-case-discovery","module":"scoping","name":"AI use-case discovery","taxonomy_name":"AI use-case discovery","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["scoping/customer-discovery","scoping/workflow-analysis","scoping/roi-model","scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","security-governance-compliance/ai-inventory-and-use-case-risk-classification","agents/workflows-vs-agents","training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision"],"tldr":"AI use-case discovery turns \"we could use AI for…\" ideas into a short ranked list that is valuable, suited\nto a model, feasible with reachable data, and safe to get wrong sometimes. A bad first pick sinks the\nprogramme's credibility.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"problem-decomposition","key":"scoping/problem-decomposition","module":"scoping","name":"Problem decomposition","taxonomy_name":"problem decomposition","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["scoping/workflow-analysis","scoping/domain-modeling","scoping/requirements-traceability","agents/task-decomposition","agents/workflows-vs-agents","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/error-analysis"],"tldr":"Problem decomposition breaks a fuzzy business goal into sub-problems, each with an input, output,\ntechnique and test. It shows which parts need a model, which are plain code, and where the chain fails,\nbecause errors in chained probabilistic steps multiply.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"workflow-analysis","key":"scoping/workflow-analysis","module":"scoping","name":"Workflow analysis","taxonomy_name":"workflow analysis","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["scoping/customer-discovery","scoping/problem-decomposition","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","agents/human-in-the-loop","system-design/workflow-orchestration","adoption/change-management"],"tldr":"Workflow analysis maps how work actually flows: steps, people, systems, handoffs, exceptions and the waits\nbetween steps. AI only improves an outcome on the step that limits it; speeding a ten-minute task inside a\nthree-day process changes almost nothing.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"domain-modeling","key":"scoping/domain-modeling","module":"scoping","name":"Domain modeling","taxonomy_name":"domain modeling","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","prompting/structured-outputs-json-mode","customer-data-evaluation/domain-prompt-engineering","rag-retrieval/knowledge-graphs","scoping/problem-decomposition","scoping/workflow-analysis"],"tldr":"Domain modeling captures the customer's entities, relationships, lifecycles, rules and vocabulary in one\nagreed model reused by schemas, prompts, retrieval and evals. A model knows generic meanings, so unstated\nmismatches (\"active\", \"closed\") produce confident, silently wrong outputs.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-access-and-permissions-discovery","key":"scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery","module":"scoping","name":"Data access & permissions discovery","taxonomy_name":"data access & permissions discovery","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management"],"tldr":"Before building, establish which data a use case needs, who owns it, how to reach it technically and\nlegally, and how source permissions carry into the AI system. Approvals are often the longest lead time,\nand export-based prototypes silently drop permissions.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"success-metrics-kpis","key":"scoping/success-metrics-kpis","module":"scoping","name":"Success metrics / KPIs","taxonomy_name":"success metrics / KPIs","kind":"metric","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","scoping/roi-model","scoping/requirements-traceability","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","agent-reliability/task-completion-rate"],"tldr":"Success metrics are how the customer judges the AI project; KPIs are the business outcomes among them.\nAgree them before building, as a tree of outcome, drivers, quality and guardrails, because accuracy\nalone proves no value and single metrics get gamed.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"baseline-measurement-before-deployment","key":"scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","module":"scoping","name":"Baseline measurement before deployment","taxonomy_name":"baseline measurement before deployment","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/workflow-analysis","scoping/roi-model","evals/a-b-testing","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement"],"tldr":"A baseline is each success metric's measured value under the current process, captured before the AI\nchanges anything. You cannot recover it later, so measure variance, stratify by case type, audit human\nquality like the AI's, and freeze the definitions.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"roi-model","key":"scoping/roi-model","module":"scoping","name":"ROI model","taxonomy_name":"ROI model","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","infra-capacity-economics/tco","infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","adoption/exec-communication"],"tldr":"An ROI model turns success metrics into money: baseline × improvement × adoption × capture rate, minus\none-time and recurring costs, over a horizon. Build ranges, sensitivity and break-even, not one heroic\nnumber, because capture rate and adoption usually decide it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"statement-of-work","key":"scoping/statement-of-work","module":"scoping","name":"Statement of work","taxonomy_name":"statement of work","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/requirements-traceability","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management","scoping/timeboxing","scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries"],"tldr":"A statement of work (SOW) is the project contract: deliverables, acceptance, customer obligations, out\nof scope and change control. AI work needs a stricter one, because outputs are probabilistic: acceptance\nis statistical on an agreed eval set, and customer dependencies are dated obligations.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"requirements-traceability","key":"scoping/requirements-traceability","module":"scoping","name":"Requirements traceability","taxonomy_name":"requirements traceability","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["scoping/statement-of-work","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","evals/golden-dataset","evals/regression-tests","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","security-governance-compliance/audit-trails"],"tldr":"Requirements traceability is the maintained link from objective to requirement, to the components and\nprompts implementing it, to eval cases and evidence. It answers \"is this met?\" and \"what does this change\nbreak?\", because a prompt or model change can silently break untested requirements.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"dependency-and-risk-management","key":"scoping/dependency-and-risk-management","module":"scoping","name":"Dependency & risk management","taxonomy_name":"dependency & risk management","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["scoping/statement-of-work","scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery","scoping/timeboxing","scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","reliability/fmea","security-governance-compliance/ai-risk-management","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits"],"tldr":"AI projects slip on things the team does not control: data, access, security reviews, SME time, quotas.\nMap dependencies with owners and need-by dates, size risks in days, and retire the biggest with cheap\nspikes, so surprises arrive in week one.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"timeboxing","key":"scoping/timeboxing","module":"scoping","name":"Timeboxing","taxonomy_name":"timeboxing","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":11,"related":["scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","scoping/problem-decomposition","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/statement-of-work","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management","evals/error-analysis"],"tldr":"Timeboxing fixes the time and lets scope flex: you commit to answer one question within a set budget,\nthen decide (ship, continue, pivot, stop) when the box closes. AI quality improves asymptotically, so\nwithout a box teams tune prompts forever.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"demos","key":"scoping/demos","module":"scoping","name":"Demos","taxonomy_name":"demos","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":12,"related":["scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/customer-discovery","evals/golden-dataset","adoption/exec-communication","strategy/stakeholder-alignment"],"tldr":"A demo is a decision instrument, not a performance. Cherry-picked LLM inputs look magical while typical\ninputs fail, so a good demo uses real workflow and data, pairs the story with eval numbers, shows a\nfailure and labels what is built.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"rapid-prototyping-poc","key":"scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","module":"scoping","name":"Rapid prototyping / PoC","taxonomy_name":"rapid prototyping / PoC","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["scoping/timeboxing","scoping/demos","scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","evals/eval-driven-development","evals/golden-dataset","enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment"],"tldr":"A rapid prototype is a cheap, disposable build asking \"what should this be?\"; a proof of concept (PoC)\nasks \"can this work?\" against pass/fail criteria on real data. Both buy information cheaply and fail\nwhen they test the easy part or never decide.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"pilot-to-production-plan","key":"scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","module":"scoping","name":"Pilot → production plan","taxonomy_name":"pilot → production plan","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","scoping/productionization","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","deployment/hypercare","adoption/change-management"],"tldr":"A pilot puts real users on real work at limited scope to measure impact against a baseline. The pilot →\nproduction plan fixes stages, entry and exit criteria, owners and the production-gap work, so a successful\npilot does not stall.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"productionization","key":"scoping/productionization","module":"scoping","name":"Productionization","taxonomy_name":"productionization","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","observability/tracing","reliability/slas-slos","deployment/runbooks","llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy"],"tldr":"Productionization turns a happy-path prototype into a system that survives real traffic, bad inputs,\noutages, model changes, adversaries and audits. The model call is a small part; plan reliability, eval\ngates, observability, security, cost and ownership as risk-ordered hardening.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"delivery","stage_index":6,"track_order":25},{"id":"enterprise-integration","tier":3,"order":25,"title":"Enterprise integration","intro":{"overview":"This module covers the work between \"the model works\" and \"the model works on this customer's systems,\nfor their people, with their rules\". It has three strands. The data strand moves customer data in.\nConnectors handle auth, enumeration, change tracking, permissions and normalization for each system of\nrecord. Ingestion picks the least invasive channel the client will approve and lands a raw copy you can\nreplay. Incremental sync, CDC and webhooks keep that copy fresh. Retry, deduplication and reconciliation\nkeep it correct when calls fail, writes are ambiguous and events go missing. Legacy integration puts an\nanti-corruption layer around systems you cannot change. The data-understanding strand makes the data fit\nto use: staged onboarding with exit criteria, profiling against the use case, canonical schemas with\nversioned per-source mappings, and per-page triage of messy document estates. The identity strand decides\nwho can see what. SSO and SAML authenticate users through the customer's IdP. SCIM tells you who exists\nand when they leave. RBAC/ABAC mapping turns their groups and attributes into your permissions, enforced\nin retrieval and agent tools. Two operating lessons tie the strands together: binding each of your\nenvironments to the customer's sandbox or production with hard guards, and expressing per-tenant\ndifferences as typed configuration and flags instead of forks. Afterwards you can scope, build and\noperate a production integration with a new enterprise customer that stays fresh, does not leak data and\ndoes not need a fork for every tenant. It matters most for FDEs, who do this in every deployment, and for\nEngineers and Architects building the shared platform underneath.","why":{"engineer":"You build the connector contract, the webhook receiver that verifies, acks fast and queues, the idempotent writers, the watermark-with-overlap and delete handling, the SCIM endpoint whose deactivation revokes tokens and agent jobs, and the reconciliation job whose repair count tells you whether the whole pipeline is healthy.","architect":"You decide where the boundaries go: one canonical schema so N sources and M consumers need N + M mappings, an anti-corruption layer in front of every legacy system, permission checks enforced in retrieval filters and agent tool calls rather than in prompts, a stable user key of issuer plus subject, and tenant differences held as versioned data so one codebase serves fifty customers.","fde":"You are the one who negotiates what the client's security and platform teams will approve, sizes the backfill against their real rate limits, profiles their data before anyone promises a date, runs the mapping sessions with their data owners, wires up their Okta or Entra tenant, and makes sure nothing built against their sandbox writes into production by accident."},"mental_model":"Think of every enterprise integration as a copy of someone else's world, held under three contracts.\nYour index, feature table or agent's view of a customer system is a derived copy of data you do not own.\nThe **freshness contract** says how far behind the source that copy may be. Connectors, sync, CDC and\nwebhooks serve it, and reconciliation proves it. The **meaning contract** says what the copy's fields and\ndocuments actually mean. Profiling, mapping, canonical schemas and document triage serve it, all at one\ntranslation boundary so source quirks do not spread. The **access contract** says who may see or change\neach piece. SSO, SAML, SCIM and RBAC/ABAC mapping serve it, re-checked on every request because the\ncustomer's directory, not yours, is the source of truth. Every lesson strengthens one contract. Sandbox\ndiscipline and tenant configuration let you change how a contract is met for one customer without\nbreaking it for the others. When something goes wrong in production, ask first which contract broke:\nstale, misread, or over-shared.","order":["enterprise-connectors","data-ingestion-from-client-systems","incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","webhooks","retry-deduplication-reconciliation","legacy-system-integration","data-onboarding","data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","client-document-parsing","sso-iam-integration","saml-sso","scim-provisioning","rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","sandbox-vs-production-environments","tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags"],"order_reasons":{"enterprise-connectors":"introduces the five responsibilities (auth, enumeration, change, permissions, normalization) that most later lessons expand on.","data-ingestion-from-client-systems":"choosing a channel the client will approve, landing raw data and checkpointing after the write are the base for any recurring pull.","incremental-synchronization-and-cdc":"once a full pull works, this shows how to fetch only changes, deletes included, and why naive watermarks miss rows.","webhooks":"adds a push channel for low lag, built on the sync and checkpoint ideas you just learned.","retry-deduplication-reconciliation":"explains the three distinct ways the channels above fail and the mechanism that covers each.","legacy-system-integration":"applies the same ingestion and safe-write habits to systems with batch windows, fixed record formats and no API.","data-onboarding":"the gated process that wraps these channels into a customer rollout, setting up the profiling and mapping stages next.","data-profiling-and-quality-assessment":"measures what the landed sample really contains before you commit to mappings or build.","data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas":"turns profiling findings into one internal model with versioned, reviewed per-source mappings.","client-document-parsing":"the unstructured counterpart to mapping, routing each page by type and keeping provenance for citations.","sso-iam-integration":"starts the identity strand: per-tenant IdP configuration, tenant-bound issuer checks and stable user keys.","saml-sso":"the older, still-required protocol, best learned after the general SSO model, along with its specific validation checklist.","scim-provisioning":"closes the gap SSO leaves by telling you who exists, which groups they are in and when they leave.","rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity":"needs both SSO claims and SCIM groups, and connects them to connector ACLs and agent scope.","sandbox-vs-production-environments":"once connectors and identity exist per customer, binds each to the right customer environment and blocks cross-wiring.","tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags":"last, because it is how every per-tenant choice from earlier lessons is stored, rolled out and audited."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are onboarding two enterprise tenants onto an AI support assistant that answers agents' questions\nfrom past tickets and policy documents. Tenant A runs a ServiceNow-like ticketing system with a REST API,\nwebhooks and a sandbox instance, and authenticates through Okta over OIDC. Tenant B exports tickets from\nan old in-house system as a nightly fixed-width file on SFTP, keeps policies as a mix of born-digital and\nscanned PDFs, and uses Entra ID over SAML. Using mock services and synthetic data (generated or provided),\nbuild the integration slice that serves both tenants from one codebase in about a week. Ingest both\nsources incrementally, with deletes. Receive Tenant A's webhooks safely and reconcile what they miss.\nProfile both datasets and map them into one canonical ticket schema. Triage and parse Tenant B's\ndocuments. Authenticate both tenants' users, provision them via SCIM, and enforce their group- and\nregion-based permissions in retrieval. Keep Tenant A's sandbox and production bindings impossible to mix\nup, and express every tenant difference as configuration.","deliverables":["A connector and ingestion service for both sources that lands immutable raw copies, advances checkpoints only after a successful write, applies deletes, throttles to a configured share of the source's rate limit, and includes a webhook receiver that verifies signatures, acks within a second, queues events and processes them idempotently.","A reconciliation job that compares source keys and content hashes with the index for each tenant, repairs drift, and reports missing, extra and stale counts per run.","A profiling report for each tenant's sample that classifies each finding as usable, fixable or blocking, with an owner, and a canonical ticket schema with versioned per-source mappings, a quarantine path and automated checks that run on every load.","A document triage pipeline for Tenant B that routes each page to text extraction, OCR or a layout model, records per-page confidence, sends low-confidence pages to a review queue, and stores page-level provenance used in citations.","OIDC login for Tenant A and SAML login for Tenant B with tenant-bound issuer and audience checks and users keyed by issuer plus subject, a SCIM 2.0 endpoint whose deactivation revokes sessions, tokens and scheduled jobs, and a per-tenant mapping config that turns IdP group IDs into roles and region attributes into ABAC rules applied as retrieval filters.","A typed, versioned tenant configuration file and a release flag for the new retriever, plus an environment binding with a guard that refuses to start when a sandbox credential points at a production index or the reverse."],"rubric":["Killing the webhook receiver for ten minutes and deleting three source records during the outage leads to all three being removed from the index on the next reconciliation run, and the run's repair count shows it.","Replaying the same webhook batch twice, and retrying a timed-out write-back, produces no duplicate index entries or duplicate notes in the mock ticketing system.","A synthetic user in Tenant B's EMEA group never retrieves an APAC ticket or a restricted policy page, a SCIM deactivation removes their access within a stated, measured time, and a Tenant A token is rejected at Tenant B's workspace.","Every canonical field can be traced to a mapping version and a source field, an unmapped status value goes to quarantine with an open question for the data owner, and no consumer reads a source's native shape.","Scanned pages with no text layer show up in the route mix and the review queue, none silently drop out of the index, and every answer cites a document and page.","Grepping the codebase for either tenant's name or ID finds nothing outside configuration and test fixtures, and enabling the new retriever for one tenant leaves the other tenant's behavior unchanged."]}},"topics":[{"id":"enterprise-connectors","key":"enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Enterprise connectors (Salesforce, SAP, ServiceNow, SharePoint, Confluence, Jira, Slack, Teams)","taxonomy_name":"enterprise connectors (Salesforce, SAP, ServiceNow, SharePoint, Confluence, Jira, Slack, Teams)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","enterprise-integration/webhooks","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security"],"tldr":"A connector pulls content, metadata and permissions out of a system of record and keeps that copy\ncurrent, or lets an agent act in it. The hard parts are auth, rate limits, change, deletions and\npermissions; missing one makes the assistant stale or leaky.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"legacy-system-integration","key":"enterprise-integration/legacy-system-integration","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Legacy system integration","taxonomy_name":"legacy system integration","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution"],"tldr":"Legacy systems hold the data AI features need but offer no modern API and cannot be changed or\noverloaded. Wrap them in an anti-corruption layer that uses the least invasive access path and\ntranslates their model into yours, so quirks stop at one boundary.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"sso-iam-integration","key":"enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"SSO / IAM integration (Okta, Entra ID)","taxonomy_name":"SSO / IAM integration (Okta, Entra ID)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["enterprise-integration/saml-sso","enterprise-integration/scim-provisioning","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","agents/mcp-authentication-authorization-transport-security","system-design/tenant-isolation","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust"],"tldr":"Enterprise identity comes from the customer's IdP (usually Okta or Entra ID) via OIDC or SAML. Safe\nintegration needs per-tenant config, tenant-bound token checks, an immutable user key, ID-based roles and\ndeprovisioning, because the IdP is the source of truth.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"saml-sso","key":"enterprise-integration/saml-sso","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"SAML SSO","taxonomy_name":"SAML SSO","kind":"concept","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","enterprise-integration/scim-provisioning","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling"],"tldr":"SAML 2.0 lets a customer's IdP vouch for a user with a signed XML assertion sent through the browser,\nand many enterprises still mandate it. XML signatures are subtle: use a maintained library and still\ncheck audience, recipient, time, correlation and replay.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"scim-provisioning","key":"enterprise-integration/scim-provisioning","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"SCIM provisioning","taxonomy_name":"SCIM provisioning","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","enterprise-integration/saml-sso","enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture"],"tldr":"SCIM 2.0 is a standard REST API you expose so the customer's IdP can create, update, deactivate and\ngroup your users automatically. SSO says who logs in; SCIM says who *exists* and when they leave, so\nleavers and stale memberships stop lingering.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","key":"enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"RBAC / ABAC mapping to customer identity","taxonomy_name":"RBAC / ABAC mapping to customer identity","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","enterprise-integration/scim-provisioning","enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","backend-fundamentals/rbac","rag-retrieval/acl-aware-retrieval","agent-reliability/tool-permissions-and-least-privilege","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","safety-security/cross-tenant-data-leakage"],"tldr":"Mapping is the per-tenant layer that turns customer IdP group IDs into your roles (RBAC) and customer\nattributes into policy inputs (ABAC). Keep it as config keyed by immutable IDs, evaluated per request,\nbecause retrieval and agents are where wrong mappings leak.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"webhooks","key":"enterprise-integration/webhooks","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Webhooks","taxonomy_name":"webhooks","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","backend-fundamentals/webhook-signature-verification-and-replay-protection","backend-fundamentals/message-queues","system-design/event-driven-architecture","fastapi-for-ai/idempotency-keys"],"tldr":"A webhook is an HTTP POST a source sends when something changes, so you learn in seconds without\npolling. Delivery is at-least-once, unordered and public, so verify, ack fast, queue, process\nidempotently, re-fetch current state, and reconcile for events that never arrive.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","key":"enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Incremental synchronization & CDC","taxonomy_name":"incremental synchronization & CDC","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["enterprise-integration/webhooks","enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","enterprise-integration/legacy-system-integration","rag-retrieval/freshness-document-deletion-index-consistency","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy"],"tldr":"Incremental sync copies only what changed; change data capture (CDC) reads every insert, update and\ndelete from the database log. Watermarks are simple but miss deletes and late commits, so the mechanism\nyou pick sets how fresh and costly an AI index is.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"retry-deduplication-reconciliation","key":"enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Retry / deduplication / reconciliation","taxonomy_name":"retry / deduplication / reconciliation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["enterprise-integration/webhooks","enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","serving-inference/retry-strategies","serving-inference/idempotency","system-design/outbox-inbox-patterns","system-design/exactly-once-processing-limitations","agent-reliability/idempotent-tool-execution","backend-fundamentals/message-queues"],"tldr":"Retry handles failed calls, deduplication makes retries safe against writes that succeeded silently, and\nreconciliation (a scheduled source-versus-target comparison) catches events that never arrived. Each\ncovers a failure the other two cannot see, so mature integrations run all three.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"sandbox-vs-production-environments","key":"enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Sandbox vs production environments","taxonomy_name":"sandbox vs production environments","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","enterprise-integration/data-onboarding","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","deployment/uat","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management"],"tldr":"Customer systems come in pairs: production with real data and side effects, and sandboxes that differ in\ndata, configuration and limits. Build and verify against the sandbox, promote deliberately, and use hard\nguards so the two never mix.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","key":"enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Tenant-specific configuration & feature flags","taxonomy_name":"tenant-specific configuration & feature flags","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/tenant-isolation","enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments","customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","prompting/prompt-versioning","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management"],"tldr":"Tenant configuration expresses each customer's differences (model, guardrails, connectors, enabled\nfeatures) as typed, versioned data resolved at runtime, and feature flags decide who gets which code\npath, so one codebase serves every customer instead of a fork per customer.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-onboarding","key":"enterprise-integration/data-onboarding","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Data onboarding","taxonomy_name":"data onboarding","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery","enterprise-integration/data-ingestion-from-client-systems","enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","security-governance-compliance/data-classification","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments"],"tldr":"Data onboarding takes a new customer from \"access granted\" to \"data loaded, validated and syncing\" in\ngated stages: inventory, sample, profile, map, SME-checked pilot, backfill and sync. The customer's data,\nnot the model, is usually the slowest and riskiest part.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-ingestion-from-client-systems","key":"enterprise-integration/data-ingestion-from-client-systems","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Data ingestion from client systems","taxonomy_name":"data ingestion from client systems","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","enterprise-integration/webhooks","enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","enterprise-integration/legacy-system-integration","deployment/private-endpoints","scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery"],"tldr":"Client ingestion pulls data you do not own through channels the client approves. Pick the least invasive\nchannel, land an immutable raw copy, advance the checkpoint only after a durable write, throttle to\nprotect the source, and reconcile for deletes the channel cannot see.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","key":"enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Data mapping & canonical schemas (schema mapping)","taxonomy_name":"data mapping & canonical schemas (schema mapping)","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["data-architecture/data-contracts","scoping/domain-modeling","system-design/domain-driven-design-and-bounded-contexts","enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","enterprise-integration/data-onboarding","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","data-architecture/data-lineage","node-ts-for-ai/runtime-schema-validation"],"tldr":"A canonical schema is your one internal model of business concepts; schema mapping is the per-source,\nversioned translation into it. With N sources and M consumers you maintain about N + M mappings instead\nof N × M, and every component reads one shape.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","key":"enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Data profiling & quality assessment","taxonomy_name":"data profiling & quality assessment","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["enterprise-integration/data-onboarding","enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","data-architecture/data-contracts","customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","llmops-platform/drift","security-governance-compliance/data-classification"],"tldr":"Profiling measures what a client's data actually contains (nulls, value sets, formats, duplicates,\nfreshness); assessment judges that against the use case as usable, fixable or blocking. Findings become\nautomated checks on every load, because AI systems degrade quietly on bad data.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"client-document-parsing","key":"enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","module":"enterprise-integration","name":"Client document parsing (PDF, scans, OCR)","taxonomy_name":"client document parsing (PDF, scans, OCR)","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["rag-retrieval/document-parsing","multimodal/ocr","multimodal/document-understanding","multimodal/vision-language-models","data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","safety-security/pii-redaction","rag-retrieval/citations-grounding"],"tldr":"Client documents mix born-digital PDFs, scans, faxes and protected files. Parse them by triage: detect\neach page's type, route it to the cheapest working method (text extraction, OCR, layout or vision model),\nscore confidence, review doubtful pages, and keep page provenance.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"delivery","stage_index":6,"track_order":26},{"id":"customer-data-evaluation","tier":3,"order":26,"title":"Customer data & evaluation","intro":{"overview":"This module is about proving, on one customer's own data and by their own experts' standard, that an\nAI system does the job they are paying for, and keeping it true after go-live. The evals module teaches\nthe measuring instrument in general; this one teaches how to build and run it inside a client\nengagement, where the data is governed, SME time is scarce, and acceptance is a contract. It starts by\nturning business KPIs into signed acceptance criteria with baselines and sample sizes, then builds the\nreference those criteria are measured against: a golden dataset agreed by client SMEs, sampled by\nstratum so rare high-cost cases are dense enough to judge, labelled under written annotation\nguidelines in facilitated labeling sessions. Domain prompt engineering turns the tacit rules those\nsessions surface into tested instructions, and eval review loops sort every failure into system, label,\nguideline or grader error. Once a system is accepted, customer-specific regression suites stop shared\nchanges from breaking one customer quietly, per-customer guardrails enforce each tenant's policy in\ncode, and fine-tuning pipelines, model integration and shadow mode let you change the model under a live\ncustomer with evidence instead of hope. Production feedback classification and feedback loops to\nproduct close the cycle, turning thumbs-down and tickets into routed failure modes, regression cases and\nroadmap decisions. Afterwards you can run a pilot from scoping to sign-off and keep a multi-customer\ndeployment healthy. It matters most for FDEs, who own the customer relationship and the acceptance\ndecision, and for architects designing a platform that serves many customers from shared components.","why":{"engineer":"You build the per-customer regression suite, the tenant guardrail engine and the leakage-safe fine-tuning pipeline, and gate every shared prompt or model change on paired, case-by-case flips for each customer rather than on a blended global score.","architect":"You design where customer specificity lives (versioned guardrail config with a platform floor, per-customer adapters, model adapters with capability checks, per-tenant suites) so that one platform serves many customers without forking code or letting averages hide a regression.","fde":"You get the sponsor to sign KPI-based acceptance criteria before results exist, run the SME labeling sessions that produce an authoritative golden set, and bring shadow-mode evidence to the customer's risk committee before any user sees a new model."},"mental_model":"Picture the customer's judgement as the only ruler that counts, and the module as the work of copying\nthat ruler faithfully and keeping it in use. First you agree what the ruler measures: business KPIs\ntranslated into proxies, thresholds and sample sizes, signed in advance. Then you manufacture the ruler:\nreal cases sampled by stratum, labelled by the customer's SMEs under a written guideline, with\ndisagreements resolved as policy and the set signed off. Every improvement, whether a domain prompt, a\nfine-tuned adapter or a different model, is a candidate measured against that ruler, and every failure\nis first asked \"is the system wrong, or is the ruler?\" After acceptance the ruler multiplies and\ntravels: frozen per-customer regression suites gate shared changes, guardrails encode each customer's\nrules, shadow mode checks the candidate on live traffic, and classified production feedback adds new\nmarks to the ruler and sends shared problems back to the product. A number you cannot trace to the\ncustomer's experts is not evidence.","order":["acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","representative-test-case-sampling","sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","labeling-sessions","domain-prompt-engineering","eval-review-loops","customer-specific-regression-suites","per-customer-guardrails","fine-tuning-pipelines-on-client-data","model-integration","shadow-mode-validation","production-feedback-classification","feedback-loops-to-product"],"order_reasons":{"acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis":"fixes what \"accepted\" means in business terms first, because it drives dataset size, strata and pilot design.","golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes":"builds the signed, governed reference set the acceptance criteria are measured against.","representative-test-case-sampling":"chooses which real cases enter that set so the score predicts production and rare strata have enough items.","sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines":"writes the shared standard SMEs apply, so labels measure the system rather than reviewer differences.","labeling-sessions":"turns the guideline into labels at speed and surfaces the tacit rules that only live disagreement reveals.","domain-prompt-engineering":"encodes those SME rules, definitions and exceptions as tested instructions, the cheapest lever once a golden set exists.","eval-review-loops":"teaches reading failures with SMEs and attributing each to system, label, guideline or grader before changing anything.","customer-specific-regression-suites":"freezes each customer's accepted cases and fixed incidents so shared changes are gated per customer.","per-customer-guardrails":"makes each tenant's rules versioned, enforced-in-code policy on top of a platform floor, tested on their own data.","fine-tuning-pipelines-on-client-data":"covers the audited, repeatable path to a per-customer adapter when prompting has plateaued.","model-integration":"shows what really changes when a new or customer-mandated model, or a fresh adapter, goes behind the system.","shadow-mode-validation":"validates that candidate model on live customer traffic with zero user impact before any exposure.","production-feedback-classification":"turns raw production signals into labelled failure modes with severity and an owning component.","feedback-loops-to-product":"routes those classified failures into shared fixes, regression cases and a reply to the customer, closing the cycle."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are the forward-deployed engineer for a vendor whose AI system classifies and drafts responses to\ninbound customer-service emails for a regional utility company. The sponsor, the head of customer\noperations, wants average handle time down 25% with no increase in misrouted complaints, and the\nregulator treats complaint misrouting as a reportable issue. Using a synthetic or anonymised set of\nabout 3,000 emails (provided or generated, including a 3% stratum of formal complaints and a 2% stratum\nof vulnerable-customer cases) and two or three volunteers acting as the utility's SMEs, run the\nengagement end to end over a week. Draft and sign acceptance criteria with a baseline, sample and label\na golden set under a written guideline, iterate a domain prompt through review loops, and freeze a\nregression suite. Then the utility mandates a move to a model hosted in its own cloud tenancy: integrate\nit, shadow it against the current system on replayed traffic, configure the utility's guardrails, and\nclassify a batch of simulated production feedback into routed issues, one of which becomes a product\nfix with a regression case.","deliverables":["A signed acceptance document mapping each business KPI (handle time, misrouting rate, complaint recall) to a proxy, a baseline measurement, a threshold, a sample size with its expected interval width, and the split between offline and pilot criteria.","A versioned golden set of at least 300 items, stratified with floors for complaints and vulnerable-customer cases and re-weighting back to the traffic mix, labelled under a written annotation guideline with a decision order, boundary examples and an unsure path, plus a double-labelled subset with agreement reported per criterion.","A labeling-session log and a domain prompt in which every rule traces to a logged SME decision and at least one golden-set case, plus an eval review log attributing every failure from two rounds to system, label, guideline or grader error.","A per-customer regression suite and harness that compares a candidate against production case by case and gates on blocking-case failures, and a guardrail config for the utility layered on a platform floor with tighten-only merging and tests on the utility's labelled data.","A model-integration and shadow-mode report for the mandated model covering adapter changes, tokenizer and context effects, prompt porting, paired flips on the regression suite, adjudicated disagreements, latency, cost and confirmation that the shadow path made no writes.","A feedback taxonomy built from at least 100 simulated production signals classified at trace level, with per-class precision against human labels, and one evidence-backed product issue carried through to a fix, a new regression case and a written reply to the reporting customer."],"rubric":["Thresholds and sample sizes are dated before any result is shown, the misrouting baseline is measured on the current human process rather than assumed to be zero, and the acceptance verdict reports each criterion with its interval, not a point estimate.","The complaint and vulnerable-customer strata each contain enough items to tell 70% from 90% recall, and the headline score is re-weighted to the real traffic mix with the re-weighting shown.","At least one review-loop failure is resolved by changing a label or the guideline rather than the prompt, and agreement on the double-labelled subset is reported with the guideline version it was measured under.","A planted change that improves the global score while breaking a utility-specific rule is caught by the per-customer suite and blocked, with the flipped cases listed.","The utility's complaint-routing rule fires in a test where the prompt instruction is bypassed, proving enforcement sits outside the prompt, and the platform floor cannot be lowered by customer config.","The shadow report gives a clear go, hold or no-go for the mandated model with its evidence, and names at least one difference (tool-call format, tokenizer or error semantics) that a smoke test would have missed."]}},"topics":[{"id":"domain-prompt-engineering","key":"customer-data-evaluation/domain-prompt-engineering","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Domain prompt engineering","taxonomy_name":"domain prompt engineering","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["prompting/prompt-engineering","prompting/few-shot","prompting/system-prompt","prompting/prompt-versioning","scoping/domain-modeling","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","customer-data-evaluation/eval-review-loops"],"tldr":"Domain prompt engineering turns one customer's tacit expertise (vocabulary, policies, exceptions, output\nconventions) into explicit definitions, ordered rules and examples. It is elicitation and testing, not\nwordsmithing: every rule traces to an SME decision and an eval case.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","key":"customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Golden dataset creation with client SMEs","taxonomy_name":"golden dataset creation with client SMEs","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery"],"tldr":"A versioned set of the customer's real inputs with reference answers their own experts agreed on, so\n\"good enough?\" is settled by the customer's judgement. The hard parts are expert time, sampling,\ndisagreement, data governance and formal sign-off.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"representative-test-case-sampling","key":"customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Representative test-case sampling","taxonomy_name":"representative test-case sampling","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/golden-dataset","evals/error-analysis","ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites"],"tldr":"Choosing which real customer inputs enter the eval set so its score predicts production. Sample recent\ntraffic by stratum with a floor per important stratum, then re-weight to the real mix: a trustworthy\noverall number plus enough cases per segment to act on.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","key":"customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"SME review & annotation guidelines","taxonomy_name":"SME review & annotation guidelines","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["evals/rubrics","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","training-adaptation/data-labeling","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","customer-data-evaluation/eval-review-loops","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias"],"tldr":"Guidelines are the written contract for how customer SMEs judge outputs: labels, definitions, decision\norder, boundary examples, an \"unsure\" path. Review protocol keeps judging blind, randomised and\nindependent, with agreement measured. Without both, eval numbers inherit unmeasured expert noise.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"labeling-sessions","key":"customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Labeling sessions","taxonomy_name":"labeling sessions","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":14,"related":["customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","customer-data-evaluation/eval-review-loops","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","training-adaptation/data-labeling","adoption/stakeholder-management"],"tldr":"A scheduled, facilitated block where customer SMEs calibrate, label independently, then resolve\ndisagreements into written guideline rules. Async requests to busy experts stall, and tacit rules surface\nonly live. The facilitator drives throughput and decisions while protecting label independence.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"eval-review-loops","key":"customer-data-evaluation/eval-review-loops","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Eval review loops","taxonomy_name":"eval review loops","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["evals/error-analysis","evals/eval-driven-development","evals/judge-calibration-and-bias","evals/llm-as-a-judge","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis"],"tldr":"The recurring cycle of running the customer eval, reading failures with SMEs, attributing each to the\nsystem, a label, a guideline gap or the grader, fixing the right thing, and re-running with regression\nchecks. A score says how much is wrong, not why.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","key":"customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Acceptance testing against customer KPIs","taxonomy_name":"acceptance testing against customer KPIs","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":18,"related":["scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","deployment/uat","evals/a-b-testing","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes"],"tldr":"The pre-agreed test that decides acceptance: business KPIs become proxies, thresholds and sample sizes,\nsigned before results are seen, then measured offline and in a pilot against a baseline. \"Scores 91%\"\ndoes not answer \"did invoice cost fall without more errors?\"","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"customer-specific-regression-suites","key":"customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Customer-specific regression suites","taxonomy_name":"customer-specific regression suites","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/regression-tests","evals/golden-dataset","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation"],"tldr":"A frozen set of one customer's own cases, run under that customer's configuration whenever a shared\nprompt, model or pipeline changes, and gated case by case against production. Averages hide one\ncustomer's breakage; this suite does not.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"shadow-mode-validation","key":"customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Shadow-mode validation","taxonomy_name":"shadow-mode validation","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","evals/a-b-testing","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","agents/sandboxing","scoping/pilot-to-production-plan"],"tldr":"Shadow mode runs a candidate model, prompt or pipeline on a copy of real traffic while only production's\nanswer reaches users. It measures agreement and judged quality on real inputs, not user outcomes, and\nis safe only if the candidate cannot cause side effects.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"per-customer-guardrails","key":"customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Per-customer guardrails","taxonomy_name":"per-customer guardrails","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["safety-security/input-output-guardrails","safety-security/content-moderation","safety-security/pii-redaction","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","agent-runtime-architecture/policy-enforcement-across-agents-and-tools","system-design/tenant-isolation","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","safety-security/nemo-guardrails"],"tldr":"Input, output and action policies resolved per tenant at request time: a platform floor nobody can\nlower, plus each customer's rules on top. They live in versioned config, are enforced in code, not the\nprompt, and are tested on each customer's labelled data.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"fine-tuning-pipelines-on-client-data","key":"customer-data-evaluation/fine-tuning-pipelines-on-client-data","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Fine-tuning pipelines on client data","taxonomy_name":"fine-tuning pipelines on client data","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":17,"related":["training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","training-adaptation/peft","data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","customer-data-evaluation/model-integration"],"tldr":"A repeatable, audited path from a customer's records to a deployed model, usually a per-customer\nadapter, with permission, redaction, leakage-safe splits, baseline evals and lineage. It must be a\npipeline, not a notebook, because deprecations, deletion requests and new labels force reruns.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"model-integration","key":"customer-data-evaluation/model-integration","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Model integration","taxonomy_name":"model integration","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["system-design/ai-gateway","system-design/model-selection","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","python-for-ai/litellm","prompting/function-tool-calling","llmops-platform/model-registry","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","customer-data-evaluation/fine-tuning-pipelines-on-client-data"],"tldr":"Putting a different model behind a delivered system: a new provider, fine-tuned adapter or\ncustomer-mandated model. Models are not drop-in parts, so it takes an adapter, a capability check,\nre-tuned prompts and the customer's eval suite as the gate.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"production-feedback-classification","key":"customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Production feedback classification","taxonomy_name":"production feedback classification","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/error-analysis","evals/llm-as-a-judge","ml-fundamentals/precision-recall-f1","evals/inter-annotator-agreement","observability/tracing","adoption/feature-request-triage","adoption/usage-analytics","customer-data-evaluation/feedback-loops-to-product"],"tldr":"Turning raw signals (thumbs-down, comments, edits, escalations, tickets) into labelled failure modes with\na severity and an owning component. A thumbs-down rate says something is wrong, not what or who fixes\nit; labelling whole traces against a grown taxonomy does.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"feedback-loops-to-product","key":"customer-data-evaluation/feedback-loops-to-product","module":"customer-data-evaluation","name":"Feedback loops to product","taxonomy_name":"feedback loops to product","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","adoption/feature-request-triage","adoption/continuous-improvement","evals/eval-driven-development","adoption/usage-analytics","customer-data-evaluation/eval-review-loops"],"tldr":"Carrying what deployments learn back into the shared product: evidence-backed issue clusters, regular\ntriage into config, product fix or roadmap, a regression case per fix, and a reply to the reporter.\nWithout it, field teams patch the same problem separately everywhere.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"delivery","stage_index":6,"track_order":27},{"id":"deployment","tier":3,"order":27,"title":"Deployment","intro":{"overview":"This module covers what happens between \"the AI system works in our environment\" and \"the customer's\nusers depend on it, and the customer's team can run it\". Enterprise deployment is shaped by constraints\nthe builders do not control. Compliance constraints turn regimes such as HIPAA, GDPR or export control\ninto concrete rules about where data may be processed, which model providers qualify, and what logs\nmust contain and leave out. Those rules choose the deployment target: your SaaS, the customer's VPC,\ntheir on-prem cluster or an air-gapped site. That target decides the network design: private endpoints\nfor one-way calls to managed services with no internet egress, and site-to-site VPNs for two-way access\nto systems in the customer's data center. Terraform makes that infrastructure reproducible in every\ncustomer account and auditable for every reviewer. Security questionnaires and security and\narchitecture reviews are how the customer checks your claims and your design before they let you near\ntheir data. Health checks and runbooks make the system operable by people who did not build it. UAT\nproves that it does the users' job against acceptance criteria agreed in advance. Rollout criteria and\nrollback triggers set how exposure grows and what pulls it back. A deployment readiness review collects\nall of that evidence into one go/no-go decision, and hypercare carries the system through its first\nweeks of real traffic to a criteria-based handoff. Afterwards you can choose and justify a deployment\nmodel, design its network path, pass a customer security review, and run a staged go-live you could\ndefend in a postmortem. It matters most for FDEs, who live this work on every engagement, and for\nArchitects, whose early design choices decide whether a deployment can pass review at all.","why":{"engineer":"You write the Terraform modules, private endpoint DNS overrides and VPN routes that let the same system install cleanly in each customer account. You implement separate liveness, readiness, startup and synthetic checks. You wire automated rollback triggers to error, latency, cost and sampled quality signals, and you keep the runbooks that every paging alert links to.","architect":"You turn compliance regimes into data residency, provider and logging constraints before the design is fixed. You choose between SaaS, customer VPC, on-prem and air-gapped with the support cost priced in. You walk into the customer's architecture review with an accurate data-flow diagram, a threat model that covers prompt injection and tool permissions, and evidence for every control.","fde":"You run the customer-facing sequence: answer the questionnaire honestly from one source of truth, steer the security review to dated findings, set acceptance criteria and sample sizes for UAT, chair the readiness review, and run hypercare with daily triage until the customer's team meets the exit criteria and owns support."},"mental_model":"Picture deployment as a series of gates the system passes through, each one opened by evidence rather\nthan confidence. The first gate is permission: compliance constraints and the customer's policies decide\nwhat is allowed at all, and that picks the deployment target. The second is reachability: private\nendpoints and VPNs define exactly which network paths exist, and nothing else should. The third is\nreproducibility: Terraform makes the environment a reviewed diff, not a set of console clicks. The\nfourth is trust: questionnaires check your claims, and architecture reviews test your design. The fifth\nis operability: health checks tell infrastructure what to do, and runbooks tell humans. The sixth is\nacceptance: UAT shows that real users get their job done. The readiness review is the checkpoint where\nall of that evidence is inspected together, with items proven, waived with an expiry, or blocking.\nAfter it, rollout criteria widen exposure in stages, rollback triggers narrow it again, and hypercare\nholds the system under close watch until ownership moves to the customer. Every gate produces an\nartifact that the next one consumes. When a gate is skipped, you usually find out in production.","order":["compliance-constraints","customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","private-endpoints","vpn","terraform","security-questionnaires","security-and-architecture-reviews","health-checks","runbooks","uat","rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","deployment-readiness-reviews","hypercare"],"order_reasons":{"compliance-constraints":"start here, because regimes and customer policies set the limits that every later deployment choice must fit inside.","customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped":"compliance and data custody pick the deployment target, which shapes the network, packaging and support model.","private-endpoints":"the default way to reach managed services and model APIs from a customer network with no internet egress.","vpn":"the alternative for two-way access to on-prem systems, best learned against private endpoints so the trade-off is clear.","terraform":"once the target and network paths are known, IaC makes them reproducible and reviewable in every customer account.","security-questionnaires":"the first trust check a buyer runs, and answering it needs the architecture and network design you now have.","security-and-architecture-reviews":"goes deeper than the questionnaire, testing your data flows, trust boundaries and AI-specific threats against evidence.","health-checks":"makes the deployed system observable to infrastructure, as the base for runbooks and rollout signals.","runbooks":"gives the humans who respond to health alerts and known failure modes a tested way to mitigate.","uat":"real users test the system against acceptance criteria agreed in advance, which needs a working, observable environment.","rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers":"defines how exposure grows after acceptance and which signals pull it back, using the health and quality metrics from earlier lessons.","deployment-readiness-reviews":"collects evidence from every earlier lesson into one go/no-go decision, so it only makes sense once you know what that evidence is.","hypercare":"the last step, carrying the system through its first weeks of real traffic to a criteria-based handoff."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are the forward-deployed engineer taking a retrieval-augmented claims assistant from a successful\npilot to production at a regional health insurer. The assistant answers adjusters' questions from\npolicy documents and claim notes that contain protected health information. The insurer runs on AWS\nin one region, permits no internet egress from data workloads, and keeps its claims database in an\non-prem data center. Its security team has sent a 120-question questionnaire and has scheduled an\narchitecture review. Go-live is planned for 300 adjusters across two regions in six weeks. Over a\nweek, using a sandbox cloud account and synthetic claim data, produce everything that deployment\nneeds. Turn the insurer's obligations into constraints and choose the deployment model. Write Terraform\nfor the VPC, the private endpoints to the model provider and storage, and the VPN to a simulated\non-prem network. Answer a representative slice of the questionnaire and prepare the architecture\nreview pack. Implement health checks and write runbooks for the top failure modes. Design and run a\nsmall UAT. Write the staged rollout plan with rollback triggers. Chair a mock readiness review, and\nplan hypercare through to handoff.","deliverables":["A constraints register that maps each regime and customer policy to concrete where, who, what, how-long, how and proof constraints, with a one-page justification of the chosen deployment model and the rejected alternatives with their costs.","A Terraform repository that stands up the VPC, private endpoints with working DNS overrides, and a site-to-site VPN to a simulated on-prem network, with a reviewed plan output, remote state, and evidence that a clean apply into a second account succeeds.","Answers to at least 25 questionnaire items drawn from a single answer library, plus an architecture review pack with a data-flow diagram, trust boundaries, a threat model covering prompt injection and tool permissions, and a findings tracker with owners and due dates.","Liveness, readiness, startup and synthetic end-to-end checks running against the deployed service, and runbooks for at least five failure modes (provider outage, rate limiting, cost runaway, index staleness, an unsafe output report), each linked from its alert.","A UAT plan and results with acceptance criteria, a scoring rubric, a justified sample size, the pass rate with a confidence interval, and defects classified by cause.","A rollout ladder with stage criteria, minimum samples, hard and soft rollback triggers, a readiness review record with every item marked proven, waived with an expiry, or blocking, and a hypercare plan with a triage cadence and written exit criteria."],"rubric":["VPC flow logs show zero bytes reaching the model provider or storage through NAT or the internet, and the services reject requests that do not come through the private endpoint.","Liveness does not depend on downstream services: taking the model provider or vector store offline in a test removes instances from readiness without triggering restarts, and the synthetic check alerts.","Every questionnaire answer is traceable to a control owner and evidence, and at least one answer is an honest \"no\" with a compensating control and a dated plan, not an aspirational \"yes\".","Running each runbook in a rehearsal, by someone other than its author, mitigates the simulated failure without escalation, and any step that turns out wrong is recorded and fixed.","The UAT pass rate is reported with its interval and per-slice results, and model-quality defects are separated from integration bugs, each with a different owner.","Every rollback trigger has a numeric threshold, a minimum sample size and a named action. A replayed incident shows the trigger firing and exposure dropping to the previous stage.","Hypercare exit criteria are things the customer's team demonstrates, such as resolving a set share of tickets alone and running one runbook unassisted, not a date on the calendar."]}},"topics":[{"id":"customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","key":"deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","module":"deployment","name":"Customer VPC / on-prem / air-gapped deployment","taxonomy_name":"customer VPC / on-prem / air-gapped","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["deployment/private-endpoints","deployment/vpn","deployment/terraform","deployment/compliance-constraints","infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture","llmops-platform/ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","safety-security/supply-chain-security-for-models-and-datasets"],"tldr":"Customer VPC, on-prem and air-gapped are deployments where the customer owns the perimeter, because\ntheir data may not leave their custody. Each step inward trades your operability for their control,\nso the job becomes shipping software that installs, verifies and upgrades offline.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"private-endpoints","key":"deployment/private-endpoints","module":"deployment","name":"Private endpoints","taxonomy_name":"private endpoints","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","deployment/vpn","deployment/terraform","deployment/compliance-constraints","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","system-design/ai-gateway","infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture"],"tldr":"A private endpoint gives a managed service a private IP inside the consumer's network, so traffic never\ncrosses the internet and public access can be switched off. It pairs a network interface with a DNS\noverride; the DNS half is where most failures live.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"vpn","key":"deployment/vpn","module":"deployment","name":"VPN","taxonomy_name":"VPN","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["deployment/private-endpoints","deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","security-governance-compliance/zero-trust","infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","enterprise-integration/legacy-system-integration","enterprise-integration/data-ingestion-from-client-systems"],"tldr":"A VPN joins two private networks, or a device to a network, through an encrypted tunnel over the\ninternet, so AI workloads reach on-prem data privately. It is quick and cheap, but it joins\naddress spaces, so scope, routing and MTU need design.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"terraform","key":"deployment/terraform","module":"deployment","name":"Terraform (infrastructure as code)","taxonomy_name":"Terraform (IaC)","kind":"tool","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":19,"related":["deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","deployment/private-endpoints","deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments","infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture"],"tldr":"Terraform describes networks, GPU node pools, buckets and permissions as versioned HCL, diffs that\nagainst a state file and the cloud APIs, shows a plan and applies only the difference, so every\nenvironment and customer account is reproducible and reviewable.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"security-questionnaires","key":"deployment/security-questionnaires","module":"deployment","name":"Security questionnaires","taxonomy_name":"security questionnaires","kind":"practice","difficulty":"intro","est_minutes":15,"related":["deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","deployment/compliance-constraints","security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","security-governance-compliance/ai-security","safety-security/data-leakage","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management"],"tldr":"A security questionnaire is the buyer's written audit of a vendor: hundreds of questions on encryption,\naccess, incidents, subprocessors and AI data use. Answers become contract commitments, so answer from\none maintained source of truth, precisely and never aspirationally.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"security-and-architecture-reviews","key":"deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","module":"deployment","name":"Security & architecture reviews","taxonomy_name":"security & architecture reviews","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["deployment/security-questionnaires","deployment/compliance-constraints","deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","security-governance-compliance/threat-modeling","security-governance-compliance/ai-security","system-design/c4-diagrams","system-design/adrs","safety-security/owasp-top-10-for-llms"],"tldr":"A security and architecture review tests a proposed system's design before go-live: data flows, trust\nboundaries, identity, network paths and, for AI, prompt injection and tool permissions. Pass it with an\naccurate diagram, a threat model and evidence; leave with dated findings.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"compliance-constraints","key":"deployment/compliance-constraints","module":"deployment","name":"Compliance constraints","taxonomy_name":"compliance constraints","kind":"concept","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["security-governance-compliance/compliance-architecture","security-governance-compliance/data-residency","security-governance-compliance/audit-trails","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp","data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","deployment/security-questionnaires","deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews"],"tldr":"Compliance constraints are the rules laws, contracts and audit frameworks impose on where data is\nprocessed, who sees it, how long it is kept and what is proven. For AI they decide region, model\nprovider and logging design; deep treatment lives in security-governance-compliance.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"deployment-readiness-reviews","key":"deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","module":"deployment","name":"Deployment readiness reviews","taxonomy_name":"deployment readiness reviews","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","deployment/uat","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","deployment/runbooks","deployment/hypercare","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","scoping/pilot-to-production-plan"],"tldr":"A readiness review is a scheduled go/no-go before production traffic: every launch requirement is\nchecked against written evidence by a named owner. Each item ends up proven, waived with an expiry, or\nblocking, which turns \"we think we're ready\" into a decision.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"uat","key":"deployment/uat","module":"deployment","name":"UAT (user acceptance testing)","taxonomy_name":"UAT","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","evals/task-specific-acceptance-criteria","customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments","deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation"],"tldr":"In UAT the customer's real users run the system on realistic tasks against criteria agreed up front.\nFor AI systems it scores samples with a rubric, sizes the sample for a meaningful pass rate, and\nseparates model defects from integration bugs.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","key":"deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","module":"deployment","name":"Rollout criteria & rollback triggers","taxonomy_name":"rollout criteria & rollback triggers","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":18,"related":["llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/rollback","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","reliability/slas-slos","enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","deployment/runbooks"],"tldr":"Rollout criteria say what evidence moves a release to the next exposure stage; rollback triggers say\nwhat signal sends it back. Both are written beforehand as thresholds with minimum samples, because\npeople rationalise bad numbers mid-incident, and AI quality signals are noisy and slow.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"runbooks","key":"deployment/runbooks","module":"deployment","name":"Runbooks","taxonomy_name":"runbooks","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["deployment/health-checks","deployment/hypercare","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","observability/monitoring","adoption/handoff-to-customer-teams","delivery-ownership/production-debugging"],"tldr":"A runbook is a written, tested procedure for one operational situation: trigger, impact check, exact\nmitigation steps, recovery check and escalation. It exists because the incident handler rarely built\nthe system, so good runbooks are alert-linked, mitigation-first and rehearsed.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"health-checks","key":"deployment/health-checks","module":"deployment","name":"Health checks","taxonomy_name":"health checks","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["fastapi-for-ai/health-readiness-endpoints","backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","observability/monitoring","reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","reliability/slas-slos","deployment/runbooks","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers"],"tldr":"Health checks tell infrastructure whether an instance should get traffic or be restarted. Liveness,\nreadiness, startup and synthetic end-to-end checks answer different questions, so they stay separate;\nmixing them turns a degraded dependency into a total outage.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"hypercare","key":"deployment/hypercare","module":"deployment","name":"Hypercare","taxonomy_name":"hypercare","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":13,"related":["deployment/runbooks","deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","adoption/handoff-to-customer-teams","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","adoption/change-management","llmops-platform/model-monitoring"],"tldr":"Hypercare is a time-boxed period after go-live where the delivery team gives elevated support: closer\nmonitoring, daily triage, faster fixes and named contacts. Real users always find what UAT missed, and it\nends on written exit criteria and a handoff, not reassignment.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"delivery","stage_index":6,"track_order":28},{"id":"delivery-ownership","tier":3,"order":28,"title":"Delivery & ownership","intro":{"overview":"This module is about what happens after an AI system ships and someone has to answer for it. It starts\nwith the single failure: production debugging teaches you to rebuild the exact input the model saw\n(rendered prompt, retrieved chunks, tool results, model snapshot, sampling settings) from traces, then\nbisect and replay instead of guessing. Incident severity and escalation decides, before anything breaks,\nhow bad a failure is by its impact rather than its cause, including the silent ones that return HTTP 200\nwith wrong or unsafe content. Root-cause analysis and postmortems turn a mitigated incident into\ncontributing factors and owned actions, without blame. Failure analysis zooms out from one incident to\nhundreds of small failures, sampled, labelled by pipeline stage and ranked by harm. System reliability\ngives the owner's view of four levers: how often things fail, how big each failure is, how fast you\nnotice and how fast you recover. Operational ownership and support boundaries say who is paged for each\nlayer and how issues cross between you, the customer and vendors. SLAs turn all of this into a contract\nyour dependency chain and on-call rotation can actually back. Technical documentation keeps the system\nworking once the builders leave, and productizing turns the third bespoke build into one shared core.\nAfterwards you can run an incident end to end, write a severity scale, a postmortem, a boundary table\nand an SLA, and decide when field work should become product. It matters most for FDEs and for\nEngineers who own a live service.","why":{"engineer":"You carry the pager for an LLM pipeline, so you need trace IDs, captured prompts and a replay harness that let you reproduce a one-in-ten failure. You also need a severity scale you can apply at 3 a.m. without a debate, and postmortem actions that change the system rather than ask people to be more careful.","architect":"You set the reliability levers and the promises built on them: change gating, containment, symptom alerts and rollback time, the dependency-chain ceiling behind any SLA, and the shared core, configuration and extension points that let one design serve many customers without turning into N forks.","fde":"You go live inside someone else's environment, so you must agree the boundary table, support SLA and escalation path with the customer before launch, leave runbooks and a system card behind when you rotate off, and notice when the third customer is asking for the same build so it can become product."},"mental_model":"Picture delivery as a loop that runs on every failure, wrapped in a set of promises. The inner loop runs\nfast: detect, classify by severity, escalate to the right owner, mitigate, then debug from the trace\nuntil you can replay the failure. The outer loop runs slowly: postmortems find the contributing factors\nbehind each serious incident, and failure analysis ranks the many small failures nobody declared. Both\nfeed one prioritised list of reliability work, chosen by whichever of the four levers is cheapest to pull\nnext. Around the loops sit the promises: ownership says who acts at each layer, the SLA says what the\ncustomer is owed, and documentation lets someone who was not there carry the loop on. Productizing is the\nloop at business scale: what each deployment teaches flows back into one core, so every fix reaches\nevery customer. A promise without a loop behind it is a future breach, and a loop with no promises\naround it is unowned work.","order":["production-debugging","incident-severity-and-escalation","root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","failure-analysis","system-reliability","operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","slas","technical-documentation","productizing-repeated-customer-solutions"],"order_reasons":{"production-debugging":"the base skill of reconstructing and replaying one live failure, which every later lesson assumes.","incident-severity-and-escalation":"adds the response frame around debugging: classify by impact, decide who is woken and when to escalate.","root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems":"follows a mitigated incident to its contributing factors and owned actions, using the timeline from the first two lessons.","failure-analysis":"widens from one incident to hundreds of small failures, ranked by stage and harm, so effort goes to the dominant modes.","system-reliability":"combines incident and failure data into the owner's four levers and decides which lever to fund next.","operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries":"names who owns each layer and how issues cross between you, the customer and vendors, which an SLA needs.","slas":"writes the service and support promise, computed from the dependency ceiling and backed by the ownership and on-call model.","technical-documentation":"writes down runbooks, decisions and the system card so the loop and the promises survive handover.","productizing-repeated-customer-solutions":"the business-scale step, which assumes you can already deliver, own and document one deployment well."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are the forward-deployed engineer handing over a contract-review assistant to an insurance customer\nafter an eight-week pilot. The system retrieves clauses from the customer's document store, calls a\nhosted LLM to flag risky terms, and writes findings to their case-management tool. Two other insurers\nwant the same thing, and your team has already copied the repository once for the second. Over a week,\nprepare the deployment for production ownership. Instrument the pipeline so any flagged finding can be\ntraced to its rendered prompt, retrieved chunks and model version, and build a replay harness. Take a\nseeded incident (a prompt change that silently stopped flagging indemnity clauses for one week) through\nseverity classification, mitigation, debugging and a blameless postmortem. Run a failure analysis on 100\nsampled outputs. Then write the ownership boundary table, the SLA, the runbooks and handover documents,\nand a productizing proposal that separates the shared core from per-insurer configuration and extensions.","deliverables":["A tracing and replay harness that, given a request ID, rebuilds the exact model input and replays it N times with one variable changed, plus a short write-up of the seeded incident reproduced from its trace.","A severity scale and escalation policy with at least four levels, including content-level criteria for missed or wrong findings and cross-tenant exposure, with time-to-acknowledge and update cadence per level.","A blameless postmortem for the seeded incident: a timeline, contributing factors across prevention, detection and mitigation, and at least three owned, dated action items.","A failure-analysis report on 100 sampled outputs, labelled by failure mode and pipeline stage, with severity-weighted counts and the top three fixes ranked by expected reduction in harm.","An ownership boundary table covering model provider, your platform, the application, the customer's document store and identity provider, plus a draft SLA with service and support terms, exclusions, credits and the dependency-chain arithmetic.","A handover pack containing an architecture overview, at least two decision records, runbooks tied to specific alerts and a system card listing model and prompt versions, eval results and known failure modes, plus a productizing proposal with the core, configuration and extension split and break-even estimate."],"rubric":["Replaying the seeded incident's request ID reproduces the missed indemnity flag at the old prompt version and not at the fixed one, across repeated runs that account for sampling variance.","The postmortem names no individual as the cause, does not stop at \"the model missed it\", and each action item changes a test, an alert, a deploy gate or a guardrail rather than asking for vigilance.","The failure-analysis categories come from reading the sampled outputs, each category shows its share and stage, and the chosen fix targets the top weighted category, not the most recent complaint.","The SLA's availability figure does not exceed the product of the dependencies' availabilities, every support response time maps to a named rotation, and the internal SLO alert is stricter than the contract.","In a tabletop run of \"answers cite superseded policy documents\", a reviewer can use the boundary table and runbook alone to route the issue to its owner within one step, with no ticket bouncing.","The productizing proposal lists every difference between the existing forks and assigns each to core, configuration or extension, refusing at least one customer-specific request with a stated reason."]}},"topics":[{"id":"production-debugging","key":"delivery-ownership/production-debugging","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Production debugging","taxonomy_name":"production debugging","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["delivery-ownership/failure-analysis","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","observability/tracing","agent-reliability/deterministic-replay-and-reproducibility","agent-reliability/conversation-execution-identifiers","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","llmops-platform/rollback"],"tldr":"Production debugging finds why a live system misbehaved when you cannot pause it or rely on a repeat.\nFor LLM systems: stop the bleeding, rebuild the *exact* input from traces, bisect the pipeline, and\nreplay with one variable changed at a time.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"failure-analysis","key":"delivery-ownership/failure-analysis","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Failure analysis","taxonomy_name":"failure analysis","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["delivery-ownership/production-debugging","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","evals/error-analysis","reliability/fmea","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","rag-retrieval/retrieval-failure-diagnosis","agent-reliability/agent-trajectory-evaluation"],"tldr":"Failure analysis finds the few failure modes causing most harm across many failures. Sample without\nbias, label each by error and stage, count, weight by severity, and fix the top of the list. LLM\nfailures are diverse and quiet, so this matters.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"incident-severity-and-escalation","key":"delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Incident severity & escalation","taxonomy_name":"incident severity & escalation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["delivery-ownership/production-debugging","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","delivery-ownership/slas","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","deployment/runbooks","deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers"],"tldr":"A severity scale classifies incidents by impact, not cause, so response speed, paging and update cadence\nare decided in advance. Escalation rules add expertise or authority. AI systems need extra criteria for\nquality and safety incidents that throw no errors.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","key":"delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Root-cause analysis & postmortems","taxonomy_name":"root-cause analysis & postmortems","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":17,"related":["delivery-ownership/production-debugging","delivery-ownership/failure-analysis","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","reliability/fmea","security-governance-compliance/ai-incident-response","evals/regression-tests"],"tldr":"Root-cause analysis asks why an incident was possible; a postmortem turns the answer into owned, tracked\nwork. Complex systems rarely have one root cause, so map contributing factors across prevention,\ndetection and mitigation, blamelessly. \"The model hallucinated\" is a symptom, never a cause.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","key":"delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Operational ownership & support boundaries","taxonomy_name":"operational ownership & support boundaries","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","delivery-ownership/slas","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","deployment/hypercare","deployment/runbooks","deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","reliability/slas-slos"],"tldr":"Operational ownership means a named team is paged for a component, can change it, and answers for it.\nSupport boundaries say where that stops and how issues cross. AI deployments span many parties, so\nwithout them incidents bounce between teams unfixed.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"slas","key":"delivery-ownership/slas","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"SLAs","taxonomy_name":"SLAs","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["reliability/slas-slos","delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","delivery-ownership/system-reliability","scoping/statement-of-work","infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","system-design/model-fallback-strategies","observability/latency-percentiles"],"tldr":"An SLA is a contract, not a dashboard: covered service, exact measurement, exclusions and remedies.\nDelivery owners run service SLAs (availability, latency) and support SLAs (response by severity), and\ncommit only to what dependencies and on-call can back.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"system-reliability","key":"delivery-ownership/system-reliability","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"System reliability","taxonomy_name":"system reliability","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["reliability/high-availability","reliability/fault-tolerance","reliability/slas-slos","delivery-ownership/slas","delivery-ownership/failure-analysis","delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","agent-reliability/task-completion-rate","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments"],"tldr":"System reliability is the probability the system does the right thing for users over time. Owning it\nmeans four levers: fail less often, fail smaller, notice sooner, recover faster. For AI, \"right\"\nincludes answer quality, so a fully-up system can be unreliable.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"technical-documentation","key":"delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Technical documentation","taxonomy_name":"technical documentation","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["system-design/adrs","deployment/runbooks","fastapi-for-ai/openapi-docs","adoption/handoff-to-customer-teams","delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","deployment/security-questionnaires","llmops-platform/prompt-registry"],"tldr":"Technical documentation keeps a system working when its builders are gone. It works when each document\nhas one reader and one job, lives next to the code, has an owner and is checked for staleness. AI systems\nadd model, prompt, eval and failure-mode pages.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","key":"delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","module":"delivery-ownership","name":"Productizing repeated customer solutions","taxonomy_name":"productizing repeated customer solutions","kind":"practice","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["customer-data-evaluation/feedback-loops-to-product","adoption/feature-request-triage","strategy/platform-strategy","scoping/productionization","prompting/prompt-templates","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","agent-runtime-architecture/multi-tenant-state-isolation","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation"],"tldr":"When the field builds the same solution for a third customer, make it product: one shared core,\nper-customer configuration, extension points for unique parts. Too early bakes in the wrong\nabstraction; too late leaves N diverging forks whose upkeep scales with customers.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"delivery","stage_index":6,"track_order":29},{"id":"adoption","tier":3,"order":29,"title":"Adoption","intro":{"overview":"This module is about the gap between \"the system is live\" and \"the system is used and is paying for\nitself\". A deployed AI system can pass every eval and still fail, because people ignore it, work around\nit or trust it too much. Stakeholder management maps who can help, block or be affected, and finds the\nsecurity reviewers, data owners and team leads who can veto late. Change management names the exact\nbehavior that must change, finds what blocks it for each group, and redesigns incentives and workflow\naround the tool. Workshops pull the knowledge out of experts' heads into concrete artifacts: a mapped\nworkflow, an agreed rubric, labelled examples. User training teaches a working mental model of where the\nsystem is reliable, how it fails and how to check its output. Usage analytics instruments what people do\nwith each output (accept, edit, regenerate, abandon) against an eligible population. Adoption and\nvalue-realization measurement keeps \"they use it\" separate from \"it changed a business outcome\", using a\nbaseline or comparison group. Exec communication turns those numbers into a conclusion-first update with\na clear ask. Continuous improvement and feature-request triage route production evidence and customer\nasks into measured fixes or reasoned roadmap decisions. Handoff moves ownership to the customer's team\nthrough staged, demonstrated exit criteria. Afterwards you can plan and run a rollout, instrument it,\nprove or disprove its value with a defensible number, and leave behind a team that can run it. It\nmatters most for FDEs, and for Architects who own a platform that has to show value to keep its budget.","why":{"engineer":"You build the disposition events (accepted unchanged, edit size, regenerate, abandon) without copying raw prompts into the warehouse, and you run the improvement loop: classify failures, fix at the cheapest layer, add the cases to a frozen regression suite, gate the release, and check that the targeted failure bucket actually shrank in production.","architect":"You design measurement in from the start: an eligible-population denominator, a baseline or comparison group agreed during scoping, guardrail metrics beside the value metric, and system boundaries (repos, CI, monitoring, identities) chosen so the customer team can own the system after handoff without a rebuild.","fde":"You are the person in the room. You map stakeholders and veto holders in week one, run the workshops that settle quality criteria, train users to verify outputs, triage asks without promising forks, report to the sponsor with the conclusion and the ask first, and hand over only when the customer team has shown it can ship a gated change and handle an incident."},"mental_model":"Picture adoption as a funnel with a loop beside it. The funnel runs from eligible people, to people who\ntry the tool, to people who use it for the intended work, to work that comes out measurably better than\nthe baseline. Each layer leaks for its own reason, and each lesson is a tool for one leak. People who\nnever start are a stakeholder or change problem. People who try and drop off are usually a training,\ntrust or workflow problem. People who use it without effect are a quality or scoping problem. Usage\nanalytics shows where the funnel leaks, and value measurement shows what reaches the bottom. The loop\nbeside it takes what leaks out (failures, complaints, feature asks), sorts it by type, and feeds it back\nas fixes, training or roadmap decisions. Exec communication reports the funnel's shape to whoever funds\nit. Handoff is the moment the loop's owner changes. Usage is the leading indicator you act on weekly;\nrealized value is the lagging number that renews the contract. Never report one as the other.","order":["stakeholder-management","change-management","workshops","user-training","usage-analytics","adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","exec-communication","continuous-improvement","feature-request-triage","handoff-to-customer-teams"],"order_reasons":{"stakeholder-management":"find the sponsors, users and veto holders first, because every later step depends on knowing who must agree.","change-management":"define the behavior change and its blockers per group, which sets the target for training, analytics and measurement.","workshops":"the main tool for getting knowledge and agreement out of those stakeholders as concrete artifacts such as rubrics and workflows.","user-training":"teaches users the mental model and verification habits the change plan calls for, with drills rather than feature tours.","usage-analytics":"instruments what users do with each output, so you can see whether the change and training are working.","adoption-and-value-realization-measurement":"builds on usage data, adding a baseline or comparison group to separate engagement from business value.","exec-communication":"turns adoption and value numbers into conclusion-first updates and decisions for the people who fund the work.","continuous-improvement":"the operating loop that turns classified production failures into gated, measured fixes.","feature-request-triage":"routes customer asks, separating real features from quality failures, training gaps and model limits that belong in the improvement loop.","handoff-to-customer-teams":"the last step, which moves ownership of the whole loop to the customer once they have shown they can run it."},"capstone":{"brief":"You are the forward-deployed engineer on an AI drafting assistant for a mid-size insurer's claims team.\nIt drafts replies to policyholder emails from the claim file and policy documents. It has been live for\nfour weeks across 180 eligible adjusters in three regional teams. The sponsor, the head of claims\noperations, was sold \"30% faster first response\". The dashboard shows 1,400 requests a week, and the\nsponsor wants to know whether to expand to another 400 adjusters next quarter. Over a week, using a\nsynthetic or anonymised event log and claim dataset (provided or generated), find out what is really\nhappening and set the deployment up to succeed after you leave. Map the stakeholders and veto holders.\nWrite the behavior statement and change plan. Design the usage event schema and adoption dashboard.\nMeasure realized value against a baseline or comparison group. Run a quality-criteria workshop, design\ntraining with verification drills, triage a backlog of 30 raw asks, run one improvement cycle, write the\nexpansion recommendation for the sponsor, and draft a staged handoff plan for the insurer's own team.","deliverables":["A stakeholder map covering at least eight roles (sponsor, team leads, adjusters, IT, security, data owner, compliance, works council or HR), each with what they need, their influence or veto power, and a planned engagement and cadence.","A change plan with a one-sentence behavior statement, the blockers for each group, and incentive or workflow changes, plus a training plan with at least five verification drills built from real failure types and a measure for each.","A usage event schema with no raw prompt or claim content, and a dashboard showing weekly active over eligible, AI-assisted share of replies, week-4 cohort retention, and accepted-unchanged, edit-size and regeneration rates sliced by team and prompt version.","A value-realization report that compares first-response time against a baseline or comparison group, gives a range rather than a point, and breaks the gap between the projected 30% and the realized figure into adoption, quality and attribution parts.","A workshop agenda and output for agreeing a reply-quality rubric, a triaged backlog of the 30 asks with a classification, cluster, score and route for each, and one completed improvement cycle with new regression cases and before/after bucket rates.","A one-page executive update recommending expand, hold or re-scope, with the decision needed and the date, and a staged handoff plan with exit criteria the insurer's team must demonstrate."],"rubric":["The value figure is credited to the assistant only against a baseline or comparison group, comes with a stated range, and explicitly rules out at least one confounder such as early adopters being the fastest adjusters.","The dashboard never reports a raw request count without a denominator, and at least one team-level difference is traced to a cause (team lead, incentive or training) rather than left as noise.","The executive update gives its recommendation and the decision needed in the first two sentences, translates the error rate into cases and cost, and a reader can restate the ask after one read.","At least a third of the 30 asks are reclassified as quality failures, training gaps or model limits and routed to the matching place, and every declined ask has a stated reason sent back to the requester.","The improvement cycle adds regression cases before the fix, shows no regression on the frozen suite, and reports the targeted failure bucket's rate before and after.","Handoff exit criteria are tasks the customer team performs (a gated prompt change, a simulated incident, a credential rotation away from personal accounts), not dates or documents delivered."]}},"topics":[{"id":"change-management","key":"adoption/change-management","module":"adoption","name":"Change management","taxonomy_name":"change management","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":16,"related":["adoption/user-training","adoption/stakeholder-management","adoption/usage-analytics","adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","scoping/workflow-analysis","agents/human-in-the-loop","strategy/stakeholder-alignment"],"tldr":"Change management deliberately moves people from the old way of doing a task to the new one, so a\ndeployed AI system is actually used. Enterprise AI usually fails through an unchanged workflow, not a\nbroken model.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"user-training","key":"adoption/user-training","module":"adoption","name":"User training","taxonomy_name":"user training","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["adoption/change-management","adoption/workshops","adoption/usage-analytics","adoption/handoff-to-customer-teams","agents/human-in-the-loop","customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","security-governance-compliance/pii-protection-dlp"],"tldr":"AI user training teaches a working mental model, not a feature tour: what the system is good at, how it\nfails, how to verify output, what data it may take, and how to report problems.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"workshops","key":"adoption/workshops","module":"adoption","name":"Workshops","taxonomy_name":"workshops","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["adoption/user-training","adoption/stakeholder-management","scoping/ai-use-case-discovery","scoping/workflow-analysis","customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","evals/rubrics"],"tldr":"A workshop is a facilitated session where the people with the knowledge and authority produce a\nspecific artifact (ranked use cases, a workflow map, a rubric, labeled examples) in hours, not weeks.\nWithout a defined output and follow-up, it is a long meeting.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"stakeholder-management","key":"adoption/stakeholder-management","module":"adoption","name":"Stakeholder management","taxonomy_name":"stakeholder management","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["strategy/stakeholder-alignment","adoption/exec-communication","adoption/change-management","adoption/workshops","scoping/dependency-and-risk-management","deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","scoping/success-metrics-kpis"],"tldr":"Stakeholder management means knowing who can help, block or be affected by an AI deployment and engaging\neach on purpose, because AI projects often stall on a late \"no\" from someone never engaged, not on the\nmodel.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"exec-communication","key":"adoption/exec-communication","module":"adoption","name":"Exec communication","taxonomy_name":"exec communication","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["adoption/stakeholder-management","adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","adoption/change-management","scoping/roi-model","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","scoping/demos","strategy/stakeholder-alignment"],"tldr":"Executive communication gives a busy decision-maker the conclusion first, in business terms, with the\ndecision needed and the evidence, readable in minutes. For AI, jargon, unexplained error rates or late\nbad news can get a healthy project cancelled.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"usage-analytics","key":"adoption/usage-analytics","module":"adoption","name":"Usage analytics","taxonomy_name":"usage analytics","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","observability/tracing","observability/logging","evals/offline-vs-online-evaluation","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","safety-security/pii-redaction","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","evals/a-b-testing"],"tldr":"Usage analytics instruments an AI feature to show who uses it, how often, how deeply, and what they do\nwith its output: accept, edit, regenerate or abandon. Traces show what the system did; analytics shows\nwhat people did with it.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","key":"adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","module":"adoption","name":"Adoption & value-realization measurement","taxonomy_name":"adoption & value-realization measurement","kind":"metric","difficulty":"advanced","est_minutes":16,"related":["adoption/usage-analytics","scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","scoping/roi-model","scoping/success-metrics-kpis","evals/a-b-testing","adoption/exec-communication","customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","adoption/change-management"],"tldr":"Adoption measures whether the intended people use the system for the intended work; value realization\nmeasures whether that use changed a business outcome versus a baseline or comparison group. Keep them\napart: adoption leads weekly, realized value renews the contract.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"continuous-improvement","key":"adoption/continuous-improvement","module":"adoption","name":"Continuous improvement","taxonomy_name":"continuous improvement","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":15,"related":["evals/error-analysis","evals/regression-tests","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","customer-data-evaluation/feedback-loops-to-product","llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","llmops-platform/drift","adoption/usage-analytics"],"tldr":"Continuous improvement is the operating loop that turns production evidence into measured fixes:\nclassify failures, prioritise them, fix at the cheapest layer, encode cases in a regression suite, gate\nthe release and confirm online that the bucket shrank.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"handoff-to-customer-teams","key":"adoption/handoff-to-customer-teams","module":"adoption","name":"Handoff to customer teams","taxonomy_name":"handoff to customer teams","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","deployment/runbooks","deployment/hypercare","delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","agent-runtime-architecture/secrets-and-credential-isolation","adoption/continuous-improvement","adoption/user-training"],"tldr":"Handoff transfers *ownership* of a deployed AI system from the vendor or FDE team to the customer's\nengineers: running it, changing it safely, judging its quality. It works in stages, with exit criteria\nthe customer has demonstrated, not a calendar date.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"},{"id":"feature-request-triage","key":"adoption/feature-request-triage","module":"adoption","name":"Feature-request triage","taxonomy_name":"feature-request triage","kind":"practice","difficulty":"core","est_minutes":14,"related":["customer-data-evaluation/feedback-loops-to-product","delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","adoption/continuous-improvement","adoption/stakeholder-management","strategy/ai-roadmap","scoping/problem-decomposition"],"tldr":"Feature-request triage turns customer asks into routed decisions: capture the problem, classify what it\nreally is (often a quality failure, training gap or model limit), cluster across customers, score,\nroute, and tell the requester.","format":"crisp","review":"crisp","status":"ok"}],"stage":"delivery","stage_index":6,"track_order":30}]};
